the usual notation, the number of degrees of freedom can be approximated as the greatest integer less than or equal to*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle ((\\frac{s_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}} + \\frac{s_{2}^{2}}{n_{2}})^{2})/(((s_{1}^{4})/(n_{1}^{2}(n_{1} - 1)) + (s_{2}^{4})/(n_{2}^{2}(n_{2} - 1)))).\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "A restaurant owner believes that the mean number of customers per day has changed since he changed the menu. The number of customers per day after and before the menu change are known to be normally distributed with means $μ_{1}$ and $μ_{2}$. The table below shows the summary statistics of several randomly selected days before and after the menu change, where $n$ is the number of days in each sample, $x^(―)$ is the mean number of customers per day, and $s^{2}$ is the sample variance.\n\n|  | After Menu Change | Before Menu Change |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $n$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $x^(―)$ | $190$ | $150$ |\n| $s^{2}$ | $20^{2}$ | $30^{2}$ |\n\nAssuming that the samples are independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The end-points of the $95 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval for the difference between the mean number of customers are approximately $40 ± 30.364$.\n2. All values within the $95 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval are larger than $10$.\n3. The confidence interval data suggests that the mean number of customers per day has increased by at least $10$ since the menu change.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k, p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k, p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k∖p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.001$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$9$</td>\n<td>$1.833$</td>\n<td>$2.262$</td>\n<td>$4.297$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$10$</td>\n<td>$1.812$</td>\n<td>$2.228$</td>\n<td>$4.144$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$11$</td>\n<td>$1.796$</td>\n<td>$2.201$</td>\n<td>$4.025$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$12$</td>\n<td>$1.782$</td>\n<td>$2.179$</td>\n<td>$3.930$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$13$</td>\n<td>$1.771$</td>\n<td>$2.160$</td>\n<td>$3.852$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n*Using the usual notation, the number of degrees of freedom can be approximated as the greatest integer less than or equal to*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\left(\\frac{s_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}} + \\frac{s_{2}^{2}}{n_{2}}\\right)^{2}\\right)/(((s_{1}^{4})/(n_{1}^{2}(n_{1} - 1)) + (s_{2}^{4})/(n_{2}^{2}(n_{2} - 1)))).\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186193303 :question/problem "The interval notation $y ∈ (- ∞,-\\frac{7}{4})$ corresponds to the inequality" "The interval notation $y ∈ \\left(- ∞,-\\frac{7}{4}\\right)$ corresponds to the inequality"] [:db/cas 17592186193314 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{3}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- the $3$rd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to the $3$rd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}.$\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $3\\times 3$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the second principal component is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{3}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- the $3$rd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to the $3$rd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}.$\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V\\left(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ\\right){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $3\\times 3$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the second principal component is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186193369 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{z}{3}\\right)]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/24/2465f099a94aa966dbf33eb89aa00973e692fa97c887a9b79baff8260f5d31cd.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{z}{3}\\right)\\right]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\left(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\left[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)\\right]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)\\right]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/24/2465f099a94aa966dbf33eb89aa00973e692fa97c887a9b79baff8260f5d31cd.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\left[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186193389 :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<8) & ={\\int }_{2}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{8}\\frac{1}{50}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{100}{x}^{2}]}_{2}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<8) & ={\\int }_{2}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{8}\\frac{1}{50}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{100}{x}^{2}\\right]}_{2}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186193391 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{-3x}$ on the interval $0\\le x<\\infty ,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x={[-\\frac{k}{3}{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{3}=1\\,⟹\\,k=3.\n$$" "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{-3x}$ on the interval $0\\le x<\\infty ,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\left[-\\frac{k}{3}{e}^{-3x}\\right]}_{0}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{3}=1\\,⟹\\,k=3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186193392 :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>8) & =P(8<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{3}{x}]}_{8}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>8) & =P(8<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[-\\frac{3}{x}\\right]}_{8}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186193400 :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[3X-4]=3\\text{E}[X]-4\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate the expected value of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}x\\cdot (\\frac{x}{8}-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{24}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8})|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[3X-4] & =3\\text{E}[X]-4 \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{14}{3}-4 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we simplify using the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[3X-4]=3\\text{E}[X]-4\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate the expected value of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x}{8}-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{24}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}\\right)|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[3X-4] & =3\\text{E}[X]-4 \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{14}{3}-4 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186193802 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to conduct hypothesis tests for the difference between the means of two normal populations with *unknown* and *unequal* population variances.\n\nSuppose we have two normal populations $X$ and $Y{:}$\n\n$X \\sim N(\\mu_x, \\sigma^2_x), \\qquad Y \\sim N(\\mu_y, \\sigma^2_y)$\nwhere $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ are the population means, and $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are the population variances.\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw that the difference between the sample means is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y} \\sim N\\left(\\mu_x - \\mu_y,\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means, and $n_x$ and $n_y$ are the sample sizes. By $z$-scoring this result, we can express this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nIn most practical situations, the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are unknown and must be replaced with estimates $S_x^2$ and $S_y^2$. In this situation, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT &= ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - (μ_{x} - μ_{y}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{S_{x}^{2}}{n_{x}} + \\frac{S_{y}^{2}}{n_{y}}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfollows a $t$-distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\nWe can approximate the number of degrees of freedom $k$ in various ways.\n\n- *Method 1:* We can approximate the degrees of freedom using the so-called **Welch-Satterthwaite approximation:**\n$$\n\\displaystyle k \\approx \\frac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{s_y^2}{n_y} \\right)^2}{\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_x^2}{n_x} \\right)^2}{n_x - 1} + \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_y^2}{n_y} \\right)^2}{n_y - 1}}\n$$\nwhere the final result is rounded *down* to the nearest integer.\n- *Method 2:* We can also approximate the degrees of freedom using the formula\n$k = \\min(n_x - 1, n_y - 1)$\nwhere $\\min(a,b)$ denotes the **minimum** of $a$ and $b$.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll use the second method. You'll get practice applying the first method in separate lessons.\n\nPlease bear in mind that this method is simpler but also more conservative:\n\n- Under Method 2, our estimate for the degrees of freedom will be *smaller* than Method 1.\n- Thus, the tails of the resulting distribution will be fatter, leading to *larger* critical values for a given significance level $\\alpha$. Note that \"larger critical values\" here means larger in the absolute sense, i.e.,\n$$\n|t_{k_1}| >  |t_{k_2}|\n$$\nwhenever $k_1 < k_2$ for critical values $t_{k_1}$ and $t_{k_2}$.\n- Therefore, the probability of a Type I error (i.e., rejecting $H_0$ when it is true) *decreases.*\n\nRemember that as $k\\to\\infty$, the $t$-distribution approaches $N(0,1)$. The standard normal curve has *extremely thin* tails!\n\n![](images/5c/5c73c5fd8613e99814ab47a1a41a46a4511991d3c7eeca33eefe5913a907c10c.png)" "In this lesson, we will learn how to conduct hypothesis tests for the difference between the means of two normal populations with *unknown* and *unequal* population variances.\n\nSuppose we have two normal populations $X$ and $Y{:}$\n\n$X \\sim N(\\mu_x, \\sigma^2_x), \\qquad Y \\sim N(\\mu_y, \\sigma^2_y)$\nwhere $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ are the population means, and $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are the population variances.\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw that the difference between the sample means is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y} \\sim N\\left(\\mu_x - \\mu_y,\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means, and $n_x$ and $n_y$ are the sample sizes. By $z$-scoring this result, we can express this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nIn most practical situations, the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are unknown and must be replaced with estimates $S_x^2$ and $S_y^2$. In this situation, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT &= ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - (μ_{x} - μ_{y}))/\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{S_{x}^{2}}{n_{x}} + \\frac{S_{y}^{2}}{n_{y}}}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfollows a $t$-distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\nWe can approximate the number of degrees of freedom $k$ in various ways.\n\n- *Method 1:* We can approximate the degrees of freedom using the so-called **Welch-Satterthwaite approximation:**\n$$\n\\displaystyle k \\approx \\frac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{s_y^2}{n_y} \\right)^2}{\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_x^2}{n_x} \\right)^2}{n_x - 1} + \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_y^2}{n_y} \\right)^2}{n_y - 1}}\n$$\nwhere the final result is rounded *down* to the nearest integer.\n- *Method 2:* We can also approximate the degrees of freedom using the formula\n$k = \\min(n_x - 1, n_y - 1)$\nwhere $\\min(a,b)$ denotes the **minimum** of $a$ and $b$.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll use the second method. You'll get practice applying the first method in separate lessons.\n\nPlease bear in mind that this method is simpler but also more conservative:\n\n- Under Method 2, our estimate for the degrees of freedom will be *smaller* than Method 1.\n- Thus, the tails of the resulting distribution will be fatter, leading to *larger* critical values for a given significance level $\\alpha$. Note that \"larger critical values\" here means larger in the absolute sense, i.e.,\n$$\n|t_{k_1}| >  |t_{k_2}|\n$$\nwhenever $k_1 < k_2$ for critical values $t_{k_1}$ and $t_{k_2}$.\n- Therefore, the probability of a Type I error (i.e., rejecting $H_0$ when it is true) *decreases.*\n\nRemember that as $k\\to\\infty$, the $t$-distribution approaches $N(0,1)$. The standard normal curve has *extremely thin* tails!\n\n![](images/5c/5c73c5fd8613e99814ab47a1a41a46a4511991d3c7eeca33eefe5913a907c10c.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186193806 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ be an $n \\times m$ observation matrix with standardized data, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature.\n\nSuppose we have the following **singular value decomposition** (SVD) of $X{:}$ \n\n$$\nX = U \\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nNote that in the SVD, the columns of $U$ are orthonormal, which implies that $U^T U = I$ (the identity matrix). \n\nUsing this decomposition, the sample covariance matrix of $X$ can be derived as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T})(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}\\underbrace{({U}^{T}U)}_{I}Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\Sigma^T \\Sigma$ represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ \n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- The matrix $\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\Sigma^T \\Sigma$ is a diagonal $m \\times m$ matrix with the eigenvalues of $C$ (in descending order) on the main diagonal.\n\n- The matrix $V$ is an $m \\times m$ matrix whose columns are the unit eigenvectors of $C.$\n\nTo summarize, if we write down the singular value decomposition of the standardized observation matrix $X,$ the columns of the matrix $V$ in the SVD are the **principal components** of the data." "Let $X$ be an $n \\times m$ observation matrix with standardized data, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature.\n\nSuppose we have the following **singular value decomposition** (SVD) of $X{:}$ \n\n$$\nX = U \\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nNote that in the SVD, the columns of $U$ are orthonormal, which implies that $U^T U = I$ (the identity matrix). \n\nUsing this decomposition, the sample covariance matrix of $X$ can be derived as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T})(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}\\underbrace{({U}^{T}U)}_{I}Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V\\left(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ\\right){V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\Sigma^T \\Sigma$ represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ \n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- The matrix $\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\Sigma^T \\Sigma$ is a diagonal $m \\times m$ matrix with the eigenvalues of $C$ (in descending order) on the main diagonal.\n\n- The matrix $V$ is an $m \\times m$ matrix whose columns are the unit eigenvectors of $C.$\n\nTo summarize, if we write down the singular value decomposition of the standardized observation matrix $X,$ the columns of the matrix $V$ in the SVD are the **principal components** of the data."] [:db/cas 17592186193809 :tutorial/content "To find the total variance in terms of singular values, define the change of variables $Y = XV.$ The covariance of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{Y}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{Y}^{T}Y=\\frac{1}{n-1}(XV)^{T}(XV)={V}^{T}\\underbrace{(\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X)}_{{C}_{X}}V.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $V$ contains the eigenvectors of $C_X,$ the matrix $C_Y$ is diagonal and contains the eigenvalues $\\lambda_i$ of $C_X{:}$\n\n$$\nC_Y = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\lambda }_{1} & \\ldots  & 0 \\\\ \\vdots  & \\ddots  & \\vdots  \\\\ 0 & \\ldots  & {\\lambda }_{m}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThe total variance is defined as the trace (sum of diagonal elements) of the covariance matrix. A key property of the trace is that it is invariant under orthogonal transformations like $Y=XV.$ Therefore, the total variance of $Y$ is the same as the total variance of $X{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Total Variance} = \\text{Tr}(C_X) = \\text{Tr}(C_Y) = \\sum_{i=1}^m \\lambda_i.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\lambda_i = \\frac{\\sigma_i^2}{n-1},$ we conclude that the total variance is *proportional to* the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\textrm{Total Variance} \\:\\propto\\: \\sum_{i=1}^m \\sigma^2_i.}\n$$\n\nLet's see an example." "To find the total variance in terms of singular values, define the change of variables $Y = XV.$ The covariance of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{Y}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{Y}^{T}Y=\\frac{1}{n-1}(XV)^{T}(XV)={V}^{T}\\underbrace{\\left(\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X\\right)}_{{C}_{X}}V.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $V$ contains the eigenvectors of $C_X,$ the matrix $C_Y$ is diagonal and contains the eigenvalues $\\lambda_i$ of $C_X{:}$\n\n$$\nC_Y = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\lambda }_{1} & \\ldots  & 0 \\\\ \\vdots  & \\ddots  & \\vdots  \\\\ 0 & \\ldots  & {\\lambda }_{m}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThe total variance is defined as the trace (sum of diagonal elements) of the covariance matrix. A key property of the trace is that it is invariant under orthogonal transformations like $Y=XV.$ Therefore, the total variance of $Y$ is the same as the total variance of $X{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Total Variance} = \\text{Tr}(C_X) = \\text{Tr}(C_Y) = \\sum_{i=1}^m \\lambda_i.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\lambda_i = \\frac{\\sigma_i^2}{n-1},$ we conclude that the total variance is *proportional to* the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\textrm{Total Variance} \\:\\propto\\: \\sum_{i=1}^m \\sigma^2_i.}\n$$\n\nLet's see an example."] [:db/cas 17592186193816 :tutorial/content "Let's return to our first example, where we wish to conduct a hypothesis test to determine whether a particular coin is unfair.\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- We defined $p$ as \"the probability that the coin lands on heads when tossed randomly.\"\n- Since we want to check whether the coin is unfair, we have the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{0}:p & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {H}_{1}:p & \\ne \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The significance level is $\\alpha = 5\\%$.\n\nUnder the conditions specified in the null hypothesis, the random variable $X$ is a binomial random variable given by\n\n$$\nX\\sim B\\left(10,\\dfrac12\\right)\n$$\n\nThe probability mass function of $X$ under the null hypothesis is shown below.\n\n![](images/79/79306d39f30f044beb30aaa7f6356b7a226c0f3958597d2e230601d51474b801.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When we select a significance level $\\alpha$ in a two-tailed test, the convention is to allow $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ at either tail.\n- So if $\\alpha = 5\\%$, we have $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2} = 2.5\\%$ at either tail.\n\nNow, suppose we conduct our experiment and get $2$ heads out of $10$ tosses.\n\nIf the result $X=2$ is statistically significant, it will lie in the critical region in the *left* tail of the probability distribution of $f(x)$.\n\nWe can compute the probability of getting\n\n$$\nX\\leq 2\n$$\n\nheads under the null hypothesis using our knowledge of the binomial distribution:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( X \\le  2 ) & \\displaystyle  = P ( X = 0 ) + P ( X = 1 ) + P ( X = 2 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0} \\right) {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{0} {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10 - 0} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1} \\right) {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{1} {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10 - 1} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{2} \\right) {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{2} {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10 - 2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1 \\cdot  {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10} + 10 \\cdot  {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10} + 45 \\cdot  {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 56 \\cdot  {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.055 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 5.5 \\mathrm{\\% } .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThis probability is *larger than*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac\\alpha2 = 2.5\\%\n$$\n\nThis means that our result is *not* statistically significant. In other words, it is \"likely enough\" that this event could have occurred if the null hypothesis is true.\n\nTherefore, we should *not* reject the null hypothesis. Consequently, we conclude that the coin is fair." "Let's return to our first example, where we wish to conduct a hypothesis test to determine whether a particular coin is unfair.\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- We defined $p$ as \"the probability that the coin lands on heads when tossed randomly.\"\n- Since we want to check whether the coin is unfair, we have the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{0}:p & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {H}_{1}:p & \\ne \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The significance level is $\\alpha = 5\\%$.\n\nUnder the conditions specified in the null hypothesis, the random variable $X$ is a binomial random variable given by\n\n$$\nX\\sim B\\left(10,\\dfrac12\\right)\n$$\n\nThe probability mass function of $X$ under the null hypothesis is shown below.\n\n![](images/79/79306d39f30f044beb30aaa7f6356b7a226c0f3958597d2e230601d51474b801.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When we select a significance level $\\alpha$ in a two-tailed test, the convention is to allow $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ at either tail.\n- So if $\\alpha = 5\\%$, we have $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2} = 2.5\\%$ at either tail.\n\nNow, suppose we conduct our experiment and get $2$ heads out of $10$ tosses.\n\nIf the result $X=2$ is statistically significant, it will lie in the critical region in the *left* tail of the probability distribution of $f(x)$.\n\nWe can compute the probability of getting\n\n$$\nX\\leq 2\n$$\n\nheads under the null hypothesis using our knowledge of the binomial distribution:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( X \\le  2 ) & \\displaystyle  = P ( X = 0 ) + P ( X = 1 ) + P ( X = 2 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0} \\right) {\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} \\right)}^{0} {\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} \\right)}^{10 - 0} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1} \\right) {\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} \\right)}^{1} {\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} \\right)}^{10 - 1} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{2} \\right) {\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} \\right)}^{2} {\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} \\right)}^{10 - 2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1 \\cdot  {\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} \\right)}^{10} + 10 \\cdot  {\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} \\right)}^{10} + 45 \\cdot  {\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} \\right)}^{10} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 56 \\cdot  {\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} \\right)}^{10} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.055 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 5.5 \\mathrm{\\% } .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThis probability is *larger than*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac\\alpha2 = 2.5\\%\n$$\n\nThis means that our result is *not* statistically significant. In other words, it is \"likely enough\" that this event could have occurred if the null hypothesis is true.\n\nTherefore, we should *not* reject the null hypothesis. Consequently, we conclude that the coin is fair."] [:db/cas 17592186193825 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll use our knowledge of solving radical equations to compute inverses of radical functions. \n\nFor example, let's consider the following radical function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 3\\sqrt{x} + 2\n$$\n\nTo compute the inverse of $f(x),$ we follow the usual four steps:\n\n**Step 1:** First, we replace $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 3\\sqrt{x} + 2\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we swap $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx = 3\\sqrt{y} + 2\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Next, we solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\sqrt{y}+2 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{y} & =x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{3}(x-2))}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{9}(x-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Finally, we replace $y$ with $f^{-1}(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{9}(x - 2)^2\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0, \\qquad f(x) \\geq 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of $f^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 2, \\qquad f^{-1}(x) \\geq 0.\n$$" "In this lesson, we'll use our knowledge of solving radical equations to compute inverses of radical functions. \n\nFor example, let's consider the following radical function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 3\\sqrt{x} + 2\n$$\n\nTo compute the inverse of $f(x),$ we follow the usual four steps:\n\n**Step 1:** First, we replace $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 3\\sqrt{x} + 2\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we swap $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx = 3\\sqrt{y} + 2\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Next, we solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\sqrt{y}+2 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{y} & =x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}(x-2)\\right)}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{9}(x-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Finally, we replace $y$ with $f^{-1}(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{9}(x - 2)^2\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0, \\qquad f(x) \\geq 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of $f^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 2, \\qquad f^{-1}(x) \\geq 0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186193893 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we made use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_0] = \\beta_0,\n\\qquad\n\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_0}] = \\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now derive these. First, we will show that\n\n$$\nS_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})y_i.\n$$\n\nWe can show this is true using some straightforward manipulations of the summation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})\\overset{―}{y} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}n\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we define $k_i$ as\n\n$$\nk_i = \\dfrac{ x_i-\\overline{x}}{S_{xx}}.\n$$\n\nThe following properties can be shown using straightforward algebra:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i=0\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i x_i=1\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i^2=\\dfrac{1}{S_{xx}}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_0} = \\overline{y} - \\widehat{\\beta_1}\\overline{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite our expression for $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{{\\beta }_{0}} & =\\overset{―}{y}-{\\beta }_{1}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}}{{S}_{xx}}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're now in a position to compute $\\textrm{E}[{\\widehat{\\beta_0} }]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[{\\widehat{\\beta_0} }].$\n\n- For the mean, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\widehat{{\\beta }_{0}}] & =\\frac{1}{n}\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}]-\\overset{―}{x}\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}\\text{E}[{y}_{i}]-\\overset{―}{x}{\\sum }_{i}\\text{E}[{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}[{\\beta }_{0}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}+{\\beta }_{1}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{x}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}[{\\beta }_{0}{\\cancel{{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}}}^{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{\\cancel{{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{x}_{i}}}^{1}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\beta }_{0}}{n}{\\sum }_{i}1+\\frac{{\\beta }_{1}}{n}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\beta }_{0}}{n}\\cdot n+\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1}-\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\  & ={\\beta }_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the variance, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\widehat{{\\beta }_{0}}] & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}]+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}\\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}{\\sum }_{i}\\text{Var}[{y}_{i}]+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}^{2}\\text{Var}[{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}n{\\sigma }^{2}+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}^{2}{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}n{\\sigma }^{2}+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sigma }^{2}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sigma }^{2}+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sigma }^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{S}_{xx}} \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2}(\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo finally, we have\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_0} \\sim N\\left(\\beta_0,\\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)\\right).\n$$" "Earlier, we made use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_0] = \\beta_0,\n\\qquad\n\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_0}] = \\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now derive these. First, we will show that\n\n$$\nS_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})y_i.\n$$\n\nWe can show this is true using some straightforward manipulations of the summation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})\\overset{―}{y} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}n\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we define $k_i$ as\n\n$$\nk_i = \\dfrac{ x_i-\\overline{x}}{S_{xx}}.\n$$\n\nThe following properties can be shown using straightforward algebra:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i=0\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i x_i=1\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i^2=\\dfrac{1}{S_{xx}}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_0} = \\overline{y} - \\widehat{\\beta_1}\\overline{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite our expression for $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{{\\beta }_{0}} & =\\overset{―}{y}-{\\beta }_{1}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}}{{S}_{xx}}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're now in a position to compute $\\textrm{E}[{\\widehat{\\beta_0} }]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[{\\widehat{\\beta_0} }].$\n\n- For the mean, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\widehat{{\\beta }_{0}}] & =\\frac{1}{n}\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}]-\\overset{―}{x}\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}\\text{E}[{y}_{i}]-\\overset{―}{x}{\\sum }_{i}\\text{E}[{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}[{\\beta }_{0}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}+{\\beta }_{1}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{x}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}[{\\beta }_{0}{\\cancel{{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}}}^{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{\\cancel{{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{x}_{i}}}^{1}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\beta }_{0}}{n}{\\sum }_{i}1+\\frac{{\\beta }_{1}}{n}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\beta }_{0}}{n}\\cdot n+\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1}-\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\  & ={\\beta }_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the variance, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\widehat{{\\beta }_{0}}] & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}]+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}\\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}{\\sum }_{i}\\text{Var}[{y}_{i}]+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}^{2}\\text{Var}[{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}n{\\sigma }^{2}+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}^{2}{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}n{\\sigma }^{2}+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sigma }^{2}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sigma }^{2}+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sigma }^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{S}_{xx}} \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo finally, we have\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_0} \\sim N\\left(\\beta_0,\\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186193894 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to construct confidence intervals for the difference between the means of two normal populations with unknown and unequal variances.\n\nSuppose we have two populations $X_1$ and $X_2$,\n\n$X_1\\sim N(\\mu_1, \\sigma^2_1), \\qquad X_2\\sim N(\\mu_2, \\sigma^2_2)$,\nwhere $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$ are the population means, and $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ are the population variances.\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw that the difference between the sample means follows a standard normal distribution:\n\n$$\nZ = \\dfrac{\\overline{X_1} - \\overline{X_2}- (\\mu_1 - \\mu_2)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_1^2}{n_1} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_2^2}{n_2}}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{X}_1$ and $\\overline{X}_2$ are the sample means, and $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the sample sizes.\n\nIn most practical situations, the population variances $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ are unknown and must be replaced with estimates $S_1^2$ and $S_2^2$. In this situation, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT &= ((X^(―)_{1} - X^(―)_{2}) - (μ_{1} - μ_{2}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{S_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}} + \\frac{S_{2}^{2}}{n_{2}}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfollows a $t$-distribution with $r$ degrees of freedom, where $r$ can be approximated from sample data using the formula\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_1^2}{n_1} + \\dfrac{s_2^2}{n_2} \\right)^2}{\\dfrac{s_1^4}{n_1^2(n_1-1)} + \\dfrac{s_2^4}{n_2^2(n_2-1)}}\n$$\n\nrounded *down* to the nearest integer.\n\nFollowing the usual procedure, a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1 - \\mu_2$ is given by\n$(\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2) \\pm E$\n\nwhere\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E=t_{r, \\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{s_1^2}{n_1} + \\dfrac{s_2^2}{n_2}}\n$$ is the margin of error, and\n- $P(T > t_{r, \\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$.\n\nThis confidence interval is sometimes called a **Welch's $\\boldsymbol t$-interval,** named after its inventor, the British statistician Bernard Lewis Welch.\n\nFinally, note that if the underlying populations $X_1$ and $X_2$ are *not* normally distributed, we can still use this procedure to construct a confidence interval *provided that* the sample sizes $n_1$ and $n_2$ are sufficiently large (typically,\n\n$$\nn_1, n_2 \\geq 30\n$$\n\n).\n\nLet's see some examples." "In this lesson, we will learn how to construct confidence intervals for the difference between the means of two normal populations with unknown and unequal variances.\n\nSuppose we have two populations $X_1$ and $X_2$,\n\n$X_1\\sim N(\\mu_1, \\sigma^2_1), \\qquad X_2\\sim N(\\mu_2, \\sigma^2_2)$,\nwhere $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$ are the population means, and $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ are the population variances.\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw that the difference between the sample means follows a standard normal distribution:\n\n$$\nZ = \\dfrac{\\overline{X_1} - \\overline{X_2}- (\\mu_1 - \\mu_2)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_1^2}{n_1} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_2^2}{n_2}}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{X}_1$ and $\\overline{X}_2$ are the sample means, and $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the sample sizes.\n\nIn most practical situations, the population variances $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ are unknown and must be replaced with estimates $S_1^2$ and $S_2^2$. In this situation, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT &= ((X^(―)_{1} - X^(―)_{2}) - (μ_{1} - μ_{2}))/\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{S_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}} + \\frac{S_{2}^{2}}{n_{2}}}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfollows a $t$-distribution with $r$ degrees of freedom, where $r$ can be approximated from sample data using the formula\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_1^2}{n_1} + \\dfrac{s_2^2}{n_2} \\right)^2}{\\dfrac{s_1^4}{n_1^2(n_1-1)} + \\dfrac{s_2^4}{n_2^2(n_2-1)}}\n$$\n\nrounded *down* to the nearest integer.\n\nFollowing the usual procedure, a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1 - \\mu_2$ is given by\n$(\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2) \\pm E$\n\nwhere\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E=t_{r, \\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{s_1^2}{n_1} + \\dfrac{s_2^2}{n_2}}\n$$ is the margin of error, and\n- $P(T > t_{r, \\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$.\n\nThis confidence interval is sometimes called a **Welch's $\\boldsymbol t$-interval,** named after its inventor, the British statistician Bernard Lewis Welch.\n\nFinally, note that if the underlying populations $X_1$ and $X_2$ are *not* normally distributed, we can still use this procedure to construct a confidence interval *provided that* the sample sizes $n_1$ and $n_2$ are sufficiently large (typically,\n\n$$\nn_1, n_2 \\geq 30\n$$\n\n).\n\nLet's see some examples."] [:db/cas 17592186193895 :tutorial/content "Consider two samples of size $n_x = 14$ and $n_y = 18$ from independent normal populations $X$ and $Y$ with unequal population variances, where the unbiased estimates of $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ from these samples are\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=47\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\overline{y}=40\n$$\n\nand unbiased estimates of the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are $s_x^2=7^2$ and $s_y^2=8^2$.\n\nWe wish to carry out a hypothesis test at the $10\\%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $\\mu_x$ of $X$ is larger than the population mean $\\mu_y$ of $Y$.\n\nTo solve a problem like this, we proceed as follows.\n\nWe are told that the populations are normally distributed, but we do not know the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{S_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{S_y^2}{n_y}}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means,\n- $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ are the population means,\n- $n_x$ and $n_y$ are the sample sizes, and\n- $S_x^2$ and $S_y^2$ are the sample variances.\n\nIn our example, the null and alternative hypotheses are the following:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu_x = \\mu_y$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu_x > \\mu_y$ is the alternative (one-tailed) hypothesis\n\nWe can estimate the number of degrees of freedom $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk &= \\min (n_{x} - 1, n_{y} - 1) \\\\\n&= \\min (14 - 1, 18 - 1) \\\\\n&= \\min (13, 17) \\\\\n&= 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e.\n\n$$\n\\mu_x - \\mu_y = 0\n$$\n\nwe compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt &= (x^(―) - y^(―) - (μ_{x} - μ_{y}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{s_{x}^{2}}{n_{x}} + \\frac{s_{y}^{2}}{n_{y}}}) \\\\\n&= (47 - 40 - (0))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{7^{2}}{14} + \\frac{8^{2}}{18}}) \\\\\n&\\approx 2.635\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the critical region corresponding to our significance level.\n\nGiven a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k,p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(T > t_{k,p}) = p\n$$\n\nfor some particular values of $k$ and $p$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$13$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"PapayaWhip\">$\\color{blue}\\,1.350\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.771\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.160\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.650\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.012\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$14$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.345\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.761\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.145\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.624\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.977\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$15$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.341\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.753\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.131\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.602\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.947\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAccording to the table, the $10\\%$ one-tailed critical value for $k = 13$ degrees of freedom is\n\n$$\nt \\approx 1.350\n$$\n\nSince the alternative hypothesis is $\\mu_x > \\mu_y$, we must consider the right tail. So, our critical region is\n\n$$\nT \\geq \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.350}\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that our test statistic $(2.635)$ lies in the critical region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/81/813c8270ac07466c74e50fbc9377d83cfad9e773057d40183550d5dbd204868a.png)\n\nSo, we reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude that there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{sufficient}}$ evidence that, at the $10\\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu_x > \\mu_y$." "Consider two samples of size $n_x = 14$ and $n_y = 18$ from independent normal populations $X$ and $Y$ with unequal population variances, where the unbiased estimates of $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ from these samples are\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=47\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\overline{y}=40\n$$\n\nand unbiased estimates of the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are $s_x^2=7^2$ and $s_y^2=8^2$.\n\nWe wish to carry out a hypothesis test at the $10\\%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $\\mu_x$ of $X$ is larger than the population mean $\\mu_y$ of $Y$.\n\nTo solve a problem like this, we proceed as follows.\n\nWe are told that the populations are normally distributed, but we do not know the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{S_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{S_y^2}{n_y}}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means,\n- $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ are the population means,\n- $n_x$ and $n_y$ are the sample sizes, and\n- $S_x^2$ and $S_y^2$ are the sample variances.\n\nIn our example, the null and alternative hypotheses are the following:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu_x = \\mu_y$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu_x > \\mu_y$ is the alternative (one-tailed) hypothesis\n\nWe can estimate the number of degrees of freedom $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk &= \\min (n_{x} - 1, n_{y} - 1) \\\\\n&= \\min (14 - 1, 18 - 1) \\\\\n&= \\min (13, 17) \\\\\n&= 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e.\n\n$$\n\\mu_x - \\mu_y = 0\n$$\n\nwe compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt &= (x^(―) - y^(―) - (μ_{x} - μ_{y}))/\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{s_{x}^{2}}{n_{x}} + \\frac{s_{y}^{2}}{n_{y}}}\\right) \\\\\n&= (47 - 40 - (0))/\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{7^{2}}{14} + \\frac{8^{2}}{18}}\\right) \\\\\n&\\approx 2.635\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the critical region corresponding to our significance level.\n\nGiven a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k,p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(T > t_{k,p}) = p\n$$\n\nfor some particular values of $k$ and $p$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$13$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"PapayaWhip\">$\\color{blue}\\,1.350\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.771\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.160\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.650\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.012\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$14$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.345\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.761\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.145\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.624\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.977\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$15$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.341\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.753\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.131\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.602\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.947\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAccording to the table, the $10\\%$ one-tailed critical value for $k = 13$ degrees of freedom is\n\n$$\nt \\approx 1.350\n$$\n\nSince the alternative hypothesis is $\\mu_x > \\mu_y$, we must consider the right tail. So, our critical region is\n\n$$\nT \\geq \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.350}\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that our test statistic $(2.635)$ lies in the critical region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/81/813c8270ac07466c74e50fbc9377d83cfad9e773057d40183550d5dbd204868a.png)\n\nSo, we reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude that there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{sufficient}}$ evidence that, at the $10\\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu_x > \\mu_y$."] [:db/cas 17592186195293 :answer/value "(- ∞, \\frac{10}{3})" "\\left(- ∞, \\frac{10}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186195307 :answer/value "[- \\frac{7}{2}, \\frac{7}{2}]" "\\left[- \\frac{7}{2}, \\frac{7}{2}\\right]"] [:db/cas 17592186196260 :answer/value "\\log_{3} (2 + \\frac{1}{ε})" "\\log_{3} \\left(2 + \\frac{1}{ε}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186196263 :answer/value "\\log_{3} (2 + \\frac{7}{ε})" "\\log_{3} \\left(2 + \\frac{7}{ε}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186196267 :answer/value "\\log_{3} (2 + \\frac{1}{ε})" "\\log_{3} \\left(2 + \\frac{1}{ε}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186196268 :answer/value "\\log_{3} (2 + \\frac{7}{ε})" "\\log_{3} \\left(2 + \\frac{7}{ε}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186198015 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y-x \\leq 1, \\qquad 1 \\leq x+y \\leq 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu = y-x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad v = x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}:\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]}\n\\to\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u ( x , y ) \\\\ v ( x , y )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-x}{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{u}{v}\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y-x \\leq 1, \\qquad 1 \\leq x+y \\leq 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu = y-x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad v = x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}:\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]}\n\\to\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u ( x , y ) \\\\ v ( x , y )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-x}{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\left[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{u}{v}\\,du\\right]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}{\\left[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{0}^{1}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198016 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =  (2x+y)y^2\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\n\\qquad\nv=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u ( x , y ) \\\\ v ( x , y )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},0) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+0,0) \\\\  & =(3,0) \\\\ {\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{2},0) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+0,0) \\\\  & =(5,0) \\\\ {\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(1,3) & =(2\\cdot 1+3,3) \\\\  & =(5,3) \\\\ {\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(0,3) & =(2\\cdot 0+3,3) \\\\  & =(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 3 \\leq u \\leq 5, \\quad 0 \\leq v \\leq 3 \\big\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/c2/c296b1ee0451686608ec4b06c29d114ff1918233886d8471d3b2c118851aa25e.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} =\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y){y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{3}^{5}u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{3}^{5}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =  (2x+y)y^2\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\n\\qquad\nv=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u ( x , y ) \\\\ v ( x , y )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{2},0\\right) & =\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+0,0\\right) \\\\  & =(3,0) \\\\ {\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}\\left(\\frac{5}{2},0\\right) & =\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+0,0\\right) \\\\  & =(5,0) \\\\ {\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(1,3) & =(2\\cdot 1+3,3) \\\\  & =(5,3) \\\\ {\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(0,3) & =(2\\cdot 0+3,3) \\\\  & =(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 3 \\leq u \\leq 5, \\quad 0 \\leq v \\leq 3 \\big\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/c2/c296b1ee0451686608ec4b06c29d114ff1918233886d8471d3b2c118851aa25e.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} =\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y){y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{3}^{5}u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{3}^{5}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\right]}_{0}^{3}\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}\\right]}_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198019 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $\\Delta$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. It lies between the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. It lies between the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/79/79869cf64ec827934397588e25d06cba0c9d12c35ecbea5cdd6aaf6cfcf12e24.png)" "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $\\Delta$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=\\left(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=\\left(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. It lies between the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. It lies between the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/79/79869cf64ec827934397588e25d06cba0c9d12c35ecbea5cdd6aaf6cfcf12e24.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186198020 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2u & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}4uv⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}x-y,\\hspace{0.222em}xyz⟩{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y={v}^{2}\\,z=1} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}{v}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨{u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}{v}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}4uv⟩ \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\cdot 0+({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\cdot 0+{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\cdot 4uv \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}{v}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}4{u}^{3}{v}^{3}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2u & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}4uv⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}x-y,\\hspace{0.222em}xyz⟩{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y={v}^{2}\\,z=1} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}{v}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨{u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}{v}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}4uv⟩ \\\\  & =({u}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\cdot 0+({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\cdot 0+{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\cdot 4uv \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}{v}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}4{u}^{3}{v}^{3}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198021 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z = g(x,y)$ for $(x,y) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( -P\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial x} - Q\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial y} + R \\right) \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf F(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨-y,\\,x,\\,z+2⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{-y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{x}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨-y,\\,x,\\,z+2⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot (2x)-(x)\\cdot (2y)+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+2y]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{7x}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ given by $z = g(x,y)$ for $(x,y) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( -P\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial x} - Q\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial y} + R \\right) \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf F(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨-y,\\,x,\\,z+2⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{-y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{x}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨-y,\\,x,\\,z+2⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot (2x)-(x)\\cdot (2y)+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\left[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+2y\\right]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}+2\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left({x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{7x}{3}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198022 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+2u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+2u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\hspace{0.222em}3,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}x,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩|}_{x=1+2u+v\\,y=u+v\\,z=u-v} \\\\  & =⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}1+2u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}1+2u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\hspace{0.222em}3,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)\\cdot (-2)+(1+2u+v)\\cdot 3+(u+v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2u+2v+3+6u+3v+u+v \\\\  & =5u+6v+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}x,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5u+6v+3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5u+6v+3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[5uv+3{v}^{2}+3v]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(5u+6)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{5}{2}{u}^{2}+6u]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =10+12 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+2u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+2u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\hspace{0.222em}3,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}x,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩|}_{x=1+2u+v\\,y=u+v\\,z=u-v} \\\\  & =⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}1+2u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}1+2u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\hspace{0.222em}3,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)\\cdot (-2)+(1+2u+v)\\cdot 3+(u+v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2u+2v+3+6u+3v+u+v \\\\  & =5u+6v+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}x,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5u+6v+3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5u+6v+3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[5uv+3{v}^{2}+3v]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(5u+6)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{5}{2}{u}^{2}+6u\\right]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =10+12 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198026 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)),\\,1\\le u\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis and whose endpoints lie on circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/81/81650cc0dc43537ec916f2c68af66ed5581c861812f276dda2b23baccc2fb491.png)" "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =\\left(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)\\right),\\,1\\le u\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis and whose endpoints lie on circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/81/81650cc0dc43537ec916f2c68af66ed5581c861812f276dda2b23baccc2fb491.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186198027 :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x-y}x\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-x-y}x\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}x\\cdot [z]_{z=0}^{z=3-x-y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}x(3-x-y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3x-{x}^{2}-xy\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3x-{x}^{2}-xy\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}3x-{x}^{2}-xy\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[(3x-{x}^{2})y-\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3x-{x}^{2})(x-0)-\\frac{1}{2}x({x}^{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3}\\,dx & ={[{x}^{3}-\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =8-6 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x-y}x\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-x-y}x\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}x\\cdot [z]_{z=0}^{z=3-x-y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}x(3-x-y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3x-{x}^{2}-xy\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3x-{x}^{2}-xy\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}3x-{x}^{2}-xy\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\left[(3x-{x}^{2})y-\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{2}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3x-{x}^{2})(x-0)-\\frac{1}{2}x({x}^{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3}\\,dx & ={\\left[{x}^{3}-\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{4}\\right]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =8-6 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198028 :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{xy}\\frac{24}{x}\\,dz]dA & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{24}{x}[z]_{z=0}^{z=xy}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{24}{x}(xy-0)\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}24y\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}24y\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{x}24y\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[{\\int }_{1}^{x}24y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[12{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}12({x}^{2}-1)\\,dx \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}-1\\,dx \\\\  & =12{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-x]}_{x=1}^{x=3} \\\\  & =12(\\frac{1}{3}(27-1)-(3-1)) \\\\  & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\left[{\\int }_{0}^{xy}\\frac{24}{x}\\,dz\\right]dA & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{24}{x}[z]_{z=0}^{z=xy}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{24}{x}(xy-0)\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}24y\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}24y\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{x}24y\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[{\\int }_{1}^{x}24y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[12{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}12({x}^{2}-1)\\,dx \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}-1\\,dx \\\\  & =12{\\left[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-x\\right]}_{x=1}^{x=3} \\\\  & =12\\left(\\frac{1}{3}(27-1)-(3-1)\\right) \\\\  & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198029 :question/worked-solution "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} 6y \\, \\textrm{d}V\n= \n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[ \\int_{0}^{3+y-x} 6y \\, \\mathrm{d}z \\right] \\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{3+y-x}6y\\,dz]dA & ={∬}_{D}[6yz]_{z=0}^{z=3+y-x}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6y(3+y-x)\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate the double integral above over the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27c368075198bca9aba04c9ef0d1938b3f47bba268e39b591b4b24b02a404a9b.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}6y(3+y-x)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x-2}^{0}6y(3+y-x)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{x-2}^{0}18y+6{y}^{2}-6xy\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[9{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}]}_{y=x-2}^{y=0}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}0-9(x-2)^{2}-2(x-2)^{3}+3x(x-2)^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-2)^{2}(x-5)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+24x-20\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}-20x]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(4-24+48-40)-0 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} 6y \\, \\textrm{d}V\n= \n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[ \\int_{0}^{3+y-x} 6y \\, \\mathrm{d}z \\right] \\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{3+y-x}6y\\,dz]dA & ={∬}_{D}[6yz]_{z=0}^{z=3+y-x}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6y(3+y-x)\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate the double integral above over the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27c368075198bca9aba04c9ef0d1938b3f47bba268e39b591b4b24b02a404a9b.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}6y(3+y-x)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x-2}^{0}6y(3+y-x)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{x-2}^{0}18y+6{y}^{2}-6xy\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[9{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}]}_{y=x-2}^{y=0}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}0-9(x-2)^{2}-2(x-2)^{3}+3x(x-2)^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-2)^{2}(x-5)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+24x-20\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}-20x\\right]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(4-24+48-40)-0 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198031 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u,\\: v,\\: -3u+2v \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-3u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-3⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-3u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z+2y,\\,3y+z,\\,x-z⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=-3u+2v} \\\\  & =⟨(-3u+2v)+2v,\\,3v+(-3u+2v),\\,u-(-3u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3u+4v,\\,-3u+5v,\\,4u-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨-3u+4v,\\,-3u+5v,\\,4u-2v⟩\\cdot ⟨3,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(-3u+4v)\\cdot (3)+(-3u+5v)\\cdot (-2)+(4u-2v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-9u+12v+6u-10v+4u-2v \\\\  & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z+2y,\\,3y+z,\\,x-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}u\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is ${\\color{blue}u}.$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u,\\: v,\\: -3u+2v \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-3u+2v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-3⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-3u+2v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z+2y,\\,3y+z,\\,x-z⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=-3u+2v} \\\\  & =⟨(-3u+2v)+2v,\\,3v+(-3u+2v),\\,u-(-3u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3u+4v,\\,-3u+5v,\\,4u-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨-3u+4v,\\,-3u+5v,\\,4u-2v⟩\\cdot ⟨3,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(-3u+4v)\\cdot (3)+(-3u+5v)\\cdot (-2)+(4u-2v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-9u+12v+6u-10v+4u-2v \\\\  & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z+2y,\\,3y+z,\\,x-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}u\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is ${\\color{blue}u}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186198032 :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $\\mathbf F =  \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1+xyz\\rangle$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/97/977d93ad65c9f3690def4242f98f4de5e5c89faf02fe47d06f5ed251d8a4a292.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let $S_1$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z = 1$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_1$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z = 1$ on $S_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1+xyz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1+xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =1+xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}1+xy\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1+xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}1+xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+\\frac{1}{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[y+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let $S_2$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z = 0$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_2$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_2 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, -1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z = 0$ on $S_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1+xyz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{2} & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-1\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =-(1-0)(1-0) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}+(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $\\mathbf F =  \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1+xyz\\rangle$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/97/977d93ad65c9f3690def4242f98f4de5e5c89faf02fe47d06f5ed251d8a4a292.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let $S_1$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z = 1$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_1$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z = 1$ on $S_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1+xyz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1+xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =1+xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}1+xy\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1+xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}1+xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\left[x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y\\right]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+\\frac{1}{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\left[y+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let $S_2$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z = 0$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_2$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_2 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, -1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z = 0$ on $S_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1+xyz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{2} & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-1\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =-(1-0)(1-0) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}+(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198034 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y = h(x,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,z) = \\left\\langle x, \\: h(x,z),\\: z \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨0,\\frac{∂h}{∂z},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\frac{∂h}{∂x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{∂h}{∂z} & 1\\end{vmatrix}=⟨\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,-1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $\\mathbf j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}'_z \\times \\mathbf{r}'_x = - \\mathbf{r}'_x \\times \\mathbf{r}'_z =   \\left \\langle -\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x}, \\: 1, \\: -\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial z} \\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,z))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,1,\\,-\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}+Q-R\\frac{∂h}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y = h(x,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,z) = \\left\\langle x, \\: h(x,z),\\: z \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =\\left\\langle 1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,0\\right\\rangle  \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =\\left\\langle 0,\\frac{∂h}{∂z},\\,1\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\frac{∂h}{∂x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{∂h}{∂z} & 1\\end{vmatrix}=\\left\\langle \\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,-1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $\\mathbf j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}'_z \\times \\mathbf{r}'_x = - \\mathbf{r}'_x \\times \\mathbf{r}'_z =   \\left \\langle -\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x}, \\: 1, \\: -\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial z} \\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,z))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot \\left\\langle -\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,1,\\,-\\frac{∂h}{∂z}\\right\\rangle \\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}+Q-R\\frac{∂h}{∂z}\\right)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198035 :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}y+x{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}y+x{z}^{2}\\,dz]dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[{x}^{2}yz+\\frac{x{z}^{3}}{3}]}_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}y(1-0)+x(\\frac{1}{3}-0)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}x\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}y+\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}]}_{x=-1}^{x=2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}y({2}^{3}-(-1)^{3})+\\frac{1}{6}({2}^{2}-(-1)^{2}))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}3y+\\frac{1}{2}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}3y+\\frac{1}{2}\\,dx & ={[\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{y}{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & =(6+1)-(\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}y+x{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}y+x{z}^{2}\\,dz]dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\left[{x}^{2}yz+\\frac{x{z}^{3}}{3}\\right]}_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}y(1-0)+x\\left(\\frac{1}{3}-0\\right)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}x\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\left[{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}x\\,dx\\right]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\left[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}y+\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}\\right]}_{x=-1}^{x=2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}y({2}^{3}-(-1)^{3})+\\frac{1}{6}({2}^{2}-(-1)^{2}))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}3y+\\frac{1}{2}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}3y+\\frac{1}{2}\\,dx & ={\\left[\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{y}{2}\\right]}_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & =(6+1)-\\left(\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198036 :question/worked-solution "Since the domain of integration $R = [1,2] \\times \\left[0,\\pi/2\\right] \\times [1,4]$ is a rectangular box and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{dx}{x}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy{\\int }_{1}^{4}dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}\\,dx{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy\\,{\\int }_{1}^{4}dz & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]_{1}^{2}\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot (4-1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-0)\\cdot (0+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2} \\int_{1}^{4} \\dfrac{\\sin{y}}{x} \\: \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x = 3\\ln 2.\n$$" "Since the domain of integration $R = [1,2] \\times \\left[0,\\pi/2\\right] \\times [1,4]$ is a rectangular box and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{dx}{x}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy{\\int }_{1}^{4}dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}\\,dx{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy\\,{\\int }_{1}^{4}dz & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]_{1}^{2}\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot (4-1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot \\left(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\right)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-0)\\cdot (0+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2} \\int_{1}^{4} \\dfrac{\\sin{y}}{x} \\: \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x = 3\\ln 2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198038 :question/worked-solution "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration $u_1(x,y),$ $u_2(x,y),$ $v_1(y),$ $v_2(y),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} \\int_{z}^{2} e^{x^2} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y\n=\n\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{v_1(y)}^{v_2(y)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} e^{x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2, \\:\\: z \\leq x \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type II region, we first sketch the projection onto the $yz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE = \\left\\{ (y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fe61cd50d31086b47495f0bd0fd71308fc30286d392f4d0064cf743b93272b0.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where $z \\leq x \\leq 2.$\n\n![](images/6a/6af345af2d33b53c698e8e1d4eff10e3b3116911e14ee1b4581da7eee8528f94.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y,$ we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/53/53ec4e56ca38362b10c05d4cc657f9057005d9692e0fcde1f0846e7e80b4f478.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z = z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\nx = z \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad z = x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq x \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/5c/5ce67f398f60237d763d393d62f9e7346270b954acc7efdc7a096a5f3155f4d3.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $xy$-plane with the left curve given by $x = 0.$\n\n![](images/0d/0dbf7a35fe74730db7c2a07fc4bbe881a66412cee53bf0a606c522f5493e58c2.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{z}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}[z]_{0}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1)\\cdot [y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration $u_1(x,y),$ $u_2(x,y),$ $v_1(y),$ $v_2(y),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} \\int_{z}^{2} e^{x^2} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y\n=\n\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{v_1(y)}^{v_2(y)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} e^{x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2, \\:\\: z \\leq x \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type II region, we first sketch the projection onto the $yz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE = \\left\\{ (y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fe61cd50d31086b47495f0bd0fd71308fc30286d392f4d0064cf743b93272b0.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where $z \\leq x \\leq 2.$\n\n![](images/6a/6af345af2d33b53c698e8e1d4eff10e3b3116911e14ee1b4581da7eee8528f94.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y,$ we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/53/53ec4e56ca38362b10c05d4cc657f9057005d9692e0fcde1f0846e7e80b4f478.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z = z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\nx = z \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad z = x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq x \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/5c/5ce67f398f60237d763d393d62f9e7346270b954acc7efdc7a096a5f3155f4d3.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $xy$-plane with the left curve given by $x = 0.$\n\n![](images/0d/0dbf7a35fe74730db7c2a07fc4bbe881a66412cee53bf0a606c522f5493e58c2.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{z}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}[z]_{0}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\right]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1)\\cdot [y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198040 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v , w ) \\\\ y ( u , v , w ) \\\\ z ( u , v , w )\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right|  \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = x+y^2, \\qquad\nv=  y+z, \\qquad\nw = 2z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u ( x , y , z ) \\\\ v ( x , y , z ) \\\\ w ( x , y , z )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 4,\\,0\\le w\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+{y}^{2})^{15}z\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{15}\\cdot \\frac{w}{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{∭}_{Δ}{u}^{15}w\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{u}^{15}w\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{15}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}w\\,dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\frac{{u}^{16}}{16}]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot {[v]}_{v=0}^{v=4}\\cdot [\\frac{{w}^{2}}{2}]_{w=0}^{w=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{16}-0)\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v , w ) \\\\ y ( u , v , w ) \\\\ z ( u , v , w )\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right|  \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = x+y^2, \\qquad\nv=  y+z, \\qquad\nw = 2z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u ( x , y , z ) \\\\ v ( x , y , z ) \\\\ w ( x , y , z )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 4,\\,0\\le w\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+{y}^{2})^{15}z\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{15}\\cdot \\frac{w}{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{∭}_{Δ}{u}^{15}w\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{u}^{15}w\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{15}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}w\\,dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{{u}^{16}}{16}\\right]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot {[v]}_{v=0}^{v=4}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{{w}^{2}}{2}\\right]_{w=0}^{w=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{16}-0\\right)\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198043 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z = g(x,y) = 0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface $S_2: z = h(x,y) = -\\sqrt{4 - x^2 - y^2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane $S_1.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 0,0,1 \\rangle.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,x,-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower hemisphere $S_2.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z = h(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = 0, \\qquad\nQ = x, \\qquad\nR = -z^2 = x^2 + y^2 - 4.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z = g(x,y) = 0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface $S_2: z = h(x,y) = -\\sqrt{4 - x^2 - y^2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane $S_1.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 0,0,1 \\rangle.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,x,-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower hemisphere $S_2.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z = h(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = 0, \\qquad\nQ = x, \\qquad\nR = -z^2 = x^2 + y^2 - 4.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}\\left(-0\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4\\right)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198044 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z=g(x,y)=1-x^2$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface $S_2: z=h(x,y)=x^2+y^2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper parabolic cylinder $S_1.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = x, \\qquad\nQ = y, \\qquad\nR = 0.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot (-2x)-y\\cdot (0)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid $S_2.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = x, \\qquad\nQ = y , \\qquad\nR = 0.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot (2x)-y\\cdot (2y)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z=g(x,y)=1-x^2$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface $S_2: z=h(x,y)=x^2+y^2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper parabolic cylinder $S_1.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = x, \\qquad\nQ = y, \\qquad\nR = 0.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot (-2x)-y\\cdot (0)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid $S_2.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = x, \\qquad\nQ = y , \\qquad\nR = 0.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot (2x)-y\\cdot (2y)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198045 :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.\n$$\n\n- The flux $$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s}\n$$ along any closed curve $C \\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves $C_1, C_2 \\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\sqrt{y^3}\\right) + \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\sqrt{(5-x)^3}\\right) = 0+0 = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $\\mathbf{F}$ is source-free, we know the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ is independent of the path. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\,\\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C_2$ is the straight line from $A(5,3)$ to $B(1,3)$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ is its unit normal, shown in the diagram below. \n\n![](images/2a/2aeb26e58251923821d4058f886eada6a29b473ea70714b32e98c5338ddd0b28.png)\n\nNotice that both $\\mathbf n_1$ and $\\mathbf n_2$ are $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C_2,$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\,\\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_{C_2} P\\,\\textrm{d}y - Q\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2$ *must* be a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $\\mathbf{T}(t).$\n\nParametrizing $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5\\,i+3\\,j+t\\cdot [(i+3\\,j)-(5\\,i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(5-4t)\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,1].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}=\\sqrt{(3)^{3}}=3\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =\\sqrt{(5-x)^{3}}=\\sqrt{(5-(5-4t))^{3}}=8\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-4t)=-4, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3\\sqrt{3})\\cdot (0)-(8\\sqrt{{t}^{3}})\\cdot (-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}32{t}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{64{t}^{5/2}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{64(1)^{5/2}}{5}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{(64(0)^{5/2}}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1\\, \\textrm{d}s = \\dfrac{64}{5}.\n$$" "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.\n$$\n\n- The flux $$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s}\n$$ along any closed curve $C \\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves $C_1, C_2 \\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\sqrt{y^3}\\right) + \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\sqrt{(5-x)^3}\\right) = 0+0 = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $\\mathbf{F}$ is source-free, we know the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ is independent of the path. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\,\\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C_2$ is the straight line from $A(5,3)$ to $B(1,3)$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ is its unit normal, shown in the diagram below. \n\n![](images/2a/2aeb26e58251923821d4058f886eada6a29b473ea70714b32e98c5338ddd0b28.png)\n\nNotice that both $\\mathbf n_1$ and $\\mathbf n_2$ are $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C_2,$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\,\\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_{C_2} P\\,\\textrm{d}y - Q\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2$ *must* be a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $\\mathbf{T}(t).$\n\nParametrizing $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5\\,i+3\\,j+t\\cdot [(i+3\\,j)-(5\\,i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(5-4t)\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,1].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}=\\sqrt{(3)^{3}}=3\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =\\sqrt{(5-x)^{3}}=\\sqrt{(5-(5-4t))^{3}}=8\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-4t)=-4, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3\\sqrt{3})\\cdot (0)-(8\\sqrt{{t}^{3}})\\cdot (-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}32{t}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{64{t}^{5/2}}{5}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{64(1)^{5/2}}{5}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{(64(0)^{5/2}}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1\\, \\textrm{d}s = \\dfrac{64}{5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198046 :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} =\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $\\mathbf F =  \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\:  z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/f5/f5a443b0bb234662c8f4a2bc156b458ac09a8db3ce3cc67933f7465a19db2ef9.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let $S_1$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z = 4$ for $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_1$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z = 4$ on $S_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y) \\to (r,\\theta), \\textrm d A = r\\,\\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =4{∬}_{D}r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8\\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let $S_2$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z = 0$ for $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1.$ Therefore, for every point on $S_2,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0) = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 0 \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle =  \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 0\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} =\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $\\mathbf F =  \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\:  z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/f5/f5a443b0bb234662c8f4a2bc156b458ac09a8db3ce3cc67933f7465a19db2ef9.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let $S_1$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z = 4$ for $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_1$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z = 4$ on $S_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y) \\to (r,\\theta), \\textrm d A = r\\,\\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =4{∬}_{D}r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8\\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let $S_2$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z = 0$ for $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1.$ Therefore, for every point on $S_2,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0) = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 0 \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle =  \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 0\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198048 :question/worked-solution "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nM_y = \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\lambda(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type II plane region and $\\lambda(x,y) = x^2y .$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot {x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[{\\int }_{0}^{y}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot {[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{y}^{5}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{6}}{24}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nM_y = \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\lambda(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type II plane region and $\\lambda(x,y) = x^2y .$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot {x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[{\\int }_{0}^{y}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\right]}_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{y}^{5}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{{y}^{6}}{24}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198049 :question/worked-solution "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nM_x = \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\lambda(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is a type I plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/06/067543543031a1d8cb4ceb9a7901ad7db77629243adc6658d4a9eab3e98fe09f.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\le x \\le 1, \\: 0 \\le y \\le 1-x^2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda(x,y) =  2x.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}{M}_{x} &  = \\underset{D}{\\iint } y \\cdot  2 x  \\text{d} A \\\\  &  = \\underset{D}{\\iint } 2 x y  \\text{d} A \\\\  &  = {\\int }_{0}^{1} {\\int }_{0}^{1 - {x}^{2}} 2 x y  \\text{d} y  \\text{d} x \\\\  &  = {\\int }_{0}^{1} 2 x [ {\\int }_{0}^{1 - {x}^{2}} y  \\text{d} y ]  \\text{d} x \\\\  &  = {\\int }_{0}^{1} 2 x \\cdot  {[ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} ]}_{y = 0}^{y = 1 - {x}^{2}}  \\text{d} x \\\\  &  = {\\int }_{0}^{1} x {( 1 - {x}^{2} )}^{2}  \\text{d} x \\\\  &  = {\\int }_{0}^{1} x - 2 {x}^{3} + {x}^{5}  \\text{d} x \\\\  &  = {[ \\frac{1}{2} {x}^{2} - \\frac{1}{2} {x}^{4} + \\frac{1}{6} {x}^{6} ]}_{x = 0}^{x = 1} \\\\  &  = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  &  = \\frac{1}{6} .\\end{array}\n$$" "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nM_x = \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\lambda(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is a type I plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/06/067543543031a1d8cb4ceb9a7901ad7db77629243adc6658d4a9eab3e98fe09f.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\le x \\le 1, \\: 0 \\le y \\le 1-x^2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda(x,y) =  2x.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}{M}_{x} &  = \\underset{D}{\\iint } y \\cdot  2 x  \\text{d} A \\\\  &  = \\underset{D}{\\iint } 2 x y  \\text{d} A \\\\  &  = {\\int }_{0}^{1} {\\int }_{0}^{1 - {x}^{2}} 2 x y  \\text{d} y  \\text{d} x \\\\  &  = {\\int }_{0}^{1} 2 x [ {\\int }_{0}^{1 - {x}^{2}} y  \\text{d} y ]  \\text{d} x \\\\  &  = {\\int }_{0}^{1} 2 x \\cdot  {\\left[ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} \\right]}_{y = 0}^{y = 1 - {x}^{2}}  \\text{d} x \\\\  &  = {\\int }_{0}^{1} x {( 1 - {x}^{2} )}^{2}  \\text{d} x \\\\  &  = {\\int }_{0}^{1} x - 2 {x}^{3} + {x}^{5}  \\text{d} x \\\\  &  = {\\left[ \\frac{1}{2} {x}^{2} - \\frac{1}{2} {x}^{4} + \\frac{1}{6} {x}^{6} \\right]}_{x = 0}^{x = 1} \\\\  &  = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  &  = \\frac{1}{6} .\\end{array}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198050 :question/worked-solution "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda(x)$ for $a \\leq x \\leq b$ is given by \n\n$$\nM_O = \\int_a^b x\\, \\lambda(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x) = x+4, \\qquad \na=0, \\qquad \nb=3.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\cdot (x+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}+4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+2{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}+2\\cdot {3}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $27\\,\\textrm{kg}\\cdot \\textrm{m}.$" "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda(x)$ for $a \\leq x \\leq b$ is given by \n\n$$\nM_O = \\int_a^b x\\, \\lambda(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x) = x+4, \\qquad \na=0, \\qquad \nb=3.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\cdot (x+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}+4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+2{x}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}+2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\right)-0 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $27\\,\\textrm{kg}\\cdot \\textrm{m}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186198051 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a \\leq x \\leq b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m = \\int_a^b \\lambda (x) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle M_O = \\int_a^b \\, x\\lambda (x) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x) = x^3, \\qquad \na=0, \\qquad \nb=2.\n$$\n\nSo,  the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(32-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{x}= \\dfrac{M_O}{m} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{32}{5}\\right)}{4} = \\dfrac{8}{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that this result makes intuitive sense. Since $\\lambda$ is an increasing function of $x,$ we would expect the center of mass to be located closer to the right endpoint and further from the left endpoint." "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a \\leq x \\leq b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m = \\int_a^b \\lambda (x) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle M_O = \\int_a^b \\, x\\lambda (x) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x) = x^3, \\qquad \na=0, \\qquad \nb=2.\n$$\n\nSo,  the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}\\right]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(32-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{x}= \\dfrac{M_O}{m} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{32}{5}\\right)}{4} = \\dfrac{8}{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that this result makes intuitive sense. Since $\\lambda$ is an increasing function of $x,$ we would expect the center of mass to be located closer to the right endpoint and further from the left endpoint."] [:db/cas 17592186198057 :question/worked-solution "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\: 2x \\leq y \\leq x+2 \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69dcf02cc1883f2b04f81bd6dd486bbe24fedbb813db5778ae3b33ed1fb42e90.png)\n\n     \n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y) = |x - 2|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2x}^{x+2}\\left(\\frac{y}{8}\\right)|x-2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2x}^{x+2}\\left(\\frac{y}{8}\\right)(x-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-2)^{2}{\\int }_{2x}^{x+2}\\frac{y}{8}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-2)^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}]}_{2x}^{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-2)^{2}((x+2)^{2}-(2x)^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-4x+4)(4+4x-3{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(16-24{x}^{2}+16{x}^{3}-3{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{[16x-8{x}^{3}+4{x}^{4}-\\frac{3{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}(32-64+64-\\frac{96}{5})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\left(\\frac{64}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\: 2x \\leq y \\leq x+2 \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69dcf02cc1883f2b04f81bd6dd486bbe24fedbb813db5778ae3b33ed1fb42e90.png)\n\n     \n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y) = |x - 2|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2x}^{x+2}\\left(\\frac{y}{8}\\right)|x-2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2x}^{x+2}\\left(\\frac{y}{8}\\right)(x-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-2)^{2}{\\int }_{2x}^{x+2}\\frac{y}{8}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-2)^{2}{\\left[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}\\right]}_{2x}^{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-2)^{2}((x+2)^{2}-(2x)^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-4x+4)(4+4x-3{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(16-24{x}^{2}+16{x}^{3}-3{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\left[16x-8{x}^{3}+4{x}^{4}-\\frac{3{x}^{5}}{5}\\right]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\left(32-64+64-\\frac{96}{5}\\right)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\left(\\frac{64}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198058 :question/worked-solution "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\:\\: x \\leq y \\leq 4x-x^2 \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/62/6291950b5483f3c102df7249674412c7c11646409cb1b43df604de6bd5ace86f.png)\n\n  \n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y) = |x - 3|.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{M}{\\mathcal{A}} = \\dfrac{2M}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{x}^{4x-{x}^{2}}|x-3{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{x}^{4x-{x}^{2}}(x-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(x-3)^{2}[y]_{y=x}^{y=4x-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}-6x+9)(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}-{x}^{4}+9{x}^{3}-27{x}^{2}+27x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{9}{[-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{9{x}^{4}}{4}-9{x}^{3}+\\frac{27{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{{3}^{2}}(-\\frac{{3}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{3}^{6}}{4}-{3}^{5}+\\frac{{3}^{5}}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{3}M(-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{3}{4}-1+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27M}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\:\\: x \\leq y \\leq 4x-x^2 \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/62/6291950b5483f3c102df7249674412c7c11646409cb1b43df604de6bd5ace86f.png)\n\n  \n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y) = |x - 3|.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{M}{\\mathcal{A}} = \\dfrac{2M}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{x}^{4x-{x}^{2}}|x-3{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{x}^{4x-{x}^{2}}(x-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(x-3)^{2}[y]_{y=x}^{y=4x-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}-6x+9)(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}-{x}^{4}+9{x}^{3}-27{x}^{2}+27x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{9}{\\left[-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{9{x}^{4}}{4}-9{x}^{3}+\\frac{27{x}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{{3}^{2}}\\left(-\\frac{{3}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{3}^{6}}{4}-{3}^{5}+\\frac{{3}^{5}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{3}M\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{3}{4}-1+\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{27M}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198059 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_x$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n$R_x = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_x}{M}},$\nwhere $I_x$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_y$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n$R_y = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_y}{M}},$\nwhere $I_y$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_0$ about the origin is given by\n$R_0 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_0}{M}},$\nwhere $I_0$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\nI_y = \\iint\\limits_D x^2\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $\\mathcal{A} =  3\\cdot 1 = 3.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{M}{\\mathcal{A}} = \\dfrac{M}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}9\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3M[y]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =3M(2-1) \\\\  & =3M.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nR_y^2 =\\dfrac{3M}{M} =  3\\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad R_y = \\sqrt{3}.\n$$" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_x$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n$R_x = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_x}{M}},$\nwhere $I_x$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_y$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n$R_y = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_y}{M}},$\nwhere $I_y$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_0$ about the origin is given by\n$R_0 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_0}{M}},$\nwhere $I_0$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\nI_y = \\iint\\limits_D x^2\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $\\mathcal{A} =  3\\cdot 1 = 3.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{M}{\\mathcal{A}} = \\dfrac{M}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\left[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\right]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}9\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3M[y]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =3M(2-1) \\\\  & =3M.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nR_y^2 =\\dfrac{3M}{M} =  3\\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad R_y = \\sqrt{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198060 :question/worked-solution "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt x \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/7f/7faaecf39250dbdd1b92d789f6c217afe883c27533b563dae82ed82d228d9b4d.png)\n\nAlso, the distance between a point and the line $x=-1$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y) = |x-(-1)| = |x+1|.\n$$\n\nNext, the mass density can be written as $\\lambda(x,y) = k |y| = ky,$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find the value of $k$ by integrating the mass density function over the plate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}ky\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = k y = 4M y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}4My\\,|x+1{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}4My(x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4M{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+1)^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4M{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+1)^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2M{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+1)^{2}(x-0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2M{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2M{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2M(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2M\\cdot \\frac{17}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{17M}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt x \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/7f/7faaecf39250dbdd1b92d789f6c217afe883c27533b563dae82ed82d228d9b4d.png)\n\nAlso, the distance between a point and the line $x=-1$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y) = |x-(-1)| = |x+1|.\n$$\n\nNext, the mass density can be written as $\\lambda(x,y) = k |y| = ky,$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find the value of $k$ by integrating the mass density function over the plate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}ky\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{2}\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = k y = 4M y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}4My\\,|x+1{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}4My(x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4M{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+1)^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4M{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+1)^{2}{\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2M{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+1)^{2}(x-0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2M{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2M{\\left[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2M\\left(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2M\\cdot \\frac{17}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{17M}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198063 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_x$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n$R_x = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_x}{M}},$\nwhere $I_x$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_y$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n$R_y = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_y}{M}},$\nwhere $I_y$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_0$ about the origin is given by\n$R_0 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_0}{M}},$\nwhere $I_0$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2 + y^2) \\lambda(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq x \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 3Mx\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}x{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{[\\frac{4}{15}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3M\\cdot \\frac{4}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{0} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_x$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n$R_x = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_x}{M}},$\nwhere $I_x$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_y$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n$R_y = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_y}{M}},$\nwhere $I_y$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_0$ about the origin is given by\n$R_0 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_0}{M}},$\nwhere $I_0$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2 + y^2) \\lambda(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq x \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 3Mx\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\left[{x}^{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}x{y}^{3}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\left[\\frac{4}{15}{x}^{5}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3M\\cdot \\frac{4}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{0} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198079 :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x, y)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right).$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right))}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{\\ln }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{2}{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{2}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\dfrac{2\\ln x}{x}.$" "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x, y)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right).$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{\\left(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{\\ln }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{2}{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{2}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\dfrac{2\\ln x}{x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186198143 :question/worked-solution "A *null sequence* is a sequence that converges to zero:\n\n- Sequence I is a null sequence. By dividing the numerator and denominator by $n^2$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}\\left(\\frac{n}{{n}^{2}+1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}\\left(\\frac{\\left(\\frac{n}{{n}^{2}}\\right)}{(\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}\\left(\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{n}\\right)}{(1+\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0\\hspace{1em}{\\color{darkgreen}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is *not* a null sequence since\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{b}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}\\sqrt{n+1} \\\\  & =\\mathrm{\\infty } \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Sequence III is null. Notice that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r =\\dfrac{8}{9}$.\nSince $\\mid r \\mid < 1$, the sequence converges to zero:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{c}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{\\left(\\frac{8}{9}\\right)}^{n} \\\\  & =0\\hspace{1em}{\\color{darkgreen}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "A *null sequence* is a sequence that converges to zero:\n\n- Sequence I is a null sequence. By dividing the numerator and denominator by $n^2$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}\\left(\\frac{n}{{n}^{2}+1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}\\left(\\frac{\\left(\\frac{n}{{n}^{2}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}\\left(\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{n}\\right)}{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0\\hspace{1em}{\\color{darkgreen}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is *not* a null sequence since\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{b}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}\\sqrt{n+1} \\\\  & =\\mathrm{\\infty } \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Sequence III is null. Notice that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r =\\dfrac{8}{9}$.\nSince $\\mid r \\mid < 1$, the sequence converges to zero:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{c}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{\\left(\\frac{8}{9}\\right)}^{n} \\\\  & =0\\hspace{1em}{\\color{darkgreen}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186198191 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} \\right)= -1\n$$\n\nmeans that\n\n$$\n\\forall \\varepsilon > 0, \\: \\exists N \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\: n \\geq N \\: \\Rightarrow \\: \\left| \\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} -(-1) \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nIn other words, we need to show that for any real $\\varepsilon \\gt 0$, there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number\n\n$$\nn \\geq N\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} -(-1) \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nBefore we start the proof, we would like to figure out how $N$ may depend on $\\varepsilon$.\n\nFirst, we simplifying $\\mid a_n - L \\mid$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{a}_{n}-L| & =|\\frac{1-{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}-10}-(-1)| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1-{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}-10}+1| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1-{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}-10}+\\frac{{4}^{n}-10}{{4}^{n}-10}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1-{4}^{n}+{4}^{n}-10}{{4}^{n}-10}| \\\\  & =|-\\frac{9}{{4}^{n}-10}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{9}{{4}^{n}-10}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{4^n-10} \\gt 0\n$$\n\nfor any natural\n\n$$\nn \\geq 2\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n\\mid a_n - L \\mid = \\left| \\dfrac{9}{4^n-10} \\right| = \\dfrac{9}{4^n-10}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $\\mid a_n - L \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 2{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{{4}^{n}-10} & <ε \\\\ 9 & <ε({4}^{n}-10) \\\\ \\frac{9}{ε} & <{4}^{n}-10 \\\\ 10+\\frac{9}{ε} & <{4}^{n} \\\\ {log}_{4}(10+\\frac{9}{ε}) & <n \\\\ n & >{log}_{4}(10+\\frac{9}{ε}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, if we want\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} - (-1) \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nit's sufficient to take all values of $n$ that are larger than\n\n$$\n\\log_4\\left(10+\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe can now continue the proof:\n\n> Suppose $\\varepsilon > 0$. Let's choose some natural number $N > \\log_4\\left(10+\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon}\\right)$.\n\nNotice that, since\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon} > 0\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\log_4\\left(10+\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon}\\right) > \\log_4(10)\\approx 1.66\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\nN \\geq 2\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we'll find the upper bound for the absolute value of the sequence's $n$th term:\n\n> Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{1-{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}-10}-(-1)| & =\\frac{9}{{4}^{n}-10} \\\\  & <\\frac{9}{{4}^{{log}_{4}(10+9/ε)}-10} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{(10+\\frac{9}{ε}-10)} \\\\  & =ε.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition,\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} \\right)= -1\n$$\n\nThe full proof is given below:\n\n> Suppose $\\varepsilon > 0$. Let's choose some natural number $N > \\log_4\\left(10+\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon}\\right)$.\n> Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\mid \\frac{1 - 4^{n}}{4^{n} - 10} - (-1) \\mid \\mid < (9)/(4^{\\log_{4} (10 + 9/ε)} - 10); = (9)/((10 + \\frac{9}{ε} - 10)); = ε.\n$$\n> Therefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} = -1.\n$$" "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} \\right)= -1\n$$\n\nmeans that\n\n$$\n\\forall \\varepsilon > 0, \\: \\exists N \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\: n \\geq N \\: \\Rightarrow \\: \\left| \\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} -(-1) \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nIn other words, we need to show that for any real $\\varepsilon \\gt 0$, there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number\n\n$$\nn \\geq N\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} -(-1) \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nBefore we start the proof, we would like to figure out how $N$ may depend on $\\varepsilon$.\n\nFirst, we simplifying $\\mid a_n - L \\mid$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{a}_{n}-L| & =|\\frac{1-{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}-10}-(-1)| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1-{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}-10}+1| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1-{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}-10}+\\frac{{4}^{n}-10}{{4}^{n}-10}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1-{4}^{n}+{4}^{n}-10}{{4}^{n}-10}| \\\\  & =|-\\frac{9}{{4}^{n}-10}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{9}{{4}^{n}-10}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{4^n-10} \\gt 0\n$$\n\nfor any natural\n\n$$\nn \\geq 2\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n\\mid a_n - L \\mid = \\left| \\dfrac{9}{4^n-10} \\right| = \\dfrac{9}{4^n-10}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $\\mid a_n - L \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 2{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{{4}^{n}-10} & <ε \\\\ 9 & <ε({4}^{n}-10) \\\\ \\frac{9}{ε} & <{4}^{n}-10 \\\\ 10+\\frac{9}{ε} & <{4}^{n} \\\\ {log}_{4}\\left(10+\\frac{9}{ε}\\right) & <n \\\\ n & >{log}_{4}\\left(10+\\frac{9}{ε}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, if we want\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} - (-1) \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nit's sufficient to take all values of $n$ that are larger than\n\n$$\n\\log_4\\left(10+\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe can now continue the proof:\n\n> Suppose $\\varepsilon > 0$. Let's choose some natural number $N > \\log_4\\left(10+\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon}\\right)$.\n\nNotice that, since\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon} > 0\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\log_4\\left(10+\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon}\\right) > \\log_4(10)\\approx 1.66\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\nN \\geq 2\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we'll find the upper bound for the absolute value of the sequence's $n$th term:\n\n> Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{1-{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}-10}-(-1)| & =\\frac{9}{{4}^{n}-10} \\\\  & <\\frac{9}{{4}^{{log}_{4}(10+9/ε)}-10} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\left(10+\\frac{9}{ε}-10\\right)} \\\\  & =ε.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition,\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} \\right)= -1\n$$\n\nThe full proof is given below:\n\n> Suppose $\\varepsilon > 0$. Let's choose some natural number $N > \\log_4\\left(10+\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon}\\right)$.\n> Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\mid \\frac{1 - 4^{n}}{4^{n} - 10} - (-1) \\mid \\mid < (9)/(4^{\\log_{4} (10 + 9/ε)} - 10); = (9)/\\left(\\left(10 + \\frac{9}{ε} - 10\\right)\\right); = ε.\n$$\n> Therefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} = -1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198235 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\, \\mathrm d x \\, \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v))  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\mathrm d u \\, \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = 4x + 2y, \\qquad v = y - x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u ( x , y ) \\\\ v ( x , y )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables.\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu = 4x+2y, \\qquad v = y-x\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{u-2v}6, \\qquad y =\\dfrac{u+4v}6.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(x+y))\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(\\frac{u-2v}{6}+\\frac{u+4v}{6}))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\left(\\frac{2u+2v}{6}\\right))|\\frac{1}{6}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\sin \\,(u+v)\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\, \\mathrm d x \\, \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v))  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\mathrm d u \\, \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = 4x + 2y, \\qquad v = y - x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u ( x , y ) \\\\ v ( x , y )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables.\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu = 4x+2y, \\qquad v = y-x\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{u-2v}6, \\qquad y =\\dfrac{u+4v}6.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(x+y))\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(3\\left(\\frac{u-2v}{6}+\\frac{u+4v}{6}\\right)\\right)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(3\\left(\\frac{2u+2v}{6}\\right)\\right)|\\frac{1}{6}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\sin \\,(u+v)\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198238 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left( \\dfrac{M_y}{m}, \\dfrac{M_x}{m} \\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m = \\iint \\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle M_x = \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle M_y = \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](images/b8/b872242423692a20a9d50658c99d9e8cee7c9b2737fb96592bc2bc35b4f72c32.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\:0 \\le x \\le 1, \\: 0 \\le y \\le x \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda(x,y) = x+y.$ \n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot (x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}xy+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}xy+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}(\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overline{y} = \\dfrac{M_x}{m} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{24}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = \\dfrac{5}{12}.\n$$" "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left( \\dfrac{M_y}{m}, \\dfrac{M_x}{m} \\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m = \\iint \\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle M_x = \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle M_y = \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](images/b8/b872242423692a20a9d50658c99d9e8cee7c9b2737fb96592bc2bc35b4f72c32.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\:0 \\le x \\le 1, \\: 0 \\le y \\le x \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda(x,y) = x+y.$ \n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot (x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}xy+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}xy+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\left[\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}{\\left[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}\\right]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}-0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overline{y} = \\dfrac{M_x}{m} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{24}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = \\dfrac{5}{12}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198239 :question/worked-solution "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area $A_D$ is given by $(x_c, y_c),$ where\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x_c = \\dfrac{1}{A_D} \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\: \\textrm{d}A, \\qquad \n\\displaystyle y_c = \\dfrac{1}{A_D} \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](images/a7/a7992c54f85f85cab3d9f923b8daca62c410393b364d30bcfbf5cff5efc93bbd.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\le x \\le \\sqrt{1-y^2}, \\: -1 \\le y \\le 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\nA_D = \\dfrac12 \\cdot \\pi\\cdot 1^2 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of the centroid is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{c} & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1-{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{[y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[(1-\\frac{1}{3})-(-1+\\frac{1}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area $A_D$ is given by $(x_c, y_c),$ where\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x_c = \\dfrac{1}{A_D} \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\: \\textrm{d}A, \\qquad \n\\displaystyle y_c = \\dfrac{1}{A_D} \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](images/a7/a7992c54f85f85cab3d9f923b8daca62c410393b364d30bcfbf5cff5efc93bbd.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\le x \\le \\sqrt{1-y^2}, \\: -1 \\le y \\le 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\nA_D = \\dfrac12 \\cdot \\pi\\cdot 1^2 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of the centroid is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{c} & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\left[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1-{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\left[y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\right]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\left[\\left(1-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)-\\left(-1+\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198241 :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = \\cos{x}+\\cos{y}+\\cos{z},\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\pi.\n$$\n\nSince the angles are acute, we also have the constraints $0 \\lt x,y,z \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2.$ \n\nLet $g(x,y,z) = x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z), \\qquad g(x,y,z) = \\pi\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x &=\\lambda \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡y &=\\lambda \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡z &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sine function is one-to-one on the interval $\\left( 0, \\dfrac \\pi 2\\right),$ we can conclude that $x = y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y =x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =\\pi  \\\\ x+x+x & =\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right),\n$$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's easy to show that this point is indeed the maximum and not the minimum. To do that, we can compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right) \\\\  & ≈1.47 \\\\  & <f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = \\cos{x}+\\cos{y}+\\cos{z},\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\pi.\n$$\n\nSince the angles are acute, we also have the constraints $0 \\lt x,y,z \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2.$ \n\nLet $g(x,y,z) = x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z), \\qquad g(x,y,z) = \\pi\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x &=\\lambda \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡y &=\\lambda \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡z &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sine function is one-to-one on the interval $\\left( 0, \\dfrac \\pi 2\\right),$ we can conclude that $x = y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y =x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =\\pi  \\\\ x+x+x & =\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right),\n$$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's easy to show that this point is indeed the maximum and not the minimum. To do that, we can compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right) \\\\  & ≈1.47 \\\\  & <f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198577 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt{2},$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/61/616f6f5e952fa5634497b86ced1893c938cd76051ae0e0e0200bb323bff33238.png)" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=\\left(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=\\left(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt{2},$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/61/616f6f5e952fa5634497b86ced1893c938cd76051ae0e0e0200bb323bff33238.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186198579 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{(x+y)^{2}}{2x+y}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=2x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(0,1) & =(0+1,2(0)+1) \\\\  & =(1,1) \\\\ {\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(1,0) & =(1+0,2(1)+0) \\\\  & =(1,2) \\\\ {\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(-1,3) & =(-1+3,2(-1)+3) \\\\  & =(2,1) \\\\ {\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(0,2) & =(0+2,2(0)+2) \\\\  & =(2,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le u\\le 2,\\hspace{1em}1\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/3a/3a831bbc56c0ce5e749fcf45bd65825f297d7732158b8f9e371f2178e61b92c0.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{(x+y)^{2}}{2x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v}\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & {[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{1}^{2}\\cdot {[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{(x+y)^{2}}{2x+y}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=2x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(0,1) & =(0+1,2(0)+1) \\\\  & =(1,1) \\\\ {\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(1,0) & =(1+0,2(1)+0) \\\\  & =(1,2) \\\\ {\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(-1,3) & =(-1+3,2(-1)+3) \\\\  & =(2,1) \\\\ {\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(0,2) & =(0+2,2(0)+2) \\\\  & =(2,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le u\\le 2,\\hspace{1em}1\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/3a/3a831bbc56c0ce5e749fcf45bd65825f297d7732158b8f9e371f2178e61b92c0.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{(x+y)^{2}}{2x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v}\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & {\\left[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}\\right]}_{1}^{2}\\cdot {[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198580 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fcfc165548d8174ec0468dce53317fd0c8daa14565852c5e44d6764161723bed.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis” is true, while statement “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis” is false. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement “The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O.$” is false.\n\n- When $u=1,$ we obtain the point \n$$\n\\displaystyle (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)),\n$$\nwhose distance to the origin is $1.$\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{2},$ we obtain the point \n$$\n\\displaystyle (\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)),\n$$\nwhose distance to the origin is $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis” only." "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =\\left(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\right),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fcfc165548d8174ec0468dce53317fd0c8daa14565852c5e44d6764161723bed.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis” is true, while statement “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis” is false. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement “The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O.$” is false.\n\n- When $u=1,$ we obtain the point \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\right),\n$$\nwhose distance to the origin is $1.$\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{2},$ we obtain the point \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\right),\n$$\nwhose distance to the origin is $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis” only."] [:db/cas 17592186198582 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the curves\n\n$$\ny=x,\\,y=3x,\\,y=\\frac{1}{x},\\,y=\\frac{5}{x}.\n$$\n\nA transformation $T,$ where\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nmaps a rectangular region $Δ$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}},\\,\\,),\n$$\n\nwhat could be the missing expression?" "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the curves\n\n$$\ny=x,\\,y=3x,\\,y=\\frac{1}{x},\\,y=\\frac{5}{x}.\n$$\n\nA transformation $T,$ where\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nmaps a rectangular region $Δ$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to \\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}},\\,\\,\\right),\n$$\n\nwhat could be the missing expression?"] [:db/cas 17592186198582 :question/worked-solution "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d} \\\\  & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRewriting the equations \n\n$$\ny=x,\\,y=3x,\\,y=\\frac{1}{x},\\,y=\\frac{5}{x}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x}=1,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=3,\\,xy=1,\\,xy=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$ and $v(x,y)=xy,$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to (\\,\\frac{y}{x}\\,,\\,xy).\n$$\n\nTo find $T,$ we should express $x$ and $y$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$ Recall that we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ v &=xy.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $y=ux.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\nv=u{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}=\\frac{v}{u}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}}$ back into $y=ux,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=u\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}}=\\sqrt{uv}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation $T$ can be given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}},\\,\\sqrt{uv}).\n$$" "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d} \\\\  & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRewriting the equations \n\n$$\ny=x,\\,y=3x,\\,y=\\frac{1}{x},\\,y=\\frac{5}{x}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x}=1,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=3,\\,xy=1,\\,xy=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$ and $v(x,y)=xy,$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to \\left(\\,\\frac{y}{x}\\,,\\,xy\\right).\n$$\n\nTo find $T,$ we should express $x$ and $y$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$ Recall that we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ v &=xy.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $y=ux.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\nv=u{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}=\\frac{v}{u}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}}$ back into $y=ux,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=u\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}}=\\sqrt{uv}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation $T$ can be given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to \\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}},\\,\\sqrt{uv}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198584 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ v & 1 & 0 \\\\ u & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}v & 0 \\\\ u & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}v & 1 \\\\ u & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-1,v,v-u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨xz,\\,2y,\\,1⟩{|}_{x=uv\\,y=u+v\\,z=1-v} \\\\  & =⟨uv\\cdot (1-v),\\,2(u+v),\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨uv-u{v}^{2},\\,2u+2v,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨uv-u{v}^{2},\\,2u+2v,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,v,\\,v-u⟩ \\\\  & =(uv-u{v}^{2})\\cdot (-1)+(2u+2v)\\cdot v+1\\cdot (v-u) \\\\  & =u{v}^{2}-uv+2uv+2{v}^{2}+v-u \\\\  & =u{v}^{2}+2{v}^{2}+uv-u+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1\\cdot u{v}^{2}+2\\cdot {v}^{2}+1\\cdot uv-u+v\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+2+1=4.\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨v,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ v & 1 & 0 \\\\ u & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}v & 0 \\\\ u & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}v & 1 \\\\ u & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-1,v,v-u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨xz,\\,2y,\\,1⟩{|}_{x=uv\\,y=u+v\\,z=1-v} \\\\  & =⟨uv\\cdot (1-v),\\,2(u+v),\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨uv-u{v}^{2},\\,2u+2v,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨uv-u{v}^{2},\\,2u+2v,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,v,\\,v-u⟩ \\\\  & =(uv-u{v}^{2})\\cdot (-1)+(2u+2v)\\cdot v+1\\cdot (v-u) \\\\  & =u{v}^{2}-uv+2uv+2{v}^{2}+v-u \\\\  & =u{v}^{2}+2{v}^{2}+uv-u+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1\\cdot u{v}^{2}+2\\cdot {v}^{2}+1\\cdot uv-u+v\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+2+1=4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198586 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3x+y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{y}{2}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}-3x-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u-1,\\hspace{0.222em}2v,\\hspace{0.222em}2-3u-2v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $-1\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 3.$" "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\left\\langle 3x+y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{y}{2}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}-3x-z\\right\\rangle \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u-1,\\hspace{0.222em}2v,\\hspace{0.222em}2-3u-2v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $-1\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186198586 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2-3u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-3⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2-3u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨6,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨3x+y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{y}{2}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}-3x-z⟩|}_{x=u-1\\,y=2v\\,z=2-3u-2v} \\\\  & =⟨3(u-1)+(2v)+(2-3u-2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{2v}{2}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}-3(u-1)-(2-3u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}v-2,\\hspace{0.222em}2v+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}v-2,\\hspace{0.222em}2v+1⟩\\cdot ⟨6,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩ \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 6+(v-2)\\cdot 2+(2v+1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6+2v-4+4v+2 \\\\  & =6v-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3x+y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{y}{2}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}-3x-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6v-8)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(6v-8)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}(6v-8)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =[u]_{-1}^{1}\\cdot [3{v}^{2}-8v]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2-3u-2v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-3⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2-3u-2v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨6,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={\\left\\langle 3x+y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{y}{2}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}-3x-z\\right\\rangle |}_{x=u-1\\,y=2v\\,z=2-3u-2v} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 3(u-1)+(2v)+(2-3u-2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{2v}{2}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}-3(u-1)-(2-3u-2v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}v-2,\\hspace{0.222em}2v+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}v-2,\\hspace{0.222em}2v+1⟩\\cdot ⟨6,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩ \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 6+(v-2)\\cdot 2+(2v+1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6+2v-4+4v+2 \\\\  & =6v-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\left\\langle 3x+y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{y}{2}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}-3x-z\\right\\rangle \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6v-8)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(6v-8)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}(6v-8)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =[u]_{-1}^{1}\\cdot [3{v}^{2}-8v]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198592 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨2x,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{xz}{3}-2y,\\hspace{0.222em}2z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨2u-1,\\hspace{0.222em}uv,\\hspace{0.222em}3v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨3v,\\hspace{0.222em}-6,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩.$*" "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\left\\langle 2x,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{xz}{3}-2y,\\hspace{0.222em}2z\\right\\rangle \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨2u-1,\\hspace{0.222em}uv,\\hspace{0.222em}3v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨3v,\\hspace{0.222em}-6,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186198592 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨3v,\\hspace{0.222em}-6,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨2x,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{xz}{3}-2y,\\hspace{0.222em}2z⟩|}_{x=2u-1\\,y=uv\\,z=3v} \\\\  & =⟨2(2u-1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{(2u-1)(3v)}{3}-2uv,\\hspace{0.222em}2(3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4u-2,\\hspace{0.222em}-v,\\hspace{0.222em}6v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨4u-2,\\hspace{0.222em}-v,\\hspace{0.222em}6v⟩\\cdot ⟨3v,\\hspace{0.222em}-6,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩ \\\\  & =(4u-2)\\cdot 3v+(-v)\\cdot (-6)+6v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =12uv-6v+6v+12uv \\\\  & =24uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨2x,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{xz}{3}-2y,\\hspace{0.222em}2z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}24uv\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}24uv\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}[12{u}^{2}v]_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}12v\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =[6{v}^{2}]_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =6({2}^{2}-(-1)^{2}) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨3v,\\hspace{0.222em}-6,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={\\left\\langle 2x,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{xz}{3}-2y,\\hspace{0.222em}2z\\right\\rangle |}_{x=2u-1\\,y=uv\\,z=3v} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 2(2u-1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{(2u-1)(3v)}{3}-2uv,\\hspace{0.222em}2(3v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨4u-2,\\hspace{0.222em}-v,\\hspace{0.222em}6v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨4u-2,\\hspace{0.222em}-v,\\hspace{0.222em}6v⟩\\cdot ⟨3v,\\hspace{0.222em}-6,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩ \\\\  & =(4u-2)\\cdot 3v+(-v)\\cdot (-6)+6v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =12uv-6v+6v+12uv \\\\  & =24uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\left\\langle 2x,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{xz}{3}-2y,\\hspace{0.222em}2z\\right\\rangle \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}24uv\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}24uv\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}[12{u}^{2}v]_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}12v\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =[6{v}^{2}]_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =6({2}^{2}-(-1)^{2}) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198593 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,u+v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,-2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z-y,\\,2y-z,\\,xy⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=u+v} \\\\  & =⟨u+v-v,\\,2v-(u+v),\\,uv⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,v-u,\\,uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u,\\,v-u,\\,uv⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =u\\cdot (-1)+(v-u)\\cdot (-1)+uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-u-v+u+uv \\\\  & =uv-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z-y,\\,2y-z,\\,xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(uv-v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}uv-v\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,u+v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,-2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z-y,\\,2y-z,\\,xy⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=u+v} \\\\  & =⟨u+v-v,\\,2v-(u+v),\\,uv⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,v-u,\\,uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u,\\,v-u,\\,uv⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =u\\cdot (-1)+(v-u)\\cdot (-1)+uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-u-v+u+uv \\\\  & =uv-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z-y,\\,2y-z,\\,xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(uv-v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}uv-v\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198594 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨z+y,\\hspace{0.222em}2y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩{|}_{z=x-y} \\\\  & =⟨(x-y)+y,\\hspace{0.222em}2y+(x-y),\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{x}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{x+y}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{xy}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z+y,\\hspace{0.222em}2y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x)\\cdot 1-(x+y)\\cdot (-1)+xy)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x+x+y+xy)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+xy)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+xy)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+2x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2x+{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)+{1}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R\\right)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨z+y,\\hspace{0.222em}2y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩{|}_{z=x-y} \\\\  & =⟨(x-y)+y,\\hspace{0.222em}2y+(x-y),\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{x}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{x+y}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{xy}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z+y,\\hspace{0.222em}2y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x)\\cdot 1-(x+y)\\cdot (-1)+xy)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x+x+y+xy)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+xy)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+xy)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{2}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+2x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2x+{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)+{1}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198596 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=(2x-1)(x+y)^{2}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x-1,\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,0) & =(2(1)-1,1+0) \\\\  & =(1,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-1) & =(2(2)-1,2+(-1)) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,2) & =(2(2)-1,2+2) \\\\  & =(3,4) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,3) & =(2(1)-1,1+3) \\\\  & =(1,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/1d/1d5943d2cf79e7c77f6db3e939242d7e31068f46506fa618abd24b6ac813d32a.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x-1)(x+y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{v}^{2}\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{1}^{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 21\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=(2x-1)(x+y)^{2}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x-1,\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,0) & =(2(1)-1,1+0) \\\\  & =(1,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-1) & =(2(2)-1,2+(-1)) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,2) & =(2(2)-1,2+2) \\\\  & =(3,4) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,3) & =(2(1)-1,1+3) \\\\  & =(1,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/1d/1d5943d2cf79e7c77f6db3e939242d7e31068f46506fa618abd24b6ac813d32a.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x-1)(x+y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{v}^{2}\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\right]}_{1}^{4}\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}\\right]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 21\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198602 :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}+R)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}\\left(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}+R\\right)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?"] [:db/cas 17592186198602 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'} & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,y))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({\\color{blue}-P}\\frac{{\\color{blue}∂g}}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{{\\color{blue}∂y}}+R)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'} & =\\left\\langle 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\left\\langle 0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}=\\left\\langle -\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,y))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot \\left\\langle -\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1\\right\\rangle \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left({\\color{blue}-P}\\frac{{\\color{blue}∂g}}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{{\\color{blue}∂y}}+R\\right)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198604 :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/c7/c7b22b03b9952bfe068e9252f7336568aa8d761c32a1bc31c444ebb97ee925ba.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{D}{r}^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0\\cdot ({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\pi +0 \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/c7/c7b22b03b9952bfe068e9252f7336568aa8d761c32a1bc31c444ebb97ee925ba.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{D}{r}^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0\\cdot ({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\pi +0 \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198605 :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}\\left(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R\\right)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?"] [:db/cas 17592186198605 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'} & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,y))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({\\color{blue}-P}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}+({\\color{blue}-Q}\\hspace{0.167em})\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'} & =\\left\\langle 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\left\\langle 0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}=\\left\\langle -\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,y))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot \\left\\langle -\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1\\right\\rangle \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left({\\color{blue}-P}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}+({\\color{blue}-Q}\\hspace{0.167em})\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R\\right)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198608 :question/worked-solution "Since the domain of integration $R=[1,4]\\times [0,2]\\times [0,\\pi /2]$ is a rectangular box and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,dx{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dy{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,dx{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dy{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,dz & =(4-1)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡z]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since the domain of integration $R=[1,4]\\times [0,2]\\times [0,\\pi /2]$ is a rectangular box and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,dx{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dy{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,dx{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dy{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,dz & =(4-1)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡z]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2\\cdot \\left(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\right) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198609 :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{x}2{e}^{z+1}\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{0}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}2{e}^{z+1}\\,dz]dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{0}2[{e}^{z+1}]_{z=0}^{z=x}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{0}2({e}^{x+1}-e)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{0}2({e}^{x+1}-e)\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[2{\\int }_{-y}^{0}{e}^{x+1}-e\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2[{e}^{x+1}-ex]_{x=-y}^{x=0}\\,dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{0+1}-e(0)-({e}^{-y+1}-e(-y))dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}e-{e}^{1-y}-ey\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{1}e-{e}^{1-y}-ey\\,dy & =2{[ey+{e}^{1-y}-\\frac{e}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =2(e(1-0)+{e}^{1-1}-{e}^{1-0}-\\frac{e}{2}({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})) \\\\  & =2(e+1-e-\\frac{e}{2}) \\\\  & =2-e\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{x}2{e}^{z+1}\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{0}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}2{e}^{z+1}\\,dz]dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{0}2[{e}^{z+1}]_{z=0}^{z=x}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{0}2({e}^{x+1}-e)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{0}2({e}^{x+1}-e)\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[2{\\int }_{-y}^{0}{e}^{x+1}-e\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2[{e}^{x+1}-ex]_{x=-y}^{x=0}\\,dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{0+1}-e(0)-({e}^{-y+1}-e(-y))dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}e-{e}^{1-y}-ey\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{1}e-{e}^{1-y}-ey\\,dy & =2{\\left[ey+{e}^{1-y}-\\frac{e}{2}{y}^{2}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =2\\left(e(1-0)+{e}^{1-1}-{e}^{1-0}-\\frac{e}{2}({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(e+1-e-\\frac{e}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2-e\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198610 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=u+v} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u+v)^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u+v)^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot (-1)+{v}^{2}\\cdot (-1)+(u+v)^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}+{u}^{2}+2uv+{v}^{2} \\\\  & =2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2uv)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\color{blue}2uv}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=u+v} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u+v)^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u+v)^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot (-1)+{v}^{2}\\cdot (-1)+(u+v)^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}+{u}^{2}+2uv+{v}^{2} \\\\  & =2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2uv)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\color{blue}2uv}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198612 :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-y)=1-1=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular, as we have that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B,$ and ${n}_{2}$ is the unit normal to ${C}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e2/e22757cf7c4e2be17cb71e63c550aa39a10ee1dc5895c63acd5a45f0baa94623.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nWe have the parametrization\n\n$$\nr(t)=(1-2t)\\,i+(-1+2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=1-2t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1-y=1-(2t-1)=2-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)=-2, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-2t)\\cdot (2)-(2-2t)\\cdot (-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-2t)+(2-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3-4t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[3t-2{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (3-2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=2.\n$$" "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-y)=1-1=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular, as we have that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B,$ and ${n}_{2}$ is the unit normal to ${C}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e2/e22757cf7c4e2be17cb71e63c550aa39a10ee1dc5895c63acd5a45f0baa94623.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nWe have the parametrization\n\n$$\nr(t)=(1-2t)\\,i+(-1+2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=1-2t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1-y=1-(2t-1)=2-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)=-2, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-2t)\\cdot (2)-(2-2t)\\cdot (-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-2t)+(2-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3-4t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[3t-2{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (3-2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198613 :question/worked-solution "![](images/f8/f8704aa6c4823f6028caf14e5eef44a5a739d45476911930ae69daafc2ed05f7.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-\\frac{4x}{5}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/31/31f2ff96afa1248427c1a6dd5074c47190c70983d7c0d5dfdd3def8bd883aef8.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-\\frac{4x}{5}}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}5{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3-4x/5}5{x}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3-4x/5}5{x}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-4x/5}5{x}^{2}\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[5{x}^{2}z]_{z=0}^{z=3-4x/5}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}5{x}^{2}(3-\\frac{4x}{5})\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(15{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(15{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(15{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(15{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})[y]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2(15{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2(15{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})\\,dx & =2[5{x}^{3}-{x}^{4}]_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =2(135-81)-0 \\\\  & =108.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "![](images/f8/f8704aa6c4823f6028caf14e5eef44a5a739d45476911930ae69daafc2ed05f7.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-\\frac{4x}{5}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/31/31f2ff96afa1248427c1a6dd5074c47190c70983d7c0d5dfdd3def8bd883aef8.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-\\frac{4x}{5}}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}5{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3-4x/5}5{x}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3-4x/5}5{x}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-4x/5}5{x}^{2}\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[5{x}^{2}z]_{z=0}^{z=3-4x/5}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}5{x}^{2}\\left(3-\\frac{4x}{5}\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(15{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(15{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(15{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(15{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})[y]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2(15{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2(15{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3})\\,dx & =2[5{x}^{3}-{x}^{4}]_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =2(135-81)-0 \\\\  & =108.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198614 :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\frac{2y}{x}}}3xyz\\,dz]dA & ={∬}_{D}{[\\frac{3}{2}xy{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{\\frac{2y}{x}}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{3}{2}xy({(\\sqrt{\\frac{2y}{x}})}^{2}-0)\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{3}{2}xy\\cdot \\frac{2y}{x}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{y}^{2}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3{y}^{2}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{x-1}^{x+1}3{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{x-1}^{x+1}3{y}^{2}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{y}^{3}]_{y=x-1}^{y=x+1}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(x+1)^{3}-(x-1)^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}(x+1)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}(x-1)^{4}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(81-16)-\\frac{1}{4}(1-0) \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\frac{2y}{x}}}3xyz\\,dz]dA & ={∬}_{D}{\\left[\\frac{3}{2}xy{z}^{2}\\right]}_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{\\frac{2y}{x}}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{3}{2}xy\\left({\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{2y}{x}}\\right)}^{2}-0\\right)\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{3}{2}xy\\cdot \\frac{2y}{x}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{y}^{2}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3{y}^{2}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{x-1}^{x+1}3{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{x-1}^{x+1}3{y}^{2}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{y}^{3}]_{y=x-1}^{y=x+1}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(x+1)^{3}-(x-1)^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{4}(x+1)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}(x-1)^{4}\\right]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(81-16)-\\frac{1}{4}(1-0) \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198615 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x-y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨{e}^{x},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{y},\\hspace{0.222em}z+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨{e}^{x},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{y},\\hspace{0.222em}z+1⟩{|}_{z=-x-y} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{{e}^{x}}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{{e}^{y}}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{1-x-y}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{e}^{x},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{y},\\hspace{0.222em}z+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-({e}^{x})\\cdot (-1)-({e}^{y})\\cdot (-1)+1-x-y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+1-x-y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+1-x-y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{e}^{x}y+{e}^{y}+y-xy-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}+e+1-x-\\frac{1}{2}-1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}-x-\\frac{1}{2}+e)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{x}{2}+ex]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =e-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}+e-1 \\\\  & =2e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R\\right)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x-y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨{e}^{x},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{y},\\hspace{0.222em}z+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨{e}^{x},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{y},\\hspace{0.222em}z+1⟩{|}_{z=-x-y} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{{e}^{x}}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{{e}^{y}}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{1-x-y}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{e}^{x},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{y},\\hspace{0.222em}z+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-({e}^{x})\\cdot (-1)-({e}^{y})\\cdot (-1)+1-x-y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+1-x-y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+1-x-y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\left[{e}^{x}y+{e}^{y}+y-xy-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left({e}^{x}+e+1-x-\\frac{1}{2}-1\\right)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left({e}^{x}-x-\\frac{1}{2}+e\\right)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{x}{2}+ex\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =e-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}+e-1 \\\\  & =2e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198618 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,R=-y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-2x)-(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-2y)+(-y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,z,-y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,R=-y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-2x)-(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-2y)+(-y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,z,-y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198619 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨y,0,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cone ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2)=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-0\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨y,0,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cone ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2)=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}\\left(-y\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-0\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+2x\\right)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x\\right)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x\\right)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198620 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=16-4{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=4{x}^{2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cylinder ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(16-4{y}^{2})=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(16-4{y}^{2})=-8y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=z=16-4{y}^{2},\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 0-(16-4{y}^{2})\\cdot (-8y)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(128y-32{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cylinder ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2})=8x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=z=4{x}^{2},\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 8x-4{x}^{2}\\cdot 0+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}-8x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(128y-32{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}8x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}128y-32{y}^{3}+8x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=16-4{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=4{x}^{2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cylinder ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(16-4{y}^{2})=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(16-4{y}^{2})=-8y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=z=16-4{y}^{2},\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 0-(16-4{y}^{2})\\cdot (-8y)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(128y-32{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cylinder ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2})=8x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=z=4{x}^{2},\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 8x-4{x}^{2}\\cdot 0+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}-8x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(128y-32{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}8x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}128y-32{y}^{3}+8x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198621 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1+x$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+x)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 0+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 2x-0\\cdot 2y+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2x-x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x-1\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1+x$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+x)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 0+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 2x-0\\cdot 2y+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2x-x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x-1\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198623 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=3x-2y,\\,v=y+2z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(3x-2y)^{2}(y+2z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{2}v\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{u}^{2}v\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{u}^{2}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{u=0}^{u=3}\\cdot {[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (9-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=3x-2y,\\,v=y+2z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(3x-2y)^{2}(y+2z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{2}v\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{u}^{2}v\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{u}^{2}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}\\right]}_{u=0}^{u=3}\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (9-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}-0\\right) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198627 :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,y({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the left and right sides. \n\n![](images/1f/1f00f0e9fa8148ca3cf161322c926ea9fc16a4467991063cbdcb4f293254674d.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=2$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,y({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{1} & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{D}({r}^{2}+4)\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r({r}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}({r}^{3}+4r)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}({r}^{3}+4r)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}+2{r}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}+2) \\\\  & =9\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,0,z)=⟨0,\\,0({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =9\\pi +0 \\\\  & =9\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,y({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the left and right sides. \n\n![](images/1f/1f00f0e9fa8148ca3cf161322c926ea9fc16a4467991063cbdcb4f293254674d.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=2$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,y({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{1} & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{D}({r}^{2}+4)\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r({r}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}({r}^{3}+4r)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}({r}^{3}+4r)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}+2{r}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{4}+2\\right) \\\\  & =9\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,0,z)=⟨0,\\,0({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =9\\pi +0 \\\\  & =9\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198629 :question/worked-solution "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type I plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=3xy.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot 3xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot {[{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type I plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=3xy.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot 3xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot {[{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}\\right]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198639 :question/worked-solution "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)$ for $a\\le x\\le b$ is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)={x}^{2}+2,\\,a=1,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\cdot ({x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{3}+2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}+{2}^{2})-(\\frac{{1}^{4}}{4}+{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4} \\\\  & =6.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $6.75\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$" "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)$ for $a\\le x\\le b$ is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)={x}^{2}+2,\\,a=1,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\cdot ({x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{3}+2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+{x}^{2}\\right]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}+{2}^{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{{1}^{4}}{4}+{1}^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4} \\\\  & =6.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $6.75\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186198646 :question/worked-solution "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type II region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,y\\le x\\le 2-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/d1/d1241b56d482f24cc06d51a9aaecbc623805df0a76739201e5cbe6974e2e9c6f.png)\n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $y=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|y-1|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $y=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}(3x+2)|y-1{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}(3x+2)(y-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-2y+1)[{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}3x+2\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-2y+1){[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+2x]}_{x=y}^{x=2-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-2y+1)[\\frac{3}{2}(2-y)^{2}+2(2-y)-(\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+2y)]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =10{\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-2y+1)(1-y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =10{\\int }_{0}^{1}1-3y+3{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =10{[y-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =10(1-\\frac{3}{2}+1-\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type II region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,y\\le x\\le 2-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/d1/d1241b56d482f24cc06d51a9aaecbc623805df0a76739201e5cbe6974e2e9c6f.png)\n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $y=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|y-1|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $y=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}(3x+2)|y-1{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}(3x+2)(y-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-2y+1)[{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}3x+2\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-2y+1){\\left[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+2x\\right]}_{x=y}^{x=2-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-2y+1)\\left[\\frac{3}{2}(2-y)^{2}+2(2-y)-\\left(\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+2y\\right)\\right]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =10{\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-2y+1)(1-y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =10{\\int }_{0}^{1}1-3y+3{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =10{\\left[y-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =10\\left(1-\\frac{3}{2}+1-\\frac{1}{4}-0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198647 :question/worked-solution "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, the distance between a point on $D$ and the line $y=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|y-2|.\n$$\n\nNext, the area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 1=2.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $y=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{M}{2}|y-2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{M}{2}(y-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}({\\int }_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}\\cdot [x]_{3}^{5}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}(y-2)^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}(5-3)(\\frac{1}{3}(1-2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}(0-2)^{3}) \\\\  & =M(-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, the distance between a point on $D$ and the line $y=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|y-2|.\n$$\n\nNext, the area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 1=2.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $y=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{M}{2}|y-2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{M}{2}(y-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}({\\int }_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}\\cdot [x]_{3}^{5}\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{3}(y-2)^{3}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}(5-3)\\left(\\frac{1}{3}(1-2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}(0-2)^{3}\\right) \\\\  & =M\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{7M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198648 :question/worked-solution "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/60/60ec523346df73503dd61893994de6ec31069271b1157f153bbe988c9e694798.png)\n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|x-1|.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=3M.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{x}}|x-1{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{x}}(x-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-1)^{2}[y]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-2x+1)(\\sqrt{x}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5/2}-2{x}^{3/2}+{x}^{1/2}-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{[\\frac{2}{7}{x}^{7/2}-\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{5/2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3M(\\frac{2}{7}-\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5M}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/60/60ec523346df73503dd61893994de6ec31069271b1157f153bbe988c9e694798.png)\n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|x-1|.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=3M.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{x}}|x-1{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{x}}(x-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-1)^{2}[y]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-2x+1)(\\sqrt{x}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5/2}-2{x}^{3/2}+{x}^{1/2}-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\left[\\frac{2}{7}{x}^{7/2}-\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{5/2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3M\\left(\\frac{2}{7}-\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3}-0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5M}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198649 :question/worked-solution "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, the distance between a point and the line $y=3$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|y-3|.\n$$\n\nNext, the mass density can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k{x}^{2},$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find the value of $k$ by integrating the mass density function over the plate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}k{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =k\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{-1}^{2}\\cdot [y]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{3}(8-(-1))(3-0) \\\\  & =9k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k{x}^{2}=\\frac{M}{9}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $y=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{M}{9}{x}^{2}|y-3{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{M}{9}{x}^{2}(y-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}({\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{3}(y-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{-1}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}(y-3)^{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(8-(-1))\\cdot [\\frac{1}{3}(0+27)] \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}\\cdot 3\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =3M.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, the distance between a point and the line $y=3$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|y-3|.\n$$\n\nNext, the mass density can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k{x}^{2},$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find the value of $k$ by integrating the mass density function over the plate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}k{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =k\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\right]}_{-1}^{2}\\cdot [y]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{3}(8-(-1))(3-0) \\\\  & =9k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k{x}^{2}=\\frac{M}{9}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $y=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{M}{9}{x}^{2}|y-3{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{M}{9}{x}^{2}(y-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}({\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{3}(y-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\right]}_{-1}^{2}\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{3}(y-3)^{3}\\right]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(8-(-1))\\cdot \\left[\\frac{1}{3}(0+27)\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}\\cdot 3\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =3M.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198650 :question/worked-solution "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2x},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f040a5500a1415b8617300f34074bb16d895889354ed7e722dbd668a233daebb.png)\n\nAlso, the distance between a point and the line $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|x-(-2)|=|x+2|.\n$$\n\nNext, the mass density can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k|y|=ky,$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find the value of $k$ by integrating the mass density function over the plate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}ky\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2x}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k\\cdot {[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=ky=\\frac{3M}{2}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\frac{3M}{2}y\\,|x+2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\frac{3M}{2}y(x+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+2)^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2x}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+2)^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}(x+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{4}+8{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{[\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5}+2{x}^{4}+\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}[\\frac{2}{5}+2+\\frac{8}{3}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}[\\frac{76}{15}] \\\\  & =\\frac{38M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2x},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f040a5500a1415b8617300f34074bb16d895889354ed7e722dbd668a233daebb.png)\n\nAlso, the distance between a point and the line $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|x-(-2)|=|x+2|.\n$$\n\nNext, the mass density can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k|y|=ky,$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find the value of $k$ by integrating the mass density function over the plate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}ky\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2x}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=ky=\\frac{3M}{2}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\frac{3M}{2}y\\,|x+2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\frac{3M}{2}y(x+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+2)^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2x}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+2)^{2}{\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}(x+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{4}+8{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\left[\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5}+2{x}^{4}+\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}\\left[\\frac{2}{5}+2+\\frac{8}{3}-0\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}\\left[\\frac{76}{15}\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{38M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198651 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 2=4.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{8}{3}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{4}{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{[\\frac{4}{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}(4+9-(\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}(13-\\frac{5}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}\\cdot \\frac{34}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{17M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{0}^{2}=\\frac{17}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{3}}.\n$$" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 2=4.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\left[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}\\right]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{8}{3}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{4}{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\left[\\frac{4}{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}\\right]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}\\left(4+9-\\left(\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}\\left(13-\\frac{5}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}\\cdot \\frac{34}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{17M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{0}^{2}=\\frac{17}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{3}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198652 :question/worked-solution "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is a type II plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/34/34c6fb2eead75cb5217e7b33b5b98400b3f82e8ec29c91de9b8db5b22cefa6d7.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2},\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=x{y}^{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x={y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{y}^{7}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{8}}{16}]}_{y=-1}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is a type II plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/34/34c6fb2eead75cb5217e7b33b5b98400b3f82e8ec29c91de9b8db5b22cefa6d7.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2},\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=x{y}^{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{3}\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{x=0}^{x={y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}-0\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{y}^{7}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{{y}^{8}}{16}\\right]}_{y=-1}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198663 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{3}{7}Mr\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot {[-\\frac{{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M\\cdot (\\frac{32}{5}-\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (-0+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M\\cdot \\frac{31}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{31M}{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{31M}{35}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{31}{35}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{3}{7}Mr\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot {\\left[-\\frac{{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}\\right]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M\\cdot \\left(\\frac{32}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\left(-0+\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M\\cdot \\frac{31}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{31M}{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{31M}{35}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{31}{35}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198664 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le x\\}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}My\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{[\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}\\cdot \\frac{16}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{12M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{12}{5}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le x\\}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}My\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\left[\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{5}\\right]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}\\cdot \\frac{16}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{12M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{12}{5}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198665 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=1\\cdot 2=2.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{6}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{6}\\frac{1}{3}(1-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{4}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}[y]_{4}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}(6-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=1\\cdot 2=2.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{6}{\\left[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\right]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{6}\\frac{1}{3}(1-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{4}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}[y]_{4}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}(6-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198794 :question/worked-solution "The given set contains the roots $q\\in Q$ of some quadratic polynomial $p(q).$\n\nSince the quadratic polynomial has roots ${q}_{1}=-1$ and ${q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=3,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(q) & =a(q-{q}_{1})(q-{q}_{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (q-(-1))(q-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =3(q+1)(q-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =3({q}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}q-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =3{q}^{2}+2q-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the given set as follows:\n\n$$\n{-1,\\frac{1}{3}}={q\\in Q\\,:\\,3{q}^{2}+2q-1=0}\n$$" "The given set contains the roots $q\\in Q$ of some quadratic polynomial $p(q).$\n\nSince the quadratic polynomial has roots ${q}_{1}=-1$ and ${q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=3,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(q) & =a(q-{q}_{1})(q-{q}_{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (q-(-1))\\left(q-\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =3(q+1)\\left(q-\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\left({q}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}q-\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =3{q}^{2}+2q-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the given set as follows:\n\n$$\n{-1,\\frac{1}{3}}={q\\in Q\\,:\\,3{q}^{2}+2q-1=0}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198797 :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x|>5 \\\\ 2|x|>5 \\\\ |x|>\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx<-\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in I=(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2})\\cup (\\frac{5}{2},\\infty ).\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|2n|>5$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={…,-4,-3}\\cup {3,4,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|2q|>5$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,B,$ and $C.$\"" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x|>5 \\\\ 2|x|>5 \\\\ |x|>\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx<-\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in I=\\left(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)\\cup \\left(\\frac{5}{2},\\infty \\right).\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|2n|>5$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={…,-4,-3}\\cup {3,4,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|2q|>5$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,B,$ and $C.$\""] [:db/cas 17592186198945 :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles of the triangle. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $0<x,y,z<\\pi .$ \n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=\\pi \n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z &=\\lambda \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z &=\\lambda \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0<z<\\pi ,$ we have that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\ne 0.$ On the other hand, if $y-x=\\pi \\cdot n,$ with $n$ an integer, we obtain that $x>\\pi $ or $y>\\pi ,$ which contradicts the constraints. Therefore, we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =\\pi  \\\\ x+x+x & =\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\n$$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & <f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles of the triangle. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $0<x,y,z<\\pi .$ \n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=\\pi \n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z &=\\lambda \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z &=\\lambda \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0<z<\\pi ,$ we have that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\ne 0.$ On the other hand, if $y-x=\\pi \\cdot n,$ with $n$ an integer, we obtain that $x>\\pi $ or $y>\\pi ,$ which contradicts the constraints. Therefore, we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =\\pi  \\\\ x+x+x & =\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),\n$$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & <f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198951 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{1+(x-y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,du]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}du]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}[u]_{0}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,dv \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡v]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{1+(x-y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,du\\right]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}du]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}[u]_{0}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,dv \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡v]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198952 :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{x+y}4z\\,dz]dA & ={∬}_{D}[2{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=x+y}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2((x+y)^{2}-0)\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}+4xy+2{y}^{2}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}+4xy+2{y}^{2}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}2{x}^{2}+4xy+2{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}2{x}^{2}+4xy+2{y}^{2}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2{x}^{2}y+2x{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}(x-0)+2x({x}^{2}-0)+\\frac{2}{3}({x}^{3}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{14}{3}{x}^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}({1}^{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{x+y}4z\\,dz]dA & ={∬}_{D}[2{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=x+y}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2((x+y)^{2}-0)\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}+4xy+2{y}^{2}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}+4xy+2{y}^{2}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}2{x}^{2}+4xy+2{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}2{x}^{2}+4xy+2{y}^{2}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\left[2{x}^{2}y+2x{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}(x-0)+2x({x}^{2}-0)+\\frac{2}{3}({x}^{3}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{14}{3}{x}^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{4}\\right]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}({1}^{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198955 :question/worked-solution "Writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}30x\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x-y}30x\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x-y}30x\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x-y}30x\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}[30xz]_{z=0}^{z=1-x-y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}30x(1-x-y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}30x-30{x}^{2}-30xy\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}30x-30{x}^{2}-30xy\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}30x-30{x}^{2}-30xy\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[30xy-30{x}^{2}y-15x{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1-x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}30x(1-x)-30{x}^{2}(1-x)-15x(1-x)^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}15{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+15x\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}15{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+15x\\,dx & ={[\\frac{15}{4}{x}^{4}-10{x}^{3}+\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{15}{4}-10+\\frac{15}{2})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}30x\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x-y}30x\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x-y}30x\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x-y}30x\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}[30xz]_{z=0}^{z=1-x-y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}30x(1-x-y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}30x-30{x}^{2}-30xy\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}30x-30{x}^{2}-30xy\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}30x-30{x}^{2}-30xy\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[30xy-30{x}^{2}y-15x{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1-x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}30x(1-x)-30{x}^{2}(1-x)-15x(1-x)^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}15{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+15x\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}15{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+15x\\,dx & ={\\left[\\frac{15}{4}{x}^{4}-10{x}^{3}+\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{2}\\right]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{15}{4}-10+\\frac{15}{2}\\right)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198956 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{x}}{y}^{2}\\cdot 12Mxy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{x}}12x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{1}[3x{y}^{4}]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{3}-3{x}^{9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =M{[\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{3}{10}{x}^{10}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =M(\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9M}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9M}{20}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{x}}{y}^{2}\\cdot 12Mxy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{x}}12x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{1}[3x{y}^{4}]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{3}-3{x}^{9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =M{\\left[\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{3}{10}{x}^{10}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =M\\left(\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{10}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{9M}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9M}{20}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198957 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{3}{8}Mr\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}M{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{4}{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}M({\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}M\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot ({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{{\\sin }^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}(1-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{8M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{3}{8}Mr\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}M{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{4}{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}M({\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}M\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot ({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}{\\left[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{{\\sin }^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}\\right]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}\\left(1-\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{8M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{8M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198962 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot 4xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4x{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4x{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{4{x}^{7}}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{8}}{6}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{6}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{85}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{85}{2}\\right)}{21}=\\frac{85}{42}.\n$$" "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=\\left(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot 4xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4x{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4x{\\left[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{4{x}^{7}}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{{x}^{8}}{6}\\right]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{6}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{85}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{85}{2}\\right)}{21}=\\frac{85}{42}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198964 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+y,\\,v=x+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,-1\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡u]_{u=\\pi /6}^{u=\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=-1}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+y,\\,v=x+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,-1\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡u]_{u=\\pi /6}^{u=\\pi /2}\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=-1}^{w=1} \\\\  & =\\left(1-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-0\\right)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198965 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\sqrt{y}+x\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\frac{1}{z+1}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y,\\,v=\\sqrt{y}+x,\\,w=\\frac{1}{z+1}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -(z+1)^{-2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -(z+1)^{-2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-(z+1)^{-2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(z+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={w}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{w}^{-2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{5}{w}^{-2}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\cdot [-{w}^{-1}]_{w=1}^{w=5} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\sqrt{y}+x\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\frac{1}{z+1}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y,\\,v=\\sqrt{y}+x,\\,w=\\frac{1}{z+1}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -(z+1)^{-2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -(z+1)^{-2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-(z+1)^{-2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(z+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={w}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{w}^{-2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{5}{w}^{-2}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\cdot [-{w}^{-1}]_{w=1}^{w=5} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}+1\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198966 :question/worked-solution "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area ${A}_{D}$ is given by $({x}_{c},{y}_{c}),$ where\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\,\\text{d}A,\\,{y}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](images/53/536f542f535513430311b29f43f6fbafb37832d6dfde3b9c81e82237cb011821.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nand ${A}_{D}=\\frac{32}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-coordinate of the centroid is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c} & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{32}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{x}^{2}}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{32}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{64}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(16-8{x}^{2}+{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{64}{[16x-\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{64}[(32-\\frac{64}{3}+\\frac{32}{5})-(-32+\\frac{64}{3}-\\frac{32}{5})] \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area ${A}_{D}$ is given by $({x}_{c},{y}_{c}),$ where\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\,\\text{d}A,\\,{y}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](images/53/536f542f535513430311b29f43f6fbafb37832d6dfde3b9c81e82237cb011821.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nand ${A}_{D}=\\frac{32}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-coordinate of the centroid is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c} & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{32}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{x}^{2}}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{32}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\left[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{64}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(16-8{x}^{2}+{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{64}{\\left[16x-\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\right]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{64}\\left[\\left(32-\\frac{64}{3}+\\frac{32}{5}\\right)-\\left(-32+\\frac{64}{3}-\\frac{32}{5}\\right)\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198969 :question/worked-solution "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\le z\\le 1}\n$$\n\nThe region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/30/30e43d707479d17f45fb74c023d16706ba9308ffc4421cb17a23d7c2ef16fbee.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/44/4429082d4ab0d6fdef75d79b1db02944f0c53741fb0ccf19d8a4ef46a253698f.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{y}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{2}\n$$\n\n- The left boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a9/a941e1bfabb21561b622080775c3c5389a86cf4150f1fb28dd692a022f607620.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane with the top curve given by $z=\\sqrt{1}=1.$\n\n![](images/20/20e339260b0947f92d8fedfa47e899ed53753e03e30c4b816edec06d092d9a01.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}{[y]}_{0}^{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{z}^{2}\\,{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{z}^{2}\\,{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{e}^{{z}^{3}}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{e-1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{e-1}{3}[x]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\le z\\le 1}\n$$\n\nThe region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/30/30e43d707479d17f45fb74c023d16706ba9308ffc4421cb17a23d7c2ef16fbee.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/44/4429082d4ab0d6fdef75d79b1db02944f0c53741fb0ccf19d8a4ef46a253698f.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{y}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{2}\n$$\n\n- The left boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a9/a941e1bfabb21561b622080775c3c5389a86cf4150f1fb28dd692a022f607620.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane with the top curve given by $z=\\sqrt{1}=1.$\n\n![](images/20/20e339260b0947f92d8fedfa47e899ed53753e03e30c4b816edec06d092d9a01.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}{[y]}_{0}^{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{z}^{2}\\,{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{z}^{2}\\,{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\left[\\frac{{e}^{{z}^{3}}}{3}\\right]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{e-1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{e-1}{3}[x]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198970 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$*" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186198970 :question/worked-solution "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,y),$ ${u}_{2}(x,y),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt[4]{\\pi },\\,\\,{z}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type III region, we first sketch the projection onto the $xz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bb477b93fb92174307bee87130d9957b135cdafaa4167f78f3d603c4192391d.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where ${z}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }.$\n\n![](images/cf/cf966e9ec5fbcf11727d46bb3906f05e4b30df4c9bef86a6eab4850b967d7ba0.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/88b5e19c3e263d160db8d041fd3504b25d4a8a8f7ae8e111cdeb5b153c3cd21f.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\ny={z}^{2}\\,⟺\\,z=\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{y}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b7/b748578888fdda878b0d44bb036310644c3437c1922aafd49cab6c165a9cf132.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xy$-plane with the top curve given by $y=\\sqrt{\\pi }.$\n\n![](images/60/60e35d0e77314aaecc60b0eda3c039afa0bdd44d49f68ad39e27c8856a9390e0.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}z\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2}){[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\frac{y}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\frac{y}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right))\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{x}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,y),$ ${u}_{2}(x,y),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt[4]{\\pi },\\,\\,{z}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type III region, we first sketch the projection onto the $xz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bb477b93fb92174307bee87130d9957b135cdafaa4167f78f3d603c4192391d.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where ${z}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }.$\n\n![](images/cf/cf966e9ec5fbcf11727d46bb3906f05e4b30df4c9bef86a6eab4850b967d7ba0.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/88b5e19c3e263d160db8d041fd3504b25d4a8a8f7ae8e111cdeb5b153c3cd21f.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\ny={z}^{2}\\,⟺\\,z=\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{y}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b7/b748578888fdda878b0d44bb036310644c3437c1922aafd49cab6c165a9cf132.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xy$-plane with the top curve given by $y=\\sqrt{\\pi }.$\n\n![](images/60/60e35d0e77314aaecc60b0eda3c039afa0bdd44d49f68ad39e27c8856a9390e0.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2}\\right)[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}z\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2}\\right){\\left[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\frac{y}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\frac{y}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y\\right]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\left[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2}\\right)\\right]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[-\\frac{x}{4}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198972 :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy+yz+xz\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy+yz+xz\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+z)x+yz\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(y+z)\\,{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}+yz[x]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(y+z)(2-0)+yz(2-0)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}2(y+z)+2yz\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$, treating $z$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}2(y+z)+2yz\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}2(y+z)+2yz\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}2(1+z)y+2z\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}((1+z)[{y}^{2}]_{1}^{2}+2z[y]_{1}^{2})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}((1+z)(4-1)+2z(2-1))\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3(1+z)+2z)\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3+5z)\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3+5z)\\text{d}z & ={[3z+\\frac{5}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3(1-0)+\\frac{5}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy+yz+xz\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy+yz+xz\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+z)x+yz\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(y+z)\\,{\\left[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{0}^{2}+yz[x]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(y+z)(2-0)+yz(2-0)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}2(y+z)+2yz\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$, treating $z$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}2(y+z)+2yz\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}2(y+z)+2yz\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}2(1+z)y+2z\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}((1+z)[{y}^{2}]_{1}^{2}+2z[y]_{1}^{2})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}((1+z)(4-1)+2z(2-1))\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3(1+z)+2z)\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3+5z)\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3+5z)\\text{d}z & ={\\left[3z+\\frac{5}{2}{z}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3(1-0)+\\frac{5}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198973 :question/worked-solution "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is a type II plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f8454539cbe01d45842ab03386e06bbbdca35d400d0e214563843d99cddd5a85.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le y,\\,1\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=4y.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot 4y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y[{\\int }_{0}^{y}2x\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y({y}^{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & =8-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is a type II plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f8454539cbe01d45842ab03386e06bbbdca35d400d0e214563843d99cddd5a85.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le y,\\,1\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=4y.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot 4y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y[{\\int }_{0}^{y}2x\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y({y}^{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}\\right]}_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & =8-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198974 :question/worked-solution "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area ${A}_{D}$ is given by $({x}_{c},{y}_{c}),$ where\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\,\\text{d}A,\\,{y}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of the centroid is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{c} & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{\\int }_{-x}^{x}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x[y]_{y=-x}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area ${A}_{D}$ is given by $({x}_{c},{y}_{c}),$ where\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\,\\text{d}A,\\,{y}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of the centroid is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{c} & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{\\int }_{-x}^{x}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x[y]_{y=-x}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\left[\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}\\right]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186198975 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](images/78/78f9da3d48727d2cb3c447be0f4050a044c4aabe29179fd31f04cd7b6c66eabd.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1-{y}^{2},\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)={y}^{2}.$ \n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{y}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot (1-2{y}^{2}+{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}-2{y}^{4}+{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}-2{y}^{4}+{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{2}{5}{y}^{5}+\\frac{1}{7}{y}^{7}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{105}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{8}{105}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{4}{15}\\right)}=\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$" "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=\\left(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](images/78/78f9da3d48727d2cb3c447be0f4050a044c4aabe29179fd31f04cd7b6c66eabd.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1-{y}^{2},\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)={y}^{2}.$ \n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{y}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{x=0}^{x=1-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot (1-2{y}^{2}+{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}-2{y}^{4}+{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}-2{y}^{4}+{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{2}{5}{y}^{5}+\\frac{1}{7}{y}^{7}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{105}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{8}{105}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{4}{15}\\right)}=\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186199007 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 5,\\,0\\le x+2y\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+2y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 5,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+2y){e}^{(x+2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{[\\frac{{e}^{{v}^{2}}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{5}(e-1)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)[u]_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)(5-1) \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 5,\\,0\\le x+2y\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+2y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 5,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+2y){e}^{(x+2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{\\left[\\frac{{e}^{{v}^{2}}}{2}\\right]}_{0}^{1}du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{5}(e-1)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)[u]_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)(5-1) \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186199016 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical points in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\ny=x=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ | $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\n$$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $10+\\sqrt{2}$ | $10-\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),\n$$ and its minimum value is $10-\\sqrt{2}.$" "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical points in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\ny=x=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right),\\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)$ | $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\n$$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $10+\\sqrt{2}$ | $10-\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right),\n$$ and its minimum value is $10-\\sqrt{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186199457 :tutorial/content "Recall that the moments of inertia $I_x$ and $I_y$ of a thin plate $D$ about the $x$- and $y$- axes are given by\n\n$$\nI_x = \\iint\\limits_D y^2\\,\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm dA \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\nI_y = \\iint\\limits_D x^2\\,\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm dA,\n$$\n\nwhere the factors $y^2$ and $x^2$ represent the squared distances from a point on the plate to the $x$- and $y$-axes, respectively.\n\nIn general, the **moment of inertia about an axis $l$** of a plane lamina occupying the region $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the point $(x,y)$ to the axis $l.$\n\nFor example, consider the thin plate that occupies the region\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwith mass density function $\\lambda(x,y) = 3x+6.$ Let's find its moment of inertia about the line $y=2.$\n\n![](images/24/2451a992f9ecdea8aa1775b64b3a182e0df3f7eab8cac87307c49ed164c6b813.png)\n\nThe distance between a point $(x,y)$ on $D$ and the line $y=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y) = |2 - y|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $y=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3x+6)\\,|2-y{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}3(x+2)(y-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}3(y-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}]}_{-1}^{1}\\cdot [(y-2)^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (-1+8) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the moments of inertia $I_x$ and $I_y$ of a thin plate $D$ about the $x$- and $y$- axes are given by\n\n$$\nI_x = \\iint\\limits_D y^2\\,\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm dA \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\nI_y = \\iint\\limits_D x^2\\,\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm dA,\n$$\n\nwhere the factors $y^2$ and $x^2$ represent the squared distances from a point on the plate to the $x$- and $y$-axes, respectively.\n\nIn general, the **moment of inertia about an axis $l$** of a plane lamina occupying the region $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the point $(x,y)$ to the axis $l.$\n\nFor example, consider the thin plate that occupies the region\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwith mass density function $\\lambda(x,y) = 3x+6.$ Let's find its moment of inertia about the line $y=2.$\n\n![](images/24/2451a992f9ecdea8aa1775b64b3a182e0df3f7eab8cac87307c49ed164c6b813.png)\n\nThe distance between a point $(x,y)$ on $D$ and the line $y=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y) = |2 - y|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $y=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3x+6)\\,|2-y{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}3(x+2)(y-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}3(y-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{-1}^{1}\\cdot [(y-2)^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot (-1+8) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186199468 :tutorial/content "Remember that when we measure the flux of a vector field through a *closed* surface, we always calculate with respect to an outward-pointing unit normal vector.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\langle x, \\,0, \\, -y^2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the conical solid shown below. \n\n![](images/0b/0be83ceada68d0b56be4872f21652a7d18932ff059ea704576ca3cce892fa87b.png)\n\n \n\nLet's calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$ \n\nFirst, since $S$ is a closed surface, we have that\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z = g(x,y) = 2 - \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}$ is oriented *upward*, and\n\n- the lower surface $S_2: z = h(x,y) = 0$ is oriented *downward*.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- First, we consider the upper cone $S_1.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = x, \\qquad\nQ = 0, \\qquad\nR = -y^2.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\right)-0\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\right)+(-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Next, we consider the lower plane $S_2.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 0,0,-1 \\rangle.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨x,0,-{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across the entire surface is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that when we measure the flux of a vector field through a *closed* surface, we always calculate with respect to an outward-pointing unit normal vector.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\langle x, \\,0, \\, -y^2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the conical solid shown below. \n\n![](images/0b/0be83ceada68d0b56be4872f21652a7d18932ff059ea704576ca3cce892fa87b.png)\n\n \n\nLet's calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$ \n\nFirst, since $S$ is a closed surface, we have that\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z = g(x,y) = 2 - \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}$ is oriented *upward*, and\n\n- the lower surface $S_2: z = h(x,y) = 0$ is oriented *downward*.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- First, we consider the upper cone $S_1.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = x, \\qquad\nQ = 0, \\qquad\nR = -y^2.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\right)-0\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\right)+(-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Next, we consider the lower plane $S_2.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 0,0,-1 \\rangle.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨x,0,-{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across the entire surface is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186199470 :tutorial/content "Let's now derive a general formula for the flux through an *upward-oriented* Cartesian surface.\n\nFor a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nConsider a surface $S$ given by the equation $z=g(x,y).$ The canonical parameterization of the surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y) = \\left\\langle x,\\: y,\\: g(x,y) \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nComputing the tangent vectors to the grid curves, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'} & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the $\\mathbf k$-component is positive, which indicates that our surface is upward-oriented.\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,y))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize as follows:\n\n- *For a surface $S$ given by $z = g(x,y)$ for $(x,y) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( -P\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial x} - Q\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial y} + R \\right) \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe have analogous results for surfaces of the form $y = h(x,z)$ and $x=k(y,z){:}$\n\n- *For a surface $S$ given by $y = h(x,z)$ for $(x,z) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( -P\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x} + Q - R\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial z} \\right) \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\n*where the unit normal to the surface is chosen such that the $\\mathbf j$-component is positive.*\n\n- *For a surface $S$ given $x = k(y,z)$ for $(y,z) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( P - Q\\dfrac{\\partial k}{\\partial y} - R\\dfrac{\\partial k}{\\partial z} \\right) \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\n*where the unit normal to the surface is chosen such that the $\\mathbf i$-component is positive.*" "Let's now derive a general formula for the flux through an *upward-oriented* Cartesian surface.\n\nFor a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nConsider a surface $S$ given by the equation $z=g(x,y).$ The canonical parameterization of the surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y) = \\left\\langle x,\\: y,\\: g(x,y) \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nComputing the tangent vectors to the grid curves, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'} & =\\left\\langle 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\left\\langle 0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}=\\left\\langle -\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the $\\mathbf k$-component is positive, which indicates that our surface is upward-oriented.\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,y))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot \\left\\langle -\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1\\right\\rangle \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R\\right)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize as follows:\n\n- *For a surface $S$ given by $z = g(x,y)$ for $(x,y) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( -P\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial x} - Q\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial y} + R \\right) \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe have analogous results for surfaces of the form $y = h(x,z)$ and $x=k(y,z){:}$\n\n- *For a surface $S$ given by $y = h(x,z)$ for $(x,z) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( -P\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x} + Q - R\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial z} \\right) \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\n*where the unit normal to the surface is chosen such that the $\\mathbf j$-component is positive.*\n\n- *For a surface $S$ given $x = k(y,z)$ for $(y,z) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( P - Q\\dfrac{\\partial k}{\\partial y} - R\\dfrac{\\partial k}{\\partial z} \\right) \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\n*where the unit normal to the surface is chosen such that the $\\mathbf i$-component is positive.*"] [:db/cas 17592186199471 :tutorial/content "Repeated integrals can feature three or more variables. In this lesson, we'll learn how to evaluate repeated integrals containing three variables.\n\nFor example, let's consider the repeated integral below:\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^2 \\int_0^2 \\int_0^1 x(y+z)^2 \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this integral, we first integrate with respect to the inner variable $(z),$ then with respect to the middle variable $(y),$ and finally with respect to the outer variable $(x).$ \n\nWe can write the first step more explicitly as\n\n$$\n%\\int_{1}^2 \\int_0^2 \\int_0^1 x(y+z)^2 \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x=\n\\int_{1}^2 \\int_0^2 \\left[ \\int_0^1 x(y+z)^2 \\:\\textrm{d}z\\right] \\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x.\n%= \\int_{1}^2 \\left[ \\int_0^2 \\left[ \\int_0^1 x(y+z)^2 \\:\\textrm{d}z \\right] \\textrm{d}y \\right] \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe now carry out the integration.\nFirst, we integrate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $z,$ *treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}x(y+z)^{2}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{[\\frac{(y+z)^{3}}{3}]}_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(\\frac{(y+1)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3})\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}x((y+1)^{3}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x((y+1)^{3}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe next step is to integrate with respect to $y,$ which we can write more explicitly as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13 \\int_{1}^2 \\left[ \\int_0^2x \\left( (y+1)^3 - y^3 \\right) \\textrm{d}y \\right] \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y,$ *treating $x$ as a constant,* we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}x((y+1)^{3}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}x{[(\\frac{(y+1)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4})]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}x[(\\frac{81}{4}-\\frac{16}{4})-(\\frac{1}{4}-0)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{64}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}16x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}16x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{[8{x}^{2}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(32-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 24 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^2 \\int_0^2 \\int_0^1 x(y+z)^2 \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x = 8.\n$$" "Repeated integrals can feature three or more variables. In this lesson, we'll learn how to evaluate repeated integrals containing three variables.\n\nFor example, let's consider the repeated integral below:\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^2 \\int_0^2 \\int_0^1 x(y+z)^2 \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this integral, we first integrate with respect to the inner variable $(z),$ then with respect to the middle variable $(y),$ and finally with respect to the outer variable $(x).$ \n\nWe can write the first step more explicitly as\n\n$$\n%\\int_{1}^2 \\int_0^2 \\int_0^1 x(y+z)^2 \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x=\n\\int_{1}^2 \\int_0^2 \\left[ \\int_0^1 x(y+z)^2 \\:\\textrm{d}z\\right] \\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x.\n%= \\int_{1}^2 \\left[ \\int_0^2 \\left[ \\int_0^1 x(y+z)^2 \\:\\textrm{d}z \\right] \\textrm{d}y \\right] \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe now carry out the integration.\nFirst, we integrate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $z,$ *treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}x(y+z)^{2}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{\\left[\\frac{(y+z)^{3}}{3}\\right]}_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\left(\\frac{(y+1)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}x((y+1)^{3}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x((y+1)^{3}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe next step is to integrate with respect to $y,$ which we can write more explicitly as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13 \\int_{1}^2 \\left[ \\int_0^2x \\left( (y+1)^3 - y^3 \\right) \\textrm{d}y \\right] \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y,$ *treating $x$ as a constant,* we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}x((y+1)^{3}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}x{\\left[\\left(\\frac{(y+1)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}\\right)\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\left[\\left(\\frac{81}{4}-\\frac{16}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}-0\\right)\\right]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{64}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}16x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}16x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{[8{x}^{2}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(32-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 24 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^2 \\int_0^2 \\int_0^1 x(y+z)^2 \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x = 8.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186199473 :tutorial/content "Let's calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle (x-y)^2, \\:(x+y)^2, \\: 12xy \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n \n\nthrough the surface $S$ defined by the Cartesian equation \n\n$$\nz=2y-2x\n$$\n\nfor $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ \n\nFor surfaces like this, substituting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain a parametrization \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u,\\: v,\\: 2v-2u \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nRecall that for a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨(x-y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(x+y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}12xy⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=2v-2u} \\\\  & =⟨(u-v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u+v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}12uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨(u-v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u+v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}12uv⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)^{2}\\cdot 2+(u+v)^{2}\\cdot (-2)+12uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨(x-y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(x+y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}12xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4uv\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4uv\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2u\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =[{u}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot ({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle (x-y)^2, \\:(x+y)^2, \\: 12xy \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n \n\nthrough the surface $S$ defined by the Cartesian equation \n\n$$\nz=2y-2x\n$$\n\nfor $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ \n\nFor surfaces like this, substituting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain a parametrization \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u,\\: v,\\: 2v-2u \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nRecall that for a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v-2u)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v-2u)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨(x-y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(x+y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}12xy⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=2v-2u} \\\\  & =⟨(u-v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u+v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}12uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨(u-v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u+v)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}12uv⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)^{2}\\cdot 2+(u+v)^{2}\\cdot (-2)+12uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨(x-y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(x+y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}12xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4uv\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4uv\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2u\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =[{u}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot ({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186199475 :tutorial/content "![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\n  \n\nOur goal is to find the image of the region $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nLet's first find the images of the horizontal sides:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 2.$ Substituting $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a line segment that lies on a line passing through the origin. The slope of this line must be\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{\\sin \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg)}{\\cos \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg)} = \\tan\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg),\n$$\n\nmeaning that the line makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. \nThe endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/ca644fa90870697e9cea1740c6432d43f682c650521217f19ea6f87c09cba0fd.png)\n\n- Similarly, we can find the image of the top side, where $v=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 2.$\n\n![](images/11/118c9d18e4d8158014643771c9fa018b1b15da38184f693b89d07f9fadd3c748.png)" "![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\n  \n\nOur goal is to find the image of the region $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nLet's first find the images of the horizontal sides:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 2.$ Substituting $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=\\left(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a line segment that lies on a line passing through the origin. The slope of this line must be\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{\\sin \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg)}{\\cos \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg)} = \\tan\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg),\n$$\n\nmeaning that the line makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. \nThe endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/ca644fa90870697e9cea1740c6432d43f682c650521217f19ea6f87c09cba0fd.png)\n\n- Similarly, we can find the image of the top side, where $v=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 2.$\n\n![](images/11/118c9d18e4d8158014643771c9fa018b1b15da38184f693b89d07f9fadd3c748.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186199481 :tutorial/content "Let's evaluate the following repeated integral using the rules we just discussed.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\int_{-1}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} (x+y+z)  \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}z\n$$\n\nFirst, we apply the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} x  \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}z\n+\n\\int_{-1}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} y  \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}z\n+\n\\int_{-1}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} z  \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}z\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the domain of integration $R = [0,2] \\times [0,1] \\times [-1,1]$ is a rectangular box, and each integrand can be expressed as $f(x) \\cdot g(y) \\cdot h(z).$ So, we can rewrite our sum of repeated integrals as follows:\n\nIndeed:\n\n\\[\nx = \\underbrace{x}_{f(x)} \\cdot \\underbrace{1}_{g(y)} \\cdot \\underbrace{1}_{h(z)}\n\\qquad\\qquad\ny = \\underbrace{1}_{f(x)} \\cdot \\underbrace{y}_{g(y)} \\cdot \\underbrace{1}_{h(z)}\n\\qquad\\qquad\nz = \\underbrace{1}_{f(x)} \\cdot \\underbrace{1}_{g(y)} \\cdot \\underbrace{z}_{h(z)}\n\\]\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2} x  \\, \\textrm{d}x  \\int_{0}^{1} \\textrm{d}y \\int_{-1}^{1} \\textrm{d}z\n+\n\\int_{0}^{2} \\textrm{d}x  \\int_{0}^{1} y \\, \\textrm{d}y \\int_{-1}^{1} \\textrm{d}z\n+\n\\int_{0}^{2} \\textrm{d}x  \\int_{0}^{1} \\textrm{d}y \\int_{-1}^{1} z \\, \\textrm{d}z\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate each of these separately:\n\n- For the first product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z & =(2-0)\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{-1}^{1}z\\,\\text{d}z & =(2-0)\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+y+z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z=4+2+0=6\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's evaluate the following repeated integral using the rules we just discussed.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\int_{-1}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} (x+y+z)  \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}z\n$$\n\nFirst, we apply the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} x  \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}z\n+\n\\int_{-1}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} y  \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}z\n+\n\\int_{-1}^{1} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} z  \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}z\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the domain of integration $R = [0,2] \\times [0,1] \\times [-1,1]$ is a rectangular box, and each integrand can be expressed as $f(x) \\cdot g(y) \\cdot h(z).$ So, we can rewrite our sum of repeated integrals as follows:\n\nIndeed:\n\n\\[\nx = \\underbrace{x}_{f(x)} \\cdot \\underbrace{1}_{g(y)} \\cdot \\underbrace{1}_{h(z)}\n\\qquad\\qquad\ny = \\underbrace{1}_{f(x)} \\cdot \\underbrace{y}_{g(y)} \\cdot \\underbrace{1}_{h(z)}\n\\qquad\\qquad\nz = \\underbrace{1}_{f(x)} \\cdot \\underbrace{1}_{g(y)} \\cdot \\underbrace{z}_{h(z)}\n\\]\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2} x  \\, \\textrm{d}x  \\int_{0}^{1} \\textrm{d}y \\int_{-1}^{1} \\textrm{d}z\n+\n\\int_{0}^{2} \\textrm{d}x  \\int_{0}^{1} y \\, \\textrm{d}y \\int_{-1}^{1} \\textrm{d}z\n+\n\\int_{0}^{2} \\textrm{d}x  \\int_{0}^{1} \\textrm{d}y \\int_{-1}^{1} z \\, \\textrm{d}z\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate each of these separately:\n\n- For the first product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z & =(2-0)\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{-1}^{1}z\\,\\text{d}z & =(2-0)\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}\\right]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+y+z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z=4+2+0=6\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186199490 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we saw how to calculate the total moment about the origin of a collection of masses distributed along the $x$-axis. We'll now extend our understanding to include moments of thin rods.\n\nConsider a thin rod between the points $x=1$ and $x=3$ along the $x$-axis with mass density function $\\lambda(x) = 3x^2 \\, \\textrm{kg/m},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/c9/c9ee70d28476ebda61ccce5c6342a7f1a48a3e0f4dc26ee1af147d6f3d34a218.png)\n\nHow can we find the total moment of the rod about the origin?\n\nTo answer this, we once again consider a small piece of rod located in the interval $(x, x+\\Delta x),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fd2dc58c503333a365b140de3ef1b559477788953dab14549478ca031df98125.png)\n\n \n\nIf $\\Delta{x}$ is very small, we can approximate this piece as a point mass. \n\nThe moment of this piece about the origin, denoted $\\Delta{M_O},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{M}_{O} & ≈x\\cdot Δm \\\\  & ≈x\\cdot \\lambda (x)Δx \\\\  & =x\\cdot 3{x}^{2}Δx \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, to find the moment of the entire rod about the origin, we sum the moments of all such pieces and take the limit as $\\Delta x.$ By doing so, we get \n\n$$\nM_O = \\int_{1}^{3} 3x^3 \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral, we find that the total moment about $O$ of the rod is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(3)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{3(1)^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =60\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn general, the moment about the origin of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda(x)$ for $a \\leq x \\leq b$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_O = \\int_a^b x\\, \\lambda(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" "In a previous lesson, we saw how to calculate the total moment about the origin of a collection of masses distributed along the $x$-axis. We'll now extend our understanding to include moments of thin rods.\n\nConsider a thin rod between the points $x=1$ and $x=3$ along the $x$-axis with mass density function $\\lambda(x) = 3x^2 \\, \\textrm{kg/m},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/c9/c9ee70d28476ebda61ccce5c6342a7f1a48a3e0f4dc26ee1af147d6f3d34a218.png)\n\nHow can we find the total moment of the rod about the origin?\n\nTo answer this, we once again consider a small piece of rod located in the interval $(x, x+\\Delta x),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fd2dc58c503333a365b140de3ef1b559477788953dab14549478ca031df98125.png)\n\n \n\nIf $\\Delta{x}$ is very small, we can approximate this piece as a point mass. \n\nThe moment of this piece about the origin, denoted $\\Delta{M_O},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{M}_{O} & ≈x\\cdot Δm \\\\  & ≈x\\cdot \\lambda (x)Δx \\\\  & =x\\cdot 3{x}^{2}Δx \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, to find the moment of the entire rod about the origin, we sum the moments of all such pieces and take the limit as $\\Delta x.$ By doing so, we get \n\n$$\nM_O = \\int_{1}^{3} 3x^3 \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral, we find that the total moment about $O$ of the rod is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}\\right]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(3)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{3(1)^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =60\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn general, the moment about the origin of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda(x)$ for $a \\leq x \\leq b$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_O = \\int_a^b x\\, \\lambda(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186199500 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to find the points of intersection of the elliptic paraboloid \n$z=x^2+y^2$\nand the line with the equation\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = -y = -z+1.\n$$\nThe line, paraboloid, and their points of intersection are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5159ac99a1bc9f73c01df3c2cc2e6fb2093a3d68e00731609d7b62a6fa55149d.png)\n\n \n\nThe trick is to express the equation of the line in parametric form, substitute the parametric equations into the equation of the surface, and solve for the parameter.\n\nSo, first, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\nx = -y = -z+1 = t  \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = t \\\\ y = - t \\\\ z = - t + 1\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nOur intersection points must belong to the line and the surface simultaneously. So, substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ (-t+1) & =(t)^{2}+(-t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get\n\n$$\n2t^2+t - 1=0.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are\n$t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $t=-1.$ \n\nSubstituting these values back into the parametric equations of the line\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y=-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z=-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+1=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $$\n\\displaystyle (\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}).\n$$\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(-1)=-1 \\\\ y=-(-1)=1 \\\\ z=-(-1)+1=2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2})\n$$ and $(-1,1,2).$ \n\nLet's see another example." "Suppose we wish to find the points of intersection of the elliptic paraboloid \n$z=x^2+y^2$\nand the line with the equation\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = -y = -z+1.\n$$\nThe line, paraboloid, and their points of intersection are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5159ac99a1bc9f73c01df3c2cc2e6fb2093a3d68e00731609d7b62a6fa55149d.png)\n\n \n\nThe trick is to express the equation of the line in parametric form, substitute the parametric equations into the equation of the surface, and solve for the parameter.\n\nSo, first, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\nx = -y = -z+1 = t  \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = t \\\\ y = - t \\\\ z = - t + 1\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nOur intersection points must belong to the line and the surface simultaneously. So, substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ (-t+1) & =(t)^{2}+(-t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get\n\n$$\n2t^2+t - 1=0.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are\n$t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $t=-1.$ \n\nSubstituting these values back into the parametric equations of the line\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y=-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z=-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+1=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(-1)=-1 \\\\ y=-(-1)=1 \\\\ z=-(-1)+1=2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\n$$ and $(-1,1,2).$ \n\nLet's see another example."] [:db/cas 17592186199504 :tutorial/content "Here, we derive the formula for the second-degree Taylor polynomial of a multivariable function in vector form.\n\nSuppose that the function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined by\n\n$$\nf : {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{array} \\right]} \\longrightarrow z\n$$\n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{a}.$\nBy restricting $\\mathbf x$ to a line through $\\mathbf{a},$ we can consider $f$ as a function of one variable. Then, we'll use the second-degree Taylor polynomial of a single-variable function to derive the formula.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{x}_0$ be a constant vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ Then, the line through $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{x}_0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{a} + t(\\mathbf{x}_0 - \\mathbf{a}), \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}(0) = \\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{x}(1) = \\mathbf{x}_0.$\n\nNow, we define a function $F: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ by restricting the domain of $f$ to this line:\n\n$$\nF(t)\n= f(\\mathbf{x}(t))\n%= f(\\mathbf{a} + t(\\mathbf{x}_0 - \\mathbf{a}))\n$$\n\nThe second-degree Taylor polynomial of this single-variable function about $t=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(t) \\approx F(0) + F'(0)t + \\dfrac12F''(0)t^2.\n$$\n\nWe can find the first derivative of $F$ using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a+t({x}_{0}-a)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot ({x}_{0}-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{x}}$ is a row vector and $(\\mathbf{x}_0 - \\mathbf{a})$ is a column vector, this is a scalar quantity and, hence, equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot ({x}_{0}-a) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot ({x}_{0}-a))}^{T} \\\\  & =({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith this, we can find the second derivative of $F{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}F}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}) \\\\  & =({x}_{0}-a)^{T}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & =({x}_{0}-a)^{T}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =({x}_{0}-a)^{T}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}f}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({x}_{0}-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first and second derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) &={f}^{′}(x(t))({x}_{0}-a) \\\\ {F}^{″}(t) &=({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(x(t))({x}_{0}-a).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the function and its derivatives at $t=0$ and using the fact that $\\mathbf{x}(0) = \\mathbf{a},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}F ( 0 ) = f ( \\mathbf{x} ( 0 ) ) \\\\ {F}^{\\prime } ( 0 ) = {𝒇}^{\\prime } ( \\mathbf{x} ( 0 ) ) ( {\\mathbf{x}}_{0} - \\mathbf{a} ) \\\\ {F}^{\\prime\\prime } ( 0 ) = ( {\\mathbf{x}}_{0} - \\mathbf{a} {)}^{T} {𝒇}^{\\prime\\prime } ( \\mathbf{x} ( 0 ) ) ( {\\mathbf{x}}_{0} - \\mathbf{a} )\\end{array} \\right. }\n\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}F ( 0 ) = f ( \\mathbf{a} ) \\\\ {F}^{\\prime } ( 0 ) = {𝒇}^{\\prime } ( \\mathbf{a} ) ( {\\mathbf{x}}_{0} - \\mathbf{a} ) \\\\ {F}^{\\prime\\prime } ( 0 ) = ( {\\mathbf{x}}_{0} - \\mathbf{a} {)}^{T} {𝒇}^{\\prime\\prime } ( \\mathbf{a} ) ( {\\mathbf{x}}_{0} - \\mathbf{a} ) .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nPutting these values into the second-degree Taylor polynomial of a single-variable function about $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & ≈F(0)+{F}^{′}(0)t+\\frac{1}{2}{F}^{″}(0){t}^{2} \\\\  & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)({x}_{0}-a)t+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)({x}_{0}-a){t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using this polynomial to approximate the value of $F(1),$ and the fact that $F(1) = f(\\mathbf{x}(1)) = f(\\mathbf{x}_0),$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)({x}_{0}-a)\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)({x}_{0}-a)\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\ f({x}_{0}) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)({x}_{0}-a)+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)({x}_{0}-a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\mathbf{x}_0 \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is an arbitrary constant. Therefore, we can relax the assumption that $\\mathbf{x}_0$ is fixed and replace it with a general point $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ near $\\mathbf{a}$ to obtain the formula for the second-degree Taylor polynomial of a multivariable function in vector form:\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{a}) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\dfrac{1}{2} (\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a}) (\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n$$" "Here, we derive the formula for the second-degree Taylor polynomial of a multivariable function in vector form.\n\nSuppose that the function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined by\n\n$$\nf : {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{array} \\right]} \\longrightarrow z\n$$\n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{a}.$\nBy restricting $\\mathbf x$ to a line through $\\mathbf{a},$ we can consider $f$ as a function of one variable. Then, we'll use the second-degree Taylor polynomial of a single-variable function to derive the formula.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{x}_0$ be a constant vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ Then, the line through $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{x}_0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{a} + t(\\mathbf{x}_0 - \\mathbf{a}), \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}(0) = \\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{x}(1) = \\mathbf{x}_0.$\n\nNow, we define a function $F: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ by restricting the domain of $f$ to this line:\n\n$$\nF(t)\n= f(\\mathbf{x}(t))\n%= f(\\mathbf{a} + t(\\mathbf{x}_0 - \\mathbf{a}))\n$$\n\nThe second-degree Taylor polynomial of this single-variable function about $t=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(t) \\approx F(0) + F'(0)t + \\dfrac12F''(0)t^2.\n$$\n\nWe can find the first derivative of $F$ using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a+t({x}_{0}-a)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot ({x}_{0}-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{x}}$ is a row vector and $(\\mathbf{x}_0 - \\mathbf{a})$ is a column vector, this is a scalar quantity and, hence, equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot ({x}_{0}-a) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot ({x}_{0}-a)\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & =({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith this, we can find the second derivative of $F{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}F}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\right) \\\\  & =({x}_{0}-a)^{T}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & =({x}_{0}-a)^{T}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =({x}_{0}-a)^{T}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}f}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({x}_{0}-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first and second derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) &={f}^{′}(x(t))({x}_{0}-a) \\\\ {F}^{″}(t) &=({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(x(t))({x}_{0}-a).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the function and its derivatives at $t=0$ and using the fact that $\\mathbf{x}(0) = \\mathbf{a},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}F ( 0 ) = f ( \\mathbf{x} ( 0 ) ) \\\\ {F}^{\\prime } ( 0 ) = {𝒇}^{\\prime } ( \\mathbf{x} ( 0 ) ) ( {\\mathbf{x}}_{0} - \\mathbf{a} ) \\\\ {F}^{\\prime\\prime } ( 0 ) = ( {\\mathbf{x}}_{0} - \\mathbf{a} {)}^{T} {𝒇}^{\\prime\\prime } ( \\mathbf{x} ( 0 ) ) ( {\\mathbf{x}}_{0} - \\mathbf{a} )\\end{array} \\right. }\n\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}F ( 0 ) = f ( \\mathbf{a} ) \\\\ {F}^{\\prime } ( 0 ) = {𝒇}^{\\prime } ( \\mathbf{a} ) ( {\\mathbf{x}}_{0} - \\mathbf{a} ) \\\\ {F}^{\\prime\\prime } ( 0 ) = ( {\\mathbf{x}}_{0} - \\mathbf{a} {)}^{T} {𝒇}^{\\prime\\prime } ( \\mathbf{a} ) ( {\\mathbf{x}}_{0} - \\mathbf{a} ) .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nPutting these values into the second-degree Taylor polynomial of a single-variable function about $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & ≈F(0)+{F}^{′}(0)t+\\frac{1}{2}{F}^{″}(0){t}^{2} \\\\  & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)({x}_{0}-a)t+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)({x}_{0}-a){t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using this polynomial to approximate the value of $F(1),$ and the fact that $F(1) = f(\\mathbf{x}(1)) = f(\\mathbf{x}_0),$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)({x}_{0}-a)\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)({x}_{0}-a)\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\ f({x}_{0}) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)({x}_{0}-a)+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)({x}_{0}-a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\mathbf{x}_0 \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is an arbitrary constant. Therefore, we can relax the assumption that $\\mathbf{x}_0$ is fixed and replace it with a general point $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ near $\\mathbf{a}$ to obtain the formula for the second-degree Taylor polynomial of a multivariable function in vector form:\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{a}) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\dfrac{1}{2} (\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a}) (\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186199558 :tutorial/content "Recall that any rational number can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{m}{n}\n$$\n\nwhere $m,n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. This means that any rational number can be defined using a pair of integers:\n\n$$\n(m, n) \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{Z}\n$$\n\nThis, in turn, means that $\\mathbb{Q}$ can be injectively embedded into the Cartesian product $\\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{Z}$, and we already know that $\\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{Z}$ is countably infinite.\n\nTherefore, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *The set of rational numbers is countably infinite, i.e.,*\n> $\\mid \\mathbb{Q} \\mid = \\aleph_0$.\n\nLet's describe how we can \"count\" all the elements of the set $\\mathbb{Q}^+$, the set of all positive rationals. We'll then use this to construct a bijection from $\\mathbb N$ to $\\mathbb Q$.\n\nFirst, we write down the elements of $\\mathbb Q^+$ in an infinite array that extends forever to the right and bottom, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f0ec1463b9b71ed6050c1d58b0911b3ca42b425dbdd36d83bc51f0679fef4fac.png)\n\nWe skip any equivalent fraction representations if the corresponding number appears somewhere in the previous rows. For example, notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{2}=\\dfrac11 = 1\n$$\n\nso we can skip this in the second row of the table.\n\nNow, we can traverse the array in the manner shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d9b12de7f732fdec24d234a26be3888c37a220e2c12052215fa9609640040999.png)\n\nBy assigning each natural number $n$ to the $n$-th distinct fraction encountered in this path, we define a bijection $f: \\mathbb N\\to \\mathbb Q^+$ between $\\mathbb{N}$ and $\\mathbb{Q}^+$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= \\frac{1}{1} = 1 \\\\\nf(2) &= \\frac{2}{1} = 2 \\\\\nf(3) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nf(4) &= \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use this to construct a bijection $g: \\mathbb N\\to \\mathbb Q$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(1) &= 0 \\\\\ng(2) &= f(1) = 1 \\\\\ng(3) &= -f(1) =-1 \\\\\ng(4) &= f(2) = 2 \\\\\ng(5) &= -f(2) =-2 \\\\\ng(6) &= f(3) = \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOr, more compactly:\n\n$$\ng(n) = \n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}0 , \\hspace{1em} & n = 1 \\\\ f ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} n ) , \\hspace{1em} & n \\text{ even} \\\\ - f ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} ( n - 1 ) ) , \\hspace{1em} & n > 1 \\text{ and odd}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$" "Recall that any rational number can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{m}{n}\n$$\n\nwhere $m,n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. This means that any rational number can be defined using a pair of integers:\n\n$$\n(m, n) \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{Z}\n$$\n\nThis, in turn, means that $\\mathbb{Q}$ can be injectively embedded into the Cartesian product $\\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{Z}$, and we already know that $\\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{Z}$ is countably infinite.\n\nTherefore, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *The set of rational numbers is countably infinite, i.e.,*\n> $\\mid \\mathbb{Q} \\mid = \\aleph_0$.\n\nLet's describe how we can \"count\" all the elements of the set $\\mathbb{Q}^+$, the set of all positive rationals. We'll then use this to construct a bijection from $\\mathbb N$ to $\\mathbb Q$.\n\nFirst, we write down the elements of $\\mathbb Q^+$ in an infinite array that extends forever to the right and bottom, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f0ec1463b9b71ed6050c1d58b0911b3ca42b425dbdd36d83bc51f0679fef4fac.png)\n\nWe skip any equivalent fraction representations if the corresponding number appears somewhere in the previous rows. For example, notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{2}=\\dfrac11 = 1\n$$\n\nso we can skip this in the second row of the table.\n\nNow, we can traverse the array in the manner shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d9b12de7f732fdec24d234a26be3888c37a220e2c12052215fa9609640040999.png)\n\nBy assigning each natural number $n$ to the $n$-th distinct fraction encountered in this path, we define a bijection $f: \\mathbb N\\to \\mathbb Q^+$ between $\\mathbb{N}$ and $\\mathbb{Q}^+$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= \\frac{1}{1} = 1 \\\\\nf(2) &= \\frac{2}{1} = 2 \\\\\nf(3) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nf(4) &= \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use this to construct a bijection $g: \\mathbb N\\to \\mathbb Q$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(1) &= 0 \\\\\ng(2) &= f(1) = 1 \\\\\ng(3) &= -f(1) =-1 \\\\\ng(4) &= f(2) = 2 \\\\\ng(5) &= -f(2) =-2 \\\\\ng(6) &= f(3) = \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOr, more compactly:\n\n$$\ng(n) = \n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}0 , \\hspace{1em} & n = 1 \\\\ f \\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} n \\right) , \\hspace{1em} & n \\text{ even} \\\\ - f \\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} ( n - 1 ) \\right) , \\hspace{1em} & n > 1 \\text{ and odd}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186199596 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we saw how to define the limit of a null sequence (i.e., a sequence whose limit equals zero) and how to construct a formal proof that a given sequence is null.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll extend our understanding to include sequences that converge a finite (not necessarily zero) limit. If such a limit exists, it's usually denoted as $L$.\n\nConsider the following sequence:\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{n+1}{n}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nIt's easy to see that the limit of this sequence is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{L}}={\\color{red}{1}}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}a_{n} &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{n + 1}{n}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{n}{n} + \\frac{1}{n}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(1 + \\frac{1}{n}) \\\\\n&= 1 + 0 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that for any $\\varepsilon > 0$, there exists a natural number $N(\\varepsilon)$ such that the distance between $a_n$ and\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{L}} = {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nis *smaller than* $\\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n\\forall n\\geq N,\\qquad \\mid a_n - {\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nLet's draw a few sketches to illustrate this idea.\n\n- If we select $\\varepsilon = 0.5$, then $\\mid a_n-{\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all $n \\geq 3$.\n\n![](images/be/bee8ffa92b56e2e8dedc214322481b13a3ab8cd4b73d84a5c1300d10dbd43d4f.png)\n\n- If $\\varepsilon = 0.3$, then $\\mid a_n-{\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all $n \\geq 4$.\n\n![](images/d2/d27e06c19457ab1c89386cde995c043c6d6d55169ec8fdc32509690bf1880767.png)\n\n- If $\\varepsilon = 0.1$, then $\\mid a_n-{\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all $n \\geq 11$.\n\n![](images/56/564c5ddf12f0171c5d55da0d669f9f7bff2726707c72e473fa9e8eb6704bd937.png)\n\nThe key idea is this: By making $\\varepsilon$ smaller and smaller, we can *always* find an index $N$ where the distance between $a_n$ and $\\color{red}L$ is smaller than $\\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$" "In previous lessons, we saw how to define the limit of a null sequence (i.e., a sequence whose limit equals zero) and how to construct a formal proof that a given sequence is null.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll extend our understanding to include sequences that converge a finite (not necessarily zero) limit. If such a limit exists, it's usually denoted as $L$.\n\nConsider the following sequence:\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{n+1}{n}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nIt's easy to see that the limit of this sequence is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{L}}={\\color{red}{1}}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}a_{n} &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{n + 1}{n}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{n}{n} + \\frac{1}{n}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{n}\\right) \\\\\n&= 1 + 0 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that for any $\\varepsilon > 0$, there exists a natural number $N(\\varepsilon)$ such that the distance between $a_n$ and\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{L}} = {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nis *smaller than* $\\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n\\forall n\\geq N,\\qquad \\mid a_n - {\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nLet's draw a few sketches to illustrate this idea.\n\n- If we select $\\varepsilon = 0.5$, then $\\mid a_n-{\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all $n \\geq 3$.\n\n![](images/be/bee8ffa92b56e2e8dedc214322481b13a3ab8cd4b73d84a5c1300d10dbd43d4f.png)\n\n- If $\\varepsilon = 0.3$, then $\\mid a_n-{\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all $n \\geq 4$.\n\n![](images/d2/d27e06c19457ab1c89386cde995c043c6d6d55169ec8fdc32509690bf1880767.png)\n\n- If $\\varepsilon = 0.1$, then $\\mid a_n-{\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all $n \\geq 11$.\n\n![](images/56/564c5ddf12f0171c5d55da0d669f9f7bff2726707c72e473fa9e8eb6704bd937.png)\n\nThe key idea is this: By making $\\varepsilon$ smaller and smaller, we can *always* find an index $N$ where the distance between $a_n$ and $\\color{red}L$ is smaller than $\\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186199705 :answer/value "H(t) = 3\\sin (\\frac{π}{2}t) + 5" "H(t) = 3\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{2}t\\right) + 5"] [:db/cas 17592186199707 :answer/value "H(t) =-6\\sin (\\frac{3π}{2}t) + 2" "H(t) =-6\\sin \\left(\\frac{3π}{2}t\\right) + 2"] [:db/cas 17592186203752 :question/worked-solution "Given a population of $N$ individuals in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $np > 5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n- $n(1-p) > 5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nThe factor\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}\n$$\n\nis the finite population correction factor for the variance. This is used in cases of finite samples where\n\n- $n\\gt 5\\%\\cdot N$ (i.e., the sample comprises more than $5\\%$ of the population), and\n- The sample is conducted without replacement.\n\nIf a sample is conducted from a finite population *with replacement*, then the sample data are independent, and a finite population correction factor is not required.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn = 30, \\qquad p = 0.25, \\qquad N = 100\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle n & \\displaystyle  > 5 \\mathrm{\\% } \\cdot  100 = 5 \\\\ \\displaystyle n p & \\displaystyle  = 30 ( 0.25 ) = 7.5 > 5 \\\\ \\displaystyle n ( 1 - p ) & \\displaystyle  = 30 ( 0.75 ) = 22.5 > 5\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nwe can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p} \\mid ∼N(p, (p(1 - p))/(n) \\cdot \\frac{N - n}{N - 1}); ∼N(0.25, (0.25(1 - 0.25))/(30) \\cdot \\frac{70}{99}); ∼N(0.25, \\frac{7}{1584})\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n\n$$\nP\\left(0.35 \\lt \\widehat{\\,p} \\lt 0.5\\right)\n$$\n\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0.35 < \\widehat{p} < 0.5) &= P(\\frac{0.35 - 0.25}{\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{1584}}} < Z < \\frac{0.5 - 0.25}{\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{1584}}}) \\\\\n&\\approx P(1.50 < Z < 3.76) \\\\\n&= P(Z < 3.76) - P(Z < 1.50) \\\\\n&= Φ(3.76) - Φ(1.50)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP\\left(0.35 \\lt \\widehat{\\,p} \\lt 0.5\\right) \\approx \\Phi(3.76) - \\Phi(1.50)\n$$" "Given a population of $N$ individuals in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $np > 5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n- $n(1-p) > 5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nThe factor\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}\n$$\n\nis the finite population correction factor for the variance. This is used in cases of finite samples where\n\n- $n\\gt 5\\%\\cdot N$ (i.e., the sample comprises more than $5\\%$ of the population), and\n- The sample is conducted without replacement.\n\nIf a sample is conducted from a finite population *with replacement*, then the sample data are independent, and a finite population correction factor is not required.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn = 30, \\qquad p = 0.25, \\qquad N = 100\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle n & \\displaystyle  > 5 \\mathrm{\\% } \\cdot  100 = 5 \\\\ \\displaystyle n p & \\displaystyle  = 30 ( 0.25 ) = 7.5 > 5 \\\\ \\displaystyle n ( 1 - p ) & \\displaystyle  = 30 ( 0.75 ) = 22.5 > 5\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nwe can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p} \\mid ∼N\\left(p, (p(1 - p))/(n) \\cdot \\frac{N - n}{N - 1}\\right); ∼N\\left(0.25, (0.25(1 - 0.25))/(30) \\cdot \\frac{70}{99}\\right); ∼N\\left(0.25, \\frac{7}{1584}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n\n$$\nP\\left(0.35 \\lt \\widehat{\\,p} \\lt 0.5\\right)\n$$\n\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0.35 < \\widehat{p} < 0.5) &= P\\left(\\frac{0.35 - 0.25}{\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{1584}}} < Z < \\frac{0.5 - 0.25}{\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{1584}}}\\right) \\\\\n&\\approx P(1.50 < Z < 3.76) \\\\\n&= P(Z < 3.76) - P(Z < 1.50) \\\\\n&= Φ(3.76) - Φ(1.50)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP\\left(0.35 \\lt \\widehat{\\,p} \\lt 0.5\\right) \\approx \\Phi(3.76) - \\Phi(1.50)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186203753 :question/worked-solution "Given a population of $N$ individuals in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $np > 5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n- $n(1-p) > 5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nThe factor\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}\n$$\n\nis the finite population correction factor for the variance. This is used in cases of finite samples where\n\n- $n\\gt 5\\%\\cdot N$ (i.e., the sample comprises more than $5\\%$ of the population), and\n- the sample is conducted without replacement.\n\nIf a sample is conducted from a finite population *with replacement*, then the sample data are independent, and a finite population correction factor is not required.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn = 32, \\qquad p = 0.8, \\qquad N = 182\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle n & \\displaystyle  > 5 \\mathrm{\\% } \\cdot  182 = 9.1 \\\\ \\displaystyle n p & \\displaystyle  = 32 ( 0.8 ) = 25.6 > 5 \\\\ \\displaystyle n ( 1 - p ) & \\displaystyle  = 32 ( 0.2 ) = 6.4 > 5\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nwe can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p} \\mid ∼N(p, (p(1 - p))/(n) \\cdot \\frac{N - n}{N - 1}); ∼N(0.8, (0.8(1 - 0.8))/(32) \\cdot \\frac{150}{181}); ∼N(0.8, \\frac{3}{724})\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n\n$$\nP\\left(\\widehat{\\,p} \\geq \\dfrac{24}{32}\\right) = P\\left(\\widehat{\\,p} \\geq 0.75 \\right)\n$$\n\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(\\widehat{p} \\ge 0.75) &= P(Z \\ge \\frac{0.75 - 0.8}{\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{724}}}) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\ge - 0.78) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(Z < - 0.78) \\\\\n&= 1 - Φ(-0.78)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.78) = 0.2177\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(\\widehat{p} \\ge 0.75) &= 1 - Φ(-0.78) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.2177 \\\\\n&= 0.7823\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a population of $N$ individuals in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $np > 5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n- $n(1-p) > 5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nThe factor\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}\n$$\n\nis the finite population correction factor for the variance. This is used in cases of finite samples where\n\n- $n\\gt 5\\%\\cdot N$ (i.e., the sample comprises more than $5\\%$ of the population), and\n- the sample is conducted without replacement.\n\nIf a sample is conducted from a finite population *with replacement*, then the sample data are independent, and a finite population correction factor is not required.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn = 32, \\qquad p = 0.8, \\qquad N = 182\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle n & \\displaystyle  > 5 \\mathrm{\\% } \\cdot  182 = 9.1 \\\\ \\displaystyle n p & \\displaystyle  = 32 ( 0.8 ) = 25.6 > 5 \\\\ \\displaystyle n ( 1 - p ) & \\displaystyle  = 32 ( 0.2 ) = 6.4 > 5\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nwe can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p} \\mid ∼N\\left(p, (p(1 - p))/(n) \\cdot \\frac{N - n}{N - 1}\\right); ∼N\\left(0.8, (0.8(1 - 0.8))/(32) \\cdot \\frac{150}{181}\\right); ∼N\\left(0.8, \\frac{3}{724}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n\n$$\nP\\left(\\widehat{\\,p} \\geq \\dfrac{24}{32}\\right) = P\\left(\\widehat{\\,p} \\geq 0.75 \\right)\n$$\n\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(\\widehat{p} \\ge 0.75) &= P\\left(Z \\ge \\frac{0.75 - 0.8}{\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{724}}}\\right) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\ge - 0.78) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(Z < - 0.78) \\\\\n&= 1 - Φ(-0.78)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.78) = 0.2177\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(\\widehat{p} \\ge 0.75) &= 1 - Φ(-0.78) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.2177 \\\\\n&= 0.7823\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186203802 :question/worked-solution "Let us call $n_1, n_2$ the sample sizes of eighth and ninth-grade students, and $\\widehat{\\,p}_1, \\widehat{\\,p}_2$ the proportion of students who vote in student elections in the two samples.\n\nSince $n_1=62$, $n_2=70$,\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}_1=\\dfrac{48}{62}=\\dfrac{24}{31}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}_2=\\dfrac{35}{70} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n- $n_1 \\widehat{\\,p}_1 = 48> 5$,\n- $n_1(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_1) = 62 - 48 = 14> 5$,\n- $n_2 \\widehat{\\,p}_2 = 35> 5$,\n- $n_2(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_2) = 70- 35 = 35> 5$.\n\nAs a result, we may use a normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(\\widehat{\\,p}_1 - \\widehat{\\,p}_2) - (p_1-p_2)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}_1(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_1)}{n_1}+\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}_2(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_2)}{n_2}}\\phantom{\\mid}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}_1, \\widehat{\\,p}_2$ are the sample proportions,\n- $p_1, p_2$ are the population proportions,\n- $n_1, n_2$ are the sample sizes.\n\nThen, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population difference of proportions $p_1-p_2$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}_1-\\widehat{\\,p}_2 \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}_1(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_1)}{n_1}+\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}_2(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_2)}{n_2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nWe are interested in finding a $98\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.98=0.02 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.01\n$$\n\nWe are given that that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 2.326) = 0.01\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.01} = 2.326\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $98\\%$ confidence interval for the difference of the population proportions $p_1-p_2$ is the following:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{24}{31} - \\frac{1}{2}) ± 2.326 \\cdot \\sqrt{(\\frac{48}{62}(1 - \\frac{48}{62}))/(62) + (\\frac{35}{70}(1 - \\frac{35}{70}))/(70)}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n$0.274 ± 0.186$.\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The end-points of our confidence interval are $0.274 \\pm 0.186$.\n- Statement II is false. All values within the confidence interval are positive:\n$(0.274 - 0.186, \\, 0.274 + 0.186) = (0.088, \\, 0.460)$\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are positive, this suggests that the proportion of eighth-grade students who vote in student elections is greater than that of ninth-graders. In other words, $p_1 > p_2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "Let us call $n_1, n_2$ the sample sizes of eighth and ninth-grade students, and $\\widehat{\\,p}_1, \\widehat{\\,p}_2$ the proportion of students who vote in student elections in the two samples.\n\nSince $n_1=62$, $n_2=70$,\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}_1=\\dfrac{48}{62}=\\dfrac{24}{31}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}_2=\\dfrac{35}{70} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n- $n_1 \\widehat{\\,p}_1 = 48> 5$,\n- $n_1(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_1) = 62 - 48 = 14> 5$,\n- $n_2 \\widehat{\\,p}_2 = 35> 5$,\n- $n_2(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_2) = 70- 35 = 35> 5$.\n\nAs a result, we may use a normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(\\widehat{\\,p}_1 - \\widehat{\\,p}_2) - (p_1-p_2)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}_1(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_1)}{n_1}+\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}_2(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_2)}{n_2}}\\phantom{\\mid}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}_1, \\widehat{\\,p}_2$ are the sample proportions,\n- $p_1, p_2$ are the population proportions,\n- $n_1, n_2$ are the sample sizes.\n\nThen, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population difference of proportions $p_1-p_2$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}_1-\\widehat{\\,p}_2 \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}_1(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_1)}{n_1}+\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}_2(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_2)}{n_2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nWe are interested in finding a $98\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.98=0.02 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.01\n$$\n\nWe are given that that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 2.326) = 0.01\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.01} = 2.326\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $98\\%$ confidence interval for the difference of the population proportions $p_1-p_2$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{24}{31} - \\frac{1}{2}\\right) ± 2.326 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{48}{62}\\left(1 - \\frac{48}{62}\\right)\\right)/(62) + \\left(\\frac{35}{70}\\left(1 - \\frac{35}{70}\\right)\\right)/(70)}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n$0.274 ± 0.186$.\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The end-points of our confidence interval are $0.274 \\pm 0.186$.\n- Statement II is false. All values within the confidence interval are positive:\n$(0.274 - 0.186, \\, 0.274 + 0.186) = (0.088, \\, 0.460)$\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are positive, this suggests that the proportion of eighth-grade students who vote in student elections is greater than that of ninth-graders. In other words, $p_1 > p_2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186203857 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ with respect to a matrix $X \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ is\n$$\n\\nabla_X f(X)\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{11}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{12}}} & \\cdots  & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{1 n}}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{21}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{22}}} & \\cdots  & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{2 n}}} \\\\ \\vdots  & \\vdots  & \\ddots  & \\vdots  \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{m 1}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{m 2}}} & \\cdots  & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{m n}}}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $X = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{array} \\right]} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 2}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(X) = (\\det(X))^2 = (x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})^2.\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{22} & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{21}) \\\\ 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{12}) & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\det (X)\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $X = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}- {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ - 4 & 10\\end{array} \\right]},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{X}f(X) & =2(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10-(-4)\\cdot \\frac{3}{2})\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 4 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-5-(-6))\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 4 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The gradient of a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ with respect to a matrix $X \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ is\n$$\n\\nabla_X f(X)\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{11}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{12}}} & \\cdots  & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{1 n}}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{21}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{22}}} & \\cdots  & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{2 n}}} \\\\ \\vdots  & \\vdots  & \\ddots  & \\vdots  \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{m 1}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{m 2}}} & \\cdots  & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  {x}_{m n}}}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $X = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{array} \\right]} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 2}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(X) = (\\det(X))^2 = (x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})^2.\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{22} & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{21}) \\\\ 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{12}) & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\det (X)\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $X = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}- {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ - 4 & 10\\end{array} \\right]},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{X}f(X) & =2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10-(-4)\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 4 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-5-(-6))\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 4 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186204583 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y)=y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}={y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\right)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 10 & 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\right)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 10 & 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 10 & 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 130 & 260 \\\\ 0 & -80 & -160\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y\\right)=y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}={y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\right)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 10 & 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\right)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 10 & 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 10 & 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 130 & 260 \\\\ 0 & -80 & -160\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186204589 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{1} \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 2{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}({0}^{2}+{2}^{2}) \\\\ 0+2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{1} \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 2{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}({0}^{2}+{2}^{2}) \\\\ 0+2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})\\right) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})\\right) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186204616 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = 2\\cos (2x + \\frac{π}{4})$. Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." "Consider the function $f(x) = 2\\cos \\left(2x + \\frac{π}{4}\\right)$. Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186204617 :question/problem "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})?$  Assume that $n$ is an integer." "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)?$  Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186204618 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = 5\\cos (x - \\frac{π}{6})$. Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." "Consider the function $f(x) = 5\\cos \\left(x - \\frac{π}{6}\\right)$. Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186205135 :tutorial/content "Let's now prove that the $k$th sample moment is an unbiased estimator of the $k$th theoretical moment.\n\nSuppose we have an I.I.D. random sample of size $n{:}$\n$X_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad X_3, \\quad \\ldots,\\quad X_n$\n\nThe $k$th theoretical moment, denoted $\\mu_k$, is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mu_k = \\textrm{E}[X_i^k] = \\sum_{x\\in S} x^k \\cdot p(x)\n$$\n\nand the $k$th sample moment is\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nRecall that $M_k$ is an unbiased estimate of $\\mu_k$ if\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[M_k] = \\mu_k\n$$\n\nWe can show this is true using the properties of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[M_{k}] &= E[\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}^{k}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot E[∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}^{k}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot (E[X_{1}^{k}] + E[X_{2}^{k}] + ⋯ + E[X_{n}^{k}]) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot \\underbrace{(μ_{k} + μ_{k} + ⋯ + μ_{k})}_{n\\text{ times}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot nμ_{k} \\\\\n&= μ_{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." "Let's now prove that the $k$th sample moment is an unbiased estimator of the $k$th theoretical moment.\n\nSuppose we have an I.I.D. random sample of size $n{:}$\n$X_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad X_3, \\quad \\ldots,\\quad X_n$\n\nThe $k$th theoretical moment, denoted $\\mu_k$, is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mu_k = \\textrm{E}[X_i^k] = \\sum_{x\\in S} x^k \\cdot p(x)\n$$\n\nand the $k$th sample moment is\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nRecall that $M_k$ is an unbiased estimate of $\\mu_k$ if\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[M_k] = \\mu_k\n$$\n\nWe can show this is true using the properties of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[M_{k}] &= E\\left[\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}^{k}\\right] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot E[∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}^{k}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot (E[X_{1}^{k}] + E[X_{2}^{k}] + ⋯ + E[X_{n}^{k}]) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot \\underbrace{(μ_{k} + μ_{k} + ⋯ + μ_{k})}_{n\\text{ times}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot nμ_{k} \\\\\n&= μ_{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required."] [:db/cas 17592186205148 :tutorial/content "We wish to prove that\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c}{S}_{x x} = \\sum _{i}^{n} {x}_{i}^{2} - \\frac{1}{n} {( \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i} )}^{2} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of $S_{xx}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {S}_{x x} & \\displaystyle  = \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} ( {x}_{i} - \\overline{x} {)}^{2}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFirst, we expand the binomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} ( {x}_{i}^{2} - 2 {x}_{i} \\cdot  \\overline{x} + {\\overline{x}}^{2} )\\end{array}\n$$\n\nDistributing the summation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i}^{2} - \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} 2 {x}_{i} \\cdot  \\overline{x} + \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {\\overline{x}}^{2} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, since $2, \\overline x,$ and $\\overline{x}^2$ are constants, we can write this as\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i}^{2} - 2 \\overline{x} \\cdot  \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i} + {\\overline{x}}^{2} \\cdot  \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} 1 .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the following results:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i = n\\overline x, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^n 1 = n\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}{S}_{x x} &  = \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i}^{2} - 2 \\overset{–}{x} \\cdot  n \\overset{–}{x} + {\\overset{–}{x}}^{2} \\cdot  n \\\\  &  = \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i}^{2} - 2 n {\\overset{–}{x}}^{2} + n {\\overset{–}{x}}^{2} \\\\  &  = \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i}^{2} - n {\\overset{–}{x}}^{2} \\\\  &  = \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i}^{2} - n \\cdot  {( \\frac{1}{n} \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i} )}^{2} \\\\  &  = \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i}^{2} - n \\cdot  \\frac{1}{{n}^{2}} \\cdot  {( \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i} )}^{2} \\\\  &  = \\sum _{i}^{n} {x}_{i}^{2} - \\frac{1}{n} {( \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i} )}^{2}\\end{array}\n$$\nas required.\n\nThe proofs for $S_{yy}$ and $S_{xy}$ are similar." "We wish to prove that\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c}{S}_{x x} = \\sum _{i}^{n} {x}_{i}^{2} - \\frac{1}{n} {( \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i} )}^{2} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of $S_{xx}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {S}_{x x} & \\displaystyle  = \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} ( {x}_{i} - \\overline{x} {)}^{2}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFirst, we expand the binomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} ( {x}_{i}^{2} - 2 {x}_{i} \\cdot  \\overline{x} + {\\overline{x}}^{2} )\\end{array}\n$$\n\nDistributing the summation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i}^{2} - \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} 2 {x}_{i} \\cdot  \\overline{x} + \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {\\overline{x}}^{2} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, since $2, \\overline x,$ and $\\overline{x}^2$ are constants, we can write this as\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i}^{2} - 2 \\overline{x} \\cdot  \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i} + {\\overline{x}}^{2} \\cdot  \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} 1 .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the following results:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i = n\\overline x, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^n 1 = n\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}{S}_{x x} &  = \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i}^{2} - 2 \\overset{–}{x} \\cdot  n \\overset{–}{x} + {\\overset{–}{x}}^{2} \\cdot  n \\\\  &  = \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i}^{2} - 2 n {\\overset{–}{x}}^{2} + n {\\overset{–}{x}}^{2} \\\\  &  = \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i}^{2} - n {\\overset{–}{x}}^{2} \\\\  &  = \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i}^{2} - n \\cdot  {\\left( \\frac{1}{n} \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i} \\right)}^{2} \\\\  &  = \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i}^{2} - n \\cdot  \\frac{1}{{n}^{2}} \\cdot  {( \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i} )}^{2} \\\\  &  = \\sum _{i}^{n} {x}_{i}^{2} - \\frac{1}{n} {( \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i} )}^{2}\\end{array}\n$$\nas required.\n\nThe proofs for $S_{yy}$ and $S_{xy}$ are similar."] [:db/cas 17592186205152 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a sample of size $n$, where the observations are divided into $K$ groups. We want to determine whether the population mean varies by group.\n\nIn other words, we want to test the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0{:}\\: \\mu_1 = \\mu_2 = \\ldots = \\mu_K$\n- $H_1{:}$ There is at least one population mean that differs from the others,\n\nwhere $\\mu_1, \\mu_2, \\ldots, \\mu_K$ are the population means of each group.\n\nWe've already introduced the $F$-ratio test statistic for this problem:\n\n$$\nW = \\dfrac{\\text{MSB}}{\\text{MSW}}\n$$\n\nNow, we will discuss the distribution of $W$.\n\nLet's assume that the data points are independent and follow a normal distribution with equal mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2{:}$\n\n$$\nX_{jk} \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2), \\qquad j=1, 2, \\ldots, n_k, \\quad k=1, 2, \\ldots, K\n$$\n\nwhere $n_k$ is the size of the $k$th group.\n\nAlso, recall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SST} = \\sum_{k=1}^K\\sum_{j=1}^{n_k} (X_{jk} - \\overline{X})^2, \\qquad \\text{SSW} = \\sum_{k=1}^K \\sum_{j=1}^{n_k} (X_{jk} - \\overline{X}_k)^2, \\qquad \\text{SSB} = \\sum_{k=1}^K n_k (\\overline{X}_k - \\overline{X})^2\n$$\n\nGiven a sample of $n$ independent normal random variables with variance $\\sigma^2$, for the sample variance $S^2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(n-1)S^2}{\\sigma^2} \\sim\\chi^2(n-1)\n$$\n\nWe can use this result to find the distribution of $\\text{SST}/\\sigma^2$. Since\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SST} = (n-1)S^2\n$$\n\nit follows that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{SST}}{\\sigma^2} = \\dfrac{(n-1)S^2}{\\sigma^2}\\sim \\chi^2(n-1)\n$$\n\nWe can also apply this result to *each* group mean:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sigma^2}\\sum_{j=1}^{n_k}(X_{jk} - \\overline{X}_k)^2 \\sim \\chi^2(n_k-1), \\quad k = 1, \\ldots, K\n$$\n\nMoreover, the above sums are independent. Since each group variance contributes $(n_k - 1)$ degrees of freedom, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\text{SSW}}{\\sigma^2} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma^2}\\sum_{k=1}^K\\sum_{j=1}^{n_k}(X_{jk} - \\overline{X}_k)^2 \\sim \\chi^2(n - K)\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that $\\text{SSB}$ and $\\text{SSW}$ are independent. Therefore, since\n\n$$\n\\text{SST} = \\text{SSB} + \\text{SSW}\n$$\n\nwe have that\n$\\text{SSB}/\\sigma^2$ follows a chi-squared distribution with\n\n$$\nn-1 - (n-K) = K-1\n$$\n\ndegrees of freedom.\n\nNotice that our test statistic $W$ is the ratio of two independent chi-squared random variables divided by their respective degrees of freedom:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{SSB}}{ {\\sigma }^{2}}} )}{( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{SSW}}{ {\\sigma }^{2}}} )}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{n - K}{K - 1}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{SSB}}{\\cancel{ {\\sigma }^{2}}}} )}{( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{SSW}}{\\cancel{ {\\sigma }^{2}}}} )}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{n - K}{K - 1}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{SSB}}{K - 1}} )}{( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{SSW}}{n - K}} )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{MSB}}{\\text{MSW}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = W\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $W$ follows a Fisher's $F$-distribution with $\\nu_1=K-1$ and $\\nu_2=n-K$ degrees of freedom.\n\n$$\nW\\sim F(K-1, n-K)\n$$" "Suppose we have a sample of size $n$, where the observations are divided into $K$ groups. We want to determine whether the population mean varies by group.\n\nIn other words, we want to test the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0{:}\\: \\mu_1 = \\mu_2 = \\ldots = \\mu_K$\n- $H_1{:}$ There is at least one population mean that differs from the others,\n\nwhere $\\mu_1, \\mu_2, \\ldots, \\mu_K$ are the population means of each group.\n\nWe've already introduced the $F$-ratio test statistic for this problem:\n\n$$\nW = \\dfrac{\\text{MSB}}{\\text{MSW}}\n$$\n\nNow, we will discuss the distribution of $W$.\n\nLet's assume that the data points are independent and follow a normal distribution with equal mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2{:}$\n\n$$\nX_{jk} \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2), \\qquad j=1, 2, \\ldots, n_k, \\quad k=1, 2, \\ldots, K\n$$\n\nwhere $n_k$ is the size of the $k$th group.\n\nAlso, recall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SST} = \\sum_{k=1}^K\\sum_{j=1}^{n_k} (X_{jk} - \\overline{X})^2, \\qquad \\text{SSW} = \\sum_{k=1}^K \\sum_{j=1}^{n_k} (X_{jk} - \\overline{X}_k)^2, \\qquad \\text{SSB} = \\sum_{k=1}^K n_k (\\overline{X}_k - \\overline{X})^2\n$$\n\nGiven a sample of $n$ independent normal random variables with variance $\\sigma^2$, for the sample variance $S^2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(n-1)S^2}{\\sigma^2} \\sim\\chi^2(n-1)\n$$\n\nWe can use this result to find the distribution of $\\text{SST}/\\sigma^2$. Since\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SST} = (n-1)S^2\n$$\n\nit follows that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{SST}}{\\sigma^2} = \\dfrac{(n-1)S^2}{\\sigma^2}\\sim \\chi^2(n-1)\n$$\n\nWe can also apply this result to *each* group mean:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sigma^2}\\sum_{j=1}^{n_k}(X_{jk} - \\overline{X}_k)^2 \\sim \\chi^2(n_k-1), \\quad k = 1, \\ldots, K\n$$\n\nMoreover, the above sums are independent. Since each group variance contributes $(n_k - 1)$ degrees of freedom, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\text{SSW}}{\\sigma^2} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma^2}\\sum_{k=1}^K\\sum_{j=1}^{n_k}(X_{jk} - \\overline{X}_k)^2 \\sim \\chi^2(n - K)\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that $\\text{SSB}$ and $\\text{SSW}$ are independent. Therefore, since\n\n$$\n\\text{SST} = \\text{SSB} + \\text{SSW}\n$$\n\nwe have that\n$\\text{SSB}/\\sigma^2$ follows a chi-squared distribution with\n\n$$\nn-1 - (n-K) = K-1\n$$\n\ndegrees of freedom.\n\nNotice that our test statistic $W$ is the ratio of two independent chi-squared random variables divided by their respective degrees of freedom:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{SSB}}{ {\\sigma }^{2}}} \\right)}{\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{SSW}}{ {\\sigma }^{2}}} \\right)}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{n - K}{K - 1}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{SSB}}{\\cancel{ {\\sigma }^{2}}}} \\right)}{\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{SSW}}{\\cancel{ {\\sigma }^{2}}}} \\right)}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{n - K}{K - 1}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{SSB}}{K - 1}} \\right)}{\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{SSW}}{n - K}} \\right)}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{MSB}}{\\text{MSW}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = W\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $W$ follows a Fisher's $F$-distribution with $\\nu_1=K-1$ and $\\nu_2=n-K$ degrees of freedom.\n\n$$\nW\\sim F(K-1, n-K)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186205195 :tutorial/content "When working with scalar-valued functions defined on matrices, it's often helpful to compute gradients using component-wise differentiation and the chain rule. This approach breaks down the computation into simpler intermediate steps and allows us to clearly see the dependencies.\n\nConsider a *scalar* function $L(\\mathbf A),$ defined as\n\n$$\nL(\\mathbf{A}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\right)^T \\left( \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{A} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 3}$, $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$, and $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nSuppose we want to compute the gradient $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$, which is a matrix of the same shape as $\\mathbf{A}$. The entry in the $i$th row and $j$th column of the gradient matrix is given by \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}.\n$$\n\nTo simplify the differentiation, we define $\\mathbf y \\in\\mathbb R^{2\\times 1}$ as \n$$\n\\mathbf{y} \n= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write $L$ in terms of $\\mathbf y$ as \n\n$$\nL = \\frac{1}{2} \\mathbf{y}^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}$, we apply the multivariable chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}} = \\sum_{k=1}^2 \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNote that the summation is from $k=1$ to $k=2$ because $\\mathbf y$ has two components, and we're differentiating with respect to each of them.\n\nNow, let's analyze the two factors of the product that appear in this summation.\n\n- We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{y}L & ={∇}_{y}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} = y_k.\n$$\n\n- Writing $\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}$ in component form\n\n$$\ny_i = \\sum_{j=1}^3 a_{ij} x_j - b_i,\n$$\n\nwe have\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}} =\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{x}_{j} , & i = k , \\\\ 0 , & \\text{otherwise} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\nOnly the $k$th row of $\\mathbf{A}$ affects $y_k$.\n\nPutting our two results together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}{x}_{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause all terms vanish except the one where $k=i$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T.\n$$\n\nIn the next example, we will use this formula to compute the gradient for specific vectors." "When working with scalar-valued functions defined on matrices, it's often helpful to compute gradients using component-wise differentiation and the chain rule. This approach breaks down the computation into simpler intermediate steps and allows us to clearly see the dependencies.\n\nConsider a *scalar* function $L(\\mathbf A),$ defined as\n\n$$\nL(\\mathbf{A}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\right)^T \\left( \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{A} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 3}$, $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$, and $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nSuppose we want to compute the gradient $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$, which is a matrix of the same shape as $\\mathbf{A}$. The entry in the $i$th row and $j$th column of the gradient matrix is given by \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}.\n$$\n\nTo simplify the differentiation, we define $\\mathbf y \\in\\mathbb R^{2\\times 1}$ as \n$$\n\\mathbf{y} \n= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write $L$ in terms of $\\mathbf y$ as \n\n$$\nL = \\frac{1}{2} \\mathbf{y}^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}$, we apply the multivariable chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}} = \\sum_{k=1}^2 \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNote that the summation is from $k=1$ to $k=2$ because $\\mathbf y$ has two components, and we're differentiating with respect to each of them.\n\nNow, let's analyze the two factors of the product that appear in this summation.\n\n- We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{y}L & ={∇}_{y}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} = y_k.\n$$\n\n- Writing $\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}$ in component form\n\n$$\ny_i = \\sum_{j=1}^3 a_{ij} x_j - b_i,\n$$\n\nwe have\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}} =\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{x}_{j} , & i = k , \\\\ 0 , & \\text{otherwise} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\nOnly the $k$th row of $\\mathbf{A}$ affects $y_k$.\n\nPutting our two results together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}{x}_{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause all terms vanish except the one where $k=i$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T.\n$$\n\nIn the next example, we will use this formula to compute the gradient for specific vectors."] [:db/cas 17592186205950 :answer/value "(-\\frac{12}{17}, \\frac{36}{17})" "\\left(-\\frac{12}{17}, \\frac{36}{17}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186205951 :answer/value "(\\frac{36}{17},-\\frac{12}{17})" "\\left(\\frac{36}{17},-\\frac{12}{17}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186205952 :answer/value "(\\frac{12}{17},-\\frac{36}{17})" "\\left(\\frac{12}{17},-\\frac{36}{17}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186205953 :answer/value "(-\\frac{36}{17}, \\frac{12}{17})" "\\left(-\\frac{36}{17}, \\frac{12}{17}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186205954 :answer/value "(\\frac{9}{17}, \\frac{12}{17})" "\\left(\\frac{9}{17}, \\frac{12}{17}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186206113 :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{3}, 0)" "\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}, 0\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186206115 :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{1}{3})" "\\left(0,-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186206213 :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{7}, 0)" "\\left(\\frac{3}{7}, 0\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186206217 :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{3}{7})" "\\left(0,-\\frac{3}{7}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186206513 :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{3}, 0)" "\\left(\\frac{2}{3}, 0\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186206514 :answer/value "(-\\frac{2}{3}, 0)" "\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}, 0\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186208307 :question/worked-solution "To write the table, we first express $y$ as a function of $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{4}y &= -5 - \\frac{1}{2}x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{4}{3}(-5 - \\frac{1}{2}x) \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{20}{3} - \\frac{2}{3}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we’ll analyze each of the given values of $x$ in turn.\n\n- Consider $x = 0$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{20}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Consider $x = 3$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{20}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}(3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{3}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{26}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Consider $x = 6$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{20}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}(6) \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{3}-4 \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the table is the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle -\\dfrac{20}{3}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle -\\dfrac{26}{3}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle -\\dfrac{32}{3}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" "To write the table, we first express $y$ as a function of $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{4}y &= -5 - \\frac{1}{2}x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{4}{3}\\left(-5 - \\frac{1}{2}x\\right) \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{20}{3} - \\frac{2}{3}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we’ll analyze each of the given values of $x$ in turn.\n\n- Consider $x = 0$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{20}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Consider $x = 3$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{20}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}(3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{3}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{26}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Consider $x = 6$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{20}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}(6) \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{3}-4 \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the table is the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle -\\dfrac{20}{3}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle -\\dfrac{26}{3}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle -\\dfrac{32}{3}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186208392 :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we consider both possible cases for the absolute value.\n\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\left|\\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6}\\right| = \\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6},\n$$ then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{3}{5}x-\\frac{11}{6}| & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}x-\\frac{11}{6} & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{14}{6}+\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{25}{6} \\\\ x & =\\frac{25}{6}\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{125}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\left|\\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6}\\right| = -\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6}\\right),\n$$ then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{3}{5}x-\\frac{11}{6}| & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -(\\frac{3}{5}x-\\frac{11}{6}) & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}x+\\frac{11}{6} & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{7}{3}-\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{14}{6}-\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{3}{6} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{6}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{5}{3}\\right) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{15}{18} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{125}{18} + \\left(-\\dfrac{5}{6}\\right) = \\dfrac{125}{18} - \\dfrac{15}{18} = \\dfrac{110}{18} = \\dfrac{55}{9}\n$$" "To solve this equation, we consider both possible cases for the absolute value.\n\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\left|\\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6}\\right| = \\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6},\n$$ then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{3}{5}x-\\frac{11}{6}| & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}x-\\frac{11}{6} & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{14}{6}+\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{25}{6} \\\\ x & =\\frac{25}{6}\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{125}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\left|\\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6}\\right| = -\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6}\\right),\n$$ then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{3}{5}x-\\frac{11}{6}| & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\left(\\frac{3}{5}x-\\frac{11}{6}\\right) & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}x+\\frac{11}{6} & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{7}{3}-\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{14}{6}-\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{3}{6} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{6}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{5}{3}\\right) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{15}{18} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{125}{18} + \\left(-\\dfrac{5}{6}\\right) = \\dfrac{125}{18} - \\dfrac{15}{18} = \\dfrac{110}{18} = \\dfrac{55}{9}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186208393 :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the absolute value by adding $11$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid s - \\frac{7}{6} \\mid - 11 &= -5 \\\\\n\\mid s - \\frac{7}{6} \\mid &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we consider both possible cases for the absolute value.\n\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\left| s - \\dfrac{7}{6} \\right| = s - \\dfrac{7}{6},\n$$ then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|s-\\frac{7}{6}| & =6 \\\\ s-\\frac{7}{6} & =6 \\\\ s & =\\frac{7}{6}+6 \\\\ s & =\\frac{7}{6}+\\frac{36}{6} \\\\ s & =\\frac{43}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\left| s - \\dfrac{7}{6} \\right| = -\\!\\left(s - \\dfrac{7}{6} \\right),\n$$ then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|s-\\frac{7}{6}| & =6 \\\\ -\\hspace{-0.167em}(s-\\frac{7}{6}) & =6 \\\\ -s+\\frac{7}{6} & =6 \\\\ -s & =6-\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ -s & =\\frac{36}{6}-\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ -s & =\\frac{29}{6} \\\\ s & =-\\frac{29}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{43}{6} + \\left(-\\dfrac{29}{6}\\right) = \\dfrac{14}{6} = \\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$" "First, we isolate the absolute value by adding $11$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid s - \\frac{7}{6} \\mid - 11 &= -5 \\\\\n\\mid s - \\frac{7}{6} \\mid &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we consider both possible cases for the absolute value.\n\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\left| s - \\dfrac{7}{6} \\right| = s - \\dfrac{7}{6},\n$$ then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|s-\\frac{7}{6}| & =6 \\\\ s-\\frac{7}{6} & =6 \\\\ s & =\\frac{7}{6}+6 \\\\ s & =\\frac{7}{6}+\\frac{36}{6} \\\\ s & =\\frac{43}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\left| s - \\dfrac{7}{6} \\right| = -\\!\\left(s - \\dfrac{7}{6} \\right),\n$$ then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|s-\\frac{7}{6}| & =6 \\\\ -\\hspace{-0.167em}\\left(s-\\frac{7}{6}\\right) & =6 \\\\ -s+\\frac{7}{6} & =6 \\\\ -s & =6-\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ -s & =\\frac{36}{6}-\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ -s & =\\frac{29}{6} \\\\ s & =-\\frac{29}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{43}{6} + \\left(-\\dfrac{29}{6}\\right) = \\dfrac{14}{6} = \\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186208519 :question/worked-solution "A system of equations has infinitely many solutions when both equations represent the same line. This occurs when both equations have the same slope and the same $y$-intercept.\n\nIn other words, the two equations represent the same line if one equation is a constant multiple of the other.\n\nWe start by eliminating fractions from the first equation to make comparison easier.\n\nWe multiply both sides of the first equation by $24$ (the least common multiple of $6$ and $8$):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n24(\\frac{5}{6}x - \\frac{1}{8}y) &= 24(3) \\\\\n20x - 3y &= 72\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the system becomes\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}20 x - 3 y = 72 \\\\ r x - 9 y = s\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nNow, if one equation is a constant multiple of the other, all coefficients and constants are proportional. That means\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{20} = \\frac{(-9)}{(-3)} = \\frac{s}{72}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $r$, we focus on the proportion\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{20}= \\frac{(-9)}{(-3)} = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, multiplying both sides by $20$, we find that the value of $r$ is\n\n$$\nr = 3 \\cdot 20 = 60\n$$" "A system of equations has infinitely many solutions when both equations represent the same line. This occurs when both equations have the same slope and the same $y$-intercept.\n\nIn other words, the two equations represent the same line if one equation is a constant multiple of the other.\n\nWe start by eliminating fractions from the first equation to make comparison easier.\n\nWe multiply both sides of the first equation by $24$ (the least common multiple of $6$ and $8$):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n24\\left(\\frac{5}{6}x - \\frac{1}{8}y\\right) &= 24(3) \\\\\n20x - 3y &= 72\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the system becomes\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}20 x - 3 y = 72 \\\\ r x - 9 y = s\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nNow, if one equation is a constant multiple of the other, all coefficients and constants are proportional. That means\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{20} = \\frac{(-9)}{(-3)} = \\frac{s}{72}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $r$, we focus on the proportion\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{20}= \\frac{(-9)}{(-3)} = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, multiplying both sides by $20$, we find that the value of $r$ is\n\n$$\nr = 3 \\cdot 20 = 60\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186208520 :question/worked-solution "A system of equations has infinitely many solutions when both equations represent the same line. This occurs when both equations have the same slope and the same $y$-intercept.\n\nIn other words, the two equations represent the same line if one equation is a constant multiple of the other.\n\nWe start by eliminating fractions from both equations to make the comparison easier.\n\nWe multiply both sides of the first equation by $10$ (the least common multiple of $5$, $2$, and $10$):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10(-\\frac{3}{5}x + \\frac{1}{2}y) &= 10\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) \\\\\n- 6x + 5y &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also multiply both sides of the second equation by $10{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10(ax + by) &= 10\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) \\\\\n10ax + 10by &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system becomes\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 6 x + 5 y = 7 \\\\ 10 a x + 10 b y = 7\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nNow, if one equation is a constant multiple of the other, all coefficients and constants are proportional. That means\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10a}{-6} = \\dfrac{10b}{5} = \\dfrac{7}{7}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b}\n$$\n\nwe focus on the proportion\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10a}{-6} = \\dfrac{10b}{5}\n$$\n\nIt gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 10 a \\cdot  5 & \\displaystyle  = 10 b \\cdot  ( - 6 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 50 a & \\displaystyle  = - 60 b \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{b}} & \\displaystyle  = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{60}{50}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{b}} & \\displaystyle  = \\bbox[4pt,border:1px solid lightgray]{- {\\displaystyle \\frac{6}{5}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$" "A system of equations has infinitely many solutions when both equations represent the same line. This occurs when both equations have the same slope and the same $y$-intercept.\n\nIn other words, the two equations represent the same line if one equation is a constant multiple of the other.\n\nWe start by eliminating fractions from both equations to make the comparison easier.\n\nWe multiply both sides of the first equation by $10$ (the least common multiple of $5$, $2$, and $10$):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}x + \\frac{1}{2}y\\right) &= 10\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) \\\\\n- 6x + 5y &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also multiply both sides of the second equation by $10{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10(ax + by) &= 10\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) \\\\\n10ax + 10by &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system becomes\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 6 x + 5 y = 7 \\\\ 10 a x + 10 b y = 7\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nNow, if one equation is a constant multiple of the other, all coefficients and constants are proportional. That means\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10a}{-6} = \\dfrac{10b}{5} = \\dfrac{7}{7}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b}\n$$\n\nwe focus on the proportion\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10a}{-6} = \\dfrac{10b}{5}\n$$\n\nIt gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 10 a \\cdot  5 & \\displaystyle  = 10 b \\cdot  ( - 6 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 50 a & \\displaystyle  = - 60 b \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{b}} & \\displaystyle  = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{60}{50}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{b}} & \\displaystyle  = \\bbox[4pt,border:1px solid lightgray]{- {\\displaystyle \\frac{6}{5}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186208930 :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y)={y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot y \\\\  & ={A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -29\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y\\right)={y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot y \\\\  & ={A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -29\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186208945 :question/worked-solution "This is a two-step random process: first, an integer $n$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $301,$ and then a number is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ We are asked to find the expected value of the number selected in the second step.\n\n- Let $Y$ be the first random number, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $301.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the player’s guess, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since the distribution of $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=n,$ the number $X$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=n]=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\frac{1+Y}{2}].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[1+Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+\\text{E}[Y])\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,301\\},$ we use the formula for the mean of the first $n$ integers:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{1+301}{2}=151.\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we finally get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+\\text{E}[Y]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+151) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 152 \\\\  & =76.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This is a two-step random process: first, an integer $n$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $301,$ and then a number is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ We are asked to find the expected value of the number selected in the second step.\n\n- Let $Y$ be the first random number, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $301.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the player’s guess, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since the distribution of $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=n,$ the number $X$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=n]=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}\\left[\\frac{1+Y}{2}\\right].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[1+Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+\\text{E}[Y])\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,301\\},$ we use the formula for the mean of the first $n$ integers:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{1+301}{2}=151.\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we finally get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+\\text{E}[Y]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+151) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 152 \\\\  & =76.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186208946 :question/worked-solution "This is a two-step random process: first, a level difficulty is selected uniformly from the integers $1$ through $500,$ and then a treasure chest is placed uniformly in one of the zones numbered $1$ through $d,$ where $d$ is the selected difficulty.\n\n- Let $Y$ be the difficulty level of the chosen level. Since the difficulty is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $500,$ we have $Y∼U\\{1,2,…,500\\}.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the number of the zone where the chest is placed, which is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=d,$ the zone is selected uniformly from $1$ to $d.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=d]=\\frac{d+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\frac{Y+1}{2}].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,500\\},$ we compute\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{1+500}{2}=250.5.\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (250.5+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 251.5 \\\\  & =125.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This is a two-step random process: first, a level difficulty is selected uniformly from the integers $1$ through $500,$ and then a treasure chest is placed uniformly in one of the zones numbered $1$ through $d,$ where $d$ is the selected difficulty.\n\n- Let $Y$ be the difficulty level of the chosen level. Since the difficulty is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $500,$ we have $Y∼U\\{1,2,…,500\\}.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the number of the zone where the chest is placed, which is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=d,$ the zone is selected uniformly from $1$ to $d.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=d]=\\frac{d+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}\\left[\\frac{Y+1}{2}\\right].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,500\\},$ we compute\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{1+500}{2}=250.5.\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (250.5+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 251.5 \\\\  & =125.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186209052 :question/problem "The function $g$ is defined by $$\n\\displaystyle g(x) = 2(\\frac{1}{3} - x)^{2} + \\frac{5}{3}.\n$$ What is the value of $g\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)$?$g\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) =$\n{{field-1}}" "The function $g$ is defined by $$\n\\displaystyle g(x) = 2\\left(\\frac{1}{3} - x\\right)^{2} + \\frac{5}{3}.\n$$ What is the value of $g\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)$?$g\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) =$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186209877 :tutorial/content "Now, let $\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^q$ and $f: \\mathbb{R}^q \\to \\mathbb{R}$. Suppose we want to compute the gradient \n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x})).\n$$\n\nTo do this, we need to apply the chain rule. The chain rule for total derivatives tells us that\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{1 \\times n}\n= \n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}}_{1 \\times q}\n\\cdot \n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{q \\times n}.\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the gradient of a scalar function is the transpose of the total derivative:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f}_{n \\times 1} = \n\\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n$$\n\nTo derive the chain rule in terms of gradients, we apply the identity $(AB)^T = B^T A^T$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}}_{n\\times q}\\cdot \\underbrace{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}}_{q\\times 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient chain rule is\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f = \n\\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n\\cdot \n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{y}} f.\n$$\n\nNote that the order matters; when expressed in the gradient form, the derivatives are applied *from right to left!*" "Now, let $\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^q$ and $f: \\mathbb{R}^q \\to \\mathbb{R}$. Suppose we want to compute the gradient \n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x})).\n$$\n\nTo do this, we need to apply the chain rule. The chain rule for total derivatives tells us that\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{1 \\times n}\n= \n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}}_{1 \\times q}\n\\cdot \n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{q \\times n}.\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the gradient of a scalar function is the transpose of the total derivative:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f}_{n \\times 1} = \n\\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n$$\n\nTo derive the chain rule in terms of gradients, we apply the identity $(AB)^T = B^T A^T$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}}_{n\\times q}\\cdot \\underbrace{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}}_{q\\times 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient chain rule is\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f = \n\\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n\\cdot \n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{y}} f.\n$$\n\nNote that the order matters; when expressed in the gradient form, the derivatives are applied *from right to left!*"] [:db/cas 17592186211828 :answer/value "(y - \\frac{1}{4})(y + \\frac{1}{4})" "\\left(y - \\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\left(y + \\frac{1}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186211829 :answer/value "(y - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})(y + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})" "\\left(y - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\left(y + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186211830 :answer/value "(y - \\frac{1}{2})(y + \\frac{1}{2})" "\\left(y - \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(y + \\frac{1}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186211831 :answer/value "(y - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})^{2}" "\\left(y - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)^{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186211832 :answer/value "(y + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})^{2}" "\\left(y + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)^{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186212753 :question/worked-solution "The given solutions $x = -1$,\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nand $x = 6$ are the solutions to the following equations:\n\n- $x + 1 = 0$\n- $x - \\dfrac{4}{3} = 0$\n- $x - 6 = 0$\n\nSo, according to the zero product rule, they are also the solutions of the product\n\n$$\n(x + 1)\\left(x - \\dfrac{4}{3}\\right)(x - 6) = 0\n$$\n\nTo eliminate fractions, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(x + 1)(x - \\frac{4}{3})(x - 6) &= 3 \\cdot 0 \\\\\n(3x - 4)(x + 1)(x - 6) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3x - 4)(x + 1)(x - 6) &= (3x - 4)((x + 1)(x - 6)) \\\\\n&= (3x - 4)(x^{2} - 6x + x - 6) \\\\\n&= (3x - 4)(x^{2} - 5x - 6) \\\\\n&= 3x(x^{2} - 5x - 6) - 4(x^{2} - 5x - 6) \\\\\n&= 3x^{3} - 15x^{2} - 18x - 4x^{2} + 20x + 24 \\\\\n&= 3x^{3} - 19x^{2} + 2x + 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a cubic equation that has the required solutions is\n\n$$\n3x^3 - 19x^2 + 2x + 24 = 0\n$$" "The given solutions $x = -1$,\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nand $x = 6$ are the solutions to the following equations:\n\n- $x + 1 = 0$\n- $x - \\dfrac{4}{3} = 0$\n- $x - 6 = 0$\n\nSo, according to the zero product rule, they are also the solutions of the product\n\n$$\n(x + 1)\\left(x - \\dfrac{4}{3}\\right)(x - 6) = 0\n$$\n\nTo eliminate fractions, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(x + 1)\\left(x - \\frac{4}{3}\\right)(x - 6) &= 3 \\cdot 0 \\\\\n(3x - 4)(x + 1)(x - 6) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3x - 4)(x + 1)(x - 6) &= (3x - 4)((x + 1)(x - 6)) \\\\\n&= (3x - 4)(x^{2} - 6x + x - 6) \\\\\n&= (3x - 4)(x^{2} - 5x - 6) \\\\\n&= 3x(x^{2} - 5x - 6) - 4(x^{2} - 5x - 6) \\\\\n&= 3x^{3} - 15x^{2} - 18x - 4x^{2} + 20x + 24 \\\\\n&= 3x^{3} - 19x^{2} + 2x + 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a cubic equation that has the required solutions is\n\n$$\n3x^3 - 19x^2 + 2x + 24 = 0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186212818 :question/worked-solution "We're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nand want to convert it into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we must apply two conversion factors: one for the area units (from $\\textrm{yd}^2$ to $\\textrm{ft}^2$) and one for the time units (from $\\textrm{min}$ to $\\textrm{s}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}^2}{\\textrm{min}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{yd}^2$ to $\\textrm{ft}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}^2}}{\\textrm{min}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{min}$ to $\\textrm{s}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{min}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{min}}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, the complete conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}^2}{\\textrm{min}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{yd}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{min}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- We are given the quantity $33\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}^2}{\\textrm{min}}$.\n- There are $3$ feet per yard. We can write this as follows:\n$3\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}}{\\textrm{yd}}$\nTo determine the number of square feet in one square yard, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{ft}}{\\text{yd}})}^{2} & ={3}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{\\text{ft}}^{2}}{{\\text{yd}}^{2}} \\\\  & =9\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{\\text{ft}}^{2}}{{\\text{yd}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- There are $60$ seconds per minute, or $\\dfrac{1}{60}$ minutes per second. We can write this as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{60}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{min}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 33 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{yd}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} & \\displaystyle  = 33 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{yd}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} \\cdot  9 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{ {\\text{yd}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{60}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 33 \\cdot  9 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{60}} ) {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{yd}}