+ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})\n$$" "Notice that\n\n$$\n10 = \\big(\\sqrt{10}\\big)^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the expression as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y^{2} - \\frac{1}{10} &= y^{2} - (1)/((\\sqrt{10})^{2}) \\\\ &= y^{2} - \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\ny^{2} - \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right)^{2} = \\left(y - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right)\\left(y + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186212849 :question/worked-solution "The terms of our expression have a common factor of $4$. So, we simplify the expression by factoring it out:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4m^2}{5}-36 = 4\\left(\\dfrac{m^2}{5}-9\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{m^{2}}{5} - 9 &= \\left(\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)^{2} - 3^{2} \\\\ &= (\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}} - 3)(\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}} + 3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4m^2}{5}-36 = 4\\left(\\dfrac{m}{\\sqrt5} - 3 \\right)\\left(\\dfrac{m}{\\sqrt5} + 3 \\right)\n$$" "The terms of our expression have a common factor of $4$. So, we simplify the expression by factoring it out:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4m^2}{5}-36 = 4\\left(\\dfrac{m^2}{5}-9\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{m^{2}}{5} - 9 &= \\left(\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)^{2} - 3^{2} \\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}} - 3\\right)\\left(\\frac{m}{\\sqrt{5}} + 3\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4m^2}{5}-36 = 4\\left(\\dfrac{m}{\\sqrt5} - 3 \\right)\\left(\\dfrac{m}{\\sqrt5} + 3 \\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186212854 :question/worked-solution "We start by eliminating fractions from both equations to make the comparison easier. To do that, we\n\n- multiply both sides of the first equation by $12$ (the least common multiple of $6$, $4$, and $12$), and\n- multiply both sides of the second equation by $12$ (the least common multiple of $2$, $6$, and $12$).\n\nThis gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}12 \\cdot  ( - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{6}} x + {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} y ) = 12 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{12}} \\\\ 12 \\cdot  ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} x + {\\displaystyle \\frac{t}{12}} y ) = 12 \\cdot  ( - {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} )\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 2 x + 3 y = 5 \\\\ 4 x + t y = - 10\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nA system of equations has infinitely many solutions when both equations represent the same line.\nThe two equations represent the same line if one equation is a non-zero constant multiple of the other.\n\nNow, if one equation is a constant multiple of the other, all the corresponding coefficients and constants must be proportional:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{-2}{4} = \\dfrac{3}{t} = \\dfrac{5}{-10}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{-2}{4} = \\dfrac{5}{-10} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the value of $t$, we focus on the proportion\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{3}{t}\n$$\n\nSolving for $t$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{1}{2} &= \\frac{3}{t} \\\\\n(-1) \\cdot t &= 2 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n- t &= 6 \\\\\nt &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We start by eliminating fractions from both equations to make the comparison easier. To do that, we\n\n- multiply both sides of the first equation by $12$ (the least common multiple of $6$, $4$, and $12$), and\n- multiply both sides of the second equation by $12$ (the least common multiple of $2$, $6$, and $12$).\n\nThis gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}12 \\cdot  \\left( - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{6}} x + {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} y \\right) = 12 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{12}} \\\\ 12 \\cdot  \\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} x + {\\displaystyle \\frac{t}{12}} y \\right) = 12 \\cdot  \\left( - {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} \\right)\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 2 x + 3 y = 5 \\\\ 4 x + t y = - 10\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nA system of equations has infinitely many solutions when both equations represent the same line.\nThe two equations represent the same line if one equation is a non-zero constant multiple of the other.\n\nNow, if one equation is a constant multiple of the other, all the corresponding coefficients and constants must be proportional:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{-2}{4} = \\dfrac{3}{t} = \\dfrac{5}{-10}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{-2}{4} = \\dfrac{5}{-10} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the value of $t$, we focus on the proportion\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{3}{t}\n$$\n\nSolving for $t$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{1}{2} &= \\frac{3}{t} \\\\\n(-1) \\cdot t &= 2 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n- t &= 6 \\\\\nt &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186212858 :question/worked-solution "We start by eliminating fractions from both equations to make the comparison easier. To do that, we multiply both sides of the first equation by $6$ (the least common multiple of $2$, $3$, and $6$). This gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}6 \\cdot  ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} y ) = 6 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{2}} \\\\ r x - 6 y = s\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}5 x - 2 y = 21 \\\\ r x - 6 y = s\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nA system of equations has infinitely many solutions when both equations represent the same line.\nThe two equations represent the same line if one equation is a non-zero constant multiple of the other.\n\nNow, if one equation is a constant multiple of the other, all the corresponding coefficients and constants must be proportional:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{r} = \\dfrac{-2}{-6} = \\dfrac{21}{s}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nwe focus on the proportion\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{r} = \\dfrac{21}{s}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nwe start by cross-multiplying, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5s &= 21r\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides of the equation by $s$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5s}{s} &= \\frac{21r}{s} \\\\\n5 &= \\frac{21r}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing both sides of the equation by $21$ yields the desired quantity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 &= \\frac{21r}{s} \\\\\n\\frac{5}{21} &= \\frac{21r}{21s} \\\\\n\\frac{5}{21} &= \\frac{r}{s} \\\\\n\\frac{r}{s} &= \\frac{5}{21}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We start by eliminating fractions from both equations to make the comparison easier. To do that, we multiply both sides of the first equation by $6$ (the least common multiple of $2$, $3$, and $6$). This gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}6 \\cdot  \\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} y \\right) = 6 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{2}} \\\\ r x - 6 y = s\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}5 x - 2 y = 21 \\\\ r x - 6 y = s\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nA system of equations has infinitely many solutions when both equations represent the same line.\nThe two equations represent the same line if one equation is a non-zero constant multiple of the other.\n\nNow, if one equation is a constant multiple of the other, all the corresponding coefficients and constants must be proportional:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{r} = \\dfrac{-2}{-6} = \\dfrac{21}{s}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nwe focus on the proportion\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{r} = \\dfrac{21}{s}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nwe start by cross-multiplying, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5s &= 21r\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides of the equation by $s$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5s}{s} &= \\frac{21r}{s} \\\\\n5 &= \\frac{21r}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing both sides of the equation by $21$ yields the desired quantity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 &= \\frac{21r}{s} \\\\\n\\frac{5}{21} &= \\frac{21r}{21s} \\\\\n\\frac{5}{21} &= \\frac{r}{s} \\\\\n\\frac{r}{s} &= \\frac{5}{21}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186212859 :question/worked-solution "We need to write the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{8}\\left(2^{-s}\\right) - \\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the form\n\n$$\nk\\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nFactoring out the common factor of\n\n$$\n\\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the expression, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7}{8}(2^{-s}) - (2^{-s}) &= \\frac{7}{8} \\cdot (2^{-s}) - 1 \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{7}{8} - 1) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{7}{8} - \\frac{8}{8}) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= \\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{8}(2^{-s})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to write the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{8}\\left(2^{-s}\\right) - \\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the form\n\n$$\nk\\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nFactoring out the common factor of\n\n$$\n\\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the expression, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7}{8}(2^{-s}) - (2^{-s}) &= \\frac{7}{8} \\cdot (2^{-s}) - 1 \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{7}{8} - 1\\right) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{7}{8} - \\frac{8}{8}\\right) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= \\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{8}(2^{-s})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186212861 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}\n$$\n\nWe need to write the expression\n$15(3^{x-1}) - 4(3^x)$\nin the form $k(3^x)$.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the first term using the product rule and negative exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15(3^{x - 1}) - 4(3^{x}) &= 15 \\cdot (3^{x} \\cdot 3^{-1}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot (3^{x} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 3^{x}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 5(3^{x}) - 4(3^{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining the two negative terms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(3^{x}) - 4(3^{x}) &= (5 - 4)3^{x} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 3^{x} \\\\\n&= 3^{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=1$." "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}\n$$\n\nWe need to write the expression\n$15(3^{x-1}) - 4(3^x)$\nin the form $k(3^x)$.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the first term using the product rule and negative exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15(3^{x - 1}) - 4(3^{x}) &= 15 \\cdot (3^{x} \\cdot 3^{-1}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot \\left(3^{x} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\right) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 3^{x}\\right) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 5(3^{x}) - 4(3^{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining the two negative terms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(3^{x}) - 4(3^{x}) &= (5 - 4)3^{x} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 3^{x} \\\\\n&= 3^{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=1$."] [:db/cas 17592186212875 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}5 x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{11}} = \\sqrt{3} \\\\ y = - 2 {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{11}} - 5 x )}^{2}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x,y)$. What is the value of $y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}5 x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{11}} = \\sqrt{3} \\\\ y = - 2 {\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{11}} - 5 x \\right)}^{2}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x,y)$. What is the value of $y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186212875 :question/worked-solution "From the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n5x-\\dfrac{7}{11}=\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply this equation by $-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{11} - 5x = -\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nand the expression on the left-hand side features in the second equation.\n\nSo next, we substitute $-\\sqrt{3}$ for\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{7}{11}-5x\\right)\n$$\n\nin the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -2(\\frac{7}{11} - 5x)^{2} \\\\\n&=-2(-\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\\n&=-2 \\cdot 3 \\\\\ny &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $y$ is $-6$." "From the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n5x-\\dfrac{7}{11}=\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply this equation by $-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{11} - 5x = -\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nand the expression on the left-hand side features in the second equation.\n\nSo next, we substitute $-\\sqrt{3}$ for\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{7}{11}-5x\\right)\n$$\n\nin the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -2\\left(\\frac{7}{11} - 5x\\right)^{2} \\\\\n&=-2(-\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\\n&=-2 \\cdot 3 \\\\\ny &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $y$ is $-6$."] [:db/cas 17592186212885 :question/worked-solution "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{8}y - \\dfrac{2}{8}y = 6\n$$\n\nand are asked to solve for $y$.\n\nFirst, we simplify the left-hand side by combining like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{8}y - \\frac{2}{8}y &= (\\frac{5}{8} - \\frac{2}{8})y \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{8}y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{8}y &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y$ by multiplying both sides of the equation by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{3}\n$$\n\n:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 6 \\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $y$ that satisfies the equation is $16$." "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{8}y - \\dfrac{2}{8}y = 6\n$$\n\nand are asked to solve for $y$.\n\nFirst, we simplify the left-hand side by combining like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{8}y - \\frac{2}{8}y &= \\left(\\frac{5}{8} - \\frac{2}{8}\\right)y \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{8}y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{8}y &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y$ by multiplying both sides of the equation by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{3}\n$$\n\n:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 6 \\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $y$ that satisfies the equation is $16$."] [:db/cas 17592186212900 :question/worked-solution "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n6y + \\dfrac{2}{3} = 4\n$$\n\nand are asked to find the value of $12y + 1$.\n\nTo find $12y + 1$ from the given expression, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2$ and solve for $12y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(6y + \\frac{2}{3}) &= 2 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n12y + \\frac{4}{3} &= 8 \\\\\n12y &= 8 - \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\n12y &= \\frac{20}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain $12y + 1$, we add $1$ to both sides and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12y &= \\frac{20}{3} \\\\\n12y + 1 &= \\frac{20}{3} + 1 \\\\\n12y + 1 &= \\frac{23}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $12y + 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{23}{3}\n$$" "We are given the equation\n\n$$\n6y + \\dfrac{2}{3} = 4\n$$\n\nand are asked to find the value of $12y + 1$.\n\nTo find $12y + 1$ from the given expression, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2$ and solve for $12y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\left(6y + \\frac{2}{3}\\right) &= 2 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n12y + \\frac{4}{3} &= 8 \\\\\n12y &= 8 - \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\n12y &= \\frac{20}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain $12y + 1$, we add $1$ to both sides and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12y &= \\frac{20}{3} \\\\\n12y + 1 &= \\frac{20}{3} + 1 \\\\\n12y + 1 &= \\frac{23}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $12y + 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{23}{3}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186212912 :question/worked-solution "We are given\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6}(9w - 1) - \\dfrac{1}{3}(9w - 1) = 9\n$$\n\nNotice that both terms on the left-hand side involve the same binomial expression $(9w - 1)$. So we factor it out and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{5}{6} - \\frac{1}{3})(9w - 1) &= 9 \\\\\n(\\frac{5}{6} - \\frac{2}{6})(9w - 1) &= 9 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}(9w - 1) &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, multiply both sides by $2$ and solve for $w{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9w - 1 &= 18 \\\\\n9w &= 19 \\\\\nw &= \\frac{19}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nw = \\dfrac{19}{9}\n$$" "We are given\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6}(9w - 1) - \\dfrac{1}{3}(9w - 1) = 9\n$$\n\nNotice that both terms on the left-hand side involve the same binomial expression $(9w - 1)$. So we factor it out and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(\\frac{5}{6} - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)(9w - 1) &= 9 \\\\\n\\left(\\frac{5}{6} - \\frac{2}{6}\\right)(9w - 1) &= 9 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}(9w - 1) &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, multiply both sides by $2$ and solve for $w{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9w - 1 &= 18 \\\\\n9w &= 19 \\\\\nw &= \\frac{19}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nw = \\dfrac{19}{9}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186212916 :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the expression on the left-hand side and simplify by collecting like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3}x + \\frac{1}{2}(x + 6) &= \\frac{1}{3}x + \\frac{1}{2}x + 3 \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{2})x + 3 \\\\\n&= (\\frac{2}{6} + \\frac{3}{6})x + 3 \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{6}x + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{6}x + 3 &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides by $6$ to get rid of the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 \\cdot (\\frac{5}{6}x + 3) &= 6 \\cdot (7) \\\\\n5x + 18 &= 42\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x + 18 &= 42 \\\\\n5x + 18 - 18 &= 42 - 18 \\\\\n5x &= 24 \\\\\n\\frac{5x}{5} &= \\frac{24}{5} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{24}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is\n\n$$\nx=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{24}{5}}\n$$" "First, we expand the expression on the left-hand side and simplify by collecting like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3}x + \\frac{1}{2}(x + 6) &= \\frac{1}{3}x + \\frac{1}{2}x + 3 \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{2}\\right)x + 3 \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{2}{6} + \\frac{3}{6}\\right)x + 3 \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{6}x + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{6}x + 3 &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides by $6$ to get rid of the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{5}{6}x + 3\\right) &= 6 \\cdot (7) \\\\\n5x + 18 &= 42\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x + 18 &= 42 \\\\\n5x + 18 - 18 &= 42 - 18 \\\\\n5x &= 24 \\\\\n\\frac{5x}{5} &= \\frac{24}{5} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{24}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is\n\n$$\nx=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{24}{5}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186212963 :question/worked-solution "We are asked to factor the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}(5y-3) - \\dfrac{1}{5}(5y-3)\n$$\n\nNotice that both terms in this expression contain a common factor of $(5y-3)$. So, we can factor the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{5})(5y - 3)\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract the numbers inside the first brackets. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{5})(5y - 3) &= (\\frac{5}{10} - \\frac{2}{10})(5y - 3) \\\\ &= \\frac{5 - 2}{10}(5y - 3) \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{10}(5y - 3) \\mid\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We are asked to factor the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}(5y-3) - \\dfrac{1}{5}(5y-3)\n$$\n\nNotice that both terms in this expression contain a common factor of $(5y-3)$. So, we can factor the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{5}\\right)(5y - 3)\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract the numbers inside the first brackets. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{5}\\right)(5y - 3) &= \\left(\\frac{5}{10} - \\frac{2}{10}\\right)(5y - 3) \\\\ &= \\frac{5 - 2}{10}(5y - 3) \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{10}(5y - 3) \\mid\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186212972 :question/worked-solution "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nWe'll need two conversion factors: one to convert the distance unit ($\\textrm{m}$ to $\\textrm{mi}$), and one to convert the squared time unit ($\\textrm{s}^2$ to $\\textrm{min}^2$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{m}$ to $\\textrm{mi}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\textrm{s}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{s}^2$ to $\\textrm{min}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nSo, the complete conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{m}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{s}^2}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- We are given the quantity $5.1\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}$.\n- There are $1,609$ meters in a mile. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{1,609}$ miles per meter. We can write this as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{1,609}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n- There are $60\\,\\textrm{s}$ per $\\textrm{min}$. We can write this as follows:\n$60\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{s}}{\\textrm{min}}$\nTo determine the number of square seconds in one square minute, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60\\hspace{0.167em}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{s}}{\\text{min}}\\right)}^{2} & ={60}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{\\text{s}}^{2}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}} \\\\  & =3,600\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{\\text{s}}^{2}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 5.1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = 5.1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\text{m}}} \\cdot  3 , 600 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 5.1 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 609}} \\cdot  3 , 600 ) {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{5.1 \\cdot  3 , 600}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  11.4 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} ,\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth.\n\nTherefore, the train is accelerating at a rate of approximately\n\n$$\n11.4\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$" "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nWe'll need two conversion factors: one to convert the distance unit ($\\textrm{m}$ to $\\textrm{mi}$), and one to convert the squared time unit ($\\textrm{s}^2$ to $\\textrm{min}^2$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{m}$ to $\\textrm{mi}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\textrm{s}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{s}^2$ to $\\textrm{min}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nSo, the complete conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{m}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{s}^2}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- We are given the quantity $5.1\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}$.\n- There are $1,609$ meters in a mile. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{1,609}$ miles per meter. We can write this as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{1,609}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n- There are $60\\,\\textrm{s}$ per $\\textrm{min}$. We can write this as follows:\n$60\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{s}}{\\textrm{min}}$\nTo determine the number of square seconds in one square minute, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60\\hspace{0.167em}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{s}}{\\text{min}}\\right)}^{2} & ={60}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{\\text{s}}^{2}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}} \\\\  & =3,600\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{\\text{s}}^{2}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 5.1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = 5.1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\text{m}}} \\cdot  3 , 600 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( 5.1 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 609}} \\cdot  3 , 600 \\right) {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{5.1 \\cdot  3 , 600}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  11.4 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} ,\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth.\n\nTherefore, the train is accelerating at a rate of approximately\n\n$$\n11.4\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186213452 :question/problem "Solve the equation $5x(\\frac{1}{2}x - 3)(5x + 1) = 0$." "Solve the equation $5x\\left(\\frac{1}{2}x - 3\\right)(5x + 1) = 0$."] [:db/cas 17592186213455 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(\\left(\\frac{10}{x - 3}\\right))/(4x - 12)$, where $x \\ne 3$?" "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\left(\\left(\\frac{10}{x - 3}\\right)\\right)/(4x - 12)$, where $x \\ne 3$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213458 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(2x + 6)/(\\left(\\frac{x + 3}{10}\\right))$, where $x \\ne - 3$?" "Which of the following is equivalent to $(2x + 6)/\\left(\\left(\\frac{x + 3}{10}\\right)\\right)$, where $x \\ne - 3$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213459 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle (\\left(\\frac{3x + 6}{8}\\right))/(\\left(\\frac{x + 2}{5}\\right)),\n$$ where $x \\ne - 2$?" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\left(\\frac{3x + 6}{8}\\right)\\right)/\\left(\\left(\\frac{x + 2}{5}\\right)\\right),\n$$ where $x \\ne - 2$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213472 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle (\\left(\\frac{36}{x + 7}\\right))/(\\left(\\frac{60}{x - 2}\\right)),\n$$ where $x \\ne - 7$ and $x \\ne 2$?" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\left(\\frac{36}{x + 7}\\right)\\right)/\\left(\\left(\\frac{60}{x - 2}\\right)\\right),\n$$ where $x \\ne - 7$ and $x \\ne 2$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213735 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{8}} = \\sqrt{6} \\\\ y = - 2 {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{8}} - 4 x )}^{2}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x, y)$. What is the value of $y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{8}} = \\sqrt{6} \\\\ y = - 2 {\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{8}} - 4 x \\right)}^{2}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x, y)$. What is the value of $y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186214249 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we saw how to create and apply a conversion formula to convert quantities with mixed units, such as converting from \"kilometers per hour\" to \"meters per second.\"\n\nIn a separate lesson, we saw how to convert between different units of area, such as converting from \"square meters\" to \"square centimeters\" and vice versa.\n\nBy the end of this lesson, you'll be able to create and apply conversion formulas for mixed units that contain units of area.\n\nFor example, suppose a drone maps an area at a rate of\n\n$$\n0.9\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nWhat is this rate in square meters per second?\n\nWe're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand want to convert it into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThis means we'll need two conversion factors: one for the area units (from $\\textrm{km}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$) and one for the time units (from $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{s}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{km}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{s}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, the complete conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{km}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- We are given that the drone maps at a rate of $0.9\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- There are $1,000$ meters in one kilometer. We can write this as follows:\n$1,000\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{km}}$\nTo determine the number of square meters in one square kilometer, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1,000\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{km}})}^{2} & =1,{000}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{km}}^{2}} \\\\  & =1,000,000\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{km}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- There are $60\\cdot60=3,600$ seconds per hour, or $\\dfrac{1}{3,600}$ hours per second. We can write this as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{3,600}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 0.9 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} & \\displaystyle  = 0.9 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} \\cdot  1 , 000 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 0.9 \\cdot  1 , 000 , 000 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3 , 600}} ) {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{900 , 000}{3 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 250 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone maps the area at a rate of\n\n$$\n250\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$" "In a previous lesson, we saw how to create and apply a conversion formula to convert quantities with mixed units, such as converting from \"kilometers per hour\" to \"meters per second.\"\n\nIn a separate lesson, we saw how to convert between different units of area, such as converting from \"square meters\" to \"square centimeters\" and vice versa.\n\nBy the end of this lesson, you'll be able to create and apply conversion formulas for mixed units that contain units of area.\n\nFor example, suppose a drone maps an area at a rate of\n\n$$\n0.9\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nWhat is this rate in square meters per second?\n\nWe're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand want to convert it into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThis means we'll need two conversion factors: one for the area units (from $\\textrm{km}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$) and one for the time units (from $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{s}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{km}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{s}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, the complete conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{km}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- We are given that the drone maps at a rate of $0.9\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- There are $1,000$ meters in one kilometer. We can write this as follows:\n$1,000\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{km}}$\nTo determine the number of square meters in one square kilometer, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(1,000\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}\\right)}^{2} & =1,{000}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{km}}^{2}} \\\\  & =1,000,000\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{km}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- There are $60\\cdot60=3,600$ seconds per hour, or $\\dfrac{1}{3,600}$ hours per second. We can write this as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{3,600}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 0.9 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} & \\displaystyle  = 0.9 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} \\cdot  1 , 000 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( 0.9 \\cdot  1 , 000 , 000 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3 , 600}} \\right) {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{900 , 000}{3 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 250 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone maps the area at a rate of\n\n$$\n250\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186214266 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll discuss strategies for combining exponential expressions.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{5}(9^r) + 0.9(9^r)\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the terms of the expression contain a common factor of $9^r$, so let's factor this out:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{7}{5}+0.9\\right) \\cdot 9^r\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the terms in the parentheses by converting the decimal to a fraction and then adding the fractions.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{7}{5} + 0.9) \\cdot 9^{r} &= (\\frac{7}{5} + \\frac{9}{10}) \\cdot 9^{r} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{14}{10} + \\frac{9}{10}) \\cdot 9^{r} \\\\\n&= \\frac{23}{10}(9^{r})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{5}(9^r) + 0.9(9^r) = \\dfrac{23}{10}(9^r)\n$$" "In this lesson, we'll discuss strategies for combining exponential expressions.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{5}(9^r) + 0.9(9^r)\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the terms of the expression contain a common factor of $9^r$, so let's factor this out:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{7}{5}+0.9\\right) \\cdot 9^r\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the terms in the parentheses by converting the decimal to a fraction and then adding the fractions.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(\\frac{7}{5} + 0.9\\right) \\cdot 9^{r} &= \\left(\\frac{7}{5} + \\frac{9}{10}\\right) \\cdot 9^{r} \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{14}{10} + \\frac{9}{10}\\right) \\cdot 9^{r} \\\\\n&= \\frac{23}{10}(9^{r})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{5}(9^r) + 0.9(9^r) = \\dfrac{23}{10}(9^r)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186214378 :answer/value "k ∈ (- ∞, 0)∪(\\frac{5}{2}, ∞)" "k ∈ (- ∞, 0)∪\\left(\\frac{5}{2}, ∞\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186214379 :answer/value "k ∈ (\\frac{7}{2}, ∞)" "k ∈ \\left(\\frac{7}{2}, ∞\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186214380 :answer/value "k ∈ (0, \\frac{7}{2})" "k ∈ \\left(0, \\frac{7}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186214381 :answer/value "k ∈ (0, \\frac{5}{2})" "k ∈ \\left(0, \\frac{5}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186214382 :answer/value "k ∈ (- ∞,-\\frac{5}{2})∪(\\frac{5}{2}, ∞)" "k ∈ \\left(- ∞,-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)∪\\left(\\frac{5}{2}