m$ using the points $(-4, -5)$ and $(2, 4){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= (4 - (-5))/(2 - (-4)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the $y$-intercept $b$ using the point $(2, 4){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{3}{2}x + b \\\\\n4 &= \\frac{3}{2}(2) + b \\\\\n4 &= 3 + b \\\\\nb &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}x + 1\n$$\n\nfrom which it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= f(x) + 2 \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{3}{2}x + 1\\right) + 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}x + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}x + 3\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186216834 :question/worked-solution "The line $y=4x - k$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny=-5x^2 + 10x - 1\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n-5x^2 + 10x - 1 = 4x - k\n$$\n\nWe need to determine the value of $k$ such that this equation has one real solution.\n\nA quadratic equation has exactly one real root if the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x^{2} + 10x - 1 &= 4x - k \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 10x - 1 - 4x + k &= 0 \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 6x + (k - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = -5, \\qquad b = 6, \\qquad c = k-1\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= 6^{2} - 4(-5)(k - 1) \\\\\n&= 36 + 20(k - 1) \\\\\n&= 36 + 20k - 20 \\\\\n&= 16 + 20k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have exactly one real solution, we need\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16 + 20k &= 0 \\\\\n20k &= -16 \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{16}{20} \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{4}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny=4x-\\left(-\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)=4x+\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nTo find the $x$-coordinate, we solve the original quadratic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x^{2} + 6x + (-\\frac{4}{5} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 6x - \\frac{9}{5} &= 0 \\\\\n- 25x^{2} + 30x - 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- (25x^{2} - 30x + 9) &= 0 \\\\\n- (5x - 3)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n5x - 3 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the $y$-coordinate by substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\ninto one of the two equations and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 4\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) + \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{5} + \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is\n\n$$\n\\Big(\\dfrac{3}{5},\\dfrac{16}{5}\\Big)\n$$" "The line $y=4x - k$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny=-5x^2 + 10x - 1\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n-5x^2 + 10x - 1 = 4x - k\n$$\n\nWe need to determine the value of $k$ such that this equation has one real solution.\n\nA quadratic equation has exactly one real root if the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x^{2} + 10x - 1 &= 4x - k \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 10x - 1 - 4x + k &= 0 \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 6x + (k - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = -5, \\qquad b = 6, \\qquad c = k-1\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= 6^{2} - 4(-5)(k - 1) \\\\\n&= 36 + 20(k - 1) \\\\\n&= 36 + 20k - 20 \\\\\n&= 16 + 20k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have exactly one real solution, we need\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16 + 20k &= 0 \\\\\n20k &= -16 \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{16}{20} \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{4}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny=4x-\\left(-\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)=4x+\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nTo find the $x$-coordinate, we solve the original quadratic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x^{2} + 6x + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5} - 1\\right) &= 0 \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 6x - \\frac{9}{5} &= 0 \\\\\n- 25x^{2} + 30x - 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- (25x^{2} - 30x + 9) &= 0 \\\\\n- (5x - 3)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n5x - 3 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the $y$-coordinate by substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\ninto one of the two equations and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 4\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) + \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{5} + \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is\n\n$$\n\\Big(\\dfrac{3}{5},\\dfrac{16}{5}\\Big)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186216844 :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the square root, we first square both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk^{2} &= (\\sqrt{\\frac{qℓ}{r}})^{2} \\\\\nk^{2} &= \\frac{qℓ}{r}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve for $q$. First, we multiply both sides by $r$ to remove the fraction:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {k}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{q ℓ}{r}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {k}^{2} \\cdot  r & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{q ℓ}{r}} \\cdot  r \\\\ \\displaystyle {k}^{2} \\cdot  r & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{q ℓ}{\\cancel{r}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{r} \\\\ \\displaystyle r {k}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = q ℓ\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $\\ell$ to isolate $q{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{r {k}^{2}}{ℓ}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{q ℓ}{ℓ}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{r {k}^{2}}{ℓ}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{q \\cancel{ℓ}}{\\cancel{ℓ}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{r {k}^{2}}{ℓ}} & \\displaystyle  = q\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\nq = \\dfrac{rk^2}{\\ell}\n$$" "To get rid of the square root, we first square both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk^{2} &= \\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{qℓ}{r}}\\right)^{2} \\\\\nk^{2} &= \\frac{qℓ}{r}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve for $q$. First, we multiply both sides by $r$ to remove the fraction:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {k}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{q ℓ}{r}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {k}^{2} \\cdot  r & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{q ℓ}{r}} \\cdot  r \\\\ \\displaystyle {k}^{2} \\cdot  r & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{q ℓ}{\\cancel{r}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{r} \\\\ \\displaystyle r {k}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = q ℓ\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $\\ell$ to isolate $q{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{r {k}^{2}}{ℓ}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{q ℓ}{ℓ}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{r {k}^{2}}{ℓ}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{q \\cancel{ℓ}}{\\cancel{ℓ}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{r {k}^{2}}{ℓ}} & \\displaystyle  = q\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\nq = \\dfrac{rk^2}{\\ell}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186216861 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n- $m$ be the side length of cube $M$, and\n- $n$ be the side length of cube $N$.\n\nThen,\n\n- the surface area of cube $M$ is $6m^2$, and\n- the surface area of cube $N$ is $6n^2$.\n\nWe are told that the surface area of cube $N$ is $18$ square inches greater than one half of the surface area of cube $M$. In symbols, this means that\n\n$$\n6n^2 = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 6m^2 + 18\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the obtained equation for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6n^{2} &= 3m^{2} + 18 \\\\\n\\frac{6n^{2}}{6} &= \\frac{3m^{2} + 18}{6} \\\\\nn^{2} &= \\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3 \\\\\nn &= \\sqrt{\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solutions since the side length must be positive.\n\nTherefore, the function $V$ that represents the volume of cube $N$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(m) &= n^{3} \\\\\n&= (\\sqrt{\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3})^{3} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3)^{1/2})^{3} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3)^{3})^{1/2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3)^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let\n\n- $m$ be the side length of cube $M$, and\n- $n$ be the side length of cube $N$.\n\nThen,\n\n- the surface area of cube $M$ is $6m^2$, and\n- the surface area of cube $N$ is $6n^2$.\n\nWe are told that the surface area of cube $N$ is $18$ square inches greater than one half of the surface area of cube $M$. In symbols, this means that\n\n$$\n6n^2 = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 6m^2 + 18\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the obtained equation for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6n^{2} &= 3m^{2} + 18 \\\\\n\\frac{6n^{2}}{6} &= \\frac{3m^{2} + 18}{6} \\\\\nn^{2} &= \\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3 \\\\\nn &= \\sqrt{\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solutions since the side length must be positive.\n\nTherefore, the function $V$ that represents the volume of cube $N$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(m) &= n^{3} \\\\\n&= \\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3}\\right)^{3} \\\\\n&= \\left(\\left(\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3\\right)^{1/2}\\right)^{3} \\\\\n&= \\left(\\left(\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3\\right)^{3}\\right)^{1/2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3\\right)^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186216868 :question/worked-solution "Since the relationship is linear, it can be written as\n\n$$\nw = m\\ell + c\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $m$ is the slope, and\n- $c$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ using the points\n\n$$\n(\\ell_1, w_1) = (4,22)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(\\ell_2, w_2) = (8,33){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{w_{2} - w_{1}}{ℓ_{2} - ℓ_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{33 - 22}{8 - 4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{11}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the $w$-intercept using the point\n\n$$\n(\\ell_1, w_1) = (4,22){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw &= \\frac{11}{4}ℓ + c \\\\\n22 &= \\frac{11}{4}(4) + c \\\\\n22 &= 11 + c \\\\\nc &= 11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\nw = \\dfrac{11}{4}\\ell + 11\n$$\n\nFinally, rearranging into standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw &= \\frac{11}{4}ℓ + 11 \\\\\n4w &= 4(\\frac{11}{4}ℓ + 11) \\\\\n4w &= 11ℓ + 44 \\\\\n4w - 11ℓ &= 44\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since the relationship is linear, it can be written as\n\n$$\nw = m\\ell + c\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $m$ is the slope, and\n- $c$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ using the points\n\n$$\n(\\ell_1, w_1) = (4,22)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(\\ell_2, w_2) = (8,33){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{w_{2} - w_{1}}{ℓ_{2} - ℓ_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{33 - 22}{8 - 4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{11}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the $w$-intercept using the point\n\n$$\n(\\ell_1, w_1) = (4,22){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw &= \\frac{11}{4}ℓ + c \\\\\n22 &= \\frac{11}{4}(4) + c \\\\\n22 &= 11 + c \\\\\nc &= 11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\nw = \\dfrac{11}{4}\\ell + 11\n$$\n\nFinally, rearranging into standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw &= \\frac{11}{4}ℓ + 11 \\\\\n4w &= 4\\left(\\frac{11}{4}ℓ + 11\\right) \\\\\n4w &= 11ℓ + 44 \\\\\n4w - 11ℓ &= 44\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186216979 :question/worked-solution "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y] = 0\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x{y}^{3}}(({x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x-{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y) & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\, \\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\, \\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have  \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y^2}-\\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad Q(x,y) =- \\dfrac{x^2}{y^3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x})=-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}\\right)=-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{is}$ exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x)=\\dfrac{1}{xy^3}$ $\\text{is}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y] = 0\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x{y}^{3}}(({x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x-{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y) & =0 \\\\ \\left(\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\, \\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\, \\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have  \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y^2}-\\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad Q(x,y) =- \\dfrac{x^2}{y^3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x}\\right)=-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}\\right)=-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{is}$ exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x)=\\dfrac{1}{xy^3}$ $\\text{is}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation."] [:db/cas 17592186216980 :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp\\left(\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int {x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x[(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+\\frac{2}{x})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ \\underbrace{({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\underbrace{({x}^{2}y+2)}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{P}(x,y)=x^2+xy^2,\\qquad\n\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)=x^2y+2.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\widetilde{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}y+2)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+2y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=2y,\\qquad G(x)=\\frac{x^3}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^3}{3}+\\frac{x^2y^2}{2}+2y=c.\n$$" "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp\\left(\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int {x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\left[(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(xy+\\frac{2}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y\\right] & =0 \\\\ \\underbrace{({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\underbrace{({x}^{2}y+2)}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{P}(x,y)=x^2+xy^2,\\qquad\n\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)=x^2y+2.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\widetilde{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}y+2)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+2y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=2y,\\qquad G(x)=\\frac{x^3}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^3}{3}+\\frac{x^2y^2}{2}+2y=c.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186216981 :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q} \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} - \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right) = g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x) = \\exp\\left({\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm d x}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form: \n\n$$\n(3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x  + (x) \\;\\textrm{d} y  =0\n$$\n\nLet's denote \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 3x - 4y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x-4y)=-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{x}(-4-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -\\frac{5}{x}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|) \\\\  & ={x}^{-5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(x) \\cdot \\left((3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x + x\\;\\textrm{d} y\\right) =  I(x)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nx^{-5} \\cdot \\left((3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x + x\\;\\textrm{d} y\\right) = 0.\n$$" "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q} \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} - \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right) = g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x) = \\exp\\left({\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm d x}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form: \n\n$$\n(3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x  + (x) \\;\\textrm{d} y  =0\n$$\n\nLet's denote \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 3x - 4y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x-4y)=-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{x}(-4-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡\\left(\\int -\\frac{5}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|) \\\\  & ={x}^{-5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(x) \\cdot \\left((3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x + x\\;\\textrm{d} y\\right) =  I(x)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nx^{-5} \\cdot \\left((3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x + x\\;\\textrm{d} y\\right) = 0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186216995 :question/worked-solution "The temperature $T(t)$ at time $t$ is modeled by the equation\n\n$$\nT(t) = a \\cdot b^t + T_r\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $T_r = 65^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$ is the room (ambient) temperature,\n- $a$ and $b$ are constants to be determined, and\n- $t$ is the time, in minutes.\n\nAt $t=0$, the temperature is $T(0) = 200$. Therefore, we can determine the value of $a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(0) &= 200 \\\\\na \\cdot b^{0} + 65 &= 200 \\\\\na \\cdot 1 &= 135 \\\\\na &= 135\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our model becomes\n\n$$\nT(t) = 135 \\cdot b^t + 65\n$$\n\nAt $t=5$, the temperature is $T(5) = 170$. Hence, we can determine the value of $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(5) &= 170 \\\\\n135 \\cdot b^{5} + 65 &= 170 \\\\\n135 \\cdot b^{5} &= 105 \\\\\nb^{5} &= \\frac{105}{135} \\\\\nb^{5} &= \\frac{7}{9} \\\\\nb &= \\left(\\frac{7}{9}\\right)^{1/5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function best models the temperature $T(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(t) &= 135 \\cdot (\\left(\\frac{7}{9}\\right)^{1/5})^{t} + 65 \\\\\n&= 135 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{7}{9}\\right)^{t/5} + 65\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The temperature $T(t)$ at time $t$ is modeled by the equation\n\n$$\nT(t) = a \\cdot b^t + T_r\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $T_r = 65^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$ is the room (ambient) temperature,\n- $a$ and $b$ are constants to be determined, and\n- $t$ is the time, in minutes.\n\nAt $t=0$, the temperature is $T(0) = 200$. Therefore, we can determine the value of $a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(0) &= 200 \\\\\na \\cdot b^{0} + 65 &= 200 \\\\\na \\cdot 1 &= 135 \\\\\na &= 135\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our model becomes\n\n$$\nT(t) = 135 \\cdot b^t + 65\n$$\n\nAt $t=5$, the temperature is $T(5) = 170$. Hence, we can determine the value of $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(5) &= 170 \\\\\n135 \\cdot b^{5} + 65 &= 170 \\\\\n135 \\cdot b^{5} &= 105 \\\\\nb^{5} &= \\frac{105}{135} \\\\\nb^{5} &= \\frac{7}{9} \\\\\nb &= \\left(\\frac{7}{9}\\right)^{1/5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function best models the temperature $T(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(t) &= 135 \\cdot \\left(\\left(\\frac{7}{9}\\right)^{1/5}\\right)^{t} + 65 \\\\\n&= 135 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{7}{9}\\right)^{t/5} + 65\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186217002 :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{P} \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} - \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right) = h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y) = \\exp\\left({-\\int h(y)\\,\\textrm d y}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(y)$ denotes the exponential function $e^y.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =  y^2, \\qquad Q(x,y) =  xy.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}(2y-y) \\\\  & ={y}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -({y}^{-1})\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & ={y}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot \\left(y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy\\; \\textrm{d}y \\right) = I(y)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y^{-1}\\left(y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy \\; \\textrm{d}y \\right) = 0}.\n$$" "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{P} \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} - \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right) = h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y) = \\exp\\left({-\\int h(y)\\,\\textrm d y}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(y)$ denotes the exponential function $e^y.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =  y^2, \\qquad Q(x,y) =  xy.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}(2y-y) \\\\  & ={y}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -({y}^{-1})\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & ={y}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot \\left(y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy\\; \\textrm{d}y \\right) = I(y)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y^{-1}\\left(y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy \\; \\textrm{d}y \\right) = 0}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186217382 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$55$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe table shows two values of $x$ and their corresponding values of $y$. In the $xy$-plane, the graph of the linear equation representing this relationship passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{5}, a)$. What is the value of $a$?" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$55$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe table shows two values of $x$ and their corresponding values of $y$. In the $xy$-plane, the graph of the linear equation representing this relationship passes through the point $\\left(\\frac{1}{5}, a\\right)$. What is the value of $a$?"] [:db/cas 17592186217383 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe table shows two values of $x$ and their corresponding values of $y$. In the $xy$-plane, the graph of the linear equation representing this relationship passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{3}, a)$. What is the value of $a$?" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe table shows two values of $x$ and their corresponding values of $y$. In the $xy$-plane, the graph of the linear equation representing this relationship passes through the point $\\left(\\frac{1}{3}, a\\right)$. What is the value of $a$?"] [:db/cas 17592186217461 :question/problem "The function is defined by $f(x) =-a^{x} + b$, where $a$ and $b$ are positive constants. The graph of $y = f(x)$ in the $xy$-plane passes through the point $(0, \\frac{3}{2})$, and the product of $a$ and $b$ is $\\frac{35}{2}$. What is the value of $a$?" "The function is defined by $f(x) =-a^{x} + b$, where $a$ and $b$ are positive constants. The graph of $y = f(x)$ in the $xy$-plane passes through the point $\\left(0, \\frac{3}{2}\\right)$, and the product of $a$ and $b$ is $\\frac{35}{2}$. What is the value of $a$?"] [:db/cas 17592186217469 :question/problem "The function is defined by $f(x) = 3 \\cdot a^{x} + b$, where $a$ and $b$ are constants. The graph of $y = f(x)$ in the $xy$-plane passes through the point $(0, \\frac{1}{2})$, and the product of $a$ and $b$ is $-\\frac{15}{2}$. What is the value of $a$?" "The function is defined by $f(x) = 3 \\cdot a^{x} + b$, where $a$ and $b$ are constants. The graph of $y = f(x)$ in the $xy$-plane passes through the point $\\left(0, \\frac{1}{2}\\right)$, and the product of $a$ and $b$ is $-\\frac{15}{2}$. What is the value of $a$?"] [:db/cas 17592186217763 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+xy\\,\\text{d}y=0,\\,y>0\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-4}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-5}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $y$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}.\n$$" "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+xy\\,\\text{d}y=0,\\,y>0\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-4}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\right)={{field-5}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $y$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186217763 :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=xy.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3})=3{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}(3{y}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =3{y}^{-1}-{y}^{-2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -(3{y}^{-1}-{y}^{-2})\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1/y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{y}^{-3})\\cdot \\operatorname{exp}⁡(-1/y) \\\\  & ={y}^{-3}\\,{e}^{-1/y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot ({y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+xy\\,\\text{d}y)=I(y)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}{e}^{-1/y}({y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$" "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\right)=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=xy.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3})=3{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}(3{y}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =3{y}^{-1}-{y}^{-2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -(3{y}^{-1}-{y}^{-2})\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1/y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{y}^{-3})\\cdot \\operatorname{exp}⁡(-1/y) \\\\  & ={y}^{-3}\\,{e}^{-1/y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot ({y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+xy\\,\\text{d}y)=I(y)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}{e}^{-1/y}({y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186217782 :question/worked-solution "The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{x}y+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nis a Riccati differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na(x)=1,\\,b(x)=-\\frac{4}{x},\\,c(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=\\frac{2}{x}.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+\\frac{2}{x}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}=(z+\\frac{2}{x})^{2}-\\frac{4}{x}(z+\\frac{2}{x})+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & =({z}^{2}+\\frac{4z}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})-(\\frac{4z}{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & ={z}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {z}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{z} & =x+C \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z=y-\\frac{2}{x}$ to recover the initial variable $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{1}{x+C} \\\\ y-\\frac{2}{x} & =-\\frac{1}{x+C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{x+C}+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{x}y+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nis a Riccati differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na(x)=1,\\,b(x)=-\\frac{4}{x},\\,c(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=\\frac{2}{x}.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+\\frac{2}{x}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}=\\left(z+\\frac{2}{x}\\right)^{2}-\\frac{4}{x}\\left(z+\\frac{2}{x}\\right)+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & =\\left({z}^{2}+\\frac{4z}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{4z}{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}}\\right)+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & ={z}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {z}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{z} & =x+C \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z=y-\\frac{2}{x}$ to recover the initial variable $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{1}{x+C} \\\\ y-\\frac{2}{x} & =-\\frac{1}{x+C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{x+C}+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186217792 :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n(\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\right)=\\frac{3}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant."] [:db/cas 17592186217792 :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nWe are given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int 3{x}^{-1}\\text{ }\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}[(\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ \\underbrace{(6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3})}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\underbrace{(4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1)}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int (6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+y=c.\n$$" "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\right)=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nWe are given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\right)=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int 3{x}^{-1}\\text{ }\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\left[\\left(\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y\\right] & =0 \\\\ \\underbrace{(6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3})}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\underbrace{(4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1)}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int (6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+y=c.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186217793 :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x-({x}^{3}+2{y}^{4})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{4}{y}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}y,\\hspace{2em}Q(x,y)=-{x}^{3}-2{y}^{4}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(y)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(y)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant.\n\nUse the displayed quotient only where it is defined, and use the integrating-factor method on regions with $y\\ne0$." "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x-({x}^{3}+2{y}^{4})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\right)=\\frac{4}{y}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}y,\\hspace{2em}Q(x,y)=-{x}^{3}-2{y}^{4}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(y)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(y)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant.\n\nUse the displayed quotient only where it is defined, and use the integrating-factor method on regions with $y\\ne0$."] [:db/cas 17592186217811 :question/problem "A circle has its center at $(-\\frac{7}{2},-\\frac{11}{2})$. The segment $JK^(―)$ is a chord of the circle and its slope is $-\\frac{6}{13}$. If the line $l$ is the perpendicular bisector of $JK^(―)$, what is the equation of $l$?" "A circle has its center at $\\left(-\\frac{7}{2},-\\frac{11}{2}\\right)$. The segment $JK^(―)$ is a chord of the circle and its slope is $-\\frac{6}{13}$. If the line $l$ is the perpendicular bisector of $JK^(―)$, what is the equation of $l$?"] [:db/cas 17592186218348 :tutorial/content "Now that we have practiced identifying Clairaut equations, let’s see how to solve them.\n\nThe key feature of Clairaut’s equation is its special form, which leads to a family of **straight-line** solutions and one **singular solution**.\n\nRecall that a Clairaut equation has the form\n\n$$\ny = x\\,y' + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function.\n\nTo simplify the notation, we introduce the substitution:\n\n$$\np = y'\n$$\n\nWith this substitution, the equation becomes\n\n$$\ny = xp + g(p).\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides of the substituted equation with respect to $x$. Remember, $p$ is a function of $x$, so we must use the product rule on the $xp$ term and the chain rule on $g(p)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xp)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(g(p)) \\\\ p & =(1\\cdot p+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}x})+{g}^{'}(p)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing $p' = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}p}{\\textrm{d}x}$, the expression simplifies to\n\n$$\np = p + x p' + g'(p)\\,p'.\n$$\n\nNow, if we simply subtract $p$ from both sides and factor out the common term $p'$, we arrive at the pivotal equation for finding our solutions:\n\n$$\n0 = p'(x + g'(p))\n$$\n\nSince this expression is a product that equals zero, the equation can be satisfied in two different ways. Each way provides us with one type of solution.\n\n- **Case I: The General Solution.**\nThe first possibility is that the derivative of $p$ is zero. If $p' = 0$, then $p$ must be a constant. Let's denote this constant as $c$:\n\n$$\np = c\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p = c$ back into the original Clairaut equation ($y = xp + g(p)$), we directly obtain the **general solution**\n\n$$\ny = cx + g(c).\n$$\n\nThis gives a family of straight-line solutions. Each value of $c$ produces a different line, where $c$ is the slope and $g(c)$ gives the $y$-intercept.\n\n- **Case II: The Singular Solution.**\nIf instead $x + g'(p) = 0$, then we obtain a singular solution. \nLet's first solve for $x$ in terms of $p$:\n\n$$\nx = -g'(p).\n$$\n\nThen, let's substitute this $x$ back into the original Clairaut equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(-{g}^{'}(p))p+g(p) \\\\  & =g(p)-p{g}^{'}(p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the **singular solution** defined parametrically by the equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{g}^{'}(p), \\\\ y & =g(p)-p{g}^{'}(p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let’s take a look at a concrete example of how to find the general solution to a Clairaut equation." "Now that we have practiced identifying Clairaut equations, let’s see how to solve them.\n\nThe key feature of Clairaut’s equation is its special form, which leads to a family of **straight-line** solutions and one **singular solution**.\n\nRecall that a Clairaut equation has the form\n\n$$\ny = x\\,y' + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function.\n\nTo simplify the notation, we introduce the substitution:\n\n$$\np = y'\n$$\n\nWith this substitution, the equation becomes\n\n$$\ny = xp + g(p).\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides of the substituted equation with respect to $x$. Remember, $p$ is a function of $x$, so we must use the product rule on the $xp$ term and the chain rule on $g(p)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xp)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(g(p)) \\\\ p & =\\left(1\\cdot p+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}x}\\right)+{g}^{'}(p)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing $p' = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}p}{\\textrm{d}x}$, the expression simplifies to\n\n$$\np = p + x p' + g'(p)\\,p'.\n$$\n\nNow, if we simply subtract $p$ from both sides and factor out the common term $p'$, we arrive at the pivotal equation for finding our solutions:\n\n$$\n0 = p'(x + g'(p))\n$$\n\nSince this expression is a product that equals zero, the equation can be satisfied in two different ways. Each way provides us with one type of solution.\n\n- **Case I: The General Solution.**\nThe first possibility is that the derivative of $p$ is zero. If $p' = 0$, then $p$ must be a constant. Let's denote this constant as $c$:\n\n$$\np = c\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p = c$ back into the original Clairaut equation ($y = xp + g(p)$), we directly obtain the **general solution**\n\n$$\ny = cx + g(c).\n$$\n\nThis gives a family of straight-line solutions. Each value of $c$ produces a different line, where $c$ is the slope and $g(c)$ gives the $y$-intercept.\n\n- **Case II: The Singular Solution.**\nIf instead $x + g'(p) = 0$, then we obtain a singular solution. \nLet's first solve for $x$ in terms of $p$:\n\n$$\nx = -g'(p).\n$$\n\nThen, let's substitute this $x$ back into the original Clairaut equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(-{g}^{'}(p))p+g(p) \\\\  & =g(p)-p{g}^{'}(p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the **singular solution** defined parametrically by the equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{g}^{'}(p), \\\\ y & =g(p)-p{g}^{'}(p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let’s take a look at a concrete example of how to find the general solution to a Clairaut equation."] [:db/cas 17592186218363 :tutorial/content "Now, let's find the singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny = xy' + (y')^2.\n$$\n\n**Step 1**: Differentiate the equation to find the conditions for the solution.\n\nTo begin, we introduce a parameter by setting $p = y'.$ Substituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = x p + p^2.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xp+{p}^{2}) \\\\ {y}^{'} & =(1\\cdot p+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}x})+2p\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{'}+2p{p}^{'} \\\\ 0 & =x{p}^{'}+2p{p}^{'} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{'}(x+2p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation tells us that either $p' = 0$ or $x + 2p = 0.$\n\n- The condition $p' = 0$ leads to the *general solution* of the Clairaut equation. We've already seen how to deal with this case.\n\n- The condition $x + 2p = 0$ leads to the *singular solution*, which is the envelope of the general solution's family of curves.\n\nOn the next slide, we will use the second condition to find the singular solution." "Now, let's find the singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny = xy' + (y')^2.\n$$\n\n**Step 1**: Differentiate the equation to find the conditions for the solution.\n\nTo begin, we introduce a parameter by setting $p = y'.$ Substituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = x p + p^2.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xp+{p}^{2}) \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\left(1\\cdot p+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}x}\\right)+2p\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{'}+2p{p}^{'} \\\\ 0 & =x{p}^{'}+2p{p}^{'} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{'}(x+2p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation tells us that either $p' = 0$ or $x + 2p = 0.$\n\n- The condition $p' = 0$ leads to the *general solution* of the Clairaut equation. We've already seen how to deal with this case.\n\n- The condition $x + 2p = 0$ leads to the *singular solution*, which is the envelope of the general solution's family of curves.\n\nOn the next slide, we will use the second condition to find the singular solution."] [:db/cas 17592186219871 :answer/value "(t, \\frac{3}{7}t + 1)" "\\left(t, \\frac{3}{7}t + 1\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186219872 :answer/value "(t, \\frac{2}{7}t + 1)" "\\left(t, \\frac{2}{7}t + 1\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186219873 :answer/value "(t, \\frac{3}{7}t - 1)" "\\left(t, \\frac{3}{7}t - 1\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186219874 :answer/value "(t,-\\frac{2}{7}t + 1)" "\\left(t,-\\frac{2}{7}t + 1\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186219875 :answer/value "(t,-\\frac{3}{7}t + 1)" "\\left(t,-\\frac{3}{7}t + 1\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186219883 :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{3}t - 2, t)" "\\left(\\frac{2}{3}t - 2, t\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186219884 :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{3}t - 4, t)" "\\left(\\frac{2}{3}t - 4, t\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186219885 :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{3}t + 12, t)" "\\left(\\frac{2}{3}t + 12, t\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186219886 :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{6}t - 1, t)" "\\left(\\frac{5}{6}t - 1, t\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186219887 :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2}t - 6, t)" "\\left(\\frac{3}{2}t - 6, t\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186220171 :answer/value "e^{-s}(\\frac{1}{s^{3}} + \\frac{1}{s^{2}} + \\frac{1}{s})" "e^{-s}\\left(\\frac{1}{s^{3}} + \\frac{1}{s^{2}} + \\frac{1}{s}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186220172 :answer/value "e^{-s}(\\frac{1}{s^{3}} - \\frac{1}{s^{2}} - \\frac{2}{s})" "e^{-s}\\left(\\frac{1}{s^{3}} - \\frac{1}{s^{2}} - \\frac{2}{s}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186220173 :answer/value "e^{-s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{2}{s^{2}} + \\frac{2}{s})" "e^{-s}\\left(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{2}{s^{2}} + \\frac{2}{s}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186220174 :answer/value "e^{-s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{2}{s^{2}})" "e^{-s}\\left(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{2}{s^{2}}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186220175 :answer/value "e^{2s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} - \\frac{2}{s^{2}})" "e^{2s}\\left(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} - \\frac{2}{s^{2}}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186220250 :answer/value "e^{5s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} - \\frac{14}{s^{2}})" "e^{5s}\\left(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} - \\frac{14}{s^{2}}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186220251 :answer/value "e^{-5s}(\\frac{1}{s^{3}} + \\frac{4}{s^{2}} + \\frac{1}{s})" "e^{-5s}\\left(\\frac{1}{s^{3}} + \\frac{4}{s^{2}} + \\frac{1}{s}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186220252 :answer/value "e^{-s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{14}{s^{2}} + \\frac{46}{s})" "e^{-s}\\left(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{14}{s^{2}} + \\frac{46}{s}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186220253 :answer/value "e^{-5s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{14}{s^{2}} + \\frac{46}{s})" "e^{-5s}\\left(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{14}{s^{2}} + \\frac{46}{s}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186220254 :answer/value "e^{-5s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{14}{s^{2}})" "e^{-5s}\\left(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{14}{s^{2}}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186221008 :question/worked-solution "We will solve the system using the elimination method.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $-1$, then we will get a $+3y$ term that can cancel with the $-3y$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{5}} x - 3 y = 6 &  \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}} x - 3 y = - 6 & | \\hspace{0.222em} \\times  ( - 1 )\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{5}} x - 3 y = 6 \\\\ - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}} x + 3 y = 6\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{5}} x \\cancel{- 3 y} & \\displaystyle  = 6 \\\\ \\displaystyle - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}} x \\cancel{+ 3 y} & \\displaystyle  = 6 \\\\ \\hline \\displaystyle ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{5}} x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}} x ) & \\displaystyle  = 12 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{6}{5}} x & \\displaystyle  = 12 \\\\ \\displaystyle x & \\displaystyle  = 10\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNow, substitute $x = 10$ into one of the original equations to solve for $y$. We'll use the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{5}x - 3y &= -6 \\\\\n\\frac{2}{5}(10) - 3y &= -6 \\\\\n4 - 3y &= -6 \\\\\n- 3y &= -10 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{10}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}x - y{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{5}x - y &= \\frac{3}{5}(10) - \\frac{10}{3} \\\\\n&= 6 - \\frac{10}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{18 - 10}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}x - y\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{3}\n$$" "We will solve the system using the elimination method.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $-1$, then we will get a $+3y$ term that can cancel with the $-3y$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{5}} x - 3 y = 6 &  \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}} x - 3 y = - 6 & | \\hspace{0.222em} \\times  ( - 1 )\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{5}} x - 3 y = 6 \\\\ - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}} x + 3 y = 6\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{5}} x \\cancel{- 3 y} & \\displaystyle  = 6 \\\\ \\displaystyle - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}} x \\cancel{+ 3 y} & \\displaystyle  = 6 \\\\ \\hline \\displaystyle \\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{5}} x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}} x \\right) & \\displaystyle  = 12 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{6}{5}} x & \\displaystyle  = 12 \\\\ \\displaystyle x & \\displaystyle  = 10\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNow, substitute $x = 10$ into one of the original equations to solve for $y$. We'll use the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{5}x - 3y &= -6 \\\\\n\\frac{2}{5}(10) - 3y &= -6 \\\\\n4 - 3y &= -6 \\\\\n- 3y &= -10 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{10}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}x - y{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{5}x - y &= \\frac{3}{5}(10) - \\frac{10}{3} \\\\\n&= 6 - \\frac{10}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{18 - 10}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}x - y\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{3}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221042 :question/worked-solution "First, we find $w(x)$ by adding $u(x)$ and $v(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw(x) &= u(x) + v(x) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{4}{5}x - 8) + (\\frac{2}{5}x + 6) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{4}{5}x + \\frac{2}{5}x) + (-8 + 6) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{5}x - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $x$-intercept of $w(x)$, we set $w(x) = 0$ and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw(x) &= \\frac{6}{5}x - 2 \\\\\n0 &= \\frac{6}{5}x - 2 \\\\\n\\frac{6}{5}x &= 2 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{5}{6} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept of $w(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{5}{3}}\n$$" "First, we find $w(x)$ by adding $u(x)$ and $v(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw(x) &= u(x) + v(x) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{4}{5}x - 8\\right) + \\left(\\frac{2}{5}x + 6\\right) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{4}{5}x + \\frac{2}{5}x\\right) + (-8 + 6) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{5}x - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $x$-intercept of $w(x)$, we set $w(x) = 0$ and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw(x) &= \\frac{6}{5}x - 2 \\\\\n0 &= \\frac{6}{5}x - 2 \\\\\n\\frac{6}{5}x &= 2 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{5}{6} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept of $w(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{5}{3}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221150 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2=4t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(t) &={e}^{t} \\\\ r(4t) &={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{t} \\\\ 4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- Subtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3t\\alpha_1 = e^{4t} - e^{t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_1 = \\dfrac{1}{3t}\\bigl(e^{4t}-e^{t}\\bigr).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the first equation by $4$ and subtracting the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3\\alpha_0 = 4e^{t} - e^{4t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_0 = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\bigl(4e^{t}-e^{4t}\\bigr).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}+4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t} \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-3(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-{e}^{4t} \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((4{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{t})+(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t})) \\\\  & ={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$$\ne^{At} = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ {e}^{t} - {e}^{4 t} & {e}^{4 t}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$" "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2=4t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(t) &={e}^{t} \\\\ r(4t) &={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{t} \\\\ 4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- Subtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3t\\alpha_1 = e^{4t} - e^{t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_1 = \\dfrac{1}{3t}\\bigl(e^{4t}-e^{t}\\bigr).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the first equation by $4$ and subtracting the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3\\alpha_0 = 4e^{t} - e^{4t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_0 = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\bigl(4e^{t}-e^{4t}\\bigr).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t})\\right)t+\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}+4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t} \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-3\\left(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t})\\right)t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-{e}^{4t} \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4\\left(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t})\\right)t+\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((4{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{t})+(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t})) \\\\  & ={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$$\ne^{At} = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ {e}^{t} - {e}^{4 t} & {e}^{4 t}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221151 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1= (3+2\\textrm{i}) t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2= (3-2\\textrm{i}) t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((3+2\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(3+2\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(3+2\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ (3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+2{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1} &={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1} &=\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)), \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$$\ne^{At} = e^{3t}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\cos   ( 2 t ) + 2 \\sin   ( 2 t ) & 2 \\sin   ( 2 t ) \\\\ - {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{2}} \\sin   ( 2 t ) & \\cos   ( 2 t ) - 2 \\sin   ( 2 t )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$" "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1= (3+2\\textrm{i}) t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2= (3-2\\textrm{i}) t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((3+2\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(3+2\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(3+2\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ (3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+2{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1} &={e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1} &=\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{3t}\\left(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\right)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{3t}\\left(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)), \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{1}{2t}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t+{e}^{3t}\\left(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$$\ne^{At} = e^{3t}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\cos   ( 2 t ) + 2 \\sin   ( 2 t ) & 2 \\sin   ( 2 t ) \\\\ - {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{2}} \\sin   ( 2 t ) & \\cos   ( 2 t ) - 2 \\sin   ( 2 t )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221154 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\dfrac12,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac12 - 0 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In our case,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ (1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $$\n\\displaystyle x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + \\dfrac12 = \\dfrac12.\n$$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1/2}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(\\sqrt{e}-2)}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $$\n\\displaystyle y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right) = \\dfrac{4}{7} \\left(\\sqrt{e} - 2\\right).\n$$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\dfrac12,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac12 - 0 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In our case,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ \\left(1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}\\right){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $$\n\\displaystyle x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + \\dfrac12 = \\dfrac12.\n$$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1/2}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(\\sqrt{e}-2)}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $$\n\\displaystyle y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right) = \\dfrac{4}{7} \\left(\\sqrt{e} - 2\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221156 :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a \\ge 0$ and $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}$ exists for $s > s_0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}\n= e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+a)\\big\\},\n\\qquad \\text{for} \\; s > s_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-2)\\big(t^2+3t+2\\big) \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2,$ and we have \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-2)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-2s}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+2)\\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=t^2+3t+2.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+2) & =(t+2)^{2}+3(t+2)+2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4t+4\\,+\\,3t+6\\,+\\,2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+7t+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{t^n\\big\\}=\\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$ and $\\mathcal{L} \\{1\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+2)\\} & =L\\{{t}^{2}+7t+12\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+7L\\{t\\}+12L\\{1\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+7\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+12\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{7}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{12}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-2)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-2s}\\,L\\{g(t+2)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{7}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{12}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a \\ge 0$ and $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}$ exists for $s > s_0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}\n= e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+a)\\big\\},\n\\qquad \\text{for} \\; s > s_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-2)\\big(t^2+3t+2\\big) \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2,$ and we have \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-2)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-2s}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+2)\\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=t^2+3t+2.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+2) & =(t+2)^{2}+3(t+2)+2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4t+4\\,+\\,3t+6\\,+\\,2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+7t+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{t^n\\big\\}=\\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$ and $\\mathcal{L} \\{1\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+2)\\} & =L\\{{t}^{2}+7t+12\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+7L\\{t\\}+12L\\{1\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+7\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+12\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{7}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{12}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-2)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-2s}\\,L\\{g(t+2)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\left(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{7}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{12}{s}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221160 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1}),$ and\n\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny\\left(0\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the $2$-step ABM method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-x-y \\\\  & =-0-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(-1, 0\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = 1.$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{4}+\\left(-\\frac{7}{8}\\right)=-\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =-{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{p} \\\\  & =-1-\\left(-\\frac{5}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{8}\\right)+8\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{39}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{13}{32}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new} = 1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = \\dfrac{1}{4} + \\left(-\\dfrac{13}{32}\\right) = -\\dfrac{5}{32}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{13}{32}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{5}{32}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{32}}.$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1}),$ and\n\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny\\left(0\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the $2$-step ABM method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-x-y \\\\  & =-0-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(-1, 0\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = 1.$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-1\\right)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{4}+\\left(-\\frac{7}{8}\\right)=-\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =-{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{p} \\\\  & =-1-\\left(-\\frac{5}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\left(5\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{8}\\right)+8\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-1\\right)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{39}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{13}{32}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new} = 1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = \\dfrac{1}{4} + \\left(-\\dfrac{13}{32}\\right) = -\\dfrac{5}{32}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{13}{32}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{5}{32}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{32}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186221163 :question/worked-solution "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that $A'(t) = f(t)$ and $A(0) ={ 0}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ & =A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =A(t)-A(0) \\\\  & =A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\} & =L\\{A(t)\\}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & \\,⇒\\, & du={A}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{s}e^{-st} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ we have $-\\dfrac{1}{s}A(t)e^{-st}\\bigg|_0^{\\infty} = {0}$ and\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}A(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n= \\dfrac{1}{s}\\int_0^{\\infty}{ e^{-st} A'(t)} \\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that $A'(t) = f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that $A'(t) = f(t)$ and $A(0) ={ 0}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ & =A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =A(t)-A(0) \\\\  & =A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\} & =L\\{A(t)\\}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & \\,⇒\\, & du={A}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)\\left(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\right)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\right){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{s}e^{-st} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ we have $-\\dfrac{1}{s}A(t)e^{-st}\\bigg|_0^{\\infty} = {0}$ and\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}A(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n= \\dfrac{1}{s}\\int_0^{\\infty}{ e^{-st} A'(t)} \\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that $A'(t) = f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221172 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1-0}2 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(4({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2})}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx = 0,\\qquad y = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + \\dfrac12 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(0+2{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac12, \\qquad y = \\dfrac13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = \\dfrac12 + \\dfrac12 = 1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(\\frac{1}{3}+2\\cdot {1}^{2})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)\\approx\\dfrac{1}{3}, \\qquad\ny(1)\\approx\\dfrac{14}{9}.\n$$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1-0}2 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(4({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2})}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx = 0,\\qquad y = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + \\dfrac12 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\left(0+2{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\right)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac12, \\qquad y = \\dfrac13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = \\dfrac12 + \\dfrac12 = 1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\left(\\frac{1}{3}+2\\cdot {1}^{2}\\right)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)\\approx\\dfrac{1}{3}, \\qquad\ny(1)\\approx\\dfrac{14}{9}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221178 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new}, y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\dfrac43,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac43 - 1 = \\dfrac13.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x-y+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x-y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x-y+2{x}_{\\text{new}})-\\frac{1}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{6}(2x-y+2{x}_{\\text{new}})-\\frac{1}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)-\\frac{1}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = 1 + \\dfrac13 = \\dfrac43.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+5\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & ≈0.67.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\!\\left(\\dfrac43\\right) \\approx 0.67.$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new}, y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\dfrac43,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac43 - 1 = \\dfrac13.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x-y+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x-y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x-y+2{x}_{\\text{new}})-\\frac{1}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{6}(2x-y+2{x}_{\\text{new}})-\\frac{1}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)-\\frac{1}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = 1 + \\dfrac13 = \\dfrac43.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\left(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+5\\cdot 0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & ≈0.67.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\!\\left(\\dfrac43\\right) \\approx 0.67.$"] [:db/cas 17592186221196 :question/worked-solution "Recall that multiplying the argument of a function by $k \\neq 0$ represents the horizontal stretching of the corresponding graph by a factor of $\\dfrac{1}{k}.$\n\nNow, let $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{L}x.$ Then, since $f(x)$ has a period of $2L,$ the function\n\n$$\ng(s) = f\\left(\\dfrac{L}{\\pi}s\\right)\n$$\n\nmust have a period of $2L \\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{L} = 2\\pi.$ \n\nThe Fourier series of $g(s)$ is\n\n$$\ng(s) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos(ns)+b_n\\sin(ns),\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{L}x$ and using the fact that $g(s) = f\\left(\\dfrac{L}{\\pi}s\\right),$ we get the Fourier series of $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\na_n\\cos\\bigg(\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x}\\bigg)+\nb_n\\sin\\bigg(\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x}\\bigg)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4$th cosine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, $4$th sine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=2$ and $f(x)=\\cosh(x+1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x, \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that multiplying the argument of a function by $k \\neq 0$ represents the horizontal stretching of the corresponding graph by a factor of $\\dfrac{1}{k}.$\n\nNow, let $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{L}x.$ Then, since $f(x)$ has a period of $2L,$ the function\n\n$$\ng(s) = f\\left(\\dfrac{L}{\\pi}s\\right)\n$$\n\nmust have a period of $2L \\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{L} = 2\\pi.$ \n\nThe Fourier series of $g(s)$ is\n\n$$\ng(s) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos(ns)+b_n\\sin(ns),\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{L}x$ and using the fact that $g(s) = f\\left(\\dfrac{L}{\\pi}s\\right),$ we get the Fourier series of $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\na_n\\cos\\bigg(\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x}\\bigg)+\nb_n\\sin\\bigg(\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x}\\bigg)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4$th cosine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, $4$th sine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=2$ and $f(x)=\\cosh(x+1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{2}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x, \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{2}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221198 :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{1}{L}\\int_{-L}^{L} f(x)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and $f(x)=-5x+4$ for $x \\in [-2,2).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\na_3 = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2} (-5x+4)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-5x+4\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-5 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(uv\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{3\\pi }{[(-5x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)]}_{-2}^{2}-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\cdot (-5)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{3\\pi }[(-5(2)+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-(-5(-2)+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3\\pi )]+\\frac{10}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\cdot 0+\\frac{10}{3\\pi }\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\right){[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)]}_{-2}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{20}{9{\\pi }^{2}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3\\pi )]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{9{\\pi }^{2}}(-1-(-1)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{1}{L}\\int_{-L}^{L} f(x)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and $f(x)=-5x+4$ for $x \\in [-2,2).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\na_3 = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2} (-5x+4)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-5x+4\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-5 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x\\right)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(uv\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{2}{3\\pi }{\\left[(-5x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x\\right)\\right]}_{-2}^{2}-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x\\right)\\cdot (-5)\\,\\text{d}x\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{2}{3\\pi }[(-5(2)+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-(-5(-2)+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3\\pi )]+\\frac{10}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\cdot 0+\\frac{10}{3\\pi }\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\right){\\left[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x\\right)\\right]}_{-2}^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(-\\frac{20}{9{\\pi }^{2}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3\\pi )]\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{9{\\pi }^{2}}(-1-(-1)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221200 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=5.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{107}{6}\n+ \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{250(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^2}\n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that  \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-5,5),$\n\n- continuous on $[-5,5),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-5)=F(5),$ if we extend the value at $x=5$ using the formula $F(x)=\\dfrac{5}{2}x^2-3.$\n\nTherefore, the $10$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{continuous} }$ and $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise smooth}}$\non $\\mathbb{R},$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-3) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{107}{6}\\right)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{250(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)) \\\\ 5x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{250(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)) \\\\ 5x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-50(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right) \\\\ 5x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{50(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=5.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{107}{6}\n+ \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{250(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^2}\n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that  \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-5,5),$\n\n- continuous on $[-5,5),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-5)=F(5),$ if we extend the value at $x=5$ using the formula $F(x)=\\dfrac{5}{2}x^2-3.$\n\nTherefore, the $10$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{continuous} }$ and $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise smooth}}$\non $\\mathbb{R},$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-3\\right) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{107}{6}\\right)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{250(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)\\right) \\\\ 5x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{250(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{n\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)\\right) \\\\ 5x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-50(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right) \\\\ 5x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{50(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221202 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and we are already given the coefficients for $n \\geq 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim -4+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{n^2\\pi^2}\n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-4,4).$ \nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise continuous}}$ on $\\mathbb{R},$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-4+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4}\\right))\\text{d}t \\\\ {[-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼{[-4t]}_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot [\\frac{4}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4}\\right)]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3} & ∼-4x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{192(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and we are already given the coefficients for $n \\geq 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim -4+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{n^2\\pi^2}\n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-4,4).$ \nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise continuous}}$ on $\\mathbb{R},$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\left(-4+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4}\\right)\\right)\\text{d}t \\\\ {\\left[-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{3}\\right]}_{0}^{x} & ∼{[-4t]}_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{4}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4}\\right)\\right]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3} & ∼-4x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{192(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221203 :question/worked-solution "We assume that a particular solution has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) + b_n \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y'_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & \\,-2(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & \\,\\,=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -{a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{0} &=3 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n} &=\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation,\n\n$$\na_0=-3,\n$$\n\nwhich means the constant term is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation,\n$b_n=-\\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{6}.$\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{6}\\right)\\dfrac{n\\pi}{3}-2a_n=\\dfrac{12}{n^2\\pi}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\na_n=-\\dfrac{216}{n^2\\pi(n^2\\pi^2+36)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $b_n$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{216}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼-\\frac{3}{2} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{216}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We assume that a particular solution has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) + b_n \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right)+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y'_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & \\,-2\\left(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -{a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\left(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{0} &=3 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n} &=\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation,\n\n$$\na_0=-3,\n$$\n\nwhich means the constant term is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation,\n$b_n=-\\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{6}.$\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{6}\\right)\\dfrac{n\\pi}{3}-2a_n=\\dfrac{12}{n^2\\pi}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\na_n=-\\dfrac{216}{n^2\\pi(n^2\\pi^2+36)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $b_n$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{216}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼-\\frac{3}{2} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{216}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221204 :question/worked-solution "Because the forcing function has only a constant term and sine terms, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y''_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty -b_n n^2\\pi^2 \\sin(n\\pi x)\n-\n\\left( \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n \\sin(n\\pi x) \\right)\n=\n1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(-n^2\\pi^2-1)\\sin(n\\pi x)\n=\n1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {a}_{0}}{2}} = 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} ( - {n}^{2} {\\pi }^{2} - 1 ) = {\\displaystyle \\frac{( - 1 {)}^{n + 1}}{ {n}^{3} \\pi }}\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{ {a}_{0}}{2}} = - 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{( - 1 {)}^{n}}{ {n}^{3} \\pi  ( {n}^{2} {\\pi }^{2} + 1 )}}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \n-1\n+ \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\:\n\\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^3\\pi(n^2\\pi^2+1)}\n\\cdot\n\\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$" "Because the forcing function has only a constant term and sine terms, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\right)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y''_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty -b_n n^2\\pi^2 \\sin(n\\pi x)\n-\n\\left( \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n \\sin(n\\pi x) \\right)\n=\n1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(-n^2\\pi^2-1)\\sin(n\\pi x)\n=\n1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {a}_{0}}{2}} = 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} ( - {n}^{2} {\\pi }^{2} - 1 ) = {\\displaystyle \\frac{( - 1 {)}^{n + 1}}{ {n}^{3} \\pi }}\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{ {a}_{0}}{2}} = - 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{( - 1 {)}^{n}}{ {n}^{3} \\pi  ( {n}^{2} {\\pi }^{2} + 1 )}}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \n-1\n+ \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\:\n\\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^3\\pi(n^2\\pi^2+1)}\n\\cdot\n\\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221600 :question/problem "When line $m$ is graphed in the $xy$-plane, it has an $x$-intercept of $(5, 0)$ and a $y$-intercept of $(0,-\\frac{21}{2})$. What is the slope of line $m$?" "When line $m$ is graphed in the $xy$-plane, it has an $x$-intercept of $(5, 0)$ and a $y$-intercept of $\\left(0,-\\frac{21}{2}\\right)$. What is the slope of line $m$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221925 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 7{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-4t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-4t) &={e}^{-4t} \\\\ r(t) &={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{-4t} \\\\ t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtraction the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-5t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second equation by $4$ and adding to the first one, we get\n\n$$\n5{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t}+4{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-4t}+4{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =6(-\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-4t}+4{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-6{e}^{-4t}+6{e}^{t}))+({e}^{-4t}+4{e}^{t})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(7{e}^{-4t}-2{e}^{t}) \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-2(-\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2{e}^{-4t}-2{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}7{e}^{-4t}-2{e}^{t} & -7{e}^{-4t}+7{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}-2{e}^{t} & -2{e}^{-4t}+7{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 7{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-4t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-4t) &={e}^{-4t} \\\\ r(t) &={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{-4t} \\\\ t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtraction the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-5t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second equation by $4$ and adding to the first one, we get\n\n$$\n5{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t}+4{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-4t}+4{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =6\\left(-\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t})\\right)t+\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-4t}+4{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-6{e}^{-4t}+6{e}^{t}))+({e}^{-4t}+4{e}^{t})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(7{e}^{-4t}-2{e}^{t}) \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-2\\left(-\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t})\\right)t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2{e}^{-4t}-2{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}7{e}^{-4t}-2{e}^{t} & -7{e}^{-4t}+7{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}-2{e}^{t} & -2{e}^{-4t}+7{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221928 :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-1)({t}^{2}+1)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-1)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-s}\\,L\\{g(t+1)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}+1.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+1) & =(t+1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+2t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+1)\\} & =L\\{{t}^{2}+2t+2\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+2L\\{t\\}+2L\\{1\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-1)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-s}\\,L\\{g(t+1)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-1)({t}^{2}+1)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-1)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-s}\\,L\\{g(t+1)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}+1.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+1) & =(t+1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+2t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+1)\\} & =L\\{{t}^{2}+2t+2\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+2L\\{t\\}+2L\\{1\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-1)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-s}\\,L\\{g(t+1)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-s}\\left(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{s}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221934 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(0)≈\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3+x-2{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =3+0-2\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(-1,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=0.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 3-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{9}{2}=\\frac{43}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{43}{6}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =3+{x}_{\\text{new}}-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{p} \\\\  & =3+1-2\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{43}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{31}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{43}{6}$ | $-\\frac{31}{3}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{31}{3}\\right)+8\\cdot 3-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{83}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{83}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{43}{6}$ | $-\\frac{31}{3}$ | $-\\frac{83}{36}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}+\\left(-\\frac{83}{36}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{43}{6}$ | $-\\frac{31}{3}$ | $-\\frac{83}{36}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{13}{36}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conlude that $y(1)≈\\frac{13}{36}.$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(0)≈\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3+x-2{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =3+0-2\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(-1,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=0.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 3-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{9}{2}=\\frac{43}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{43}{6}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =3+{x}_{\\text{new}}-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{p} \\\\  & =3+1-2\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{43}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{31}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{43}{6}$ | $-\\frac{31}{3}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\left(5\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{31}{3}\\right)+8\\cdot 3-0\\right)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{83}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{83}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{43}{6}$ | $-\\frac{31}{3}$ | $-\\frac{83}{36}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}+\\left(-\\frac{83}{36}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{43}{6}$ | $-\\frac{31}{3}$ | $-\\frac{83}{36}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{13}{36}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conlude that $y(1)≈\\frac{13}{36}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186221935 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{17}{16})$ is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{7}{8}.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,\\frac{1}{2})$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is ${y}_{p}^{′}=\\frac{13}{16}.$\n\nNow, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{13}{16}+8\\cdot \\frac{7}{8}-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\frac{153}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{128}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{9}{16}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{17}{16}$ | $\\frac{7}{8}$ | $\\frac{9}{32}$ | $\\frac{43}{32}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $\\frac{51}{128}$ |" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{17}{16}\\right)$ is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{7}{8}.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=\\left(1,\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is ${y}_{p}^{′}=\\frac{13}{16}.$\n\nNow, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\left(5\\cdot \\frac{13}{16}+8\\cdot \\frac{7}{8}-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\frac{153}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{128}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{9}{16}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{17}{16}$ | $\\frac{7}{8}$ | $\\frac{9}{32}$ | $\\frac{43}{32}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $\\frac{51}{128}$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186221937 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by ${y}_{p}=y+Δ{y}_{p}.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈\\frac{27}{16}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-3y+6 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-3\\cdot \\frac{27}{16}+6 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{27}{16}$ | $-\\frac{1}{16}$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(-1,2)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=-2.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{16}\\right)-(-2))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{29}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{27}{16}$ | $-\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{29}{64}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{16}+\\frac{29}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{137}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{27}{16}$ | $-\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{29}{64}$ | $\\frac{137}{64}$ |" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by ${y}_{p}=y+Δ{y}_{p}.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈\\frac{27}{16}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-3y+6 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-3\\cdot \\frac{27}{16}+6 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{27}{16}$ | $-\\frac{1}{16}$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(-1,2)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=-2.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{16}\\right)-(-2)\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{29}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{27}{16}$ | $-\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{29}{64}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{16}+\\frac{29}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{137}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{27}{16}$ | $-\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{29}{64}$ | $\\frac{137}{64}$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186221942 :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-5)({t}^{2}+4t+1)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=5,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-5)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-5s}\\,L{g(t+5)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}+4t+1.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+5) & =(t+5)^{2}+4(t+5)+1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+10t+25\\,+\\,4t+20\\,+\\,1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+14t+46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L{1}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+5)} & =L{{t}^{2}+14t+46} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+14L{t}+46L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+14\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+46\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{46}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-5)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-5s}\\,L{g(t+5)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-5s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{46}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-5)({t}^{2}+4t+1)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=5,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-5)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-5s}\\,L{g(t+5)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}+4t+1.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+5) & =(t+5)^{2}+4(t+5)+1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+10t+25\\,+\\,4t+20\\,+\\,1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+14t+46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L{1}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+5)} & =L{{t}^{2}+14t+46} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+14L{t}+46L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+14\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+46\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{46}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-5)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-5s}\\,L{g(t+5)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-5s}\\left(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{46}{s}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221944 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find $$\n\\displaystyle L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ}\n$$ given $L{f(t)}=F(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{{field-1}}\\,dτ=A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t}=A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ}=L{A(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & ⇒ & du={{field-3}} \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & ={{field-4}}(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt={{field-5}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={{field-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose we wish to find $$\n\\displaystyle L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ}\n$$ given $L{f(t)}=F(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{{field-1}}\\,dτ=A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t}=A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ}=L{A(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & ⇒ & du={{field-3}} \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & ={{field-4}}\\left(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\right)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\right){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt={{field-5}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={{field-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221944 :question/worked-solution "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ & =A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =A(t)-A(0) \\\\  & =A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ} & =L{A(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & \\,⇒\\, & du={A}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and $\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=0$ and\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt=\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ & =A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =A(t)-A(0) \\\\  & =A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ} & =L{A(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & \\,⇒\\, & du={A}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)\\left(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\right)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\right){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and $\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=0$ and\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt=\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221950 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the $2$-step ABM method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(2y-x-1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=0.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{4}{3}+1=\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}(2{y}_{p}-{x}_{\\text{new}}-1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (2\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}-2-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{10}{3}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{10}{3}+8\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{3})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 22 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{10}{3}$ | $\\frac{11}{6}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{11}{6}=\\frac{19}{6}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{10}{3}$ | $\\frac{11}{6}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{19}{6}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈\\frac{19}{6}.$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the $2$-step ABM method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(2y-x-1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-1-1\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=0.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}-0\\right)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{4}{3}+1=\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}(2{y}_{p}-{x}_{\\text{new}}-1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(2\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}-2-1\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{10}{3}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\left(5\\cdot \\frac{10}{3}+8\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 22 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{10}{3}$ | $\\frac{11}{6}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{11}{6}=\\frac{19}{6}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{10}{3}$ | $\\frac{11}{6}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{19}{6}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈\\frac{19}{6}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186221959 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{1}{2})$ is ${y}^{′}=2.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,0)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=2.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is ${y}_{p}^{′}=2.$\n\nNow, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 2+8\\cdot 2-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot 24 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ is ${y}^{′}=2.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,0)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=2.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is ${y}_{p}^{′}=2.$\n\nNow, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 2+8\\cdot 2-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot 24 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186221971 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y \\\\ 1-{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) & 1 \\\\ -2x & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (1)) & 1 \\\\ -2(1) & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda \\cdot (-1-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues $$\n\\displaystyle {\\lambda }_{1,2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since we have two complex eigenvalues with negative real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y \\\\ 1-{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y\\right)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) & 1 \\\\ -2x & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (1)) & 1 \\\\ -2(1) & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda \\cdot (-1-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues $$\n\\displaystyle {\\lambda }_{1,2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since we have two complex eigenvalues with negative real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186221979 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & =4{x}_{-1}-{y}_{-1}-8 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-2-8 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(2,-\\frac{1}{4})$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-y-8 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2-\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-8 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-(-6))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{27}{8}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{27}{8}=\\frac{25}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{27}{8}$ | $\\frac{25}{8}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =4{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{p}-8 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3-\\frac{25}{8}-8 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{27}{8}$ | $\\frac{25}{8}$ | $\\frac{7}{8}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{7}{8}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-(-6))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{99}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{27}{8}$ | $\\frac{25}{8}$ | $\\frac{7}{8}$ | $\\frac{33}{32}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=3.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{33}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{27}{8}$ | $\\frac{25}{8}$ | $\\frac{7}{8}$ | $\\frac{33}{32}$ |\n| $3$ | $\\frac{25}{32}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈\\frac{25}{32}.$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & =4{x}_{-1}-{y}_{-1}-8 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-2-8 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=\\left(2,-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-y-8 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2-\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-8 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-(-6)\\right)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{27}{8}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{27}{8}=\\frac{25}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{27}{8}$ | $\\frac{25}{8}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =4{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{p}-8 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3-\\frac{25}{8}-8 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{27}{8}$ | $\\frac{25}{8}$ | $\\frac{7}{8}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\left(5\\cdot \\frac{7}{8}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-(-6)\\right)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{99}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{27}{8}$ | $\\frac{25}{8}$ | $\\frac{7}{8}$ | $\\frac{33}{32}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=3.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{33}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{27}{8}$ | $\\frac{25}{8}$ | $\\frac{7}{8}$ | $\\frac{33}{32}$ |\n| $3$ | $\\frac{25}{32}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈\\frac{25}{32}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186222012 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1+\\frac{1}{2})}{5} \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=0.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(0.6+1)}{5} \\\\  & =0.64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈0.60,\\,y(1)≈0.64.\n$$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\left(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{5} \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=0.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(0.6+1)}{5} \\\\  & =0.64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈0.60,\\,y(1)≈0.64.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186222015 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(-1)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=-1$ to $x=1$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((3x+4y)+(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+4{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y)+2{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y)+2{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)+2{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(0)$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-1+1=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 0+6\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{2} \\\\  & =-10.50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(0)≈3.00,\\,y(1)≈-10.50.\n$$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(-1)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=-1$ to $x=1$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((3x+4y)+(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+4{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y)+2{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y)+2{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)+2{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(0)$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-1+1=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\left(3\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 0+6\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{2} \\\\  & =-10.50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(0)≈3.00,\\,y(1)≈-10.50.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186222016 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2x-3y)+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)-\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ and $y(2).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=1,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2},\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2\\cdot 2+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{7} \\\\  & ≈1.14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)≈1.00,\\,y(2)≈1.14.\n$$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2x-3y)+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)-\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ and $y(2).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=1,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\left(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2\\right) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2},\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2\\cdot 2+1\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{7} \\\\  & ≈1.14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)≈1.00,\\,y(2)≈1.14.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186222022 :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3{x}^{2}+2x$ for $x\\in [-2,2).$ The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x\\right)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3{x}^{2}+2x$ for $x\\in [-2,2).$ The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186222023 :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=6x+1$ for $x\\in [-3,3).$ The Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${b}_{5}.$\n\n${b}_{5}=$ {{field-1}}" "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x\\right)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=6x+1$ for $x\\in [-3,3).$ The Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${b}_{5}.$\n\n${b}_{5}=$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186222023 :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and $f(x)=6x+1$ for $x\\in (-3,3).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{5}=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(6x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=6x+1\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{3}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{5} & =\\frac{1}{3}(uv\\,{|}_{-3}^{3}-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{3}{5\\pi }{[(6x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{3}+\\frac{18}{5\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }(-{[(6x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{3}+6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }(-((6(3)+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi )-(6(-3)+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5\\pi ))+\\frac{18}{5\\pi }{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }(36+\\frac{18}{5\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-5\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\cdot 36 \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and $f(x)=6x+1$ for $x\\in (-3,3).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{5}=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(6x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=6x+1\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x\\right)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{3}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{5} & =\\frac{1}{3}(uv\\,{|}_{-3}^{3}-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(-\\frac{3}{5\\pi }{\\left[(6x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x\\right)\\right]}_{-3}^{3}+\\frac{18}{5\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\left(-{\\left[(6x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x\\right)\\right]}_{-3}^{3}+6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\left(-((6(3)+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi )-(6(-3)+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5\\pi ))+\\frac{18}{5\\pi }{\\left[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x\\right)\\right]}_{-3}^{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\left(36+\\frac{18}{5\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-5\\pi ))\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\cdot 36 \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186222024 :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2, & x\\in [0,2) \\\\ -1, & x\\in [-2,0)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nis shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{3}.$\n\n${a}_{3}=$ {{field-1}}" "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x\\right)\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2, & x\\in [0,2) \\\\ -1, & x\\in [-2,0)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nis shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{3}.$\n\n${a}_{3}=$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186222024 :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2.$ Also, $f(x)=2$ for $x\\in (0,2)$ and $f(x)=-1$ for $x\\in (-2,0).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3\\pi }{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)]}_{-2}^{0}+\\frac{2}{3\\pi }{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x)]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3\\pi ))+\\frac{2}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2.$ Also, $f(x)=2$ for $x\\in (0,2)$ and $f(x)=-1$ for $x\\in (-2,0).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3\\pi }{\\left[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x\\right)\\right]}_{-2}^{0}+\\frac{2}{3\\pi }{\\left[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x\\right)\\right]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3\\pi ))+\\frac{2}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186222027 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-\\frac{13}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{64(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-2,2),$\n\n- continuous on $[-2,2),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-2)=F(2),$ if we extend the value at $x=2$ using the formula $F(x)=-4{x}^{2}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the $4$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4{x}^{2}+1) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(-\\frac{13}{3}\\right)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{64(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)) \\\\ -8x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{64(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)) \\\\ -8x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-32(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right) \\\\ -8x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{32(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-\\frac{13}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{64(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-2,2),$\n\n- continuous on $[-2,2),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-2)=F(2),$ if we extend the value at $x=2$ using the formula $F(x)=-4{x}^{2}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the $4$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4{x}^{2}+1) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(-\\frac{13}{3}\\right)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{64(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ -8x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{64(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ -8x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-32(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right) \\\\ -8x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{32(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186222029 :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=x$ for $x\\in [-3,3).$ The Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x\\right)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=x$ for $x\\in [-3,3).$ The Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186222029 :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and $f(x)=x$ for $x\\in [-3,3).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{-3}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{9}{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and $f(x)=x$ for $x\\in [-3,3).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\right]}_{-3}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{9}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186222030 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{32(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-2,2).$ \nTherefore, the $4$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}8t\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{32(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ [4{t}^{2}]_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{32(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot [-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{2}\\right)]_{0}^{x} \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{64(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{32(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-2,2).$ \nTherefore, the $4$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}8t\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{32(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ [4{t}^{2}]_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{32(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\left[-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\right]_{0}^{x} \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{64(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot \\left(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186222031 :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))+{b}_{n}\\cdot n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)) \\\\  & \\,+5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)) \\\\  & \\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({b}_{n}n\\pi +5{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-{a}_{n}n\\pi +5{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the cosine and sine coefficients, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2} &=0 \\\\ {b}_{n}n\\pi +5{a}_{n} &=0 \\\\ -{a}_{n}n\\pi +5{b}_{n} &=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nNext, from the second equation, \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the third equation gives\n\n$$\n-\\left(-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{5}\\right)n\\pi +5{b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{5}\\cdot \\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$" "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\right) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))+{b}_{n}\\cdot n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)) \\\\  & \\,+5\\left(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({b}_{n}n\\pi +5{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-{a}_{n}n\\pi +5{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the cosine and sine coefficients, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2} &=0 \\\\ {b}_{n}n\\pi +5{a}_{n} &=0 \\\\ -{a}_{n}n\\pi +5{b}_{n} &=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nNext, from the second equation, \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the third equation gives\n\n$$\n-\\left(-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{5}\\right)n\\pi +5{b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{5}\\cdot \\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186222032 :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & \\,-(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}-{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}-{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=-1 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}-{a}_{n} &=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}-{b}_{n} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=2,$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation, ${b}_{n}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}.$ Substituting this into the third equation, gives\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\right)\\frac{n\\pi }{2}-{a}_{n}=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼1 & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{8}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\right)+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & \\,-\\left(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\left(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}-{a}_{n}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}-{b}_{n}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=-1 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}-{a}_{n} &=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}-{b}_{n} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=2,$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation, ${b}_{n}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}.$ Substituting this into the third equation, gives\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\right)\\frac{n\\pi }{2}-{a}_{n}=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼1 & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{8}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186222036 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=\\pi $ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼3+\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{6}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{14(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-\\pi ,\\pi ),$\n\n- continuous on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-\\pi )=F(\\pi ),$ if we extend the value at $x=\\pi $ using the formula $F(x)=\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+3.$\n\nTherefore, the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+3) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3+\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{6})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{14(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)) \\\\ 7x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{14(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot (-n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)) \\\\ 7x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-14(-1)^{n}}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx) \\\\ 7x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{14(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=\\pi $ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼3+\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{6}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{14(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-\\pi ,\\pi ),$\n\n- continuous on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-\\pi )=F(\\pi ),$ if we extend the value at $x=\\pi $ using the formula $F(x)=\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+3.$\n\nTherefore, the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2}+3\\right) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(3+\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{6}\\right)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{14(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\right) \\\\ 7x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{14(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot (-n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)) \\\\ 7x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-14(-1)^{n}}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx) \\\\ 7x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{14(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186222037 :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+16({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms to find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(16-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\left(\\frac{144-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{b}_{n}(144-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}{9}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (144-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (144-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$" "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+16\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms to find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\left(16-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\left(\\frac{144-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{b}_{n}(144-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}{9}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (144-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (144-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186222038 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and we are already given the coefficients for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{24(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-2,2).$ \nTherefore, the $4$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{24(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{2}\\right))\\text{d}t \\\\ {[-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼[-2t]_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{24(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot [\\frac{2}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{2}\\right)]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3} & ∼-2x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and we are already given the coefficients for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{24(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-2,2).$ \nTherefore, the $4$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\left(-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{24(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\right)\\text{d}t \\\\ {\\left[-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{3}\\right]}_{0}^{x} & ∼[-2t]_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{24(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{2}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\right]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3} & ∼-2x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186222039 :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{4{\\pi }^{2}}=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $1$-periodic function $(L=\\frac{1}{2})$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=2\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x))+x(-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-4\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)+4\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)-4{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+4{\\pi }^{2}y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-4\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)+4\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)-4{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+4{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-4\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)+4\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\pi A &=0 \\\\ 4\\pi B &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=0 \\\\ B &=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x).\n$$" "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{4{\\pi }^{2}}=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $1$-periodic function $\\left(L=\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=2\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x))+x(-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-4\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)+4\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)-4{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+4{\\pi }^{2}y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-4\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)+4\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)-4{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+4{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-4\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)+4\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\pi A &=0 \\\\ 4\\pi B &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=0 \\\\ B &=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186222478 :tutorial/content "Suppose a quantity increases over time at a rate proportional to its current value, such as the balance of a bank account growing with continuous interest. The Laplace transform can be used to analyze how such exponential growth behaves, especially when the function is **integrated over time** to represent total accumulation.\n\nTo illustrate this, we’ll find the Laplace transform of a function defined as the integral of an exponential function. This shows how *integration in the time domain* corresponds to *division by $s$* in the $s$-domain.\n\nSuppose we are given that \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-a}, \\qquad s>a.\n$$\n\nLet's find the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\ng(t) = \\int_{0}^{t} e^{\\tau/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\tau,\n\\qquad s > \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe apply the **Integration in Time Property**: if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)=e^{t/2}$. Thus, for $s>\\dfrac{1}{2},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{t/2}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(s-\\frac{1}{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have for $s>\\dfrac{1}{2},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\,\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{τ/2}\\,dτ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s\\,(s-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s(2s-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose a quantity increases over time at a rate proportional to its current value, such as the balance of a bank account growing with continuous interest. The Laplace transform can be used to analyze how such exponential growth behaves, especially when the function is **integrated over time** to represent total accumulation.\n\nTo illustrate this, we’ll find the Laplace transform of a function defined as the integral of an exponential function. This shows how *integration in the time domain* corresponds to *division by $s$* in the $s$-domain.\n\nSuppose we are given that \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-a}, \\qquad s>a.\n$$\n\nLet's find the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\ng(t) = \\int_{0}^{t} e^{\\tau/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\tau,\n\\qquad s > \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe apply the **Integration in Time Property**: if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)=e^{t/2}$. Thus, for $s>\\dfrac{1}{2},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{t/2}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(s-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have for $s>\\dfrac{1}{2},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\,\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{τ/2}\\,dτ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s\\,\\left(s-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s(2s-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186222480 :tutorial/content "Let's return to the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x-2y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nWe will now complete our approximation of the value of $y(1)$ using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method.\n\nFirst, recall that we previously initialized with the RK4 method and completed the predictor step of the ABM $2$-step method, resulting in the following values:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{13}8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, the slope at the point $$\n\\displaystyle (x_\\text{new},y_p) = \\left(\\dfrac12+\\dfrac12, \\dfrac{13}8\\right) = \\left(1, \\dfrac{13}8\\right)\n$$ estimated by the predictor is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{'} & =4{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-2\\cdot \\frac{13}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{13}8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $$\n\\displaystyle (x, y) = \\left(\\dfrac12, \\dfrac34\\right)\n$$ is $y' = \\dfrac12.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 1\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -2.$\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-(-2))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\frac{39}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{13}8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{13}{32}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe know the value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new}=1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = \\dfrac34 + \\dfrac{13}{32} = \\dfrac{37}{32}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{13}8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{13}{32}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{37}{32}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx \\dfrac{37}{32}.$" "Let's return to the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x-2y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nWe will now complete our approximation of the value of $y(1)$ using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton m