a-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{13}8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $$\n\\displaystyle (x, y) = \\left(\\dfrac12, \\dfrac34\\right)\n$$ is $y' = \\dfrac12.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 1\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -2.$\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\left(5\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-(-2)\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\frac{39}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{13}8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{13}{32}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe know the value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new}=1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = \\dfrac34 + \\dfrac{13}{32} = \\dfrac{37}{32}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{13}8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{13}{32}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{37}{32}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx \\dfrac{37}{32}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186222482 :tutorial/content "Let's find $e^{At}$ for <div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div> \n\nBy solving the characteristic polynomial $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we can find that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=2, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2=7.\n$$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 7\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ -7{\\alpha }_{1}t & 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=2t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2=7t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(2t) &={e}^{2t} \\\\ r(7t) &={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\Rightarrow \\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{2t} \\\\ 7t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- Subtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n5t\\alpha_1 = e^{7t} - e^{2t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_1 = \\dfrac{1}{5t}\\bigl(e^{7t}-e^{2t}\\bigr).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the first equation by $7$ and subtracting twice the second one, we get\n\n$$\n5\\alpha_0 = 7e^{2t} - 2e^{7t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_0 = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\bigl(7e^{2t}-2e^{7t}\\bigr).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =2(\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{7t}-{e}^{2t}))t+\\frac{1}{5}(7{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{7t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((2{e}^{7t}-2{e}^{2t})+(7{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{7t})) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t} \\\\ -7{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-7(\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{7t}-{e}^{2t}))t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{5}({e}^{7t}-{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}({e}^{2t}-{e}^{7t}) \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =7(\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{7t}-{e}^{2t}))t+\\frac{1}{5}(7{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{7t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((7{e}^{7t}-7{e}^{2t})+(7{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{7t})) \\\\  & ={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ne^{At} = \n\\dfrac{1}{5} \\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 7({e}^{2t}-{e}^{7t}) & 5{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Let's find $e^{At}$ for <div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div> \n\nBy solving the characteristic polynomial $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we can find that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=2, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2=7.\n$$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 7\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ -7{\\alpha }_{1}t & 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=2t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2=7t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(2t) &={e}^{2t} \\\\ r(7t) &={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\Rightarrow \\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{2t} \\\\ 7t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- Subtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n5t\\alpha_1 = e^{7t} - e^{2t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_1 = \\dfrac{1}{5t}\\bigl(e^{7t}-e^{2t}\\bigr).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the first equation by $7$ and subtracting twice the second one, we get\n\n$$\n5\\alpha_0 = 7e^{2t} - 2e^{7t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_0 = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\bigl(7e^{2t}-2e^{7t}\\bigr).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =2\\left(\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{7t}-{e}^{2t})\\right)t+\\frac{1}{5}(7{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{7t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((2{e}^{7t}-2{e}^{2t})+(7{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{7t})) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t} \\\\ -7{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-7\\left(\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{7t}-{e}^{2t})\\right)t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{5}({e}^{7t}-{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}({e}^{2t}-{e}^{7t}) \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =7\\left(\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{7t}-{e}^{2t})\\right)t+\\frac{1}{5}(7{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{7t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((7{e}^{7t}-7{e}^{2t})+(7{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{7t})) \\\\  & ={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ne^{At} = \n\\dfrac{1}{5} \\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 7({e}^{2t}-{e}^{7t}) & 5{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186222485 :tutorial/content "Where do the predictor and corrector equations of the two-step Adams–Bashforth–Moulton method come from?\n\nThe method consists of two parts: the **Adams-Bashforth** (**AB2**) **predictor**, and **Adams-Moulton** (**AM2**) **corrector**.\nWe'll begin by deriving the AB2 predictor.\n\nConsider an initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a)=c\n$$\n\nWriting the differential equation in integral form, we have\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\int_{x}^{x_\\text{new}} f(t, y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\Delta y = \\int_{x}^{x_\\text{new}} f(t, y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet $\\Delta x = x_\\text{new} - x$ be the step size, and define the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{t - x}{\\Delta x}.\n$$\n\nThen, $t = x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x$ and $\\mathrm{d}t = \\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x,$ so the integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\int_0^1 f(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x))\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x.\n$$\n\nNow, define a new function $g$ of $\\theta$ as the integrand:\n\n$$\ng(\\theta) = f(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x))\n$$\n\nThe Adams formulas result from approximating $g(\\theta)$ by a simple function that agrees with its known values, and then integrating that approximation from $0$ to $1.$ We now determine some known values.\n\n- Evaluating $g$ at $\\theta = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0) & =f(x+0\\cdot Δx,y(x+0\\cdot Δx)) \\\\  & =f(x,y) \\\\  & ={y}^{'}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, evaluating $g$ at $\\theta=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-1) & =f(x+(-1)\\cdot Δx,y(x+(-1)\\cdot Δx)) \\\\  & =f(x-Δx,y(x-Δx)) \\\\  & =f({x}_{-1},y({x}_{-1})) \\\\  & ={y}_{-1}^{'},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_{-1}$ is the previous $x$-value and $y'_{-1}$ is the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y(x_{-1})).$\n\nSince we have two known values, the simplest reasonable approximation to $g(\\theta)$ is a *straight line* passing through these two points: $(0,y')$ and $(-1,y'_{-1}).$\nThis straight line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\theta ) & ={y}^{'}+\\frac{{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'}}{0-(-1)}(\\theta -0) \\\\  & ={y}^{'}+({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by integrating this approximation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}p(\\theta )\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{'}+({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\theta )\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={({y}^{'}\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'}){\\theta }^{2})|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,0}^{\\theta \\,=\\,1} \\\\  & ={y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximation of the increment in $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy={\\int }_{0}^{1}g(\\theta )\\,d\\theta \\cdot Δx\\approx {\\int }_{0}^{1}p(\\theta )\\,d\\theta \\cdot Δx=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is this approximation that we define as the AB2 predictor:\n\n$$\n\\Delta y_p = \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1})\\cdot\\Delta x.\n$$\n\nThe Adams formulae result from approximating $g(\\theta)$ by a polynomial interpolant constructed from known function values and integrating that polynomial from $0$ to $1.$\n\nNotice that evaluating $g$ at $\\theta = 0,$ we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(0) &= f(x + 0\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + 0\\cdot\\Delta x)) \\\\[5pt]\n&= f(x, y) \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, evaluating $g$ at $\\theta=-1$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(-1)\n&= f(x + (-1)\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + (-1)\\cdot\\Delta x)) \\\\[5pt]\n&= f(x - \\Delta x, y(x - \\Delta x)) \\\\[5pt]\n&= f(x_{-1}, y(x_{-1})) \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'_{-1},\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y'_{-1}$ is the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y(x_{-1})).$\nSo, to derive the AB2 predictor, we can approximate $g(\\theta)$ by a *linear* interpolant using its values at $\\theta=0$ and $\\theta=-1.$\n\nThe Lagrange basis polynomials are\n\\[\n\\ell_0(\\theta) = \\theta + 1,\\qquad\n\\ell_{-1}(\\theta) = -\\theta,\n\\]\n\nso the interpolant is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(\\theta) &= g(0)\\ell_0(\\theta) - g(-1)\\ell_{-1}(\\theta) \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\cdot(\\theta + 1) - y'_{-1}\\cdot\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by integrating the interpolant, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_0^1 p(\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\n&= \\int_0^1 \\big(y'\\cdot(\\theta + 1) - y'_{-1}\\cdot\\theta\\big) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\int_0^1 (\\theta + 1) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta - y'_{-1} \\int_0^1 \\theta \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\left[\\dfrac12\\theta^2 + \\theta\\right]_0^1 - y'_{-1}\\left[\\dfrac12\\theta^2\\right]_0^1 \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\left(\\dfrac12 + 1 - 0\\right) - y'_{-1}\\left(\\dfrac12 - 0\\right) \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac32y' - \\dfrac12y'_{-1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the AB2 predictor is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta y \\approx \\Delta y_p &= \\int_0^1 p(\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\cdot \\Delta x \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\left(\\dfrac32y' - \\dfrac12y'_{-1}\\right)\\cdot\\Delta x \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1})\\cdot\\Delta x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Where do the predictor and corrector equations of the two-step Adams–Bashforth–Moulton method come from?\n\nThe method consists of two parts: the **Adams-Bashforth** (**AB2**) **predictor**, and **Adams-Moulton** (**AM2**) **corrector**.\nWe'll begin by deriving the AB2 predictor.\n\nConsider an initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a)=c\n$$\n\nWriting the differential equation in integral form, we have\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\int_{x}^{x_\\text{new}} f(t, y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\Delta y = \\int_{x}^{x_\\text{new}} f(t, y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet $\\Delta x = x_\\text{new} - x$ be the step size, and define the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{t - x}{\\Delta x}.\n$$\n\nThen, $t = x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x$ and $\\mathrm{d}t = \\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x,$ so the integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\int_0^1 f(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x))\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x.\n$$\n\nNow, define a new function $g$ of $\\theta$ as the integrand:\n\n$$\ng(\\theta) = f(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x))\n$$\n\nThe Adams formulas result from approximating $g(\\theta)$ by a simple function that agrees with its known values, and then integrating that approximation from $0$ to $1.$ We now determine some known values.\n\n- Evaluating $g$ at $\\theta = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0) & =f(x+0\\cdot Δx,y(x+0\\cdot Δx)) \\\\  & =f(x,y) \\\\  & ={y}^{'}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, evaluating $g$ at $\\theta=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-1) & =f(x+(-1)\\cdot Δx,y(x+(-1)\\cdot Δx)) \\\\  & =f(x-Δx,y(x-Δx)) \\\\  & =f({x}_{-1},y({x}_{-1})) \\\\  & ={y}_{-1}^{'},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_{-1}$ is the previous $x$-value and $y'_{-1}$ is the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y(x_{-1})).$\n\nSince we have two known values, the simplest reasonable approximation to $g(\\theta)$ is a *straight line* passing through these two points: $(0,y')$ and $(-1,y'_{-1}).$\nThis straight line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\theta ) & ={y}^{'}+\\frac{{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'}}{0-(-1)}(\\theta -0) \\\\  & ={y}^{'}+({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by integrating this approximation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}p(\\theta )\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{'}+({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\theta )\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\left({y}^{'}\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'}){\\theta }^{2}\\right)|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,0}^{\\theta \\,=\\,1} \\\\  & ={y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximation of the increment in $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy={\\int }_{0}^{1}g(\\theta )\\,d\\theta \\cdot Δx\\approx {\\int }_{0}^{1}p(\\theta )\\,d\\theta \\cdot Δx=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is this approximation that we define as the AB2 predictor:\n\n$$\n\\Delta y_p = \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1})\\cdot\\Delta x.\n$$\n\nThe Adams formulae result from approximating $g(\\theta)$ by a polynomial interpolant constructed from known function values and integrating that polynomial from $0$ to $1.$\n\nNotice that evaluating $g$ at $\\theta = 0,$ we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(0) &= f(x + 0\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + 0\\cdot\\Delta x)) \\\\[5pt]\n&= f(x, y) \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, evaluating $g$ at $\\theta=-1$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(-1)\n&= f(x + (-1)\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + (-1)\\cdot\\Delta x)) \\\\[5pt]\n&= f(x - \\Delta x, y(x - \\Delta x)) \\\\[5pt]\n&= f(x_{-1}, y(x_{-1})) \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'_{-1},\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y'_{-1}$ is the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y(x_{-1})).$\nSo, to derive the AB2 predictor, we can approximate $g(\\theta)$ by a *linear* interpolant using its values at $\\theta=0$ and $\\theta=-1.$\n\nThe Lagrange basis polynomials are\n\\[\n\\ell_0(\\theta) = \\theta + 1,\\qquad\n\\ell_{-1}(\\theta) = -\\theta,\n\\]\n\nso the interpolant is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(\\theta) &= g(0)\\ell_0(\\theta) - g(-1)\\ell_{-1}(\\theta) \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\cdot(\\theta + 1) - y'_{-1}\\cdot\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by integrating the interpolant, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_0^1 p(\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\n&= \\int_0^1 \\big(y'\\cdot(\\theta + 1) - y'_{-1}\\cdot\\theta\\big) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\int_0^1 (\\theta + 1) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta - y'_{-1} \\int_0^1 \\theta \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\left[\\dfrac12\\theta^2 + \\theta\\right]_0^1 - y'_{-1}\\left[\\dfrac12\\theta^2\\right]_0^1 \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\left(\\dfrac12 + 1 - 0\\right) - y'_{-1}\\left(\\dfrac12 - 0\\right) \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac32y' - \\dfrac12y'_{-1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the AB2 predictor is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta y \\approx \\Delta y_p &= \\int_0^1 p(\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\cdot \\Delta x \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\left(\\dfrac32y' - \\dfrac12y'_{-1}\\right)\\cdot\\Delta x \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1})\\cdot\\Delta x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186222486 :tutorial/content "So far, we have derived the AB2 predictor. We are left to derive the AM2 corrector.\n\nRecall that we wrote the differential equation in integral form as\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\int_x^{x_\\text{new}} f(t,y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\Delta y = \\int_x^{x_\\text{new}} f(t,y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWith the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{t-x}{\\Delta x},\n$$\n\nwe had $t = x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x$ and $\\mathrm{d}t = \\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x,$ so\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\int_0^1 f(x+\\theta\\Delta x,y(x+\\theta\\Delta x))\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nand we defined\n\n$$\ng(\\theta) = f(x+\\theta\\Delta x,y(x+\\theta\\Delta x)).\n$$\n\nFor the predictor, we considered two known values of $g(\\theta){:}$ \n\n- For $\\theta = 0,$ we have $g(0) = y'.$\n\n- For $\\theta = -1,$ we have $g(-1) = y'_{-1}.$\n\nThen, we approximated $g$ with a linear function passing through these two points.\n\nNow, for the corrector, we use *three* known values, and approximate $g(\\theta)$ by a *quadratic* passing through these three points.\n\nTo obtain a third value, we also want the slope at the *new* $x$-value $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x.$ But, the true value $y_\\text{new}$ is not yet known.\n\nInstead, we evaluate the slope at the new point estimated by the AB2 predictor! This corresponds to $\\theta = 1{:}$\n\n$$\ng(1) = f(x + \\Delta x, y(x + \\Delta x)) \\approx f(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = y'_p,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_p$ is the new $y$-value predicted by AB2, and $y'_p$ is the slope evaluated at $(x_\\text{new}, y_p).$\nSo, our three points are\n\n$$\n(-1,y'_{-1}), \\quad (0,y'), \\quad (1,y'_p).\n$$\n\nLet $q(\\theta)$ be the quadratic that passes through these three points. After simplifying, this quadratic can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\theta (\\theta +1){y}_{p}^{'}-(\\theta -1)(\\theta +1){y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta (\\theta -1){y}_{-1}^{'} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({\\theta }^{2}+\\theta ){y}_{p}^{'}-({\\theta }^{2}-1){y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}({\\theta }^{2}-\\theta ){y}_{-1}^{'}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate this approximation from $0$ to $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}q(\\theta )\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}({\\theta }^{2}+\\theta ){y}_{p}^{'}-({\\theta }^{2}-1){y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}({\\theta }^{2}-\\theta ){y}_{-1}^{'})\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}){y}_{p}^{'}-(\\frac{1}{3}-1){y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}){y}_{-1}^{'}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}{y}_{p}^{'}+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{12}{y}_{-1}^{'} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximation of the increment in $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\int_0^1 g(\\theta) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x\n\\approx \\int_0^1 q(\\theta) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x\n= \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1})\\cdot\\Delta x.\n$$\n\nIt is this approximation that we use as the AM2 corrector. Putting it together, the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$" "So far, we have derived the AB2 predictor. We are left to derive the AM2 corrector.\n\nRecall that we wrote the differential equation in integral form as\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\int_x^{x_\\text{new}} f(t,y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\Delta y = \\int_x^{x_\\text{new}} f(t,y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWith the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{t-x}{\\Delta x},\n$$\n\nwe had $t = x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x$ and $\\mathrm{d}t = \\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x,$ so\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\int_0^1 f(x+\\theta\\Delta x,y(x+\\theta\\Delta x))\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nand we defined\n\n$$\ng(\\theta) = f(x+\\theta\\Delta x,y(x+\\theta\\Delta x)).\n$$\n\nFor the predictor, we considered two known values of $g(\\theta){:}$ \n\n- For $\\theta = 0,$ we have $g(0) = y'.$\n\n- For $\\theta = -1,$ we have $g(-1) = y'_{-1}.$\n\nThen, we approximated $g$ with a linear function passing through these two points.\n\nNow, for the corrector, we use *three* known values, and approximate $g(\\theta)$ by a *quadratic* passing through these three points.\n\nTo obtain a third value, we also want the slope at the *new* $x$-value $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x.$ But, the true value $y_\\text{new}$ is not yet known.\n\nInstead, we evaluate the slope at the new point estimated by the AB2 predictor! This corresponds to $\\theta = 1{:}$\n\n$$\ng(1) = f(x + \\Delta x, y(x + \\Delta x)) \\approx f(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = y'_p,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_p$ is the new $y$-value predicted by AB2, and $y'_p$ is the slope evaluated at $(x_\\text{new}, y_p).$\nSo, our three points are\n\n$$\n(-1,y'_{-1}), \\quad (0,y'), \\quad (1,y'_p).\n$$\n\nLet $q(\\theta)$ be the quadratic that passes through these three points. After simplifying, this quadratic can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\theta (\\theta +1){y}_{p}^{'}-(\\theta -1)(\\theta +1){y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta (\\theta -1){y}_{-1}^{'} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({\\theta }^{2}+\\theta ){y}_{p}^{'}-({\\theta }^{2}-1){y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}({\\theta }^{2}-\\theta ){y}_{-1}^{'}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate this approximation from $0$ to $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}q(\\theta )\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}({\\theta }^{2}+\\theta ){y}_{p}^{'}-({\\theta }^{2}-1){y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}({\\theta }^{2}-\\theta ){y}_{-1}^{'}\\right)\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\right){y}_{p}^{'}-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}-1\\right){y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){y}_{-1}^{'}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}{y}_{p}^{'}+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{12}{y}_{-1}^{'} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximation of the increment in $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\int_0^1 g(\\theta) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x\n\\approx \\int_0^1 q(\\theta) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x\n= \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1})\\cdot\\Delta x.\n$$\n\nIt is this approximation that we use as the AM2 corrector. Putting it together, the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186222510 :tutorial/content "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x - 2y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nLet's begin the process of applying the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method to approximate the value of $y(1).$\n\nWe know $y(0)$ and wish to estimate $y(1).$ Since the method takes $2$ steps (one to initialize, one to approximate), we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1-0}2 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nWe will use a table to keep track of known values. First, we add the initial condition data:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we initialize the ABM $2$-step method by making a preliminary step with a one-step method.\n\nApplying the RK4 method with step size $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12,$ we obtain the slope $y'=-2,$ increment $\\Delta y = -\\dfrac14,$ and approximation\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)\\approx 1 + \\left(-\\dfrac14\\right) = \\dfrac34.\n$$\n\nWe add the slope to the table, and the approximation in a new row.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNote that, since RK4 is not a predictor-corrector method, there is no increment $\\Delta y_p$ or predictor $y_p$ for this step. So, we leave these entries in the first row blank.\n\nNow, we continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =4x-2y \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 1\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -2.$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-(-2))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{7}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{13}8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, our initial prediction for $y(1)$ is $y_p = \\dfrac{13}8.$" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x - 2y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nLet's begin the process of applying the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method to approximate the value of $y(1).$\n\nWe know $y(0)$ and wish to estimate $y(1).$ Since the method takes $2$ steps (one to initialize, one to approximate), we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1-0}2 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nWe will use a table to keep track of known values. First, we add the initial condition data:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we initialize the ABM $2$-step method by making a preliminary step with a one-step method.\n\nApplying the RK4 method with step size $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12,$ we obtain the slope $y'=-2,$ increment $\\Delta y = -\\dfrac14,$ and approximation\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)\\approx 1 + \\left(-\\dfrac14\\right) = \\dfrac34.\n$$\n\nWe add the slope to the table, and the approximation in a new row.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNote that, since RK4 is not a predictor-corrector method, there is no increment $\\Delta y_p$ or predictor $y_p$ for this step. So, we leave these entries in the first row blank.\n\nNow, we continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =4x-2y \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 1\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -2.$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-(-2)\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{7}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{13}8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, our initial prediction for $y(1)$ is $y_p = \\dfrac{13}8.$"] [:db/cas 17592186222514 :tutorial/content "When a function has period $2L$ (instead of $2\\pi$), the \"usual\" Fourier formulas still work, but the sine and cosine inputs must be rescaled so that each term completes an integer number of oscillations over one period. \n\nWe do this by converting the $2L$-periodic function into a $2\\pi$-periodic one using a change of variables.\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ from $x\\in[-L,L)$ to $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\nTo use the standard $2\\pi$-period Fourier formulas, define\n\n$$\ns=\\frac{\\pi}{L}x\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ng(s)=f\\!\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi}s\\right).\n$$\n\nThen $g$ is $2\\pi$-periodic.\n\nFor a $2\\pi$-periodic function $g$, the Fourier series is\n\n$$\ng(s)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\bigl(a_n\\cos(ns)+b_n\\sin(ns)\\bigr),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,ds, \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,ds,\\,n\\ge 1, \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,ds,\\,n\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $g(s)=f\\!\\left(\\dfrac{L}{\\pi}s\\right)$ and $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{L}x$.\nFirst, for $a_n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,ds \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\,(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,ds \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f\\,(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\cos \\,(n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,d\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\cos \\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\cos \\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCoefficients $b_n$ (for $n \\geq 1$) and $a_0$ can be found similarly.\n\nTo summarize, the Fourier series of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\frac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left(a_n\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the coefficients are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,dx \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\cos \\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\sin \\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,dx,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's see some concrete examples." "When a function has period $2L$ (instead of $2\\pi$), the \"usual\" Fourier formulas still work, but the sine and cosine inputs must be rescaled so that each term completes an integer number of oscillations over one period. \n\nWe do this by converting the $2L$-periodic function into a $2\\pi$-periodic one using a change of variables.\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ from $x\\in[-L,L)$ to $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\nTo use the standard $2\\pi$-period Fourier formulas, define\n\n$$\ns=\\frac{\\pi}{L}x\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ng(s)=f\\!\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi}s\\right).\n$$\n\nThen $g$ is $2\\pi$-periodic.\n\nFor a $2\\pi$-periodic function $g$, the Fourier series is\n\n$$\ng(s)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\bigl(a_n\\cos(ns)+b_n\\sin(ns)\\bigr),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,ds, \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,ds,\\,n\\ge 1, \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,ds,\\,n\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $g(s)=f\\!\\left(\\dfrac{L}{\\pi}s\\right)$ and $s=\\dfrac{\\pi}{L}x$.\nFirst, for $a_n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,ds \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\,\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,ds \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f\\,\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\cos \\,\\left(n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,d\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCoefficients $b_n$ (for $n \\geq 1$) and $a_0$ can be found similarly.\n\nTo summarize, the Fourier series of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\frac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left(a_n\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the coefficients are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,dx \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,dx,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,dx,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's see some concrete examples."] [:db/cas 17592186222518 :tutorial/content "Let's use the smoothness property to find the Laplace transform of $\\mathcal L\\{t e^{-9t}\\}.$\n\nIf a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s > a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\geq 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{ t^n f(t) \\} = (-1)^n F^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{ tf(t) \\} = -F'(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t) = e^{-9t}.$ Therefore, for $s > -9,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s+9}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{1}{s+9}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}((s+9)^{-1}) \\\\  & =-(s+9)^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(s+9)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t{e}^{-9t}} & =-{F}^{'}(s) \\\\  & =-[-\\frac{1}{(s+9)^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(s+9)^{2}},\\,s>-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's use the smoothness property to find the Laplace transform of $\\mathcal L\\{t e^{-9t}\\}.$\n\nIf a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s > a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\geq 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{ t^n f(t) \\} = (-1)^n F^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{ tf(t) \\} = -F'(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t) = e^{-9t}.$ Therefore, for $s > -9,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s+9}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{1}{s+9}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}((s+9)^{-1}) \\\\  & =-(s+9)^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(s+9)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t{e}^{-9t}} & =-{F}^{'}(s) \\\\  & =-\\left[-\\frac{1}{(s+9)^{2}}\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(s+9)^{2}},\\,s>-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186223135 :answer/value "18x^{5}(x + \\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{1}{2})" "18x^{5}\\left(x + \\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186224190 :answer/value "A(x) = x(\\frac{x}{2} - 1)" "A(x) = x\\left(\\frac{x}{2} - 1\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186224191 :answer/value "A(x) = (x - 1)(\\frac{x}{2} - 1)" "A(x) = (x - 1)\\left(\\frac{x}{2} - 1\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186224864 :question/worked-solution "We begin with the expression $60x^2 + 180x$ and compare it to the form $rx(3x + 9)$.\n\nFirst, we factor $x$ from both terms in the original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n60x^{2} + 180x &= x(60x + 180)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor out the greatest common factor from the binomial $60x + 180$. The GCF of $60$ and $180$ is $60$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(60x + 180) &= x(60)(x + 3) \\\\\n&= 60x(x + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told the expression is written as $rx(3x + 9)$. Notice that\n\n$$\n3x + 9 = 3(x + 3)\n$$\n\nso we rewrite the expression as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n60x(x + 3) &= (3 \\cdot \\frac{60}{3})x(x + 3) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{60}{3}\\right) \\cdot x \\cdot 3(x + 3) \\\\\n&= 20x(3x + 9)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the value of $r$ is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{20}$." "We begin with the expression $60x^2 + 180x$ and compare it to the form $rx(3x + 9)$.\n\nFirst, we factor $x$ from both terms in the original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n60x^{2} + 180x &= x(60x + 180)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor out the greatest common factor from the binomial $60x + 180$. The GCF of $60$ and $180$ is $60$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(60x + 180) &= x(60)(x + 3) \\\\\n&= 60x(x + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told the expression is written as $rx(3x + 9)$. Notice that\n\n$$\n3x + 9 = 3(x + 3)\n$$\n\nso we rewrite the expression as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n60x(x + 3) &= \\left(3 \\cdot \\frac{60}{3}\\right)x(x + 3) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{60}{3}\\right) \\cdot x \\cdot 3(x + 3) \\\\\n&= 20x(3x + 9)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the value of $r$ is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{20}$."] [:db/cas 17592186224880 :question/worked-solution "All terms have the same square root in the denominator. So, multiplying through by $\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}$, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {r}^{2}}{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}} \\cdot  \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} & \\displaystyle  = ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {k}^{2}}{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}} + 4 ) \\cdot  \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {r}^{2}}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {k}^{2}}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}} + 4 \\cdot  \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {r}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = {k}^{2} + 4 \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $k^2$ from both sides:\n\n$$\nr^2 - k^2 = 4 \\sqrt{r^2 - k^2}\n$$\n\nNow, let\n\n$$\nu = \\sqrt{r^2 - k^2}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting\n\n$$\nu^2= r^2 - k^2\n$$\n\ninto the equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{2} &= 4u \\\\\nu^{2} - 4u &= 0 \\\\\nu(u - 4) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, the possible values of $u$ are\n\n$$\nu = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad u = 4\n$$\n\nNow, we find the corresponding potential solutions for $r$ and verify if they are valid solutions.\n\n- If $u=0$, then we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}-{k}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={k}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\pm k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHowever, plugging $r = k$ or $r = -k$ into the original equation makes the denominator zero. Hence, $u=0$, or equivalently, $r=\\pm k$, is *not* a valid solution.\n- If $u=4$, then we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}-{k}^{2}} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{k}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={k}^{2}+16 \\\\ r & =\\pm \\sqrt{{k}^{2}+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nPlugging this into the original equation, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{k}^{2}+16}{\\sqrt{{k}^{2}+16-{k}^{2}}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{{k}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{k}^{2}+16-{k}^{2}}}+4 \\\\ \\frac{{k}^{2}+16}{\\sqrt{16}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{{k}^{2}}{\\sqrt{16}}+4 \\\\ \\frac{{k}^{2}+16}{4} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{{k}^{2}}{4}+4 \\\\ \\frac{{k}^{2}}{4}+4 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{{k}^{2}}{4}+4.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, $u=4$, or equivalently, $r=\\pm \\sqrt{k^2 + 16}$, *is* a valid solution." "All terms have the same square root in the denominator. So, multiplying through by $\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}$, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {r}^{2}}{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}} \\cdot  \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} & \\displaystyle  = \\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {k}^{2}}{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}} + 4 \\right) \\cdot  \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {r}^{2}}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {k}^{2}}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}} + 4 \\cdot  \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {r}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = {k}^{2} + 4 \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $k^2$ from both sides:\n\n$$\nr^2 - k^2 = 4 \\sqrt{r^2 - k^2}\n$$\n\nNow, let\n\n$$\nu = \\sqrt{r^2 - k^2}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting\n\n$$\nu^2= r^2 - k^2\n$$\n\ninto the equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{2} &= 4u \\\\\nu^{2} - 4u &= 0 \\\\\nu(u - 4) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, the possible values of $u$ are\n\n$$\nu = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad u = 4\n$$\n\nNow, we find the corresponding potential solutions for $r$ and verify if they are valid solutions.\n\n- If $u=0$, then we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}-{k}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={k}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\pm k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHowever, plugging $r = k$ or $r = -k$ into the original equation makes the denominator zero. Hence, $u=0$, or equivalently, $r=\\pm k$, is *not* a valid solution.\n- If $u=4$, then we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}-{k}^{2}} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{k}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={k}^{2}+16 \\\\ r & =\\pm \\sqrt{{k}^{2}+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nPlugging this into the original equation, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{k}^{2}+16}{\\sqrt{{k}^{2}+16-{k}^{2}}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{{k}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{k}^{2}+16-{k}^{2}}}+4 \\\\ \\frac{{k}^{2}+16}{\\sqrt{16}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{{k}^{2}}{\\sqrt{16}}+4 \\\\ \\frac{{k}^{2}+16}{4} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{{k}^{2}}{4}+4 \\\\ \\frac{{k}^{2}}{4}+4 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{{k}^{2}}{4}+4.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, $u=4$, or equivalently, $r=\\pm \\sqrt{k^2 + 16}$, *is* a valid solution."] [:db/cas 17592186224883 :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the denominators where possible. Note that $n^{2} + 5n + 6$ factors as $(n + 2)(n + 3)$.\n\nSo, we rewrite the expression as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{n^{2}}{(n + 2)(n + 3)} - \\dfrac{2n}{n + 2} = -\\,\\dfrac{9}{n + 3}\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n$\\textrm{LCM}\\big(n+2,\\: n+3,\\: (n+2)(n+3) \\big) = (n+2)(n+3)$.\n- The values $n=-2$ and $n=-3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {n}^{2}}{( n + 2 ) ( n + 3 )}} - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 n}{n + 2}} ) \\cdot  ( n + 2 ) ( n + 3 ) & \\displaystyle  = - \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{9}{n + 3}} \\cdot  ( n + 2 ) ( n + 3 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {n}^{2}}{( n + 2 ) ( n + 3 )}} \\cdot  ( n + 2 ) ( n + 3 ) \\hspace{0.278em} - \\hspace{0.278em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 n}{n + 2}} \\cdot  ( n + 2 ) ( n + 3 ) & \\displaystyle  = - \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{9}{n + 3}} \\cdot  ( n + 2 ) ( n + 3 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {n}^{2}}{\\cancel{( n + 2 )} \\cancel{( n + 3 )}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{( n + 2 )} \\cancel{( n + 3 )} \\hspace{0.278em} - \\hspace{0.278em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 n}{\\cancel{n + 2}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{( n + 2 )} ( n + 3 ) & \\displaystyle  = - \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{9}{\\cancel{n + 3}}} \\cdot  ( n + 2 ) \\cancel{( n + 3 )} \\\\ \\displaystyle {n}^{2} \\hspace{0.278em} - \\hspace{0.278em} 2 n ( n + 3 ) & \\displaystyle  = - 9 ( n + 2 ) .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNow, we expand both sides, move all terms to the left-hand side, and factor the result to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn^{2} - 2n^{2} - 6n &= -9n - 18 \\\\\n- n^{2} + 3n + 18 &= 0 \\\\\nn^{2} - 3n - 18 &= 0 \\\\\n(n - 6)(n + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, the possible solutions are $n = 6$ or $n = -3$.\n\nHowever, $n = -3$ is excluded because it makes the denominator zero. Therefore, the only valid solution is $n = 6$." "First, we factor the denominators where possible. Note that $n^{2} + 5n + 6$ factors as $(n + 2)(n + 3)$.\n\nSo, we rewrite the expression as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{n^{2}}{(n + 2)(n + 3)} - \\dfrac{2n}{n + 2} = -\\,\\dfrac{9}{n + 3}\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n$\\textrm{LCM}\\big(n+2,\\: n+3,\\: (n+2)(n+3) \\big) = (n+2)(n+3)$.\n- The values $n=-2$ and $n=-3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {n}^{2}}{( n + 2 ) ( n + 3 )}} - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 n}{n + 2}} \\right) \\cdot  ( n + 2 ) ( n + 3 ) & \\displaystyle  = - \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{9}{n + 3}} \\cdot  ( n + 2 ) ( n + 3 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {n}^{2}}{( n + 2 ) ( n + 3 )}} \\cdot  ( n + 2 ) ( n + 3 ) \\hspace{0.278em} - \\hspace{0.278em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 n}{n + 2}} \\cdot  ( n + 2 ) ( n + 3 ) & \\displaystyle  = - \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{9}{n + 3}} \\cdot  ( n + 2 ) ( n + 3 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {n}^{2}}{\\cancel{( n + 2 )} \\cancel{( n + 3 )}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{( n + 2 )} \\cancel{( n + 3 )} \\hspace{0.278em} - \\hspace{0.278em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 n}{\\cancel{n + 2}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{( n + 2 )} ( n + 3 ) & \\displaystyle  = - \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{9}{\\cancel{n + 3}}} \\cdot  ( n + 2 ) \\cancel{( n + 3 )} \\\\ \\displaystyle {n}^{2} \\hspace{0.278em} - \\hspace{0.278em} 2 n ( n + 3 ) & \\displaystyle  = - 9 ( n + 2 ) .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNow, we expand both sides, move all terms to the left-hand side, and factor the result to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn^{2} - 2n^{2} - 6n &= -9n - 18 \\\\\n- n^{2} + 3n + 18 &= 0 \\\\\nn^{2} - 3n - 18 &= 0 \\\\\n(n - 6)(n + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, the possible solutions are $n = 6$ or $n = -3$.\n\nHowever, $n = -3$ is excluded because it makes the denominator zero. Therefore, the only valid solution is $n = 6$."] [:db/cas 17592186224953 :question/worked-solution "To apply a transformation to the graph of a function, we modify its equation according to the direction of the shift:\n\n- A horizontal shift left by $3$ units means we replace every $x$ in the original function with $(x + 3)$.\n- A vertical shift up by $2$ units means we add $2$ to the entire function.\n\nWe start with the function:\n\n$$\nr(x) = \\dfrac{a - 5}{x} + 7\n$$\n\nTo shift left $3$ units, we replace $x$ with $x + 3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a - 5}{x + 3} + 7\n$$\n\nThen, to shift up $2$ units, we add $2$ to the whole expression:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{a - 5}{x + 3} + 7\\right) + 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, by simplifying, we find the equation that defines $s$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns(x) &= (\\frac{a - 5}{x + 3} + 7) + 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{a - 5}{x + 3} + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To apply a transformation to the graph of a function, we modify its equation according to the direction of the shift:\n\n- A horizontal shift left by $3$ units means we replace every $x$ in the original function with $(x + 3)$.\n- A vertical shift up by $2$ units means we add $2$ to the entire function.\n\nWe start with the function:\n\n$$\nr(x) = \\dfrac{a - 5}{x} + 7\n$$\n\nTo shift left $3$ units, we replace $x$ with $x + 3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a - 5}{x + 3} + 7\n$$\n\nThen, to shift up $2$ units, we add $2$ to the whole expression:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{a - 5}{x + 3} + 7\\right) + 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, by simplifying, we find the equation that defines $s$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns(x) &= \\left(\\frac{a - 5}{x + 3} + 7\\right) + 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{a - 5}{x + 3} + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186224973 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n- $m$ be the side length of cube $M$, and\n- $n$ be the side length of cube $N$.\n\nThen,\n\n- the surface area of cube $M$ is $6m^2$, and\n- the surface area of cube $N$ is $6n^2$.\n\nWe are told that the surface area of cube $N$ is $18$ square inches greater than one half of the surface area of cube $M$. In symbols, this means that\n\n$$\n6n^2 = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 6m^2 + 18\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the obtained equation for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6n^{2} &= 3m^{2} + 18 \\\\\n\\frac{6n^{2}}{6} &= \\frac{3m^{2} + 18}{6} \\\\\nn^{2} &= \\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3 \\\\\nn &= \\sqrt{\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solutions since the side length must be positive.\n\nTherefore, the function $V$ that represents the volume of cube $N$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(m) &= n^{3} \\\\\n&= (\\sqrt{\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3})^{3} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3)^{1/2})^{3} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3)^{3})^{1/2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3)^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let\n\n- $m$ be the side length of cube $M$, and\n- $n$ be the side length of cube $N$.\n\nThen,\n\n- the surface area of cube $M$ is $6m^2$, and\n- the surface area of cube $N$ is $6n^2$.\n\nWe are told that the surface area of cube $N$ is $18$ square inches greater than one half of the surface area of cube $M$. In symbols, this means that\n\n$$\n6n^2 = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 6m^2 + 18\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the obtained equation for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6n^{2} &= 3m^{2} + 18 \\\\\n\\frac{6n^{2}}{6} &= \\frac{3m^{2} + 18}{6} \\\\\nn^{2} &= \\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3 \\\\\nn &= \\sqrt{\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solutions since the side length must be positive.\n\nTherefore, the function $V$ that represents the volume of cube $N$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(m) &= n^{3} \\\\\n&= \\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3}\\right)^{3} \\\\\n&= \\left(\\left(\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3\\right)^{1/2}\\right)^{3} \\\\\n&= \\left(\\left(\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3\\right)^{3}\\right)^{1/2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{m^{2}}{2} + 3\\right)^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186225020 :question/worked-solution "The line $y=4x - k$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny=-5x^2 + 10x - 1\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n-5x^2 + 10x - 1 = 4x - k\n$$\n\nWe need to determine the value of $k$ such that this equation has one real solution.\n\nA quadratic equation has exactly one real root if the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x^{2} + 10x - 1 &= 4x - k \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 10x - 1 - 4x + k &= 0 \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 6x + (k - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = -5, \\qquad b = 6, \\qquad c = k-1\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= 6^{2} - 4(-5)(k - 1) \\\\\n&= 36 + 20(k - 1) \\\\\n&= 36 + 20k - 20 \\\\\n&= 16 + 20k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have exactly one real solution, we need\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16 + 20k &= 0 \\\\\n20k &= -16 \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{16}{20} \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{4}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny=4x-\\left(-\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)=4x+\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nTo find the $x$-coordinate, we solve the original quadratic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x^{2} + 6x + (-\\frac{4}{5} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 6x - \\frac{9}{5} &= 0 \\\\\n- 25x^{2} + 30x - 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- (25x^{2} - 30x + 9) &= 0 \\\\\n- (5x - 3)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n5x - 3 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the $y$-coordinate by substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\ninto one of the two equations and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 4\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) + \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{5} + \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is\n\n$$\n\\Big(\\dfrac{3}{5},\\dfrac{16}{5}\\Big)\n$$" "The line $y=4x - k$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny=-5x^2 + 10x - 1\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n-5x^2 + 10x - 1 = 4x - k\n$$\n\nWe need to determine the value of $k$ such that this equation has one real solution.\n\nA quadratic equation has exactly one real root if the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x^{2} + 10x - 1 &= 4x - k \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 10x - 1 - 4x + k &= 0 \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 6x + (k - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = -5, \\qquad b = 6, \\qquad c = k-1\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= 6^{2} - 4(-5)(k - 1) \\\\\n&= 36 + 20(k - 1) \\\\\n&= 36 + 20k - 20 \\\\\n&= 16 + 20k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have exactly one real solution, we need\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16 + 20k &= 0 \\\\\n20k &= -16 \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{16}{20} \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{4}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny=4x-\\left(-\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)=4x+\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nTo find the $x$-coordinate, we solve the original quadratic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x^{2} + 6x + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5} - 1\\right) &= 0 \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 6x - \\frac{9}{5} &= 0 \\\\\n- 25x^{2} + 30x - 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- (25x^{2} - 30x + 9) &= 0 \\\\\n- (5x - 3)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n5x - 3 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the $y$-coordinate by substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\ninto one of the two equations and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 4\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) + \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{5} + \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is\n\n$$\n\\Big(\\dfrac{3}{5},\\dfrac{16}{5}\\Big)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186225063 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}5 x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{11}} = \\sqrt{3} \\\\ y = - 2 {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{11}} - 5 x )}^{2}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x,y)$. What is the value of $y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}5 x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{11}} = \\sqrt{3} \\\\ y = - 2 {\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{11}} - 5 x \\right)}^{2}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x,y)$. What is the value of $y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186225063 :question/worked-solution "From the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n5x-\\dfrac{7}{11}=\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply this equation by $-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{11} - 5x = -\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nand the expression on the left-hand side features in the second equation.\n\nSo next, we substitute $-\\sqrt{3}$ for\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{7}{11}-5x\\right)\n$$\n\nin the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -2(\\frac{7}{11} - 5x)^{2} \\\\\n&=-2(-\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\\n&=-2 \\cdot 3 \\\\\ny &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $y$ is $-6$." "From the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n5x-\\dfrac{7}{11}=\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply this equation by $-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{11} - 5x = -\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nand the expression on the left-hand side features in the second equation.\n\nSo next, we substitute $-\\sqrt{3}$ for\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{7}{11}-5x\\right)\n$$\n\nin the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -2\\left(\\frac{7}{11} - 5x\\right)^{2} \\\\\n&=-2(-\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\\n&=-2 \\cdot 3 \\\\\ny &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $y$ is $-6$."] [:db/cas 17592186225069 :question/worked-solution "The temperature $T(t)$ at time $t$ is modeled by the equation\n\n$$\nT(t) = a \\cdot b^t + T_r\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $T_r = 65^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$ is the room (ambient) temperature,\n- $a$ and $b$ are constants to be determined, and\n- $t$ is the time, in minutes.\n\nAt $t=0$, the temperature is $T(0) = 200$. Therefore, we can determine the value of $a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(0) &= 200 \\\\\na \\cdot b^{0} + 65 &= 200 \\\\\na \\cdot 1 &= 135 \\\\\na &= 135\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our model becomes\n\n$$\nT(t) = 135 \\cdot b^t + 65\n$$\n\nAt $t=5$, the temperature is $T(5) = 170$. Hence, we can determine the value of $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(5) &= 170 \\\\\n135 \\cdot b^{5} + 65 &= 170 \\\\\n135 \\cdot b^{5} &= 105 \\\\\nb^{5} &= \\frac{105}{135} \\\\\nb^{5} &= \\frac{7}{9} \\\\\nb &= \\left(\\frac{7}{9}\\right)^{1/5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function best models the temperature $T(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(t) &= 135 \\cdot (\\left(\\frac{7}{9}\\right)^{1/5})^{t} + 65 \\\\\n&= 135 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{7}{9}\\right)^{t/5} + 65\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The temperature $T(t)$ at time $t$ is modeled by the equation\n\n$$\nT(t) = a \\cdot b^t + T_r\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $T_r = 65^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$ is the room (ambient) temperature,\n- $a$ and $b$ are constants to be determined, and\n- $t$ is the time, in minutes.\n\nAt $t=0$, the temperature is $T(0) = 200$. Therefore, we can determine the value of $a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(0) &= 200 \\\\\na \\cdot b^{0} + 65 &= 200 \\\\\na \\cdot 1 &= 135 \\\\\na &= 135\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our model becomes\n\n$$\nT(t) = 135 \\cdot b^t + 65\n$$\n\nAt $t=5$, the temperature is $T(5) = 170$. Hence, we can determine the value of $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(5) &= 170 \\\\\n135 \\cdot b^{5} + 65 &= 170 \\\\\n135 \\cdot b^{5} &= 105 \\\\\nb^{5} &= \\frac{105}{135} \\\\\nb^{5} &= \\frac{7}{9} \\\\\nb &= \\left(\\frac{7}{9}\\right)^{1/5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function best models the temperature $T(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(t) &= 135 \\cdot \\left(\\left(\\frac{7}{9}\\right)^{1/5}\\right)^{t} + 65 \\\\\n&= 135 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{7}{9}\\right)^{t/5} + 65\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186225086 :question/worked-solution "Since our equivalent expression has a constant factor $5$, we start by factoring $5$ out of our original expression:\n\n$$\n5a^2 - \\dfrac{4}{9}b^2 = 5\\left(a^2 - \\dfrac{4}{45}b^2\\right)\n$$\n\nTo write our bracketed expression as a difference of squares, we must write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{45}\n$$\n\nas a square. Applying the exponent rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{45} = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{45}}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nHence, we apply our formula for factoring a difference of squares:\n\n$$\na^2-b^2=(a-b)(a+b)\n$$\n\nWith $a=a$ and\n\n$$\nb=\\dfrac{2b}{3\\sqrt{5}}\n$$\n\nthis yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(a^{2} - \\frac{4}{45}b^{2}) &= 5(a^{2} - \\left(\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\right)^{2}b^{2}) \\\\\n&= 5(a^{2} - \\left(\\frac{2b}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\right)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 5(a - \\frac{2b}{3\\sqrt{5}})(a + \\frac{2b}{3\\sqrt{5}}) \\\\\n&= 5(a - \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}b)(a + \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nc = \\dfrac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\n$$" "Since our equivalent expression has a constant factor $5$, we start by factoring $5$ out of our original expression:\n\n$$\n5a^2 - \\dfrac{4}{9}b^2 = 5\\left(a^2 - \\dfrac{4}{45}b^2\\right)\n$$\n\nTo write our bracketed expression as a difference of squares, we must write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{45}\n$$\n\nas a square. Applying the exponent rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{45} = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{45}}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nHence, we apply our formula for factoring a difference of squares:\n\n$$\na^2-b^2=(a-b)(a+b)\n$$\n\nWith $a=a$ and\n\n$$\nb=\\dfrac{2b}{3\\sqrt{5}}\n$$\n\nthis yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5\\left(a^{2} - \\frac{4}{45}b^{2}\\right) &= 5\\left(a^{2} - \\left(\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\right)^{2}b^{2}\\right) \\\\\n&= 5\\left(a^{2} - \\left(\\frac{2b}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\right)^{2}\\right) \\\\\n&= 5\\left(a - \\frac{2b}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\right)\\left(a + \\frac{2b}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\right) \\\\\n&= 5\\left(a - \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}b\\right)\\left(a + \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}b\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nc = \\dfrac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186225849 :question/problem "The function is defined by $f(x) =-a^{x} + b$, where $a$ and $b$ are positive constants. The graph of $y = f(x)$ in the $xy$-plane passes through the point $(0, \\frac{3}{2})$, and the product of $a$ and $b$ is $\\frac{35}{2}$. What is the value of $a$?" "The function is defined by $f(x) =-a^{x} + b$, where $a$ and $b$ are positive constants. The graph of $y = f(x)$ in the $xy$-plane passes through the point $\\left(0, \\frac{3}{2}\\right)$, and the product of $a$ and $b$ is $\\frac{35}{2}$. What is the value of $a$?"] [:db/cas 17592186225850 :question/problem "The function is defined by $f(x) = 3 \\cdot a^{x} + b$, where $a$ and $b$ are constants. The graph of $y = f(x)$ in the $xy$-plane passes through the point $(0, \\frac{1}{2})$, and the product of $a$ and $b$ is $-\\frac{15}{2}$. What is the value of $a$?" "The function is defined by $f(x) = 3 \\cdot a^{x} + b$, where $a$ and $b$ are constants. The graph of $y = f(x)$ in the $xy$-plane passes through the point $\\left(0, \\frac{1}{2}\\right)$, and the product of $a$ and $b$ is $-\\frac{15}{2}$. What is the value of $a$?"] [:db/cas 17592186225899 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{8}} = \\sqrt{6} \\\\ y = - 2 {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{8}} - 4 x )}^{2}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x, y)$. What is the value of $y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{8}} = \\sqrt{6} \\\\ y = - 2 {\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{8}} - 4 x \\right)}^{2}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x, y)$. What is the value of $y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186226352 :answer/value "60x(\\frac{1}{y} + y^{2})" "60x\\left(\\frac{1}{y} + y^{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186228221 :answer/value "v(t) = \\frac{3}{2}\\tanh (\\frac{1}{2}gt + 0.618)" "v(t) = \\frac{3}{2}\\tanh \\left(\\frac{1}{2}gt + 0.618\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186228225 :answer/value "v(t) = 2\\tanh (\\frac{1}{2}gt + 0.973)" "v(t) = 2\\tanh \\left(\\frac{1}{2}gt + 0.973\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186228406 :answer/value "- (\\frac{x^{2}}{3} + \\frac{x}{12})e^{-x}" "- \\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{3} + \\frac{x}{12}\\right)e^{-x}"] [:db/cas 17592186228407 :answer/value "- (\\frac{x^{2}}{6} + \\frac{x}{9})e^{-x}" "- \\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{6} + \\frac{x}{9}\\right)e^{-x}"] [:db/cas 17592186228408 :answer/value "- (\\frac{x^{2}}{6} + \\frac{x}{12})e^{-x}" "- \\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{6} + \\frac{x}{12}\\right)e^{-x}"] [:db/cas 17592186228409 :answer/value "- (\\frac{x^{2}}{12} + \\frac{x}{3})e^{-x}" "- \\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{12} + \\frac{x}{3}\\right)e^{-x}"] [:db/cas 17592186228410 :answer/value "- (\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + \\frac{x}{3})e^{-x}" "- \\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + \\frac{x}{3}\\right)e^{-x}"] [:db/cas 17592186228756 :question/worked-solution "In order to convert this to a simple fraction, we must obtain a common denominator for our fractions in the denominator of the entire fraction. The common denominator is $(q + 1)(q - 4)$, as the terms are distinct linear factors.\n\nWe now multiply the entire fraction by $1$ in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(q + 1)(q - 4)}{(q + 1)(q - 4)}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n(1)/((\\frac{-2}{q + 1} + \\frac{5}{q - 4})) \\cdot ((q + 1)(q - 4))/((q + 1)(q - 4))\n$$\n\n- First, we simplify the numerator: $1\\cdot(q + 1)(q - 4) = q^2 - 3q - 4$\n- Then, we simplify the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{-2}{q + 1} + \\frac{5}{q - 4})(q + 1)(q - 4) &= (-2(q + 1)(q - 4))/(q + 1) + (5(q + 1)(q - 4))/(q - 4) \\\\\n&=-2(q - 4) + 5(q + 1) \\\\\n&=-2q + 8 + 5q + 5 \\\\\n&= 3q + 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the full simplified fraction is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{q^2 - 3q - 4}{3q + 13}\n$$" "In order to convert this to a simple fraction, we must obtain a common denominator for our fractions in the denominator of the entire fraction. The common denominator is $(q + 1)(q - 4)$, as the terms are distinct linear factors.\n\nWe now multiply the entire fraction by $1$ in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(q + 1)(q - 4)}{(q + 1)(q - 4)}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n(1)/\\left(\\left(\\frac{-2}{q + 1} + \\frac{5}{q - 4}\\right)\\right) \\cdot ((q + 1)(q - 4))/((q + 1)(q - 4))\n$$\n\n- First, we simplify the numerator: $1\\cdot(q + 1)(q - 4) = q^2 - 3q - 4$\n- Then, we simplify the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(\\frac{-2}{q + 1} + \\frac{5}{q - 4}\\right)(q + 1)(q - 4) &= (-2(q + 1)(q - 4))/(q + 1) + (5(q + 1)(q - 4))/(q - 4) \\\\\n&=-2(q - 4) + 5(q + 1) \\\\\n&=-2q + 8 + 5q + 5 \\\\\n&= 3q + 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the full simplified fraction is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{q^2 - 3q - 4}{3q + 13}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228757 :question/worked-solution "In order to convert this to a simple fraction, we must obtain a common denominator for our fractions in the denominator of the entire fraction. The common denominator is $r + 1$, as it is the only denominator of the terms in the denominator of the whole expression.\n\nWe now multiply the entire fraction by $1$ in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{r + 1}{r + 1}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n(1)/((\\frac{2}{r + 1} + r - 4)) \\cdot \\frac{r + 1}{r + 1}\n$$\n\n- First, we simplify the numerator: $1 \\cdot (r + 1) = r + 1$\n- Then, we simplify the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{2}{r + 1} + r - 4) \\cdot (r + 1) &= (2(r + 1))/(r + 1) + (r - 4)(r + 1) \\\\\n&= 2 + r^{2} + r - 4r - 4 \\\\\n&= r^{2} - 3r - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the full simplified fraction is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{r + 1}{r^2 - 3r - 2}\n$$" "In order to convert this to a simple fraction, we must obtain a common denominator for our fractions in the denominator of the entire fraction. The common denominator is $r + 1$, as it is the only denominator of the terms in the denominator of the whole expression.\n\nWe now multiply the entire fraction by $1$ in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{r + 1}{r + 1}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n(1)/\\left(\\left(\\frac{2}{r + 1} + r - 4\\right)\\right) \\cdot \\frac{r + 1}{r + 1}\n$$\n\n- First, we simplify the numerator: $1 \\cdot (r + 1) = r + 1$\n- Then, we simplify the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(\\frac{2}{r + 1} + r - 4\\right) \\cdot (r + 1) &= (2(r + 1))/(r + 1) + (r - 4)(r + 1) \\\\\n&= 2 + r^{2} + r - 4r - 4 \\\\\n&= r^{2} - 3r - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the full simplified fraction is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{r + 1}{r^2 - 3r - 2}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228783 :question/worked-solution "We're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert it to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nWe'll need to multiply by two conversion factors to change both the area unit ($\\textrm{ft}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$) and the time unit ($\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{min}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{ft}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{min}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{ft}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The painting rate is given as $480,000 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- We are given that there are $3.28$ feet per meter. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{3.28}$ meters per foot. We can write this as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{3.28} \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{ft}}\n$$\nTo determine the number of square meters in one square foot, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{3.28}\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{ft}})}^{2} & =\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{3.28}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{ft}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10.7584}\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{ft}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- There are $60$ minutes per hour, or $\\dfrac{1}{60}$ hours per minute. We can write this as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{60} \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 480 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} & \\displaystyle  = 480 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{10.7584}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{60}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 480 , 000 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{10.7584}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{60}} ) \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{480 , 000}{10.7584 \\cdot  60}} 