{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 5 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n18\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n5\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nWe're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{km}$ to $\\textrm{m}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ into $\\textrm{s}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{km}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $18 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- We are given that there are $1,000$ meters per kilometer.\n- The number of seconds in one hour is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{h} & =60\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{min} \\\\  & =60\\cdot 1\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{min} \\\\  & =60\\cdot 60\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{s} \\\\  & =3,600\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTaking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{3,600}$ hours per second.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 18 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}} & \\displaystyle  = 18 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}} \\cdot  1 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( 18 \\cdot  1 , 000 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3 , 600}} \\right) \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{18 , 000}{3 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 5 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n18\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n5\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186228789 :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\textrm{ft}$, and we want to convert this into $\\textrm{mi}$. We're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{ft}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\textrm{mi}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{ft}$ to $\\textrm{yd}$.\n\n$$\n\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\textrm{mi}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{yd}$ into $\\textrm{mi}$.\n\n$$\n\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}}} = \\textrm{mi}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{ft} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}}{\\textrm{ft}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{yd}} = \\textrm{mi}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The length given in the problem statement is $880 \\, \\textrm{ft}$.\n- We are given that there are $3$ feet per yard. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ yards per foot.\n- We are also given that there are $1,760$ yards per mile. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{1,760}$ miles per yard.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 880 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{ft} & \\displaystyle  = 880 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{ft} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{yd}}{\\text{ft}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 760}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\text{yd}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 880 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 760}} ) \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{\\text{ft}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{yd}}}{\\cancel{\\text{ft}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\cancel{\\text{yd}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{880}{3}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 760}} ) \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{mi} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{6}} \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{mi} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the steepest section is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6}\\,\\textrm{mi}\n$$\n\nlong." "We are given the units $\\textrm{ft}$, and we want to convert this into $\\textrm{mi}$. We're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{ft}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\textrm{mi}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{ft}$ to $\\textrm{yd}$.\n\n$$\n\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\textrm{mi}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{yd}$ into $\\textrm{mi}$.\n\n$$\n\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}}} = \\textrm{mi}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{ft} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}}{\\textrm{ft}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{yd}} = \\textrm{mi}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The length given in the problem statement is $880 \\, \\textrm{ft}$.\n- We are given that there are $3$ feet per yard. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ yards per foot.\n- We are also given that there are $1,760$ yards per mile. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{1,760}$ miles per yard.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 880 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{ft} & \\displaystyle  = 880 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{ft} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{yd}}{\\text{ft}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 760}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\text{yd}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( 880 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 760}} \\right) \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{\\text{ft}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{yd}}}{\\cancel{\\text{ft}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\cancel{\\text{yd}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{880}{3}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 760}} \\right) \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{mi} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{6}} \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{mi} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the steepest section is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6}\\,\\textrm{mi}\n$$\n\nlong."] [:db/cas 17592186228793 :question/worked-solution "We want to convert the toy car's speed from feet per second to yards per second.\n\nWe are given that there are $3$ feet per yard, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 \\, \\textrm{ft}}{\\textrm{yd}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of length $(\\textrm{ft})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{yd})$, we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nby the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}}{3 \\, \\textrm{ft}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nby our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{50}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{ft}}{\\text{s}}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{50}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{ft}}{\\text{s}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{yd}}{3 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{ft}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{50}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{ft}}}{\\text{s}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{yd}}{3 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{\\text{ft}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{50}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} ) \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{yd}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{150}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{yd}}{\\text{s}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the toy car's speed is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{150}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$" "We want to convert the toy car's speed from feet per second to yards per second.\n\nWe are given that there are $3$ feet per yard, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 \\, \\textrm{ft}}{\\textrm{yd}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of length $(\\textrm{ft})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{yd})$, we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nby the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}}{3 \\, \\textrm{ft}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nby our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{50}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{ft}}{\\text{s}}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{50}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{ft}}{\\text{s}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{yd}}{3 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{ft}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{50}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{ft}}}{\\text{s}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{yd}}{3 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{\\text{ft}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{50}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} \\right) \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{yd}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{150}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{yd}}{\\text{s}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the toy car's speed is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{150}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228833 :question/worked-solution "Here, we wish to simplify\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{14}{x-5}\\right)}{7x-35}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{GCF}(7x,35)=7\n$$\n\nand so we may factor our denominator to obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{14}{x-5}\\right)}{7x-35}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{14}{x-5}\\right)}{7(x-5)}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that division is equivalent to multiplication by the reciprocal, and so we may rewrite our complex fraction as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{( {\\displaystyle \\frac{14}{x - 5}} )}{7 ( x - 5 )}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{14}{x - 5}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{7 ( x - 5 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{14}{7 ( x - 5 {)}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{7 \\cdot  2}{7 ( x - 5 {)}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{7} \\cdot  2}{\\cancel{7} ( x - 5 {)}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{( x - 5 {)}^{2}}}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is expression \"III.\"" "Here, we wish to simplify\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{14}{x-5}\\right)}{7x-35}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{GCF}(7x,35)=7\n$$\n\nand so we may factor our denominator to obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{14}{x-5}\\right)}{7x-35}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{14}{x-5}\\right)}{7(x-5)}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that division is equivalent to multiplication by the reciprocal, and so we may rewrite our complex fraction as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{14}{x - 5}} \\right)}{7 ( x - 5 )}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{14}{x - 5}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{7 ( x - 5 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{14}{7 ( x - 5 {)}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{7 \\cdot  2}{7 ( x - 5 {)}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{7} \\cdot  2}{\\cancel{7} ( x - 5 {)}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{( x - 5 {)}^{2}}}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is expression \"III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186228834 :question/worked-solution "We need to write the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{8}\\left(2^{-s}\\right) - \\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the form\n\n$$\nk\\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nFactoring out the common factor of\n\n$$\n\\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the expression, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7}{8}(2^{-s}) - (2^{-s}) &= \\frac{7}{8} \\cdot (2^{-s}) - 1 \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{7}{8} - 1) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{7}{8} - \\frac{8}{8}) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= \\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{8}(2^{-s})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to write the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{8}\\left(2^{-s}\\right) - \\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the form\n\n$$\nk\\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nFactoring out the common factor of\n\n$$\n\\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the expression, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7}{8}(2^{-s}) - (2^{-s}) &= \\frac{7}{8} \\cdot (2^{-s}) - 1 \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{7}{8} - 1\\right) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\frac{7}{8} - \\frac{8}{8}\\right) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= \\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{8}(2^{-s})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228838 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}\n$$\n\nWe need to write the expression\n$15(3^{x-1}) - 4(3^x)$\nin the form $k(3^x)$.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the first term using the product rule and negative exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15(3^{x - 1}) - 4(3^{x}) &= 15 \\cdot (3^{x} \\cdot 3^{-1}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot (3^{x} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 3^{x}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 5(3^{x}) - 4(3^{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining the two negative terms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(3^{x}) - 4(3^{x}) &= (5 - 4)3^{x} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 3^{x} \\\\\n&= 3^{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=1$." "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}\n$$\n\nWe need to write the expression\n$15(3^{x-1}) - 4(3^x)$\nin the form $k(3^x)$.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the first term using the product rule and negative exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15(3^{x - 1}) - 4(3^{x}) &= 15 \\cdot (3^{x} \\cdot 3^{-1}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot \\left(3^{x} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\right) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 3^{x}\\right) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 5(3^{x}) - 4(3^{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining the two negative terms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(3^{x}) - 4(3^{x}) &= (5 - 4)3^{x} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 3^{x} \\\\\n&= 3^{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=1$."] [:db/cas 17592186228904 :question/worked-solution "A numerical method  $y_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n$ is stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda\\Delta x)| < 1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda\\Delta x > 0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -12y, \\qquad y(0) = 1.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & ={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-12{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}-12{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-12{y}_{n}-12({y}_{n}-12{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-24{y}_{n}+144{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-12{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx+72{y}_{n}\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =[1-12Δx+72(Δx)^{2}]{y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(12\\Delta x) = 1 - 12\\Delta x + 72(\\Delta x)^2.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(12Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-12Δx+72(Δx)^{2}| & <1 \\\\ |72{(Δx-\\frac{1}{12})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\left|72\\left(\\Delta x - \\dfrac1{12}\\right)^2 + \\dfrac12\\right| < 1.\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}72{(Δx-\\frac{1}{12})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & <1 \\\\ 72{(Δx-\\frac{1}{12})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(Δx-\\frac{1}{12})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{144} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{12}<Δx-\\frac{1}{12} & <\\frac{1}{12} \\\\ 0<Δx & <\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement III is false.\n\n- Finally, since it satisfies this inequality, the modified Euler method is stable for step size $\\Delta x = 0.15,$ and hence statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and IV only\"." "A numerical method  $y_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n$ is stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda\\Delta x)| < 1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda\\Delta x > 0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -12y, \\qquad y(0) = 1.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & ={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-12{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}-12{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-12{y}_{n}-12({y}_{n}-12{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-24{y}_{n}+144{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-12{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx+72{y}_{n}\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =[1-12Δx+72(Δx)^{2}]{y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(12\\Delta x) = 1 - 12\\Delta x + 72(\\Delta x)^2.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(12Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-12Δx+72(Δx)^{2}| & <1 \\\\ |72{\\left(Δx-\\frac{1}{12}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\left|72\\left(\\Delta x - \\dfrac1{12}\\right)^2 + \\dfrac12\\right| < 1.\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}72{\\left(Δx-\\frac{1}{12}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & <1 \\\\ 72{\\left(Δx-\\frac{1}{12}\\right)}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {\\left(Δx-\\frac{1}{12}\\right)}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{144} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{12}<Δx-\\frac{1}{12} & <\\frac{1}{12} \\\\ 0<Δx & <\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement III is false.\n\n- Finally, since it satisfies this inequality, the modified Euler method is stable for step size $\\Delta x = 0.15,$ and hence statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and IV only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186228920 :question/worked-solution "First, note that only two forces act on the package: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw = mg = 50g\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD = -kv^2 = -4.9v^2\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the package is\n\n$$\nF = w + D =50g - 4.9v^2.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 50g-4.9{v}^{2} & =50\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g-0.098{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dv}{dt} & =g(1-0.01{v}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{1-0.01{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-0.01{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-(0.1v)^{2}}\\,dv & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{0.1}\\text{artanh}(0.1v) & =gt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\text{artanh}(0.1v) & =\\frac{1}{10}gt+C \\\\ 0.1v & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{10}gt+C) \\\\ v(t) & =10\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{10}gt+C),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = 0.1C_1$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the package is released with an initial velocity of $9\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0) = 9,$ and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =10\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{10}g\\cdot 0+C) \\\\ 9 & =10\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) \\\\ \\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) & =0.9 \\\\ C & =\\text{artanh}(0.9)≈1.472\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\nv(t) = 10\\tanh\\left(\\dfrac1{10}g t+1.472\\right)\n$$" "First, note that only two forces act on the package: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw = mg = 50g\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD = -kv^2 = -4.9v^2\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the package is\n\n$$\nF = w + D =50g - 4.9v^2.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 50g-4.9{v}^{2} & =50\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g-0.098{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dv}{dt} & =g(1-0.01{v}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{1-0.01{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-0.01{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-(0.1v)^{2}}\\,dv & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{0.1}\\text{artanh}(0.1v) & =gt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\text{artanh}(0.1v) & =\\frac{1}{10}gt+C \\\\ 0.1v & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{10}gt+C\\right) \\\\ v(t) & =10\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{10}gt+C\\right),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = 0.1C_1$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the package is released with an initial velocity of $9\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0) = 9,$ and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =10\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{10}g\\cdot 0+C\\right) \\\\ 9 & =10\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) \\\\ \\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) & =0.9 \\\\ C & =\\text{artanh}(0.9)≈1.472\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\nv(t) = 10\\tanh\\left(\\dfrac1{10}g t+1.472\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228930 :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of coagulant in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which coagulant enters the tank minus the rate at which coagulant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which coagulant enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the coagulant enters the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *coagulant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which coagulant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which coagulant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $700\\,\\textrm{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}$ and leaves at $20\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n700 + 30t - 20t = (700+10t)\\,\\textrm{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of coagulant in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{700+10t}\\,\\textrm{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *coagulant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{700+10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{700+10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{700+10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q=1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(70+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|70+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(70+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t) = (70+t)^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(70+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q) & =(70+t)^{2}\\cdot 1.5 \\\\ (70+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(70+t)Q & =1.5(70+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2}) & =1.5(70+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 1.5(70+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(70+t)^{2} & =1.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(70+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.5(70+t)+\\frac{C}{(70+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $12\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of coagulant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 12,$ and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =12 \\\\ 0.5(70+0)+\\frac{C}{(70+0)^{2}} & =12 \\\\ 35+\\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =-23 \\\\ C & =-23(70)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.5(70+t)-\\frac{23(70)^{2}}{(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.5(70+t)-23{\\left(\\frac{70}{70+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of coagulant in the tank $8$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(8) & =0.5(70+8)-23{\\left(\\frac{70}{70+8}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =39-23{\\left(\\frac{70}{78}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈20.48\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "Let $Q$ be the amount of coagulant in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which coagulant enters the tank minus the rate at which coagulant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which coagulant enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the coagulant enters the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *coagulant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0.