  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|70+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(70+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t) = (70+t)^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(70+t)^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q\\right) & =(70+t)^{2}\\cdot 1.5 \\\\ (70+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(70+t)Q & =1.5(70+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2}) & =1.5(70+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 1.5(70+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(70+t)^{2} & =1.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(70+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.5(70+t)+\\frac{C}{(70+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $12\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of coagulant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 12,$ and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =12 \\\\ 0.5(70+0)+\\frac{C}{(70+0)^{2}} & =12 \\\\ 35+\\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =-23 \\\\ C & =-23(70)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.5(70+t)-\\frac{23(70)^{2}}{(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.5(70+t)-23{\\left(\\frac{70}{70+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of coagulant in the tank $8$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(8) & =0.5(70+8)-23{\\left(\\frac{70}{70+8}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =39-23{\\left(\\frac{70}{78}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈20.48\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186228931 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =36\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $96\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t) = 96 + 24t - 16t = 8(12+t)\\,\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{8(12 + t)}\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(16\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{12+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =36-\\frac{2Q}{12+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{2}{12 + t}\\,Q = 36.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(12+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|12+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(12+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t) = (12+t)^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{12+t}\\,Q) & =(12+t)^{2}\\cdot 36 \\\\ (12+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(12+t)Q & =36(12+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{2}) & =36(12+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 36(12+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(12+t)^{2} & =36\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(12+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =12(12+t)+\\frac{C}{(12+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $32\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 32,$ and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =32 \\\\ 12(12+0)+\\frac{C}{(12+0)^{2}} & =32 \\\\ 12\\cdot 12+\\frac{C}{(12)^{2}} & =32 \\\\ 144+\\frac{C}{{12}^{2}} & =32 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{12}^{2}} & =-112 \\\\ C & =-112(12)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =12(12+t)-\\frac{112(12)^{2}}{(12+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =12(12+t)-112{\\left(\\frac{12}{12+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $t_o$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $240\\,\\textrm{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V(t_o) = 240$ to solve for $t_o{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =240 \\\\ 96+24{t}_{o}-16{t}_{o} & =240 \\\\ 96+8{t}_{o} & =240 \\\\ 8{t}_{o} & =144 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =18\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(18) & =12(12+18)-112{\\left(\\frac{12}{12+18}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =360-112{\\left(\\frac{12}{30}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈342.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(24\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =36\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $96\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t) = 96 + 24t - 16t = 8(12+t)\\,\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{8(12 + t)}\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(16\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{16Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{12+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =36-\\frac{2Q}{12+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{2}{12 + t}\\,Q = 36.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(12+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|12+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(12+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t) = (12+t)^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12+t)^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{12+t}\\,Q\\right) & =(12+t)^{2}\\cdot 36 \\\\ (12+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(12+t)Q & =36(12+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{2}) & =36(12+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 36(12+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(12+t)^{2} & =36\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(12+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =12(12+t)+\\frac{C}{(12+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $32\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 32,$ and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =32 \\\\ 12(12+0)+\\frac{C}{(12+0)^{2}} & =32 \\\\ 12\\cdot 12+\\frac{C}{(12)^{2}} & =32 \\\\ 144+\\frac{C}{{12}^{2}} & =32 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{12}^{2}} & =-112 \\\\ C & =-112(12)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =12(12+t)-\\frac{112(12)^{2}}{(12+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =12(12+t)-112{\\left(\\frac{12}{12+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $t_o$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $240\\,\\textrm{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V(t_o) = 240$ to solve for $t_o{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =240 \\\\ 96+24{t}_{o}-16{t}_{o} & =240 \\\\ 96+8{t}_{o} & =240 \\\\ 8{t}_{o} & =144 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =18\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(18) & =12(12+18)-112{\\left(\\frac{12}{12+18}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =360-112{\\left(\\frac{12}{30}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈342.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228944 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}1,\\hspace{0.222em} & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ -1,\\hspace{0.222em} & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd}\\end{cases} \\\\ g(x) & =u(x-1)+u\\hspace{-0.167em}(x-\\frac{1}{2})+u\\hspace{-0.167em}(x-\\frac{1}{3})+⋯ \\\\ h(x) & =⌈\\frac{x}{2}⌉\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $\\lceil x \\rceil$ is the ceiling function." "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}1,\\hspace{0.222em} & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ -1,\\hspace{0.222em} & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd}\\end{cases} \\\\ g(x) & =u(x-1)+u\\hspace{-0.167em}\\left(x-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+u\\hspace{-0.167em}\\left(x-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+⋯ \\\\ h(x) & =⌈\\frac{x}{2}⌉\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $\\lceil x \\rceil$ is the ceiling function."] [:db/cas 17592186228946 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}-3y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot \\sin \\,(2t-\\pi )\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-3L\\{y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot \\sin \\,(2t-\\pi )\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $\\mathcal L\\left\\{y' \\right\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y' \\right\\}  = sY(s) - y(0)   = sY(s) + 2\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n2u_{\\pi/2}(t)\\cdot \\sin\\!\\left(2t-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $f(t)=2\\sin(2t-\\pi).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\} = e^{-as} \\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\pi )\\} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L\\{2\\sin \\,(2\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\pi )\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L\\{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-3L\\{y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\pi )\\} \\\\ (sY(s)+2)-3Y(s) & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s-3)Y(s)+2 & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s-3)Y(s) & =-2+\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{2}{s-3}+\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}-3y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot \\sin \\,(2t-\\pi )\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-3L\\{y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot \\sin \\,(2t-\\pi )\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $\\mathcal L\\left\\{y' \\right\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y' \\right\\}  = sY(s) - y(0)   = sY(s) + 2\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n2u_{\\pi/2}(t)\\cdot \\sin\\!\\left(2t-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $f(t)=2\\sin(2t-\\pi).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\} = e^{-as} \\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u\\,\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\pi )\\} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L\\{2\\sin \\,\\left(2\\,\\left(t+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\pi \\right)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L\\{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-3L\\{y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\pi )\\} \\\\ (sY(s)+2)-3Y(s) & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s-3)Y(s)+2 & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s-3)Y(s) & =-2+\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{2}{s-3}+\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228947 :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential $e^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+4)$ and a repeated linear factor $s^2,$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)} = \\dfrac{A}{s} + \\dfrac{B}{s^2} + \\dfrac{C}{s+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1 = As(s+4) + B(s+4) + Cs^2.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A, B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1 = A(0) + B(4) + C(0) \\implies B = \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n1 = A(0) + B(0) + C(16) \\implies C = \\dfrac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of $s^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n0 = A + C \\implies A = -C = -\\dfrac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s) = -\\dfrac{1}{16s} + \\dfrac{1}{4s^2} + \\dfrac{1}{16(s+4)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t) = \\mathcal L^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{-\\frac{1}{16s}+\\frac{1}{4{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{16(s+4)}\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{t}{4}+\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the $e^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\ne^{-as}\\cdot F(s)  = \\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L^{-1} \\{ e^{-as}F(s) \\} = u(t-a) \\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =u(t-3)[-\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{t-3}{4}+\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4(t-3)}] \\\\  & =u(t-3)(\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4(t-3)}+\\frac{4t-13}{16}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t) = -2e^{-4t} + u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t) = \\dfrac{1}{16}e^{-4(t-3)} + \\dfrac{4t-13}{16}.\n$$" "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential $e^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+4)$ and a repeated linear factor $s^2,$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)} = \\dfrac{A}{s} + \\dfrac{B}{s^2} + \\dfrac{C}{s+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1 = As(s+4) + B(s+4) + Cs^2.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A, B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1 = A(0) + B(4) + C(0) \\implies B = \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n1 = A(0) + B(0) + C(16) \\implies C = \\dfrac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of $s^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n0 = A + C \\implies A = -C = -\\dfrac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s) = -\\dfrac{1}{16s} + \\dfrac{1}{4s^2} + \\dfrac{1}{16(s+4)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t) = \\mathcal L^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{-\\frac{1}{16s}+\\frac{1}{4{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{16(s+4)}\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{t}{4}+\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the $e^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\ne^{-as}\\cdot F(s)  = \\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L^{-1} \\{ e^{-as}F(s) \\} = u(t-a) \\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =u(t-3)\\left[-\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{t-3}{4}+\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4(t-3)}\\right] \\\\  & =u(t-3)\\left(\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4(t-3)}+\\frac{4t-13}{16}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t) = -2e^{-4t} + u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t) = \\dfrac{1}{16}e^{-4(t-3)} + \\dfrac{4t-13}{16}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228949 :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of nutrient in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which nutrient enters the tank minus the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.01\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the nutrient enters the tank at a rate of $40\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *nutrient* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.01\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.01\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $800\\,\\textrm{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{800}\\,\\textrm{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $40\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *nutrient* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{40Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.4-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{20}Q = 0.4.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $Q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{20}Q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nQ_c(t) = Ae^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nQ_p(t) = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $Q_p = \\alpha$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q_p}{\\mathrm{d}t} = (\\alpha)' = 0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0 + \\dfrac{1}{20}\\alpha = 0.4\n  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n  \\alpha = 8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is $Q_p(t) = 8.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t) = Q_c(t) + Q_p(t) = Ae^{-t/20} + 8.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $7\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved nutrient. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 7,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =7 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+8 & =7 \\\\ A & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\nQ(t) = 8 - e^{-t/20}.\n$$" "Let $Q$ be the amount of nutrient in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which nutrient enters the tank minus the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.01\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the nutrient enters the tank at a rate of $40\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *nutrient* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0.01\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0.01\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $800\\,\\textrm{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{800}\\,\\textrm{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $40\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *nutrient* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{40Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.4-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{20}Q = 0.4.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $Q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{20}Q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nQ_c(t) = Ae^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nQ_p(t) = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $Q_p = \\alpha$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q_p}{\\mathrm{d}t} = (\\alpha)' = 0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0 + \\dfrac{1}{20}\\alpha = 0.4\n  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n  \\alpha = 8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is $Q_p(t) = 8.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t) = Q_c(t) + Q_p(t) = Ae^{-t/20} + 8.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $7\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved nutrient. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 7,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =7 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+8 & =7 \\\\ A & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\nQ(t) = 8 - e^{-t/20}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228950 :question/worked-solution "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots $\\lambda_1 \\geq \\lambda_2$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = c_1y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle y_1(x) = x^{\\lambda_1} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n(\\lambda_1)x^n\n$$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n\n- $a_n(\\lambda_1)$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda = \\lambda_1.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $5\\lambda^2-3\\lambda=0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (5\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is $\\lambda_1 = \\dfrac35.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = x^{3/5}\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n.\n$$\n\nWith $\\lambda = \\dfrac35,$ the recurrence relation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{6}{(\\frac{3}{5}+n)(5(\\frac{3}{5}+n)-3)}\\,{a}_{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5n(n+\\frac{3}{5})}\\,{a}_{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{n(5n+3)}\\,{a}_{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, with $a_0\\neq0$ arbitrary, we evaluate the first three coefficients using the given recurrence relation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & \\ne 0, \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{6}{1(5\\cdot 1+3)}\\,{a}_{0}=\\frac{6}{8}{a}_{0}=\\frac{3}{4}{a}_{0}, \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{6}{2(5\\cdot 2+3)}\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{6}{26}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}{a}_{0}=\\frac{9}{52}{a}_{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first Frobenius solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & ={x}^{3/5}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} \\\\  & ={x}^{3/5}({a}_{0}+\\frac{3}{4}{a}_{0}x+\\frac{9}{52}{a}_{0}{x}^{2}+⋯) \\\\  & ={a}_{0}({x}^{3/5}+\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{8/5}+\\frac{9}{52}{x}^{13/5}+⋯).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots $\\lambda_1 \\geq \\lambda_2$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = c_1y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle y_1(x) = x^{\\lambda_1} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n(\\lambda_1)x^n\n$$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n\n- $a_n(\\lambda_1)$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda = \\lambda_1.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $5\\lambda^2-3\\lambda=0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (5\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is $\\lambda_1 = \\dfrac35.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = x^{3/5}\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n.\n$$\n\nWith $\\lambda = \\dfrac35,$ the recurrence relation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{6}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}+n\\right)\\left(5\\left(\\frac{3}{5}+n\\right)-3\\right)}\\,{a}_{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5n\\left(n+\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}\\,{a}_{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{n(5n+3)}\\,{a}_{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, with $a_0\\neq0$ arbitrary, we evaluate the first three coefficients using the given recurrence relation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & \\ne 0, \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{6}{1(5\\cdot 1+3)}\\,{a}_{0}=\\frac{6}{8}{a}_{0}=\\frac{3}{4}{a}_{0}, \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{6}{2(5\\cdot 2+3)}\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{6}{26}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}{a}_{0}=\\frac{9}{52}{a}_{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first Frobenius solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & ={x}^{3/5}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} \\\\  & ={x}^{3/5}\\left({a}_{0}+\\frac{3}{4}{a}_{0}x+\\frac{9}{52}{a}_{0}{x}^{2}+⋯\\right) \\\\  & ={a}_{0}\\left({x}^{3/5}+\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{8/5}+\\frac{9}{52}{x}^{13/5}+⋯\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228952 :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\nL(ay_1 + by_2) = aL(y_1) + bL(y_2)\n$$\n\nfor functions $y_1(x), y_2(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}})+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =+{a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} \\\\  & +a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & +ab({y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a[{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}]+b[{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $D(ay_1+by_2)  \\neq aD(y_1) + bD(y_2),$ the operator $D$ is nonlinear." "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\nL(ay_1 + by_2) = aL(y_1) + bL(y_2)\n$$\n\nfor functions $y_1(x), y_2(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\left(a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\right)+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =+{a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} \\\\  & +a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & +ab\\left({y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a\\left[{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\right]+b\\left[{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\right] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $D(ay_1+by_2)  \\neq aD(y_1) + bD(y_2),$ the operator $D$ is nonlinear."] [:db/cas 17592186228955 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 0.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise functions $u(t-2)$ and $u(t-6),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2) & =\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<2 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 2\\end{cases} \\\\ u(t-6) & =\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<6 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 6.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nu(t-2) + u(t - 6) = \n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}0 , \\hspace{1em} & t < 2 \\\\ 1 , \\hspace{1em} & 2 \\le  t < 6 \\\\ 2 , \\hspace{1em} & t \\ge  6.\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nSo, for our Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{6}1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the second integral diverges for $s \\leq 0.$\n\nFor $s > 0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{6}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}]}_{2}^{6}+2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{6}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-sa}-{e}^{-6s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}(0-{e}^{-6s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-6s}}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} =\\dfrac{e^{-2s}}{s} +\\dfrac{e^{-6s}}{s}, \n\\qquad \ns > 0.\n$$" "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 0.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise functions $u(t-2)$ and $u(t-6),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2) & =\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<2 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 2\\end{cases} \\\\ u(t-6) & =\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<6 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 6.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nu(t-2) + u(t - 6) = \n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}0 , \\hspace{1em} & t < 2 \\\\ 1 , \\hspace{1em} & 2 \\le  t < 6 \\\\ 2 , \\hspace{1em} & t \\ge  6.\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nSo, for our Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{6}1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the second integral diverges for $s \\leq 0.$\n\nFor $s > 0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{6}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\left[-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right]}_{2}^{6}+2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{6}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-sa}-{e}^{-6s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}(0-{e}^{-6s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-6s}}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} =\\dfrac{e^{-2s}}{s} +\\dfrac{e^{-6s}}{s}, \n\\qquad \ns > 0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229274 :question/problem "Which expression is equivalent to $(\\left(\\frac{12}{x + 2}\\right))/(6x + 12)$, where $x \\ne - 2$?" "Which expression is equivalent to $\\left(\\left(\\frac{12}{x + 2}\\right)\\right)/(6x + 12)$, where $x \\ne - 2$?"] [:db/cas 17592186229275 :question/problem "Which expression is equivalent to $(\\left(\\frac{14}{x - 5}\\right))/(7x - 35)$, where $x \\ne 5$?" "Which expression is equivalent to $\\left(\\left(\\frac{14}{x - 5}\\right)\\right)/(7x - 35)$, where $x \\ne 5$?"] [:db/cas 17592186229280 :question/problem "What is the equivalent simple fraction for the expression?\n\n$(1)/((\\frac{3}{x - 3} + x + 2))$" "What is the equivalent simple fraction for the expression?\n\n$(1)/\\left(\\left(\\frac{3}{x - 3} + x + 2\\right)\\right)$"] [:db/cas 17592186229281 :question/problem "What is the equivalent simple fraction for the expression?\n\n$(1)/((\\frac{2}{r + 1} + r - 4))$" "What is the equivalent simple fraction for the expression?\n\n$(1)/\\left(\\left(\\frac{2}{r + 1} + r - 4\\right)\\right)$"] [:db/cas 17592186229282 :question/problem "What is the equivalent simple fraction for the following expression?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle (1)/((\\frac{4}{y + 5} + \\frac{3}{y - 2}))\n$$" "What is the equivalent simple fraction for the following expression?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle (1)/\\left(\\left(\\frac{4}{y + 5} + \\frac{3}{y - 2}\\right)\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229283 :question/problem "What is the equivalent simple fraction for the following expression?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle (1)/((\\frac{-2}{q + 1} + \\frac{5}{q - 4}))\n$$" "What is the equivalent simple fraction for the following expression?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle (1)/\\left(\\left(\\frac{-2}{q + 1} + \\frac{5}{q - 4}\\right)\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229639 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the trapezoidal method on the test equation $y^{′} = - λy$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\ge 0, λ = 3$, and step size $Δx$?\n\n1. The stability function is $R(3Δx) = \\frac{1 - 3Δx/2}{1 + 3Δx/2}$.\n2. The stability region is $$\n\\displaystyle \\mid \\frac{9}{2}(Δx - \\frac{2}{3})^{2} + \\frac{1}{2} \\mid < 1.\n$$\n3. The method is absolutely stable.\n4. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx = 2.5$.\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by $y_{n + 1} = R(λΔx)y_{n}$." "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the trapezoidal method on the test equation $y^{′} = - λy$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\ge 0, λ = 3$, and step size $Δx$?\n\n1. The stability function is $R(3Δx) = \\frac{1 - 3Δx/2}{1 + 3Δx/2}$.\n2. The stability region is $$\n\\displaystyle \\mid \\frac{9}{2}\\left(Δx - \\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{2} + \\frac{1}{2} \\mid < 1.\n$$\n3. The method is absolutely stable.\n4. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx = 2.5$.\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by $y_{n + 1} = R(λΔx)y_{n}$."] [:db/cas 17592186229668 :question/worked-solution "First, note that only two forces act on the body: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=25g\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=25g-k{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 25g-k{v}^{2} & =25\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g-\\frac{k}{25}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the body's velocity is $1.25\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $7.35\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values and $g=9.8$ into the ODE, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n7.35=g-\\frac{k}{25}\\cdot {1.25}^{2}\\,⟹\\,k=39.2\\,\\text{kg/m}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-1.568{v}^{2}\n$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dv}{dt} & =g(1-0.16{v}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{1-0.16{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-0.16{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-(0.4v)^{2}}\\,dv & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{0.4}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.4v) & =gt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.4v) & =\\frac{2}{5}gt+C \\\\ 0.4v & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{2}{5}gt+C) \\\\ v(t) & =2.5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{2}{5}gt+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=0.4{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the body is released from rest. So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0)=0,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =2.5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{2}{5}g\\cdot 0+C) \\\\ 0 & =2.5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) \\\\ \\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) & =0 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t):$\n\n$$\nv(t)=2.5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{2}{5}gt).\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $\\frac{2}{5}g,$ we have\n\n$$\nv(t)=2.5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3.92t).\n$$" "First, note that only two forces act on the body: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=25g\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=25g-k{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 25g-k{v}^{2} & =25\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g-\\frac{k}{25}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the body's velocity is $1.25\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $7.35\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values and $g=9.8$ into the ODE, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n7.35=g-\\frac{k}{25}\\cdot {1.25}^{2}\\,⟹\\,k=39.2\\,\\text{kg/m}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-1.568{v}^{2}\n$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dv}{dt} & =g(1-0.16{v}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{1-0.16{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-0.16{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-(0.4v)^{2}}\\,dv & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{0.4}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.4v) & =gt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.4v) & =\\frac{2}{5}gt+C \\\\ 0.4v & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{5}gt+C\\right) \\\\ v(t) & =2.5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{5}gt+C\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=0.4{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the body is released from rest. So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0)=0,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =2.5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{5}g\\cdot 0+C\\right) \\\\ 0 & =2.5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) \\\\ \\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) & =0 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t):$\n\n$$\nv(t)=2.5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{5}gt\\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $\\frac{2}{5}g,$ we have\n\n$$\nv(t)=2.5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3.92t).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229692 :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $300L$ of water containing $6kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.03kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{25}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{25})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "An aquarium initially holds $300L$ of water containing $6kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.03kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α\\left(1 - \\frac{t}{25}\\right) + β\\left(1 - \\frac{t}{25}\\right)^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186229693 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which minerals enter the tank minus the rate at which minerals leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which minerals enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a brine solution containing $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =4.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which minerals exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of brine in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine; brine enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed brine leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=180+6t-4t=2(90+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =4.8-\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q=4.8.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(90+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(90+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(90+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(90+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q) & =(90+t)^{2}\\cdot 4.8 \\\\ (90+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(90+t)Q & =4.8(90+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(90+t)^{2}) & =4.8(90+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(90+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 4.8(90+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(90+t)^{2} & =4.8\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(90+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =1.6(90+t)+\\frac{C}{(90+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $54\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=54,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =54 \\\\ 1.6(90+0)+\\frac{C}{(90+0)^{2}} & =54 \\\\ 144+\\frac{C}{(90)^{2}} & =54 \\\\ C & =-90(90)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =1.6(90+t)-\\frac{90(90)^{2}}{(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =1.6(90+t)-90{\\left(\\frac{90}{90+t}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $300\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=300$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 2(90+{t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 90+{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =60\\,\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(60) & =1.6(90+60)-90{\\left(\\frac{90}{90+60}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =240-90{\\left(\\frac{9}{15}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =207.6\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which minerals enter the tank minus the rate at which minerals leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which minerals enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a brine solution containing $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =4.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which minerals exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of brine in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine; brine enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed brine leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=180+6t-4t=2(90+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =4.8-\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q=4.8.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(90+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(90+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(90+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(90+t)^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q\\right) & =(90+t)^{2}\\cdot 4.8 \\\\ (90+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(90+t)Q & =4.8(90+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(90+t)^{2}) & =4.8(90+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(90+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 4.8(90+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(90+t)^{2} & =4.8\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(90+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =1.6(90+t)+\\frac{C}{(90+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $54\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=54,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =54 \\\\ 1.6(90+0)+\\frac{C}{(90+0)^{2}} & =54 \\\\ 144+\\frac{C}{(90)^{2}} & =54 \\\\ C & =-90(90)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =1.6(90+t)-\\frac{90(90)^{2}}{(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =1.6(90+t)-90{\\left(\\frac{90}{90+t}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $300\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=300$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 2(90+{t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 90+{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =60\\,\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(60) & =1.6(90+60)-90{\\left(\\frac{90}{90+60}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =240-90{\\left(\\frac{9}{15}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =207.6\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229698 :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of stabilizer in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which stabilizer enters the tank minus the rate at which stabilizer leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which stabilizer enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the stabilizer enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *stabilizer* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which stabilizer exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which stabilizer leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $600\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $15\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n600+15t-10t=(600+5t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of stabilizer in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min},$ *stabilizer* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{120+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{2Q}{120+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{120+t}Q=0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(120+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|120+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(120+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(120+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(120+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(120+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{120+t}Q) & =(120+t)^{2}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\ (120+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(120+t)Q & =0.6(120+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(120+t)^{2}) & =0.6(120+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(120+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 0.6(120+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(120+t)^{2} & =0.6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(120+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(120+t)+\\frac{C}{(120+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of stabilizer. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=5,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =5 \\\\ 0.2(120+0)+\\frac{C}{(120+0)^{2}} & =5 \\\\ 24+\\frac{C}{{120}^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{120}^{2}} & =-19 \\\\ C & =-19(120)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(120+t)-\\frac{19(120)^{2}}{(120+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.2(120+t)-19{\\left(\\frac{120}{120+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of stabilizer in the tank $10$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(10) & =0.2(120+10)-19{\\left(\\frac{120}{120+10}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =26-19{\\left(\\frac{12}{13}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈9.81\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "Let $Q$ be the amount of stabilizer in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which stabilizer enters the tank minus the rate at which stabilizer leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which stabilizer enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the stabilizer enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *stabilizer* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which stabilizer exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which stabilizer leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $600\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $15\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n600+15t-10t=(600+5t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of stabilizer in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min},$ *stabilizer* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(10\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{120+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{2Q}{120+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{120+t}Q=0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(120+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|120+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(120+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(120+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(120+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(120+t)^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{120+t}Q\\right) & =(120+t)^{2}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\ (120+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(120+t)Q & =0.6(120+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(120+t)^{2}) & =0.6(120+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(120+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 0.6(120+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(120+t)^{2} & =0.6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(120+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(120+t)+\\frac{C}{(120+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of stabilizer. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=5,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =5 \\\\ 0.2(120+0)+\\frac{C}{(120+0)^{2}} & =5 \\\\ 24+\\frac{C}{{120}^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{120}^{2}} & =-19 \\\\ C & =-19(120)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(120+t)-\\frac{19(120)^{2}}{(120+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.2(120+t)-19{\\left(\\frac{120}{120+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of stabilizer in the tank $10$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(10) & =0.2(120+10)-19{\\left(\\frac{120}{120+10}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =26-19{\\left(\\frac{12}{13}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈9.81\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186229700 :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t}+f(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find the function $f(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x=-2t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t=(t-1){e}^{t}\n$$" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x\\left(1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t}+f(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find the function $f(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x=-2t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t=(t-1){e}^{t}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229710 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡2x+2{\\sin }^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+(B-\\frac{1}{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $B$ as\n\n$$\nB:=(B-\\frac{1}{2})\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\underbrace{-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|}_{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|.\n$$" "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡2x+2{\\sin }^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\left(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\left(B-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $B$ as\n\n$$\nB:=\\left(B-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\underbrace{-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|}_{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229711 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)={\\sec }^{3}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡x+{\\sin }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{{\\sec }^{3}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x{\\sec }^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{\\cos }^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Then, we have $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and our integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{\\cos }^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\sec }^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{\\sec }^{3}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x{\\sec }^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{\\cos }^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2}{\\sec }^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\underbrace{\\,-\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$" "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)={\\sec }^{3}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡x+{\\sin }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{{\\sec }^{3}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x{\\sec }^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{\\cos }^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Then, we have $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and our integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{\\cos }^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\sec }^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{\\sec }^{3}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x{\\sec }^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{\\cos }^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}{\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\right)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\underbrace{\\,-\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229724 :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of pollutant in the basin at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which pollutant enters the basin minus the rate at which pollutant leaves the basin.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the basin.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the pollutant enters the basin at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *pollutant* enters the basin is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the basin.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the basin remains $750\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of pollutant in the basin, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *pollutant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{25}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.9-\\frac{Q}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}Q=0.9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/25},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{25}\\alpha =0.9\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =22.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=22.5.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/25}+22.5.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the basin initially contains $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved pollutant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/25}+22.5 & =6 \\\\ A & =-16.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=22.5-16.5{e}^{-t/25}.\n$$" "Let $Q$ be the amount of pollutant in the basin at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which pollutant enters the basin minus the rate at which pollutant leaves the basin.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the basin.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the pollutant enters the basin at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *pollutant* enters the basin is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the basin.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the basin remains $750\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of pollutant in the basin, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *pollutant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{25}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.9-\\frac{Q}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}Q=0.9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/25},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{25}\\alpha =0.9\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =22.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=22.5.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/25}+22.5.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the basin initially contains $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved pollutant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/25}+22.5 & =6 \\\\ A & =-16.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=22.5-16.5{e}^{-t/25}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229726 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP)\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $P,Q,$ and $f$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nIf the associated homogeneous BVP has nontrivial solutions, then the inhomogeneous BVP has either no solution or infinitely many solutions. \n\nTo find the solution of an inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP):\n\n- First, we find the general solution ${y}_{h}(x)$ of the corresponding homogeneous equation.\n\n- Then, we find a particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ of the inhomogeneous equation.\n\n- Finally, we apply the boundary conditions to determine the arbitrary constants.\n\nHowever, in this particular case, we can integrate the equation directly. By doing so, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+Ax+B.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that the derivative ${y}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+A.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(0)=0$ gives\n\n$$\nB=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+Ax.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the condition $y(1)-{y}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{2}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{3}+A)-(1+A) & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}+A-1-A & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ A-A & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the boundary value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+Ax.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3},\\,g(x)=x.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The associated homogeneous BVP\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)-{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions. Therefore, the inhomogeneous BVP has either infinitely many solutions or no solution. Since we have found a solution that contains an arbitrary constant and satisfies both boundary conditions, the BVP has infinitely many solutions." "Suppose we have the second-order inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP)\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $P,Q,$ and $f$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nIf the associated homogeneous BVP has nontrivial solutions, then the inhomogeneous BVP has either no solution or infinitely many solutions. \n\nTo find the solution of an inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP):\n\n- First, we find the general solution ${y}_{h}(x)$ of the corresponding homogeneous equation.\n\n- Then, we find a particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ of the inhomogeneous equation.\n\n- Finally, we apply the boundary conditions to determine the arbitrary constants.\n\nHowever, in this particular case, we can integrate the equation directly. By doing so, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+Ax+B.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that the derivative ${y}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+A.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(0)=0$ gives\n\n$$\nB=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+Ax.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the condition $y(1)-{y}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{2}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}+A\\right)-(1+A) & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}+A-1-A & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ A-A & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the boundary value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+Ax.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3},\\,g(x)=x.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The associated homogeneous BVP\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)-{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions. Therefore, the inhomogeneous BVP has either infinitely many solutions or no solution. Since we have found a solution that contains an arbitrary constant and satisfies both boundary conditions, the BVP has infinitely many solutions."] [:db/cas 17592186229744 :question/worked-solution "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots ${\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{1}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1}){x}^{n}\n$$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n\n- ${a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1})$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda ={\\lambda }_{1}.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $3{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda =0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (3\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2/3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nWith $\\lambda =\\frac{2}{3},$ the recurrence relation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{4}{(\\frac{2}{3}+n)(3(\\frac{2}{3}+n)-2)}\\,{a}_{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3n(n+\\frac{2}{3})}\\,{a}_{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n(3n+2)}\\,{a}_{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, with ${a}_{0}\\ne 0$ arbitrary, we evaluate the first three coefficients using the given recurrence relation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & \\ne 0, \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{4}{1(3\\cdot 1+2)}\\,{a}_{0}=\\frac{4}{5}{a}_{0}, \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{4}{2(3\\cdot 2+2)}\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first Frobenius solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & ={x}^{2/3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} \\\\  & ={x}^{2/3}({a}_{0}+\\frac{4}{5}{a}_{0}x+\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{0}{x}^{2}+⋯) \\\\  & ={a}_{0}({x}^{2/3}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{5/3}+\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{8/3}+⋯).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots ${\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{1}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1}){x}^{n}\n$$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n\n- ${a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1})$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda ={\\lambda }_{1}.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $3{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda =0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (3\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2/3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nWith $\\lambda =\\frac{2}{3},$ the recurrence relation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{4}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}+n\\right)\\left(3\\left(\\frac{2}{3}+n\\right)-2\\right)}\\,{a}_{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3n\\left(n+\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}\\,{a}_{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n(3n+2)}\\,{a}_{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, with ${a}_{0}\\ne 0$ arbitrary, we evaluate the first three coefficients using the given recurrence relation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & \\ne 0, \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{4}{1(3\\cdot 1+2)}\\,{a}_{0}=\\frac{4}{5}{a}_{0}, \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{4}{2(3\\cdot 2+2)}\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first Frobenius solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & ={x}^{2/3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} \\\\  & ={x}^{2/3}\\left({a}_{0}+\\frac{4}{5}{a}_{0}x+\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{0}{x}^{2}+⋯\\right) \\\\  & ={a}_{0}\\left({x}^{2/3}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{5/3}+\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{8/3}+⋯\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186230190 :tutorial/content "Now, consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny = x\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}-2\\right) + 2\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2 - 3\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right).\n$$\n\nSuppose we apply the substitution $y'(x) = t.$ Using the techniques shown earlier in the lesson (differentiating with respect to $x$), we can reduce this to the following first-order linear equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} - \\dfrac12 x = 2t-\\dfrac32.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of integrating factors, we compute $I(t)$ based on the coefficient $P(t) = -\\dfrac12{:}$\n\n$$\nI(t) = e^{\\int -1/2\\,\\textrm dt} = e^{-t/2}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ converts the left-hand side into an exact derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-t/2} & =(2t-\\frac{3}{2}){e}^{-t/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{-t/2}) & =(2t-\\frac{3}{2}){e}^{-t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{-t/2} & =\\int (2t-\\frac{3}{2}){e}^{-t/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{-t/2} & =2\\int t{e}^{-t/2}\\,\\text{d}t-\\frac{3}{2}\\int {e}^{-t/2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts for the first integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{-t/2} & =2(-2t-4){e}^{-t/2}-\\frac{3}{2}(-2){e}^{-t/2}+C \\\\ x{e}^{-t/2} & =(-4t-8){e}^{-t/2}+3{e}^{-t/2}+C \\\\ x{e}^{-t/2} & =(-4t-5){e}^{-t/2}+C \\\\ x(t) & =-4t-5+C{e}^{t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to construct the function $y,$ we simply substitute $y' = t$ and $x = x(t)$ into our original equation. Note that the solution $y(t)$ is dependent on the parameter $t.$\n\nLet's see another example." "Now, consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny = x\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}-2\\right) + 2\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2 - 3\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right).\n$$\n\nSuppose we apply the substitution $y'(x) = t.$ Using the techniques shown earlier in the lesson (differentiating with respect to $x$), we can reduce this to the following first-order linear equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} - \\dfrac12 x = 2t-\\dfrac32.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of integrating factors, we compute $I(t)$ based on the coefficient $P(t) = -\\dfrac12{:}$\n\n$$\nI(t) = e^{\\int -1/2\\,\\textrm dt} = e^{-t/2}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ converts the left-hand side into an exact derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-t/2} & =\\left(2t-\\frac{3}{2}\\right){e}^{-t/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{-t/2}) & =\\left(2t-\\frac{3}{2}\\right){e}^{-t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{-t/2} & =\\int \\left(2t-\\frac{3}{2}\\right){e}^{-t/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{-t/2} & =2\\int t{e}^{-t/2}\\,\\text{d}t-\\frac{3}{2}\\int {e}^{-t/2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts for the first integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{-t/2} & =2(-2t-4){e}^{-t/2}-\\frac{3}{2}(-2){e}^{-t/2}+C \\\\ x{e}^{-t/2} & =(-4t-8){e}^{-t/2}+3{e}^{-t/2}+C \\\\ x{e}^{-t/2} & =(-4t-5){e}^{-t/2}+C \\\\ x(t) & =-4t-5+C{e}^{t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to construct the function $y,$ we simply substitute $y' = t$ and $x = x(t)$ into our original equation. Note that the solution $y(t)$ is dependent on the parameter $t.$\n\nLet's see another example."] [:db/cas 17592186230196 :tutorial/content "Suppose a body of mass $125\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is released from rest and falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the body's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\geq 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction. The air resistance on the body is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=49\\,\\textrm{kg/m}$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nAssuming the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2,$ and the only forces acting on the body are the weight due to gravity and drag, let's find the equation for the velocity $v(t).$\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the body: the force due to gravity, given by the weight $$w = mg = 125g,$$ and the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction): $$D = -kv^2 = -49v^2$$ Hence, the resultant force acting on the body is $$F = w + D = 125g - 49v^2.$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 125g-49{v}^{2} & =125\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g-0.392{v}^{2} \\\\ & =g-0.04g{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get $$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dv}{dt} & =g(1-0.04{v}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{1-0.04{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-0.04{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-(0.2v)^{2}}\\,dv & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{0.2}\\text{artanh}(0.2v) & =gt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\text{artanh}(0.2v) & =\\frac{1}{5}gt+C \\\\ 0.2v & =\\operatorname{tanh}(\\frac{1}{5}gt+C) \\\\ v(t) & =5\\operatorname{tanh}(\\frac{1}{5}gt+C),\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $C = 0.2C_1$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the body is released from rest. So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0) = 0,$ and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =5\\operatorname{tanh}(\\frac{1}{5}g\\cdot 0+C) \\\\ 0 & =5\\operatorname{tanh}(C) \\\\ \\operatorname{tanh}(C) & =0 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t){:}$\n\n$$v(t) = 5\\tanh\\left(\\dfrac1{5} g t\\right)$$" "Suppose a body of mass $125\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is released from rest and falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the body's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\geq 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction. The air resistance on the body is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=49\\,\\textrm{kg/m}$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nAssuming the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2,$ and the only forces acting on the body are the weight due to gravity and drag, let's find the equation for the velocity $v(t).$\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the body: the force due to gravity, given by the weight $$w = mg = 125g,$$ and the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction): $$D = -kv^2 = -49v^2$$ Hence, the resultant force acting on the body is $$F = w + D = 125g - 49v^2.$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 125g-49{v}^{2} & =125\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g-0.392{v}^{2} \\\\ & =g-0.04g{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get $$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dv}{dt} & =g(1-0.04{v}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{1-0.04{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-0.04{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-(0.2v)^{2}}\\,dv & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{0.2}\\text{artanh}(0.2v) & =gt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\text{artanh}(0.2v) & =\\frac{1}{5}gt+C \\\\ 0.2v & =\\operatorname{tanh}\\left(\\frac{1}{5}gt+C\\right) \\\\ v(t) & =5\\operatorname{tanh}\\left(\\frac{1}{5}gt+C\\right),\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $C = 0.2C_1$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the body is released from rest. So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0) = 0,$ and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =5\\operatorname{tanh}\\left(\\frac{1}{5}g\\cdot 0+C\\right) \\\\ 0 & =5\\operatorname{tanh}(C) \\\\ \\operatorname{tanh}(C) & =0 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t){:}$\n\n$$v(t) = 5\\tanh\\left(\\dfrac1{5} g t\\right)$$"] [:db/cas 17592186230205 :tutorial/content "In the previous lesson, we focused on dilution problems in which the incoming solution contained none of the substance being modeled. In those cases, the rate at which the substance entered the container was zero.\n\nIn many practical situations, however, the incoming solution *does* contain some amount of the substance. When this happens,\n\n- the rate of entry is no longer zero, and\n\n- the resulting differential equation takes the form of a first-order linear differential equation.\n\nFor example, suppose a storage tank initially holds $650\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g}$ of dissolved chlorine.\n\n- A solution containing $20\\,\\textrm{g/L}$ of dissolved chlorine is pumped into the tank at a rate of $5\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\n- At the same time, the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $5\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nTo construct the model for the amount of chlorine $Q$ in the tank, we compare the rates of chlorine entering and leaving. The derivative \n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which chlorine enters the tank minus the rate at which chlorine leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}}\n$$\n\nWe compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which chlorine enters the tank.\n\nThe rate of chlorine entering is the concentration times the flow rate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =100\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which chlorine exits the tank.\n\nSince the volume remains constant at $650\\,\\textrm{L},$ the concentration in the tank is $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{Q}{650}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{L}}.\n$$ So, the rate of chlorine exiting is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{650}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{650}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{130}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe net rate of change $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_\\text{in} - Q'_\\text{out}$ is the difference between the rate in and the rate out. Combining this with the initial condition $Q(0) = 1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g},$ we can state the complete initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 100 - \\dfrac{1}{130}Q\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{130}Q = 100, \\quad Q(0) = 1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g}}\n$$" "In the previous lesson, we focused on dilution problems in which the incoming solution contained none of the substance being modeled. In those cases, the rate at which the substance entered the container was zero.\n\nIn many practical situations, however, the incoming solution *does* contain some amount of the substance. When this happens,\n\n- the rate of entry is no longer zero, and\n\n- the resulting differential equation takes the form of a first-order linear differential equation.\n\nFor example, suppose a storage tank initially holds $650\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g}$ of dissolved chlorine.\n\n- A solution containing $20\\,\\textrm{g/L}$ of dissolved chlorine is pumped into the tank at a rate of $5\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\n- At the same time, the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $5\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nTo construct the model for the amount of chlorine $Q$ in the tank, we compare the rates of chlorine entering and leaving. The derivative \n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which chlorine enters the tank minus the rate at which chlorine leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}}\n$$\n\nWe compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which chlorine enters the tank.\n\nThe rate of chlorine entering is the concentration times the flow rate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(20\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(20\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =100\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which chlorine exits the tank.\n\nSince the volume remains constant at $650\\,\\textrm{L},$ the concentration in the tank is $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{Q}{650}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{L}}.\n$$ So, the rate of chlorine exiting is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{650}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{650}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{130}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe net rate of change $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_\\text{in} - Q'_\\text{out}$ is the difference between the rate in and the rate out. Combining this with the initial condition $Q(0) = 1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g},$ we can state the complete initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 100 - \\dfrac{1}{130}Q\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{130}Q = 100, \\quad Q(0) = 1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186230211 :tutorial/content "A differential operator $D$ is **linear** if the following linearity condition is satisfied.\n\n$$\nD(ay_1(x) + by_2(x)) = aD(y_1(x)) + bD(y_2(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- First, let's consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD_1(y) = x\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} +x^2y\n$$\n\nThis operator is indeed linear. To see this, we need to check that the linearity condition above holds. So we start by applying our operator to the function $y = a y_1(x) + b y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+{x}^{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince derivatives distribute over sums, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1})+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(b{y}_{2})+{x}^{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =ax\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{1})+bx\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{2})+a{x}^{2}{y}_{1}+b{x}^{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a\\underbrace{(x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}{y}_{1})}_{{D}_{1}({y}_{1})}+b\\underbrace{(x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}{y}_{2})}_{{D}_{1}({y}_{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, since $D_1(ay_1 + by_2) = aD_1(y_1) + bD_1(y_2),$ the operator $D_1$ is linear.\n\n- Now, let's consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD_2(y) = y\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nThis operator is *not* linear. To see this, we need to check that the linearity condition above does not hold. So we start by applying our operator to the function $y = a y_1(x) + b y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+ab{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+ab{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to $aD_2(y_1) + bD_2(y_2),$ we see that they are *not* the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2}) & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\ne {D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the operator $D_2$ is *not* linear." "A differential operator $D$ is **linear** if the following linearity condition is satisfied.\n\n$$\nD(ay_1(x) + by_2(x)) = aD(y_1(x)) + bD(y_2(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- First, let's consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD_1(y) = x\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} +x^2y\n$$\n\nThis operator is indeed linear. To see this, we need to check that the linearity condition above holds. So we start by applying our operator to the function $y = a y_1(x) + b y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+{x}^{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince derivatives distribute over sums, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1})+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(b{y}_{2})+{x}^{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =ax\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{1})+bx\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{2})+a{x}^{2}{y}_{1}+b{x}^{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a\\underbrace{\\left(x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}{y}_{1}\\right)}_{{D}_{1}({y}_{1})}+b\\underbrace{\\left(x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}{y}_{2}\\right)}_{{D}_{1}({y}_{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, since $D_1(ay_1 + by_2) = aD_1(y_1) + bD_1(y_2),$ the operator $D_1$ is linear.\n\n- Now, let's consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD_2(y) = y\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nThis operator is *not* linear. To see this, we need to check that the linearity condition above does not hold. So we start by applying our operator to the function $y = a y_1(x) + b y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\left(a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\right) \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+ab{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+ab{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to $aD_2(y_1) + bD_2(y_2),$ we see that they are *not* the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2}) & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\ne {D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the operator $D_2$ is *not* linear."] [:db/cas 17592186230286 :answer/value "\\sin (\\sqrt{5}x)(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{nb_{n}}{\\sqrt{5}})" "\\sin (\\sqrt{5}x)\\left(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{nb_{n}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186230287 :answer/value "\\cos (\\sqrt{5}x)(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{nb_{n}}{\\sqrt{5}})" "\\cos (\\sqrt{5}x)\\left(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{nb_{n}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186230289 :answer/value "\\sin (\\sqrt{5}x)(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{nb_{n}}{\\sqrt{5}})" "\\sin (\\sqrt{5}x)\\left(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{nb_{n}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186231290 :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ and $a_n$ for $n=0,1,2,\\ldots$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary $a_0,$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n \\geq 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $\\color{blue}n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =10({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 7x{y}^{′} & =7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (3x-1)y & =-{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(3{a}_{n-1}-{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for ${\\color{blue}n=0},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } &= (10{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +7\\lambda -1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } \\\\ &= (10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[10((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}+(3{a}_{n-1}-{a}_{n})]{x}^{\\lambda +n} &= {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[10(\\lambda +n)^{2}{a}_{n}-3(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}-{a}_{n}+3{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\ &= {x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(10(\\lambda +n)^{2}-3(\\lambda +n)-1){a}_{n}+3{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(10(\\lambda +n)^{2}-3(\\lambda +n)-1){a}_{n}+3{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -1){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(10(\\lambda +n)^{2}-3(\\lambda +n)-1){a}_{n}+3{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficients of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary $a_0\\neq0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -1){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (2\\lambda -1)(5\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we're interested in $\\lambda\\in(0,1),$ we take $\\lambda=\\dfrac12.$\n\nSimilarly, setting the coefficients of $x^n$ for $n \\geq 1$ equal to $0,$ we obtain the following recurrence relation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10(\\lambda +n)^{2}-3(\\lambda +n)-1){a}_{n}+3{a}_{n-1} & =0 \\\\ (2(\\lambda +n)-1)(5(\\lambda +n)+1){a}_{n}+3{a}_{n-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $\\lambda=\\dfrac12$ into the above and solve for $a_n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2(\\frac{1}{2}+n)-1)(5(\\frac{1}{2}+n)+1){a}_{n} & =-3{a}_{n-1} \\\\ 2n(5n+\\frac{7}{2}){a}_{n} & =-3{a}_{n-1} \\\\ n(10n+7){a}_{n} & =-3{a}_{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{n} & =-\\frac{3{a}_{n-1}}{n(10n+7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=n(10n+7).$" "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ and $a_n$ for $n=0,1,2,\\ldots$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary $a_0,$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n \\geq 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $\\color{blue}n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =10({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 7x{y}^{′} & =7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (3x-1)y & =-{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(3{a}_{n-1}-{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for ${\\color{blue}n=0},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } &= (10{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +7\\lambda -1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } \\\\ &= (10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[10((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}+7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}+(3{a}_{n-1}-{a}_{n})]{x}^{\\lambda +n} &= {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[10(\\lambda +n)^{2}{a}_{n}-3(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}-{a}_{n}+3{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\ &= {x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(10(\\lambda +n)^{2}-3(\\lambda +n)-1){a}_{n}+3{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(10(\\lambda +n)^{2}-3(\\lambda +n)-1){a}_{n}+3{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -1){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(10(\\lambda +n)^{2}-3(\\lambda +n)-1){a}_{n}+3{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficients of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary $a_0\\neq0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -1){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (2\\lambda -1)(5\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we're interested in $\\lambda\\in(0,1),$ we take $\\lambda=\\dfrac12.$\n\nSimilarly, setting the coefficients of $x^n$ for $n \\geq 1$ equal to $0,$ we obtain the following recurrence relation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10(\\lambda +n)^{2}-3(\\lambda +n)-1){a}_{n}+3{a}_{n-1} & =0 \\\\ (2(\\lambda +n)-1)(5(\\lambda +n)+1){a}_{n}+3{a}_{n-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $\\lambda=\\dfrac12$ into the above and solve for $a_n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}+n\\right)-1\\right)\\left(5\\left(\\frac{1}{2}+n\\right)+1\\right){a}_{n} & =-3{a}_{n-1} \\\\ 2n\\left(5n+\\frac{7}{2}\\right){a}_{n} & =-3{a}_{n-1} \\\\ n(10n+7){a}_{n} & =-3{a}_{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{n} & =-\\frac{3{a}_{n-1}}{n(10n+7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=n(10n+7).$"] [:db/cas 17592186231297 :question/worked-solution "The circuit is a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\cos(4t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $30\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.002\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 30I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C=\\dfrac{q}{C}=\\dfrac{q}{0.002}=500q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n6\\cos(4t)=30I+500q+5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(30I+500q+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =30\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+500\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+100I=-\\dfrac{24}{5}\\sin(4t).\n$$" "The circuit is a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\cos(4t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $30\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.002\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 30I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C=\\dfrac{q}{C}=\\dfrac{q}{0.002}=500q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n6\\cos(4t)=30I+500q+5\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(30I+500q+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\\right) \\\\ -24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =30\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+500\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+100I=-\\dfrac{24}{5}\\sin(4t).\n$$"]]
